window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("python-for-ai", {"topics":[{"key":"python-for-ai/type-hints","sources":["van Rossum, Lehtosalo, Langa, 2014 — PEP 484: Type Hints","Official docs: typing module","Official docs: mypy"],"why":"Python is dynamically typed: a name can hold anything, and a function discovers what it was given\nonly when it tries to use it. That is fine for a fifty-line script. It falls apart in exactly the\ncode AI engineers write all day: pipelines that pass dictionaries of dictionaries from a provider\nSDK through five helper functions into a prompt template, where the first symptom of a wrong shape\nis `KeyError: 'content'` deep inside a retry loop, or a `TypeError` about concatenating `str` and\n`NoneType` two hours into an evaluation run.\n\nThe naive alternative is documentation and discipline: write the expected shape in a docstring and\ntrust callers. Docstrings drift, nobody checks them, and a refactor that renames a field silently\nbreaks every caller. The second alternative, runtime assertions everywhere, catches the error but\nonly on the code path that executes, and it clutters every function with checks that belong to the\ncontract, not the logic.\n\nType hints solve this by making the contract machine-readable. A static checker walks the whole\ncall graph without running anything and reports every place where a `Message | None` flows into a\nparameter typed `Message`. The same annotations let libraries do runtime work for free: Pydantic\nbuilds validators from them, FastAPI builds request parsers and OpenAPI schemas, and the OpenAI and\nAnthropic SDKs ship fully typed so your editor knows that `response.choices[0].message.content` can\nbe `None`. One source of truth serves the checker, the runtime and the human reader.","how":"### What the interpreter does with an annotation\nAn annotation is an expression written after a colon. For a function, `def f(x: int) -> str`\nrecords `int` and `str` as metadata, exposed as `f.__annotations__`, a dict\n`{\"x\": int, \"return\": str}`. For a class or module, `name: list[str] = []` stores the type in\n`__annotations__` of that scope. Nothing else happens. Calling `f(\"hello\")` runs normally, because\nCPython performs no checking; the annotation is metadata. This design choice is deliberate: the\nlanguage keeps near-zero runtime cost and full backward compatibility, and leaves enforcement to\ntools.\n\n*When* the annotation expression is evaluated depends on the interpreter version. Up to Python 3.13\nit is evaluated eagerly at definition time, so a forward reference to a class defined later in the\nmodule fails with `NameError` unless it is quoted (`\"Message\"`) or the module opts into postponed\nevaluation with `from __future__ import annotations`, which stores every annotation as a string.\nLibraries that need the real type then call `typing.get_type_hints(obj)` to resolve the strings.\nPython 3.14 (PEP 649 and PEP 749) makes evaluation lazy by default: annotations are computed only\nwhen something first reads `__annotations__` (or calls `annotationlib.get_annotations`), so a\nforward reference no longer fails at `def` time, but it can still fail later if the name is\nundefined when a library finally inspects it. Check which version your project runs when a library\ncomplains about unresolved forward references.\n\n### What a checker does with it\nA static checker builds a model of every expression's type from the annotations plus inference. The\ngradual-typing rule is the key design choice: any unannotated value has type `Any`, and `Any` is\ncompatible with everything in both directions. That is *because* the authors needed typing to be\nadoptable one function at a time in a huge untyped ecosystem; a checker that refused to run on\npartially typed code would never have been adopted. The cost is that `Any` is a hole: once a value\nis `Any`, every downstream error is invisible. Most \"mypy said it was fine\" surprises trace back to\nan `Any` that entered from `json.loads`, an untyped library or a missing annotation.\n\nThe important vocabulary:\n\n- `X | None` (older spelling `Optional[X]`) says the value may be absent; the checker forces you to\n  narrow it with `if x is not None` before using it. Provider SDK responses are full of these.\n- Built-in generics `list[str]`, `dict[str, float]`, `tuple[int, ...]` describe containers. Prefer\n  abstract inputs (`Sequence[str]`, `Mapping[str, float]`, `Iterable[str]`) and concrete outputs\n  (`list[str]`) so callers can pass tuples or generators.\n- `Callable[[str, int], bool]` types a function value; `Awaitable[T]` and `Coroutine` type async\n  results, which matters when wrapping an async client.\n- `Any` means \"opt out\"; `object` means \"anything, but you may only use what every object supports\n  until you narrow it (for example with `isinstance`)\". Choosing\n  `object` over `Any` for genuinely unknown inputs keeps the hole small.\n- `Annotated[T, meta]` attaches extra data to a type without changing it; Pydantic's `Field` and\n  FastAPI's `Depends` use this so the same annotation carries both the static type and runtime\n  constraints.\n- `TYPE_CHECKING` lets you import a type only for the checker, breaking import cycles and avoiding\n  loading heavy packages (torch, transformers) just for an annotation.\n- `cast(T, value)` tells the checker \"trust me\" with no runtime effect; `reveal_type(x)` prints what\n  the checker thinks `x` is, which is the fastest way to debug an inference surprise.\n\n### Narrowing: how the checker learns more inside a branch\nA declared type is an upper bound; inside control flow the checker tracks something tighter. After\n`if msg.content is not None:`, `msg.content` is `str` in that block, *because* the checker models\nthe test as evidence. The same happens for `isinstance(x, ToolCall)`, `if not x: return`,\n`assert x is not None`, and a `match` case. Narrowing is what makes `X | None` usable rather than a\nnuisance: you pay one explicit check at the point of use and the rest of the block is precise. When\nthe test lives in a helper (`def is_tool_turn(m) -> bool`), the checker cannot see inside it and\nnarrows nothing; annotate it `-> TypeIs[ToolTurn]` (3.13, or `typing_extensions`) or the older\n`-> TypeGuard[ToolTurn]` so a call to the helper narrows like an `isinstance`. Narrowing ends when\nthe name is reassigned, and checkers are conservative about carrying it into a nested function or\nlambda that runs later, which is why you sometimes copy `msg.content` into a local first.\n\n### Where types come from for libraries you did not write\nA checker only knows a third-party package's types if the package ships them inline and says so\nwith a `py.typed` marker file (PEP 561), or if a separate stub package exists (`types-requests`,\n`types-PyYAML`) containing `.pyi` signature-only files. Otherwise every import from it is `Any`,\nsilently. mypy reports \"missing library stubs or py.typed marker\" for exactly this case; silencing\nit with `ignore_missing_imports` makes the error go away and keeps the hole. The major LLM SDKs ship\ninline types; many older data and ML packages are partially typed or not at all, so the boundary\nwith them is where to put your own typed wrapper.\n\n### Why runtime libraries read them\nPydantic, dataclasses and `attrs` introspect annotations at class creation, FastAPI does it when a\nroute is registered, and libraries such as `instructor` reuse Pydantic's models. A `BaseModel` with `temperature: float = 0.7` becomes a\nvalidator that coerces `\"0.7\"` to `0.7` and rejects `\"hot\"`. This is the bridge between static and\nruntime typing: the annotation you wrote for the checker becomes the schema that validates a model's\nJSON output and the JSON Schema you hand to a tool-calling API. Writing the type once and getting\nall three behaviours is the main practical payoff in AI work.","example":"A thin typed wrapper around a chat completion call. The checker will flag any caller that forgets\n`content` can be `None`, and `Annotated` carries a constraint Pydantic enforces at runtime.\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom collections.abc import Sequence\nfrom typing import Annotated, Any, Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nRole = Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\"]\n\nclass Message(BaseModel):\n    role: Role\n    content: str\n\nclass GenConfig(BaseModel):\n    model: str\n    temperature: Annotated[float, Field(ge=0.0, le=2.0)] = 0.7\n    max_tokens: Annotated[int, Field(gt=0)] = 512\n\ndef to_wire(messages: Sequence[Message]) -> list[dict[str, str]]:\n    # Sequence accepts list or tuple; the return is a concrete list for the SDK.\n    return [m.model_dump() for m in messages]\n\ndef first_text(response: Any) -> str | None:\n    # SDK responses are typed in the real SDK; Any here stands in for \"unknown JSON\".\n    choice = response[\"choices\"][0]\n    return choice[\"message\"].get(\"content\")   # may be None for tool-call turns\n\ndef reply(messages: Sequence[Message], cfg: GenConfig, response: Any) -> str:\n    text = first_text(response)\n    if text is None:                 # the checker forces this narrowing\n        raise ValueError(\"model returned no text content\")\n    return text.strip()\n\nraw = {\"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini\", \"temperature\": \"0.2\"}       # e.g. parsed from a YAML config\ncfg = GenConfig.model_validate(raw)                        # \"0.2\" coerced to 0.2 by Pydantic\nprint(cfg.temperature, type(cfg.temperature))            # 0.2 <class 'float'>\n# GenConfig(model=\"x\", temperature=3.0) raises a ValidationError: le=2.0\n```\n\nRun `mypy` on this file and delete the `if text is None` branch: the checker reports that\n`str | None` has no attribute `strip`. That is the whole value proposition, delivered before the\nfirst API call. Now do the opposite experiment: keep the branch deleted but also delete\n`first_text`'s return annotation. mypy goes silent, because an unannotated function is not\nchecked and returns `Any`, and `Any.strip()` is accepted. Same bug, same file, no error: that is\nthe `Any` hole from the previous section made concrete, and why the annotation on the function\nthat touches raw JSON matters more than any other in the file. Note the division of labour on the last lines: writing\n`GenConfig(model=\"x\", temperature=\"0.2\")` directly would run fine (Pydantic coerces it) but a\nchecker that understands Pydantic's `__init__` through `dataclass_transform` flags the `str`\nargument (Pydantic's mypy plugin, if enabled, deliberately types `__init__` parameters as `Any`\nunless you set `init_typed = True`), which is why untyped input\ngoes through `model_validate` at the boundary instead.","tradeoffs":["You gain compile-time detection of shape bugs across the call graph, but you pay the annotation effort up front; the payoff flips negative for throwaway notebooks and one-off scripts whose whole life is a single run, where the runtime error *is* the cheapest check.","Gradual typing lets you adopt incrementally, but every `Any` leak silently disables checking downstream, so a codebase that is \"typed\" at 60% often catches far fewer bugs than it looks.","Annotations double as runtime schemas for Pydantic and FastAPI, saving duplicated validation code, but it couples your static types to a library's interpretation of them (for example, how a `float` field coerces strings), which can surprise you when the library changes major version.","Precise types on third-party SDKs make the editor useful and catch `None` cases, yet the SDKs' deeply nested union types can make simple code verbose; wrapping them in your own small models usually pays for itself once the same response shape is consumed in more than one place."],"pitfalls":["Annotating a parameter as `list[str]` when callers pass tuples or generators; the symptom is a wall of checker errors at call sites, fixed by accepting `Sequence[str]` or `Iterable[str]`.","Treating annotations as enforcement; the symptom is a function typed `-> int` that happily returns a string at runtime because nothing checked it, discovered much later in a `TypeError`.","Letting `json.loads` output (typed `Any`) flow through the pipeline untouched; the symptom is a clean mypy run followed by `KeyError` in production, because `Any` suppressed every downstream error. Validate at the boundary with Pydantic or a `TypedDict` so the type becomes concrete.","Forward-reference `NameError` at import time (Python 3.13 and earlier) when one class annotates a field with another class defined below it; fix by quoting the name or using `from __future__ import annotations`, and know that some runtime libraries then need `get_type_hints` to resolve the strings.","Writing an `is_valid(x) -> bool` helper and expecting the checker to narrow after `if is_valid(x):`; the symptom is the same \"may be None\" error right after the check. Annotate the helper with `TypeIs`/`TypeGuard` or inline the test.","Using `Optional[X]` without a default and expecting it to mean \"optional argument\"; `Optional` only means \"may be None\", and the checker will still require the argument be passed."],"when":"Annotate every function that crosses a boundary: public module functions, anything that takes or\nreturns provider SDK objects, tool handlers an agent calls, and data structures that get serialized.\nInside a tight private helper, inference is usually enough. Run a checker in CI once annotations\nexist; hints without a checker are documentation that rots. Measure the `Any` surface: mypy's\n`--strict` flag or `warn_return_any` shows where typing is leaking. In day-to-day AI work, type hints\nare what let you refactor a prompt-assembly pipeline with confidence and what make Pydantic models\nand tool schemas possible at all. In interviews, the expected depth is knowing that annotations are\ninert at runtime, how gradual typing and `Any` interact, and how Pydantic turns an annotation into a\nvalidator and a JSON Schema.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes typed wrappers around SDK calls and tool handlers so mypy catches `None` content and wrong field names before an eval run burns tokens.","architect":"Sets the policy: which boundaries must be fully typed, whether CI blocks on the checker, and that shared message and tool schemas live in one typed package.","fde":"Reads a customer's untyped integration code and adds annotations at the seams first, turning vague \"it sometimes breaks\" reports into concrete checker errors within a day."},"check":{"q":"A colleague says \"we added type hints everywhere and mypy passes, but we still get\n`KeyError` and `AttributeError: 'NoneType'` in production from our LLM pipeline\". What is the most\nlikely cause and how would you fix it?","a":"Passing mypy with those runtime errors almost always means `Any` is leaking. The provider\nresponse or `json.loads` output enters as `Any`, so every attribute access and key lookup\ndownstream is unchecked; the pipeline is \"typed\" syntactically but not semantically. The fix is to\nmake the boundary concrete: parse the raw response into a Pydantic model or a `TypedDict`\nimmediately, with `content: str | None` where the API can omit it, then let the checker force\nnarrowing. Enable `warn_return_any` and `disallow_any_generics` to find remaining holes, and check\nthat third-party stubs are installed so SDK calls are not silently `Any` either."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does CPython do with the annotation in `def f(x: int) -> str` when `f(\"a\")` is called?","options":["Raises TypeError because \"a\" is not an int","Coerces \"a\" to int if possible, otherwise raises","Nothing; the annotation is stored in `f.__annotations__` and never checked","Emits a RuntimeWarning about a type mismatch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Annotations are inert metadata (evaluated at definition time up to 3.13, lazily from 3.14). Enforcement is the job of static checkers or libraries that explicitly read the annotations, never the interpreter itself."},{"q":"Why can a fully \"type-hinted\" codebase still let shape bugs through mypy?","options":["mypy only checks functions that have docstrings","Values typed `Any` (from `json.loads`, untyped libraries or missing stubs) disable checking downstream","mypy ignores `Optional` types by default","Type hints are only checked inside classes, not module-level functions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gradual typing makes `Any` compatible with everything, so once a value is `Any` every operation on it is accepted. Untyped JSON and missing stubs are the usual sources."},{"q":"Which annotation best fits a parameter that will only be iterated over, so callers may pass a list, tuple or generator?","options":["`list[str]`","`Any`","`Iterable[str]`","`dict[str, str]`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`Iterable[str]` is the least demanding contract that still guarantees iteration, so all those callers type-check. `list[str]` rejects tuples and generators; `Any` discards checking entirely."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","sources":["Levkivskyi, Lehtosalo, Langa, 2017 — PEP 544: Protocols: Structural subtyping (static duck typing)","Traut, 2022 — PEP 695: Type Parameter Syntax","Official docs: typing module"],"why":"Python code is written against behaviour, not names. \"Anything with an `embed(texts)` method\" is a\ncomplete contract, and half the value of the language is that an OpenAI client, a\nsentence-transformers model and a test stub can all satisfy it without agreeing on a base class.\nThe original type-hint design was nominal: a value was compatible with `Embedder` only if its class\ninherited from `Embedder`. That forces one of two bad outcomes. Either you write adapter classes\nwhose only purpose is to inherit from your abstract base so the checker is satisfied, or you type\nthe parameter as `Any` and lose checking exactly where it matters most, at the seam between your\ncode and a vendor's.\n\nGenerics solve a different hole. Without them, a utility like `first(xs)` must be typed\n`list[Any] -> Any`, and the element type is lost the moment it passes through. The checker then\ncannot tell you that `first(messages).content` is wrong when `messages` holds tool-call records.\nWriting one copy of the function per element type is obviously absurd. What is needed is a\nplaceholder type that is bound per call: \"whatever the elements are, that is what comes out\".\n\nProtocols (PEP 544) and type variables give the checker both capabilities while keeping runtime\nbehaviour unchanged. The design goal was explicit: make the existing duck-typed idioms of the\nstandard library (`Iterable`, `Sized`, file-like objects) expressible, rather than forcing Python\ninto a Java-shaped type system.","how":"### Structural versus nominal compatibility\nNominal typing asks \"is this class a declared subclass?\". Structural typing asks \"does this object\nhave the required members with compatible types?\". `collections.abc.Iterable` is already treated\nstructurally by checkers: any class with `__iter__` is an `Iterable`, no registration needed. A\n`Protocol` class lets you define your own such interfaces:\n\n```python\nfrom collections.abc import Sequence\nfrom typing import Protocol\nclass Embedder(Protocol):\n    dim: int\n    def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str]) -> list[list[float]]: ...\n```\n\nAny class with an `int` attribute `dim` and a compatible `embed` method is an `Embedder` to the\nchecker. Compatibility is checked member by member: attribute types, then each method's parameter\nand return types. Parameter *names* are checker-dependent: pyright rejects an implementation that\nrenames `texts`, because a caller might pass `texts=` by keyword, while mypy deliberately ignores\npositional parameter names when matching protocol methods. Declare parameters positional-only with\n`/` when names should not matter, and both checkers agree. The checker verifies the match at the point where an object is used as an `Embedder`, which\nis *why* errors show up at call sites rather than at the implementing class: the implementation\nnever declared any intent. Adding `impl: Embedder = MyEmbedder()` in a test moves the error earlier.\n\n`@runtime_checkable` additionally makes `isinstance(obj, Embedder)` work. It only checks that the\nnamed attributes exist, not their signatures or types, because a full structural check at runtime\nwould be slow and ambiguous. Treat it as a cheap plugin-discovery check, not validation.\n\nCompare this with an abstract base class (`abc.ABC` plus `@abstractmethod`): the ABC is nominal,\nenforces at instantiation time (a subclass missing a method cannot be constructed), and can carry\nshared default implementations. Protocols enforce only statically and carry no behaviour. Use the\nABC when you own every implementation; use the Protocol when implementations come from outside.\n\n### What a protocol member actually promises\nA bare attribute declaration (`dim: int`) promises a *readable and writable* attribute, because a\nconsumer typed against the protocol is allowed to assign `e.dim = 768`. So a class exposing `dim`\nas a read-only `@property` does not satisfy it, and for the same reason as `list` invariance (next\nsection), a settable attribute must match its type exactly, not just a subtype. If consumers only\nread it, declare it in the protocol as `@property def dim(self) -> int: ...`, which accepts plain\nattributes, properties and subtypes alike. Ask for the least you need, here as everywhere.\n\nA protocol can also be generic, which is where the two halves of this lesson meet:\n`class Store[T](Protocol): def get(self, key: str) -> T | None: ...` (or `Protocol[T]` on older\nversions) lets a Redis-backed `Store[Session]` and an in-memory `Store[Session]` stand in for each\nother while the checker still knows what `get` returns. And when the interface is a single call\nwith keyword arguments or overloads that `Callable[...]` cannot express (a streaming callback taking\n`token: str, *, final: bool`), a *callback protocol* with only `__call__` defined types it exactly.\n\n### Generics: type variables\nA `TypeVar` is a placeholder the checker binds per use. `def first[T](xs: Sequence[T]) -> T` (PEP\n695 syntax, Python 3.12+) or the older `T = TypeVar(\"T\")` plus `def first(xs: Sequence[T]) -> T`\ntells the checker that the return type equals the element type. A generic class,\n`class Index[T]:` or `class Index(Generic[T]):`, carries the binding across its methods, so\n`Index[Document].nearest(...)` returns a `Document`.\n\nTwo refinements exist because unconstrained `T` is often too loose. A bound,\n`T = TypeVar(\"T\", bound=BaseModel)`, says T can be any subclass of `BaseModel`, so the checker\nallows `.model_dump()` on it. A constrained TypeVar, `TypeVar(\"N\", int, float)`, says T is exactly\none of those types, which is how `sum`-like helpers avoid accepting strings.\n\n### Variance, or why `list[Dog]` is not a `list[Animal]`\nA function typed `def rename(xs: list[Animal]) -> None` may legally `xs.append(Cat())`. If the\nchecker accepted a `list[Dog]` there, the caller's list would now contain a `Cat`. So mutable\ncontainers are *invariant*: `list[Dog]` is compatible only with `list[Dog]`. Read-only views are\n*covariant*: a `Sequence[Dog]` is a `Sequence[Animal]` because nothing can be inserted. Function\nparameters are *contravariant*: a callback accepting `Animal` can be used where one accepting `Dog`\nis required. This is the single most common \"but it obviously should work\" checker error, and the\nfix is almost always to accept `Sequence[T]` or `Iterable[T]` for inputs. When you declare your own\ngeneric classes with the old syntax you must state variance (`TypeVar(\"T_co\", covariant=True)`);\nthe PEP 695 syntax infers it from how the parameter is used.\n\n### ParamSpec: typing decorators\nEvery retry, tracing and caching decorator in an AI codebase wraps arbitrary functions. Typed as\n`Callable[..., Any]`, the decorator erases the wrapped function's signature and every call through\nit becomes unchecked. `ParamSpec` captures \"whatever parameters the wrapped function has\" so that\n`Callable[P, R] -> Callable[P, R]` preserves argument and return types through the wrapper. `Self`\n(3.11) solves the related problem of fluent builders and `copy`-style methods returning the subclass\ntype rather than the base.","example":"A structural `Embedder` satisfied by two unrelated classes, a generic index that remembers what it\nstores, and a signature-preserving decorator. On Python 3.12+ the `Generic[T]` base can be replaced\nby `class Index[T]:`.\n\n```python\nimport functools, zlib\nfrom collections.abc import Callable, Sequence\nfrom typing import Generic, ParamSpec, Protocol, TypeVar, runtime_checkable\n\n@runtime_checkable\nclass Embedder(Protocol):\n    dim: int\n    def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str]) -> list[list[float]]: ...\n\nclass HashEmbedder:                       # no inheritance from Embedder anywhere\n    dim = 16\n    def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str]) -> list[list[float]]:\n        return [self._one(t) for t in texts]\n    def _one(self, text: str) -> list[float]:\n        v = [0.0] * self.dim              # bag of words; crc32 is stable across runs, hash() is not\n        for w in text.lower().split(): v[zlib.crc32(w.encode()) % self.dim] += 1.0\n        return v\n\nclass OpenAIEmbedder:                     # wraps a vendor SDK; also structurally an Embedder\n    dim = 1536\n    def __init__(self, client) -> None: self.client = client\n    def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str]) -> list[list[float]]:\n        r = self.client.embeddings.create(model=\"text-embedding-3-small\", input=list(texts))\n        return [d.embedding for d in r.data]\n\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")\nclass Index(Generic[T]):\n    def __init__(self, embedder: Embedder) -> None:\n        self.embedder = embedder\n        self.items: list[T] = []                    # annotated, or the checker sees list[Any]\n        self.vecs: list[list[float]] = []\n    def add(self, item: T, text: str) -> None:\n        self.items.append(item); self.vecs.append(self.embedder.embed([text])[0])\n    def nearest(self, text: str) -> T:              # the checker knows this is a T\n        q = self.embedder.embed([text])[0]\n        dot = lambda v: sum(a * b for a, b in zip(q, v))\n        return self.items[max(range(len(self.vecs)), key=lambda i: dot(self.vecs[i]))]\n\nP = ParamSpec(\"P\"); R = TypeVar(\"R\")\ndef logged(fn: Callable[P, R]) -> Callable[P, R]:   # keeps fn's exact signature\n    @functools.wraps(fn)\n    def inner(*args: P.args, **kwargs: P.kwargs) -> R:\n        print(\"calling\", fn.__name__); return fn(*args, **kwargs)\n    return inner\n\nidx: Index[int] = Index(HashEmbedder())\nidx.add(42, \"the answer\"); idx.add(7, \"a prime\")\nprint(idx.nearest(\"answer\"))                       # 42 (int, per the checker)\nprint(isinstance(HashEmbedder(), Embedder))        # True: attribute presence only\n```\n\nChange `nearest` to return `self.vecs[0]` and the checker reports `list[float]` is not `T` (only\nbecause `self.vecs` is annotated; an unannotated `[]` would leak `Any` and hide the error); pass\n`idx: Index[int] = Index(object())` and it reports `object` is missing `embed`.","tradeoffs":["Protocols decouple your contracts from vendor classes, but mismatch errors surface at call sites instead of at the implementation, so without an explicit `impl: Proto = Impl()` check in tests a broken implementation is only reported where it is first used.","`@runtime_checkable` gives a cheap plugin check, but it verifies attribute names only, so using it as validation of untrusted objects becomes harmful the moment signatures matter.","Generics make element types flow through containers and pipelines, but nested type variables and variance annotations raise the reading cost; for a small private helper `list[Any]` can be the honest choice.","An ABC enforces at instantiation and can hold shared defaults, which a Protocol cannot; when you own all implementations and want shared behaviour, the ABC wins despite being nominal.","`ParamSpec` preserves signatures through decorators at the cost of more boilerplate per decorator; skipping it turns every decorated function into `Callable[..., Any]` and silently disables checks."],"pitfalls":["Declaring an input as `list[Message]` and getting \"incompatible type list[ToolMessage]\" from callers; the cause is invariance of mutable containers, the fix is `Sequence[Message]`.","Implementing a Protocol method with a different parameter name (`def embed(self, docs)`); pyright reports it as incompatible with the protocol while mypy accepts it, so a team using both sees CI disagree. Match the names or declare the protocol's parameters positional-only.","Relying on `isinstance(x, SomeProtocol)` to confirm a vendor object is safe to call; the check passes with the wrong signature and the failure appears later as a `TypeError` inside the call.","Using a bare `TypeVar` in a signature where it appears only once (`def f(x: T) -> None`); it relates nothing to anything (pyright warns about exactly this), usually a sign `object` was intended.","Declaring a protocol attribute as `dim: int` when every implementation exposes a read-only property; the symptom is \"protocol member dim is not settable\" errors on classes that look correct. Declare it as a `@property` in the protocol.","Writing a decorator typed `Callable[..., Any]` and wondering why mypy never catches wrong arguments to decorated LLM-call functions; the signature was erased."],"when":"Reach for a Protocol whenever the *consumer* defines the interface and implementations come from\nelsewhere: an `LLMClient` your agent loop calls, an `Embedder` your retriever uses, a `Store` your\nmemory layer writes to. It makes test doubles trivial, because a stub only needs the methods, and it\nkeeps vendor SDKs out of your type signatures. Use an ABC instead when you ship a base class with\nshared behaviour that your own subclasses extend. Use generics for anything container-shaped whose\nelement type varies: `Cache[K, V]`, `Pipeline[In, Out]`, `Result[T]`. Use `ParamSpec` on every\ndecorator in a typed codebase. Measure success by how few `Any` and adapter-only classes remain. In\ninterviews, expect to explain structural versus nominal typing, why `list` is invariant and what\n`ParamSpec` fixes.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Types the LLM client, embedder and store as Protocols so a fake can replace each in tests without monkeypatching vendor modules.","architect":"Defines the handful of Protocols that form the boundary between agent core and providers, so swapping a vendor is an implementation change, not a refactor.","fde":"Fits a customer's existing classes (their search service, their auth client) into the product's interfaces by matching method shapes instead of asking them to subclass anything."},"check":{"q":"Your team has a `Retriever` abstract base class with three in-house subclasses. A customer\nwants to plug in their vendor's search client, which already exposes a compatible `search(query,\nk)` method. A teammate proposes writing an adapter subclass. Is there a better option, and what do\nyou give up?","a":"Define `Retriever` as a `Protocol` with the `search` signature; the vendor class then\nsatisfies it structurally with no wrapper, and the checker verifies the signature match at the\ncall site. What you give up is the ABC's instantiation-time enforcement and any shared default\nmethods, and you should make parameter names match or declare them positional-only (pyright checks\nnames; mypy does not). A common\ncompromise is both: a Protocol for consumers to depend on, and an ABC that your own\nimplementations inherit for shared behaviour, which still satisfies the Protocol."},"quiz":[{"q":"What makes a class compatible with a `Protocol` in the eyes of a type checker?","options":["It must inherit from the Protocol class or be registered with it","It must have the Protocol's members with compatible types and signatures, regardless of inheritance","It must be decorated with `@runtime_checkable`","It must define `__protocol__` returning the Protocol name"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Protocols are structural: the checker compares members. Inheritance is allowed but never required, and `@runtime_checkable` only affects `isinstance`, not static compatibility."},{"q":"Why does a checker reject passing a `list[Dog]` to a parameter typed `list[Animal]`?","options":["Because `Dog` is not a subclass of `Animal` in the checker's eyes","Because lists cannot be generic over user-defined classes","Because the function could append a non-`Dog` `Animal`, corrupting the caller's list, so `list` is invariant","Because `list` is covariant and `Animal` is more specific than `Dog`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mutable containers are invariant precisely to prevent writes of the wrong subtype. A read-only `Sequence[Animal]` accepts `list[Dog]` because nothing can be inserted through it."},{"q":"What problem does `ParamSpec` solve for a retry decorator?","options":["It lets the decorator run the wrapped function in a thread","It validates argument values at runtime before the call","It preserves the wrapped function's parameter types so calls through the decorator stay type-checked","It makes the decorator usable on async functions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without `ParamSpec`, the decorator's return type is `Callable[..., Any]` and every call to a decorated function loses argument checking. `ParamSpec` carries the original signature through."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","sources":["Lehtosalo, 2019 — PEP 589: TypedDict","Lee, Levkivskyi, Lehtosalo, 2019 — PEP 586: Literal Types","Warsaw, Bendersky, Furman, 2013 — PEP 435: Adding an Enum type to the Python standard library","Official docs: enum module"],"why":"Provider responses, tool-call arguments and agent state arrive as dicts. Typed as\n`dict[str, Any]`, the checker accepts `msg[\"contnt\"]` and `msg[\"role\"] = \"User\"`, and both bugs\nsurface as a 400 from the API or a silently skipped branch. Converting every dict into a class\nfixes the checking but costs allocations, forces conversion in both directions, and is often the\nwrong representation: the dict *is* what the SDK consumes, what gets stored in a graph state\nobject, and what gets logged. What is missing is a type for \"a dict with exactly these keys and\nthese value types\". That is `TypedDict`.\n\nThe second problem is small vocabularies. `role: str` admits `\"User\"`, `\"assistant \"` and\n`\"function\"`; `finish_reason: str` admits anything. Without a way to say \"one of these three\nstrings\", the checker cannot flag a typo, cannot tell you a `match` statement is missing a case,\nand cannot stop a new team member inventing a fourth role. `Literal` names the set with no runtime\nobject at all, which is the right cost when the strings only travel over the wire.\n\nSometimes the vocabulary needs behaviour: map each finish reason to an action, attach a retry\npolicy to each error class, refer to the set from twenty modules. A Literal's values are plain\nstrings at runtime, so there is nothing to hang a method on, and the mapping ends up in a separate\ndict that can drift from the type. `Enum` makes each member a singleton object with a name, a value,\nand whatever methods you add, at the cost of a conversion step at the JSON boundary.","how":"### TypedDict: a dict with a declared shape\nA `TypedDict` is declared like a class but creates nothing new at runtime: `Message(role=\"user\",\ncontent=\"hi\")` returns an ordinary `dict`, and `isinstance(x, Message)` raises `TypeError`. The\nclass exists only so the checker knows the keys and value types. This design was chosen *because*\nthe whole point is to type data you already hold as dicts without changing a single runtime\nobject; a wrapper class would defeat that. Consequences follow: there is no validation, so a dict\nfrom `json.loads` (typed `Any`) must be validated by something (Pydantic's `TypeAdapter`\ncan validate a `TypedDict`); otherwise it is simply trusted, because the checker accepts `Any`\nwherever a `TypedDict` is expected without a word of complaint.\n\nKeys are all required by default. `total=False` makes all of them optional; `NotRequired[T]` and\n`Required[T]` (3.11) mark individual keys, which matters for API payloads where `tool_calls` is\npresent only on some turns. Reading an optional key with `d.get(\"k\")` yields `T | None`, so the\nchecker forces a narrowing branch. `ReadOnly[T]` (3.13) marks keys that must not be assigned.\nTypedDicts are structural: one with a superset of keys is assignable where the subset is expected\n(provided the shared keys have the same value types and required-ness), which lets a generic `Message` function accept richer provider-specific dicts. Keys that are not\nvalid identifiers (`\"content-type\"`) use the functional form `TypedDict(\"Headers\", {...})`.\n\n### Literal: a finite set, checked statically\n`Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\"]` is a type whose only inhabitants are those exact strings\n(ints, bools, bytes and enum members are also allowed). The checker narrows on comparison: inside\n`if role == \"user\":` the type of `role` is `Literal[\"user\"]`. The payoff is exhaustiveness: in a\n`match` over a Literal (or an Enum), a final `case _: assert_never(x)` makes the checker report an\nerror if any member is unhandled, which is how you learn at check time, not in production, that a\nnew role was added and one handler forgot it. At runtime the *values* carry no trace: `role` is just\na `str`, so nothing stops `\"robot\"` arriving from JSON unless something validates it. The alias\nitself does survive as an object, and `typing.get_args(Role)` returns `(\"system\", \"user\",\n\"assistant\")`; that is how Pydantic turns a Literal into a schema `enum`, and how you can iterate\nthe set when you must, though the checker sees that tuple only as `tuple[Any, ...]`. That is why\nLiteral is the right tool for values that are only compared and serialized.\n\n### Enum: a finite set of runtime objects\n`class Reason(Enum): STOP = \"stop\"` creates singleton members. Each has `.name` (`\"STOP\"`) and\n`.value` (`\"stop\"`); `Reason(\"stop\")` looks up by value, `Reason[\"STOP\"]` by name, and `for r in\nReason` iterates members in definition order. Identity comparison (`r is Reason.STOP`) works because\nthere is exactly one object per member. A member whose value duplicates an earlier one becomes an\n*alias* rather than a new member, so iteration skips it, which is a documented feature that\nsurprises people. `auto()` assigns values when you do not care what they are.\n\nPlain `Enum` members are not strings: `Reason.STOP == \"stop\"` is `False` and `json.dumps` of a\nmember raises `TypeError`. `StrEnum` (3.11) and `IntEnum` mix in the primitive type so members\ncompare equal to and serialize as their value, which is what you want for any enum that crosses a\nJSON boundary. Because members are objects, you can attach methods and properties (`reason.action`)\nand the behaviour lives next to the vocabulary rather than in scattered `if` chains.\n\n### Choosing\nTypedDict for dict-shaped data whose runtime form must stay a dict (SDK payloads, graph state,\ntool arguments); switch to a Pydantic model when you need validation or the shape is consumed in\nmany places. Literal for small string sets that are only compared and serialized. Enum when you\nneed iteration, identity, methods, or a value referenced from many modules; prefer `StrEnum` when\nit meets JSON. Pydantic accepts all three and renders both Literal and Enum as JSON Schema `enum`\nconstraints (an Enum as a named entry under `$defs` referenced by `$ref`, a multi-value Literal\ninline, a single-value Literal as `const`), so the choice barely changes what the model is shown.","example":"A wire-format chat message with an optional key, a `StrEnum` carrying behaviour, and an\nexhaustive `match` the checker will police. Requires Python 3.11+ for `NotRequired`, `StrEnum`\nand `assert_never`.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom enum import StrEnum\nfrom typing import Literal, NotRequired, TypedDict, assert_never\n\nRole = Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\", \"tool\"]\n\nclass ToolCall(TypedDict):\n    id: str\n    name: str\n    arguments: str                 # JSON text, exactly as the API sends it\n\nclass ChatMessage(TypedDict):\n    role: Role\n    content: str | None\n    tool_calls: NotRequired[list[ToolCall]]\n\nclass FinishReason(StrEnum):\n    STOP = \"stop\"\n    LENGTH = \"length\"\n    TOOL_CALLS = \"tool_calls\"\n    CONTENT_FILTER = \"content_filter\"\n\n    @property\n    def truncated(self) -> bool:\n        return self is FinishReason.LENGTH\n\ndef next_action(reason: FinishReason) -> Literal[\"return\", \"continue\", \"call_tools\", \"refuse\"]:\n    match reason:\n        case FinishReason.STOP: return \"return\"\n        case FinishReason.LENGTH: return \"continue\"\n        case FinishReason.TOOL_CALLS: return \"call_tools\"\n        case FinishReason.CONTENT_FILTER: return \"refuse\"\n        case _: assert_never(reason)   # add a member above without a case: checker error here\n\nmsg: ChatMessage = {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": None,\n                    \"tool_calls\": [{\"id\": \"c1\", \"name\": \"search\", \"arguments\": '{\"q\": \"kv cache\"}'}]}\nprint(type(msg))                                   # <class 'dict'>: no wrapper object exists\nreason = FinishReason(\"tool_calls\")                # lookup by wire value\nprint(reason.truncated, next_action(reason))       # False call_tools\nprint(json.dumps({\"finish\": reason}))              # {\"finish\": \"tool_calls\"}: StrEnum is a str\n# msg[\"role\"] = \"robot\"   -> checker error: \"robot\" is not a Role\n```\n\nRemove the `CONTENT_FILTER` case and mypy or pyright reports that `assert_never` received\n`Literal[FinishReason.CONTENT_FILTER]` (the member left over after narrowing), not `Never`. That is the exhaustiveness guarantee in action.","tradeoffs":["TypedDict costs nothing at runtime and keeps data SDK-ready, but gives no validation or methods; once the same shape is consumed in several modules, a Pydantic model's validation pays for itself.","Literal gives exhaustiveness checks for free, but its values are bare strings with no behaviour, and iterating means `get_args` on the alias; once you want methods, per-member data or the set referenced from many modules, switch to an Enum.","Enum members have identity and can carry methods, but a plain Enum needs `.value` at every JSON boundary and compares unequal to raw strings; `StrEnum` removes that cost for string vocabularies.","`NotRequired` keys model optional payload fields precisely, but every `.get()` returns `T | None` and forces narrowing; when the field is always present but nullable, a required `T | None` key is simpler."],"pitfalls":["Calling `isinstance(x, SomeTypedDict)` to validate input; it raises `TypeError` at runtime because TypedDict is not a real class, so validate with Pydantic or check keys explicitly.","Comparing a plain `Enum` member to the raw wire string (`reason == \"stop\"`); it is always `False`, and the symptom is a branch that never executes. Use `StrEnum` or compare `.value`.","Passing a plain Enum member into `json.dumps`; it fails with \"Object of type ... is not JSON serializable\", typically deep inside a logging or request-serialization call.","Assigning `json.loads(...)` output straight to a `TypedDict`-typed variable; because the result is `Any`, the checker accepts it silently and trusts a shape nobody verified, and the `KeyError` appears later. Validate with `TypeAdapter(ChatMessage).validate_python(...)` at the boundary.","Using the pre-3.11 idiom `class Reason(str, Enum)` and interpolating members into prompts or log lines; on 3.11+ `f\"{Reason.STOP}\"` renders `Reason.STOP`, not `stop`, so a prompt says \"finish reason Reason.STOP\". `StrEnum` formats as its value; or interpolate `.value` explicitly.","Passing a `TypedDict` to a helper typed `dict[str, Any]`; the checker rejects it, because that helper could insert arbitrary keys. Type read-only helpers as `Mapping[str, object]`.","Giving two Enum members the same value; the second silently becomes an alias and disappears from iteration, which looks like a \"missing\" member in a dropdown or a prompt listing valid options.","Adding a new Literal or Enum member without an `assert_never` guard in handlers; nothing warns, and the new value falls through to a default branch at runtime."],"when":"Use `TypedDict` for the messages, tool-call records and state dicts that flow into and out of\nprovider SDKs and agent frameworks, where the dict is the runtime truth and validation already\nhappened at the boundary. Use `Literal` for roles, modes and finish reasons that are compared and\nserialized but never iterated, and pair every `match` over them with `assert_never`. Use `Enum`\n(usually `StrEnum`) when a vocabulary needs behaviour, iteration or a single definition shared\nacross modules, and when the same set appears in prompts, schemas and code. Measure: count the\n`str` parameters in your codebase that really mean \"one of N values\"; each one is a latent typo.\nIn interviews, the expected depth is why TypedDict has no runtime, why Literal gives exhaustiveness,\nand what `StrEnum` changes about equality and JSON.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Types the chat-message and tool-call dicts as TypedDicts and the role and finish reason fields as Literals, so a typo fails in CI instead of as an API 400.","architect":"Decides which vocabularies become shared Enums in a common package, so every service agrees on the same finish-reason and error-class names and schemas.","fde":"Maps a customer's status codes and category strings onto Enums with behaviour, turning their undocumented string conventions into one checked, iterable definition."},"check":{"q":"An agent's `finish_reason` field is a plain `str`. You want the checker to catch unhandled\nvalues when a provider adds a new one, you need to serialize it to JSON in logs, and one module\nneeds to map each value to a retry policy. Which of TypedDict, Literal or Enum do you pick and why?","a":"A `StrEnum`. Literal would give exhaustiveness checking, but its members are bare strings,\nso the retry-policy mapping would live in a separate dict keyed by those strings that can drift\nfrom the type (a checker will not tell you the dict lacks a new key). A plain\nEnum carries the mapping as a method or property but fails `json.dumps` and compares unequal to the\nwire string, costing a conversion at every boundary. `StrEnum` members are strings, so they\nserialize and compare transparently, you can still `match` over them with `assert_never` for\nexhaustiveness, and the policy lives on the member. TypedDict is irrelevant here: it types a\ndict's shape, not a single value's vocabulary."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `Message(role=\"user\", content=\"hi\")` return at runtime if `Message` is a TypedDict?","options":["An instance of `Message` with attribute access","A plain `dict`; the TypedDict class exists only for the type checker","A frozen mapping that rejects new keys","A `types.SimpleNamespace`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"TypedDict adds no runtime type. The checker uses the declaration to verify keys and values, but the object you hold is an ordinary dict, which is why `isinstance` against it fails."},{"q":"Why does `match` with a final `case _: assert_never(x)` help when `x` is a Literal or Enum?","options":["It raises at runtime for any unexpected value, replacing validation","It makes the match statement faster by skipping unreachable cases","The checker reports an error if any member of the set is not handled by an earlier case","It converts the Literal into an Enum automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"After every member is matched, the checker narrows `x` to `Never`; if one is missing, `x` still has a type and `assert_never` is a static error. It documents and enforces exhaustiveness."},{"q":"Which statement about a plain `Enum` member such as `Reason.STOP = \"stop\"` is correct?","options":["`Reason.STOP == \"stop\"` is `True` because the value is a string","`json.dumps(Reason.STOP)` produces `\"stop\"`","`Reason(\"stop\")` returns the member, and two members with the same value are aliases of one","Members can be created at runtime by assignment, such as `Reason.NEW = \"new\"`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lookup by value is supported and duplicate values create aliases. Equality with the raw string and direct JSON serialization only hold for `StrEnum`, which mixes in `str`."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pydantic","sources":["Official docs: Pydantic","Official docs: JSON Schema"],"why":"Type hints are inert: a function typed `-> Ticket` will happily return a dict with a misspelled\nkey. The data that flows through an AI system is almost entirely untrusted at the point it arrives:\nHTTP request bodies, model completions that are supposed to be JSON, tool arguments a model\ninvented, environment variables, YAML configs, rows from a customer's export. Every one of those\ncan be malformed, and a language model will malform them creatively: a string where an int was\nasked for, an extra field, a value outside the allowed set, valid-looking JSON with a trailing\nsentence.\n\nThe naive alternatives are each inadequate. Hand-written `if \"title\" not in data` checks grow into\nhundreds of lines that drift from the types they claim to enforce, and their error messages are\nwhatever each author bothered to write. A `TypedDict` or `dataclass` documents the shape for the\nchecker but validates nothing. A separate schema language (a JSON Schema file maintained next to\nthe class) duplicates the definition and the two disagree within a month.\n\nPydantic collapses these into one artifact. The annotated class is simultaneously the static type,\nthe runtime validator, the serializer and the JSON Schema source. For LLM work this is the lever:\nthe schema you send to the model and the validator you run on its answer are generated from the\nsame class, so they cannot disagree, and the validation error list is structured enough to feed\nback to the model for a repair attempt. FastAPI, the OpenAI and Anthropic SDK structured-output\nhelpers, `instructor`, LangChain and most agent frameworks build on exactly this property.\n(SDK support for passing a Pydantic class directly varies by provider and version; the generated\nJSON Schema is the portable part.)","how":"### From annotations to a validator\nAt class creation, `BaseModel`'s metaclass reads `__annotations__` and each `Field(...)` and\ncompiles them into a *core schema*, a tree describing every field's type and constraints. In\nPydantic v2 that schema is executed by `pydantic-core`, a Rust extension, which is why validation is\nfast enough to run on every request. The compile happens once per class, *because* building the\nvalidator is far more expensive than running it, and models are defined once and used millions of\ntimes.\n\n`Model.model_validate(obj)` validates a Python object (dict, attributes), `model_validate_json(s)`\nparses JSON text directly in Rust without an intermediate `json.loads`, and constructing\n`Model(**kwargs)` validates keyword arguments. On failure a `ValidationError` carries a list of\nerrors, each with `loc` (the path to the bad field), `msg` and a machine-readable `type`. That\nstructure, not a single string, is the design choice that makes \"send the errors back to the model\nand retry\" practical.\n\n### Lax versus strict\nBy default Pydantic validates in *lax* mode: `\"1200\"` becomes `1200` for an `int` field, `\"true\"`\nbecomes `True`. This is deliberate, because the inputs it was built for (query strings, env vars,\nmodel output) arrive as text. `ConfigDict(strict=True)` or `Field(strict=True)` turns coercion off\nwhen exact types matter, such as distinguishing `1` from `\"1\"` in an ID field. V2 tightened some\nrules relative to v1; for example an `int` is no longer accepted for a `str` field by default.\nUnknown fields are *ignored* by default; `ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")` makes them errors, which is\nwhat you want for tool arguments, where a hallucinated field is a signal, not noise.\n\n### The order things happen in\nKnowing the pipeline tells you where each piece of custom logic belongs. For one\n`model_validate(data)` call:\n\n1. `@model_validator(mode=\"before\")` sees the raw input (a dict, or whatever was passed) and can\n   reshape it, for example unwrapping `{\"ticket\": {...}}` that a model insisted on adding.\n2. Each field, in declaration order, is looked up by name or alias, then runs its\n   `field_validator(mode=\"before\")` functions on the raw value, then the core type validation and\n   coercion (`\"1200\"` to `1200`), then declared constraints (`ge`, `min_length`), then its\n   `mode=\"after\"` validators, which receive an already-typed value. `mode=\"wrap\"` surrounds the core\n   step and can short-circuit it.\n3. Errors from every field are collected rather than stopping at the first, *because* a repair\n   prompt that lists all five problems converges in one retry instead of five.\n4. If all fields passed, `@model_validator(mode=\"after\")` runs on the built instance; this is where\n   cross-field invariants such as `start < end` go, since only here are all fields typed and present.\n\nValidation happens at construction only. Assigning `t.affected_users = \"lots\"` afterwards is not\nchecked unless the model sets `validate_assignment=True`, and `Model.model_construct(**data)` skips\nvalidation entirely, which is the fast path for data you already validated and a hole for data you\ndid not. `frozen=True` makes instances immutable and hashable.\n\n### Validators, serialization, schema\n`field_validator` is for single-field normalisation and checks, `model_validator` for the\nreshaping and cross-field cases above. `Field(alias=\"userId\")` maps a wire name onto a Python name\n(set `populate_by_name=True` to accept both).\n`@computed_field` exposes derived values in dumps. `model_dump()` produces a dict and\n`model_dump_json()` a string, with `exclude`, `by_alias` and `mode=\"json\"` to control the output.\n`model_json_schema()` emits a JSON Schema for the class, including `enum` for `Literal` and `Enum`\nfields, `minimum`/`maximum` from `ge`/`le`, and `description` from `Field(description=...)`, which\nis text the model actually reads. `TypeAdapter(list[Ticket])` gives validation and schema for\ntypes that are not models, including `TypedDict`s and plain containers. Discriminated unions\n(`Field(discriminator=\"type\")`) route a payload to the right variant by a tag field, which is how\nyou validate \"one of these N tool calls\".\n\n### Against the alternatives\nStdlib `dataclasses` are lighter and have no validation; use them for internal records you\nconstruct yourself. `TypedDict` is static-only and keeps data as dicts; use it for wire shapes that\nmust stay dicts, and validate them with a `TypeAdapter` if needed. `attrs` offers validators but no\nschema generation. `msgspec` is faster with a smaller feature set. Pydantic's edge is the single\ndefinition serving checker, validator, serializer and schema, which is why the AI ecosystem\nstandardized on it. Settings management lives in the separate `pydantic-settings` package.\n\n### Sharp edges\nValidation has a cost: do it at boundaries, not inside hot loops where the data was already\nvalidated. Lax coercion can mask bugs (a float `1.0` silently becoming `1`). `Optional` fields\nrender as `anyOf` with `null` in the schema, and some providers' strict structured-output modes\nrequire every property to be listed as required with `additionalProperties: false`; the SDK helpers\nrewrite the schema for you, a hand-built schema must do it itself. V1 and v2 APIs differ\n(`validator` became `field_validator`, `.dict()` became `.model_dump()`, `parse_obj` became\n`model_validate`, the inner `Config` class became `model_config = ConfigDict(...)`), so copying\nold snippets produces deprecation warnings or errors.","example":"Structured extraction with a schema generated from the model and a validation-error message fed\nback for a repair attempt. `extra=\"forbid\"` makes an invented field fail loudly.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict, Field, ValidationError, field_validator\n\nclass Ticket(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")          # hallucinated keys are errors\n    title: str = Field(min_length=3, max_length=120, description=\"Short summary of the issue\")\n    severity: Literal[\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]\n    affected_users: int = Field(ge=0)\n    tags: list[str] = Field(default_factory=list)\n\n    @field_validator(\"tags\")\n    @classmethod\n    def normalise(cls, v: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n        return sorted({t.strip().lower() for t in v if t.strip()})\n\nschema = Ticket.model_json_schema()      # send this as the tool/response schema\nprint(schema[\"properties\"][\"severity\"][\"enum\"])          # ['low', 'medium', 'high']\nprint(schema[\"properties\"][\"affected_users\"][\"minimum\"]) # 0\n\ndef parse_or_feedback(text: str) -> Ticket | str:\n    try:\n        return Ticket.model_validate_json(text)\n    except ValidationError as e:\n        issues = \"; \".join(f\"{'.'.join(map(str, err['loc']))}: {err['msg']}\" for err in e.errors())\n        return f\"Your JSON was rejected. Fix these fields and resend: {issues}\"\n\nraw = '{\"title\": \"Login broken\", \"severity\": \"critical\", \"affected_users\": \"1200\", \"tags\": [\"Auth\", \"auth \"]}'\nprint(parse_or_feedback(raw))\n# Your JSON was rejected. Fix these fields and resend: severity: Input should be 'low', 'medium' or 'high'\nt = parse_or_feedback(raw.replace(\"critical\", \"high\"))\nprint(t)        # title='Login broken' severity='high' affected_users=1200 tags=['auth']\nprint(type(t.affected_users))   # <class 'int'>: \"1200\" was coerced in lax mode\nprint(t.model_dump_json())      # {\"title\":\"Login broken\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"affected_users\":1200,\"tags\":[\"auth\"]}\n```\n\nThe first call shows the pipeline at work: `affected_users` passed (lax coercion of `\"1200\"`\nhappened in the core step), `tags` passed (the after-validator received a real `list[str]`), and\nonly `severity` failed, yet the error list would have named every failing field had there been more.\nThe second call succeeds for the same reasons, and the after-validator collapsed `\"Auth\"` and\n`\"auth \"` into one tag. Because of `extra=\"forbid\"`, the schema also carries\n`\"additionalProperties\": false`, so the model is told the same rule the validator enforces. The feedback string is what an\n`instructor`-style retry loop sends back to the model.","tradeoffs":["One class gives you static type, validator, serializer and schema, but it couples all four to Pydantic's interpretation; a major-version change (v1 to v2) touches every boundary at once.","Lax coercion makes real-world text inputs just work, but it can hide type confusion upstream; flip to strict mode for identifiers, money and anything where `\"1\"` and `1` must differ.","Rust-backed validation is fast per call, yet running it on every object in a tight loop still dominates when the data was already validated; validate once at the boundary and pass models.","Rich validation and nested models make schemas expressive, but deeply nested or recursive schemas can confuse models and some providers' strict modes; flatten or simplify when the model's output quality drops.","`extra=\"forbid\"` catches hallucinated fields early, but on *inputs you do not control* (a vendor adding a field) it turns a harmless addition into an outage; use it for model output, not for third-party webhooks."],"pitfalls":["Leaving `extra` at the default `ignore` for tool arguments, so a model's misspelled key vanishes silently; the symptom is a tool receiving a default value and behaving as if the argument was never supplied.","Hand-editing a JSON Schema instead of generating it from the model; the symptom is validation rejecting responses that the schema allowed, or vice versa, after one side changed.","Catching `ValidationError` and logging `str(e)` only; you lose the structured `loc`/`type` list that would let you retry the model with precise feedback or aggregate failure modes.","Mixing v1 and v2 idioms (`@validator`, `.dict()`, class `Config`) in a v2 project; the symptom is a wall of deprecation warnings, then breakage when the compatibility shims are removed.","Using a mutable default like `tags: list[str] = []`; Pydantic copies defaults per instance so this does not share state, but readers assume it does, and `default_factory` states the intent.","Mutating a validated model and assuming the new value was checked; with the default `validate_assignment=False`, `t.severity = \"critical\"` succeeds and the bad value surfaces later in serialization or a downstream consumer.","Validating inside a per-token streaming loop; the symptom is CPU-bound latency that disappears when you accumulate chunks and validate once at the end."],"when":"Reach for Pydantic at every trust boundary: HTTP requests and responses, LLM structured output,\ntool-call arguments, configuration, and any file or message produced by another system. Define the\nmodel, generate the schema from it, validate the input with it, and pass the resulting object\ninward. Do not reach for it for small internal records you construct yourself in a hot path; a\n`dataclass` is lighter there. Measure the error distribution from `ValidationError.errors()` over\ntime: the `type` field tells you whether the model keeps failing on the same constraint, which is\na prompt or schema problem, not a code problem. In day-to-day AI work Pydantic is the connective\ntissue between prompts, SDKs and your code; in interviews, be ready to explain lax versus strict,\nhow the schema and the validator stay in sync, and what you do when the model's JSON fails.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines response and tool-argument models, generates schemas from them, and builds the validate-then-retry loop around model output with structured error feedback.","architect":"Decides where validation happens (edge, service, agent core), which models are shared across services, and the strictness policy for inputs you control versus vendor inputs.","fde":"Models a customer's messy export or API payload as Pydantic classes on day one, so every malformed record is reported with a field path instead of a stack trace."},"check":{"q":"A model is asked to return JSON matching a Pydantic class. About 5% of responses fail\nvalidation. Walk through how you would diagnose and reduce that rate without just retrying blindly.","a":"First log `ValidationError.errors()` as structured data and aggregate by `loc` and `type`.\nIf one field dominates, it is usually a schema or prompt problem: an ambiguous `description`, an\nenum the model keeps paraphrasing, or a nested optional the provider's strict mode handles badly;\nfix the schema or the field description. Check that the schema sent to the model is generated from\nthe same class that validates, not a stale copy. Consider `extra=\"forbid\"` so hallucinated fields\nsurface. Then add a bounded repair loop that sends the exact error list back to the model, and\nmeasure the residual failure rate; if it is still material, simplify the schema or split the\nextraction into two smaller calls."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Pydantic compile a model's annotations into a core schema once at class creation?","options":["To register the model with FastAPI automatically","Building the validator is expensive while running it is cheap, and models are defined once but validated many times","Because annotations are only available at class creation time","To make the model immutable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compilation happens once per class and the compiled validator (executed in Rust) runs on every call. Paying the build cost per validation would make request-time validation far too slow."},{"q":"With default settings, what happens when an LLM returns `{\"title\": \"x\", \"severty\": \"high\"}` for a model whose field is `severity`?","options":["A `ValidationError` for the unknown field `severty`","`severty` is ignored and validation fails only because `severity` is missing","`severty` is matched to `severity` by fuzzy matching","The model instance is created with `severity=None`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`extra` defaults to `ignore`, so the misspelled key is dropped. The required `severity` is then missing. Set `extra=\"forbid\"` to make the hallucinated field itself an error."},{"q":"Which is the strongest reason to generate the JSON Schema from the Pydantic model rather than writing it by hand?","options":["Generated schemas are smaller","Hand-written schemas cannot express enums","The schema the model is given and the validator applied to its answer cannot drift apart","Providers reject schemas that were not produced by Pydantic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One definition serves both roles. A hand-maintained copy will eventually allow something the validator rejects or vice versa, producing failures that are hard to attribute."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/dataclasses","sources":["Smith, 2017 — PEP 557: Data Classes","Official docs: dataclasses module"],"why":"Every system accumulates classes that are mostly data: a `GenerationConfig` with a model name and a\ntemperature, a `RetrievedChunk` with text and a score, an agent `Step` with a tool name, arguments\nand output. Written by hand, each needs an `__init__` that assigns every field, a `__repr__` so\nlogs are readable, an `__eq__` so tests can compare them, and sometimes `__hash__` so they can be\ncache keys. That is thirty lines of boilerplate per class, and the boilerplate is where the bugs\nlive: a field added to `__init__` but forgotten in `__eq__`, a repr that omits the one field you\nneeded in the incident log, a `__hash__` that disagrees with `__eq__` and silently breaks sets.\n\nThe naive alternatives each fail in a specific way. A plain dict has no field names the checker\ncan verify, no repr beyond braces and no way to attach methods. A tuple or `NamedTuple` is\nimmutable and hashable but also iterable and indexable, so `chunk[1]` compiles and the meaning of\nposition 1 changes when you add a field. Writing the dunder methods by hand is correct until the\nsecond person edits the class.\n\nDataclasses generate the methods from the single source of truth, the annotated field list, so\nthey cannot disagree with it. The design choice to generate code at class creation (rather than\nuse a dynamic `__getattr__` trick) was made so the resulting class is an ordinary class: fast\nattribute access, normal inheritance, nothing magic at call time.","how":"### What the decorator generates\n`@dataclass` inspects the class's `__annotations__` in definition order and, for each annotated\nname, creates a field. It then synthesizes methods and attaches them to the class:\n\n- `__init__(self, a, b=default)` assigning each field, with fields that have defaults after those\n  that do not (the same rule as function parameters, and for the same reason).\n- `__repr__` printing `ClassName(a=..., b=...)`, which is what makes agent traces readable.\n- `__eq__` comparing instances field by field as tuples, only when the other object is the same\n  class.\n- With `order=True`, `__lt__` and friends comparing field tuples in order.\n- With `frozen=True`, `__setattr__` and `__delattr__` that raise `FrozenInstanceError`.\n- `__hash__` only when it is safe: `frozen=True` with `eq=True` gives a hash of the field tuple;\n  `eq=True` alone sets `__hash__ = None`, making instances unhashable *because* a mutable object\n  whose hash can change would corrupt any set or dict it lives in. `unsafe_hash=True` overrides\n  this when you promise not to mutate.\n- With `slots=True` (3.10), a `__slots__` declaration, which removes the per-instance `__dict__`,\n  cuts memory per instance and speeds attribute access, and rejects assignment to undeclared\n  attributes. That last property catches typos like `cfg.temprature = 0.5`.\n- With `kw_only=True` (3.10), all fields become keyword-only, which lets you add fields in any\n  order and avoids positional-argument mistakes on classes with many same-typed fields.\n\n### Defaults and `field()`\n`x: list[str] = []` is rejected with `ValueError: mutable default ... use default_factory`,\nbecause a class-level default is evaluated once and would be shared by every instance; the module\nchecks for this explicitly since the aliasing bug is otherwise silent. The check is a heuristic,\nnot a guarantee: it rejects defaults whose type is unhashable (`list`, `dict`, `set`; before 3.11\nexactly those three), so a mutable but hashable object, such as an instance of an ordinary class,\npasses and is shared. `field(default_factory=list)`\ncalls the factory per instance. `field()` also controls participation: `repr=False` hides large\npayloads from logs, `compare=False` excludes a timestamp from equality, `init=False` marks a\nderived field set in `__post_init__`.\n\n### Hooks and helpers\n`__post_init__` runs after the generated `__init__` and is where derived fields and cheap\ninvariant checks go (`if not 0 <= self.temperature <= 2: raise ValueError`). `InitVar[T]` declares\nan init-only parameter that is passed to `__post_init__` but not stored. `dataclasses.replace(obj,\n**changes)` returns a new instance with some fields changed, which is the idiom for \"modifying\" a\nfrozen instance. `asdict(obj)` converts recursively into dicts and lists, deep-copying along the\nway, so it is convenient for logging but expensive for large nested structures; `fields(cls)`\nexposes the field metadata for generic code. `match_args` (3.10) lets instances be destructured in\n`match` statements positionally.\n\n### Inheritance and frozen derived fields\nFields are collected by walking the class's bases in reverse MRO order: base-class fields come\nfirst, in their declaration order, then the subclass's, with a redeclared name keeping its original\nposition. The generated `__init__` is one flat parameter list in that order, *which is why* a\nsubclass cannot add a required field after a base that has a default: the signature would be\n`(model, temperature=0.0, tenant)`, which Python rejects. `kw_only=True` on the subclass, or a\n`_: KW_ONLY` sentinel before the new fields, moves them out of the positional sequence and the\nconflict disappears.\n\nFrozen classes have one sharp interaction with `__post_init__`. The generated `__init__` sets\nfields through `object.__setattr__` to bypass the freeze, but your `__post_init__` does not get that\nprivilege: `self.n_tokens = ...` raises `FrozenInstanceError`. A derived field on a frozen class\n(`n_tokens: int = field(init=False)`) must be set with `object.__setattr__(self, \"n_tokens\", ...)`,\nthe same escape hatch the decorator itself uses.\n\n### Where it stops\nA dataclass does not validate types: `GenConfig(temperature=\"hot\")` constructs happily. It does not\nparse JSON, generate schemas or coerce input. Those are Pydantic's responsibilities, and Pydantic\neven offers `pydantic.dataclasses.dataclass` that adds validation to the stdlib form. The dividing\nline: dataclasses for records *you* construct from already-trusted values; Pydantic for data that\narrives from outside.","example":"A frozen, slotted config that works as a cache key, and a mutable step record with a per-instance\ndefault list. `replace` produces a varied config without touching the original.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field, replace, asdict\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)\nclass GenConfig:\n    model: str\n    temperature: float = 0.0\n    max_tokens: int = 256\n    stop: tuple[str, ...] = ()            # tuple, not list, so the instance stays hashable\n\n    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        if not 0.0 <= self.temperature <= 2.0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"temperature out of range: {self.temperature}\")\n\n@dataclass(slots=True)\nclass Step:\n    tool: str\n    args: dict[str, object]\n    started: float = field(default_factory=time.time)\n    output: str | None = field(default=None, repr=False)   # keep big payloads out of logs\n    notes: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)         # a fresh list per instance\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=1024)\ndef completion(prompt: str, cfg: GenConfig) -> str:        # cfg is hashable, so it can be a key\n    return f\"<{cfg.model}@{cfg.temperature}> {prompt[:24]}\"\n\nbase = GenConfig(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\")\nhot = replace(base, temperature=0.9)                       # new object; base unchanged\ncompletion(\"Summarise the KV cache\", base)\ncompletion(\"Summarise the KV cache\", GenConfig(\"gpt-4o-mini\"))\nprint(completion.cache_info().hits)                        # 1: equal configs are the same key\ntry:\n    base.temperature = 1.0\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(type(e).__name__)                                # FrozenInstanceError\n\ns1, s2 = Step(\"search\", {\"q\": \"a\"}), Step(\"search\", {\"q\": \"b\"})\ns1.notes.append(\"retried\"); print(s2.notes)                # []: default_factory did its job\nprint(s1)                                                  # Step(tool='search', args={'q': 'a'}, started=..., notes=['retried'])\nprint(asdict(s1)[\"notes\"] is s1.notes)                     # False: asdict deep-copies\n```\n\nChange `stop` to `list[str]` with a `default_factory` and `lru_cache` raises `TypeError:\nunhashable type`, because a frozen dataclass's hash is built from its fields and a list has none.","tradeoffs":["You get correct, consistent dunder methods for free, but no validation; the moment input comes from outside the process, a Pydantic model is the right tool despite its extra weight.","`frozen=True` gives hashability and safe sharing across threads and cache keys, but every update allocates a new object via `replace`, which matters only in tight loops over large records.","`slots=True` saves memory and catches attribute typos, but instances lose `__dict__`, so libraries that stash attributes on objects or rely on `vars(obj)` break.","`order=True` gives sorting for free, but it compares fields positionally in declaration order, which is rarely the ordering you actually mean for a domain object.","`asdict` is convenient for logging and serialization, yet it deep-copies the whole tree; for large nested records a hand-written `to_dict` (or `vars(obj)`, on non-slotted classes) is cheaper."],"pitfalls":["Assuming the mutable-default check catches everything; `field(default=[])` is rejected like `= []`, but `field(default=RetryPolicy())` (a hashable object of an ordinary class) or an unannotated class attribute on a base class is silently shared, and the symptom is one instance's changes showing up in every other instance.","Adding `__eq__` by hand or setting `eq=True` on a mutable class and then using instances as dict keys; the symptom is `TypeError: unhashable type` or, with `unsafe_hash`, items vanishing from sets after mutation.","Putting a required field after a defaulted one in a subclass; the symptom is `TypeError: non-default argument follows default argument` at import, fixed by `kw_only=True`.","Assigning a derived field in `__post_init__` of a frozen dataclass; the symptom is `FrozenInstanceError` raised from inside the constructor, which looks like the object was frozen too early. Use `object.__setattr__` for that one assignment.","Expecting `GenConfig(temperature=\"0.7\")` to coerce or fail; it stores the string, and the error appears later as a `TypeError` in arithmetic. Validate in `__post_init__` or use Pydantic.","Storing a list in a frozen dataclass and assuming the whole object is immutable; the reference is frozen but the list is not, and hashing fails or, worse, the \"immutable\" config changes."],"when":"Use a dataclass for records you construct from trusted values inside your process: configs,\nintermediate results, agent steps, cache keys, test fixtures. Prefer `frozen=True` for anything\nshared across tasks or used as a key, and `slots=True` for anything created in large numbers\n(retrieved chunks, token events). Switch to Pydantic when the data crosses a trust boundary or\nneeds a schema, and to `NamedTuple` only when tuple unpacking is genuinely the interface. Measure\nmemory with `tracemalloc` when instance counts reach the millions; slots remove the per-instance\n`__dict__`, which can be a large share of a small record's footprint, though the saving is smaller\non 3.11+ where instance dicts are stored more compactly, so measure rather than assume.\nDay to day, dataclasses are the default container for anything with more than two fields; in\ninterviews, expect to explain `default_factory`, when `__hash__` is generated and why, and the line\nbetween dataclasses and Pydantic.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Models generation configs and agent steps as frozen, slotted dataclasses so they are hashable cache keys with readable reprs in traces.","architect":"Sets the rule that internal records are dataclasses and boundary types are Pydantic, so validation cost and schema generation stay where they belong.","fde":"Turns a customer's ad-hoc dicts into dataclasses during an integration, giving their pipeline checked field names and reprs that make the first incident debuggable."},"check":{"q":"You want to memoize LLM calls keyed on the prompt plus a configuration object holding the\nmodel, temperature and a list of stop sequences. The config is a mutable dataclass and `lru_cache`\nraises `TypeError: unhashable type`. Explain why and how you would fix it.","a":"A dataclass with `eq=True` (the default) and `frozen=False` sets `__hash__ = None`, because\na hashable object must not change its hash, and a mutable record can. Even with `frozen=True`, the\n`list` of stop sequences is unhashable, so the generated hash, which hashes the field tuple, fails.\nThe fix is `@dataclass(frozen=True)` with `stop: tuple[str, ...]` instead of a list, which gives a\nstable hash and lets `replace` produce variants. If the config really must be mutable, derive an\nexplicit immutable key (a tuple of the fields) and cache on that instead."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does `@dataclass` reject `tags: list[str] = []` with a ValueError?","options":["Lists cannot be dataclass fields","The default would be evaluated once and shared by every instance, so the module demands `default_factory`","Empty lists are falsy and would break `__eq__`","Type hints for lists require `List[str]` from `typing`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A class-level default is a single object. Sharing it across instances is the classic aliasing bug, so dataclasses raise at definition time and `field(default_factory=list)` creates one per instance."},{"q":"Under which setting does `@dataclass` generate a usable `__hash__`?","options":["`eq=True` alone","`order=True` alone","`frozen=True` together with `eq=True`","`slots=True` alone"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hashing requires the hash to be stable, which a mutable object cannot guarantee; `eq=True` without `frozen` therefore sets `__hash__ = None`. Frozen plus eq hashes the field tuple safely."},{"q":"What is the main thing a dataclass does not do that Pydantic does?","options":["Generate a readable `__repr__`","Support default values","Validate and coerce field values at construction","Support inheritance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dataclasses generate methods from annotations but never check them; `Config(temperature=\"hot\")` succeeds. Pydantic validates, coerces and reports structured errors, which is why it owns boundaries."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/object-model-and-mutability","sources":["Official docs: The Python Language Reference, Data model","Official docs: copy module"],"why":"A chat helper is written as `def ask(question, history=[])`. It works in the unit test, then in\nproduction every user sees every other user's messages. A prompt template dict is `.copy()`-ed per\ntenant and the `tools` list inside it still accumulates everyone's tools. An embedding matrix is\nsliced for normalisation and the original rows change too. A tensor is updated with an in-place\n`add_` and autograd raises on the backward pass. None of these are library bugs; they are the same\nfact seen four times: Python passes and stores *references to objects*, and mutating through any\nreference is visible through all of them.\n\nThe intuition most engineers bring from C-like languages, \"a variable holds a value and assignment\ncopies it\", is wrong here and nothing in the syntax warns you. `a = b` is indistinguishable from a\ncopy until someone mutates. The naive defence, \"copy everything defensively\", costs memory and time\nthat AI workloads cannot spare (copying a gigabyte of embeddings per request) and still fails because\nshallow copies share their contents. The real defence is a correct model: know what an object is, know\nwhich names point at it, know which operations create a new object and which change the existing one,\nand prefer immutable values for anything shared. That model is also what makes concurrency with\nthreads and async tasks tractable, because immutable data cannot race.","how":"### Objects, names and identity\nEvery value is an object with an identity (`id()`, its address in CPython), a type and a value.\nNamespaces (module globals, function locals, instance `__dict__`) are mappings from names to\nobjects. `x = expr` binds the name `x` to whatever object `expr` produced; `y = x` binds a second name\nto the *same* object, so `x is y` is `True`. Nothing was copied, because the language is designed so\nthat binding is O(1) regardless of what is bound. `==` asks whether two objects have equal values;\n`is` asks whether they are the same object. Use `is` only for singletons like `None`: CPython caches\nsmall integers and interns some strings, so `is` on numbers or text gives answers that depend on\nimplementation details.\n\n### Everything is an object, including functions and classes\nFunctions, classes and modules are ordinary objects too, which is what turns several \"language\nquirks\" into one rule. A function object stores its default values in `f.__defaults__`, built when\n`def` executes; a class object stores its body's assignments in its own `__dict__`. Neither is\nre-created per call or per instance, so anything mutable stored there is shared by construction.\n\n### Attribute lookup: where `obj.x` comes from\nReading `obj.x` follows a fixed search order: first data descriptors on the type (a `property`,\na slot), then the instance's own `__dict__`, then the class and its bases in MRO order (where plain\nfunctions become bound methods). *Writing* `obj.x = v` is different: unless a descriptor\nintercepts it, it always stores into the instance `__dict__`. That asymmetry is the whole mechanism\nbehind the class-attribute bug. With `class Agent: history = []`, `self.history.append(m)` *reads*\n`history`, finds the class's single list, and mutates it for every agent; `self.history = [m]`\nwould instead have created a private instance attribute that shadows the class one. Same-looking\ncode, opposite sharing.\n\n### Operators are method calls on the type\n`a + b` calls `type(a).__add__(a, b)`, `a[i] = v` calls `__setitem__`, and `a += b` calls\n`__iadd__` if the type defines it, otherwise falls back to `a = a + b`. Which one you get is a\nproperty of the type, not the syntax, and the next section is that rule applied.\n\n### Mutable versus immutable\nImmutable objects (`int`, `float`, `str`, `bytes`, `tuple`, `frozenset`, `bool`, `None`) cannot\nchange; every operation yields a new object. `s += \"x\"` therefore *rebinds* `s` and any other name\nstill sees the old string. Mutable objects (`list`, `dict`, `set`, `bytearray`, user-defined class\ninstances, NumPy arrays, PyTorch tensors) change in place: `lst += [1]` calls `__iadd__`, which\nextends the existing list, and every alias sees the change. The same `+=` spelling does opposite\nthings, which is why the distinction has to live in your head and not in the syntax. Methods that\nmutate conventionally return `None` (`list.sort`, `dict.update`, `random.shuffle`) precisely so\nthat `x = lst.sort()` fails loudly rather than letting you believe you got a copy.\n\n### Function arguments\nPython passes object references by assignment. Inside a function, a parameter is a new local name\nbound to the caller's object. Mutating that object (`items.append`) is visible to the caller;\nrebinding the name (`items = []`) is not. Default argument values are evaluated *once*, at `def`\ntime, and stored on the function object, which is why a mutable default is shared by all calls and\n`None` with an in-body `if x is None: x = []` is the idiom.\n\n### Containers hold references\nA list of lists built with `[[0] * 3] * 3` holds three references to one inner list; changing\n`grid[0][0]` changes every row. Shallow copies (`list(x)`, `x[:]`, `dict.copy()`, `copy.copy`)\ncreate a new outer container that references the *same* inner objects. `copy.deepcopy` recurses, at\nO(size) cost, and fails on objects that cannot be copied or pickled, such as locks and sockets, and\ntherefore on most SDK clients that hold them. Class attributes are shared\nby all instances: `class Agent: history = []` is one list for every agent; the per-instance version\nmust be created in `__init__`.\n\n### Hashing and equality\nDict keys and set members must be hashable, and the data model requires equal objects to hash\nequally. Mutable built-ins are unhashable for that reason; a user class is hashable by identity\nunless it defines `__eq__` without `__hash__`, in which case it becomes unhashable, which is the rule\n`@dataclass` applies. Mutating an object after using it as a key (through a custom `__hash__`) makes\nit unfindable.\n\n### Arrays and tensors\nNumPy basic slicing (`a[:, :10]`, `a[::2]`) returns a *view* sharing the buffer, so normalising the\nslice normalises the original; `a.copy()` or fancy indexing returns an independent array. PyTorch\ndistinguishes in-place methods by a trailing underscore (`t.add_()`), and autograd refuses in-place\nchanges to values it still needs for the backward pass. In both libraries in-place operations exist\nto avoid allocating gigabytes, so the trade is explicit: memory versus aliasing risk.\n\n### Lifetime\nCPython frees an object when its reference count drops to zero; cycles are collected by the garbage\ncollector later. A large tensor stays resident while any name, container, closure, cached traceback\nor notebook output history references it, which is why `del x` sometimes frees nothing.","example":"Four aliasing bugs that occur in real LLM code, each followed by the fix: a shared mutable default\nargument that leaks conversation history between callers, a shallow-copied prompt template that\nleaks tools between tenants, the `+=` difference between a list and a string, and nested-list\nmultiplication that aliases rows. Every print shows the surprising result and, where applicable, the\ncorrect version beside it.\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\ndef ask(question: str, history: list[dict] = []) -> list[dict]:   # BUG: one list for every call\n    history.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question})\n    return history\n\nprint(len(ask(\"a\")), len(ask(\"b\")))              # 1 2  -> the second caller sees the first turn\n\ndef ask_ok(question: str, history: list[dict] | None = None) -> list[dict]:\n    history = [] if history is None else history  # fresh list per call unless one is passed\n    return [*history, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n\nBASE = {\"system\": \"You are a support bot.\", \"tools\": [\"search\"]}\n\ndef per_tenant(tenant: str) -> dict:\n    cfg = BASE.copy()                            # shallow: cfg[\"tools\"] IS BASE[\"tools\"]\n    cfg[\"tools\"].append(f\"{tenant}_crm\")         # leaks into every later tenant\n    return cfg\n\nper_tenant(\"acme\"); print(BASE[\"tools\"])        # ['search', 'acme_crm']\n\ndef per_tenant_ok(tenant: str) -> dict:\n    cfg = copy.deepcopy(BASE)                    # or rebuild; or store BASE as tuples\n    cfg[\"tools\"] = [*cfg[\"tools\"], f\"{tenant}_crm\"]\n    return cfg\n\na = [1, 2]; b = a; c = a[:]\nb += [3]                                        # list.__iadd__ mutates the shared list in place\nprint(a is b, a, c)                             # True [1, 2, 3] [1, 2]  (c is a shallow copy)\ns = \"x\"; t = s; s += \"y\"                        # str is immutable: += rebinds s only\nprint(t)                                        # x\ngrid = [[0] * 2] * 2; grid[0][0] = 9            # both rows are the same inner list\nprint(grid, [[0] * 2 for _ in range(2)])        # [[9, 0], [9, 0]] [[0, 0], [0, 0]]\n```","tradeoffs":["Immutable values (tuples, frozen dataclasses, `MappingProxyType`) are safe to share across tenants, threads and tasks and usable as cache keys, but every change allocates; for hot loops over large state, controlled in-place mutation wins.","In-place array and tensor operations avoid copying gigabytes, at the cost of aliasing and autograd hazards; when memory is not the constraint, out-of-place operations are easier to reason about.","`deepcopy` guarantees isolation but is O(size), can be slower than reconstructing the object, and fails on unpicklable members such as clients and locks; rebuild from a factory instead.","Freezing a container is shallow: a tuple of lists still has mutable lists inside, so the protection must be applied at every level or the invariant is only cosmetic."],"pitfalls":["A mutable default argument; the symptom is state leaking between calls, and tests that pass in isolation but fail when run together.","A mutable class attribute used as per-instance state; every `Agent` shares one `history` and the symptom is cross-conversation contamination.","Shallow-copying a nested config; the symptom is tenant A's tools or headers appearing in tenant B's requests.","`x = lst.sort()` or `cfg = d.update(...)`; the symptom is `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute`, because mutating methods return `None` by convention.","Mutating a list or dict while iterating over it; lists skip elements silently, dicts raise `RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration`.","Normalising a NumPy slice or a tensor view in place and discovering the source matrix changed; the symptom is retrieval scores that drift between runs.","GPU memory that does not come back after `del`; another reference (a list, an exception traceback, a notebook `Out[]` entry) is still holding the tensor.","`t[0] += [x]` where `t` is a tuple holding a list; the list's `__iadd__` mutates it in place, then the store back into the tuple raises `TypeError`, so the symptom is an exception *and* a changed list, which looks impossible until you split `+=` into its two steps.","Using `is` to compare strings or integers; it works in tests because of interning and fails on values built at runtime."],"when":"This is not a tool you choose; it is the model you need every time a value is shared. Reach for\nimmutability by default for anything passed between layers, stored in a cache, or used by more than\none task: tuples instead of lists for fixed sequences, frozen dataclasses for configs, `None` plus\nin-body construction for defaults. Reach for deliberate mutation when memory or speed demands it,\nand contain it inside one function with an explicit docstring note. The measures are simple: no\nmutable defaults in the codebase (linters flag them), no class-level mutable attributes, copies at\nevery tenant boundary. In interviews, \"what does this print\" questions about `+=`, default\narguments and nested list multiplication are the standard probe of this model.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds per-request message lists from scratch, keeps shared prompt templates immutable, and uses views and in-place tensor ops only where the memory saving is measured.","architect":"Sets the rule that cross-tenant and cross-request state is immutable or copied at the boundary, and designs caches around hashable, frozen keys.","fde":"Diagnoses \"customer B saw customer A's context\" incidents by tracing which shared object was mutated, usually a template or default argument in a customisation layer."},"check":{"q":"A multi-tenant service builds each request's config with `cfg = DEFAULTS.copy()` then\n`cfg[\"headers\"][\"X-Tenant\"] = tenant`. Under load, requests intermittently carry the wrong tenant\nheader. Explain the mechanism and give two fixes with their costs.","a":"`dict.copy()` is shallow: the new `cfg` is a different outer dict, but `cfg[\"headers\"]` is the\nvery same inner dict object that `DEFAULTS[\"headers\"]` references, so every request writes its\ntenant into one shared dict. With concurrency, request B's write lands between A's write and A's\nHTTP call, so A sends B's header. Fix one: `copy.deepcopy(DEFAULTS)` per request, which isolates\neverything at O(size) cost per call and breaks if DEFAULTS ever holds a client or lock. Fix two,\nusually better: make DEFAULTS immutable (tuples, `MappingProxyType`, a frozen dataclass) and build the\nper-request headers as a new dict `{**DEFAULTS[\"headers\"], \"X-Tenant\": tenant}`, which costs only the\nsmall structure that actually changes and makes the sharing impossible rather than merely avoided."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `def f(x, acc=[]): acc.append(x); return acc` return on the second call `f(2)` after `f(1)`?","options":["`[2]`, because the default is re-evaluated on every call","`[1, 2]`, because the default list was created once at definition time and is shared","A `TypeError`, because mutable defaults are rejected","`[1, 2]` only if both calls happen in the same thread"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Default values are evaluated once when `def` executes and stored on the function object. Every call that omits `acc` mutates that one list, which is why `None` plus in-body creation is the idiom."},{"q":"Why does `s += \"a\"` leave other names bound to the old string unchanged, while `lst += [1]` changes every alias of the list?","options":["Strings are copied on assignment and lists are not","The `+=` operator is only defined for mutable types","`str` is immutable so `+=` rebinds the name to a new object, whereas `list.__iadd__` extends the existing object in place","Lists are stored by reference and strings by value in the interpreter"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Same spelling, different semantics: immutable types have no in-place operator, so a new object is created and only the assigned name moves. Mutable types implement in-place augmentation and all references observe it."},{"q":"After `row = emb[0]; row /= row.sum()` with a float32 NumPy embedding matrix `emb`, what happened to `emb`?","options":["Nothing; indexing returns an independent copy of the row","Its first row was modified, because basic indexing returns a view sharing the same buffer","It was converted to a float array but its values are unchanged","A `ValueError` was raised because views cannot be modified in place"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Basic slicing and indexing in NumPy return views, so an in-place operation on the view writes into the original buffer. `emb[0].copy()` or an out-of-place expression avoids the side effect."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","sources":["Official docs: Python Language Reference and Library Reference, Built-in Exceptions","Official docs: Python Tutorial, Errors and Exceptions","Katriel, Selivanov, van Rossum, 2021 — PEP 654: Exception Groups and except*"],"why":"An LLM call can fail a dozen different ways, and the correct response differs for each. A rate limit\nwants a wait and a retry. A timeout wants a retry, maybe with a smaller request. A context-length\nerror wants the input shrunk and never a plain retry. Output that fails schema validation wants a\ncorrective re-prompt. An authentication error or content-policy refusal wants no retry at all and a\nhuman. A `KeyError` in your own parsing code wants a bug fix, not a retry loop that quietly triples\nyour bill. If all of these arrive as \"an exception\", the handler can only pick one policy for all.\n\nThe naive alternatives each break. `except Exception: retry()` retries the unretryable, including\nyour own bugs, and hides them behind success-after-retry statistics. Return codes and `None`\nresults are ignored by the first caller that forgets to check, lose the stack trace, and spread\n`if result is None` through every layer. Matching on message text (`if \"rate limit\" in str(e)`)\ncouples your logic to a vendor's wording and breaks on the next SDK release. Catching the vendor's\nown exception classes everywhere couples the whole application to one provider, so switching or\nadding a fallback model means touching every call site.\n\nCustom hierarchies solve this because `except` matches by class *and all subclasses*. A tree whose\ninner nodes are handling policies (`Transient`, `Permanent`) and whose leaves carry structured data\n(`RateLimited.retry_after`, `ContextTooLong.limit`) lets each layer catch at the granularity it can act\non. Translating vendor exceptions into that tree once, at the adapter, keeps the rest of the code\nvendor-neutral.","how":"### Raising, unwinding and catching\n`raise X(...)` creates an exception instance, attaches a traceback, and unwinds the stack frame by\nframe until a `try` block with a matching `except` is found. Matching uses `isinstance`, so\n`except Transient` catches every subclass; handlers are tested in order, which is why a subclass\nhandler must appear before its base or it is unreachable. `else` runs when no exception occurred,\n`finally` always runs, and a bare `raise` inside a handler re-raises the current exception with its\noriginal traceback intact. Since Python 3.11 a `try` block costs nothing when no exception is raised;\nraising and unwinding still costs, so exceptions are for failures, not for routine control flow in\nhot loops.\n\n### The built-in tree and what `except Exception` means\nEverything derives from `BaseException`. `Exception` is the branch for errors a program may want to\nrecover from. `KeyboardInterrupt`, `SystemExit`, `GeneratorExit` and `asyncio.CancelledError` hang\ndirectly off `BaseException` *so that* `except Exception` does not swallow them: a handler that\ncatches \"any error\" must not stop Ctrl-C, interpreter shutdown or task cancellation. Catching\n`BaseException` or writing a bare `except:` breaks exactly those mechanisms.\n\n### Designing the hierarchy\nStart with one base per subsystem (`LLMError(Exception)`) so a caller can always say \"anything from\nthe LLM layer\". Under it, split by handling policy, not by source: `Transient` (safe to retry) versus\n`Permanent` (do not retry), optionally `Recoverable` (retry after changing something). Leaves carry\nthe data the handler needs as attributes: `retry_after` seconds, token counts and limits, the list of\nvalidation errors. Give leaves a clear `__str__` for logs. Keep the tree shallow; three levels is\nusually enough, and every class must correspond to a different decision somewhere, otherwise it is\nnoise.\n\nTwo refinements keep the tree honest. First, a leaf may also inherit a built-in when callers outside\nyour code already catch that built-in: `class ProviderTimeout(Transient, TimeoutError)` is caught by\nyour retry loop *and* by any generic `except TimeoutError` in a framework, because `except` checks\nthe whole MRO. Second, when the handling decision depends on data rather than kind (the same\n`ProviderHTTPError` is retryable for 503 and not for 400), an attribute such as `retryable: bool`\ncan beat a class split; the rule of thumb is classes for decisions made by `except`, attributes for\ndecisions made by an `if` inside the handler. Prefer classes where you can, because an `except`\nclause cannot forget to check a flag.\n\n### Chaining: keep the cause\nRaising a domain exception inside an `except` block links the original automatically as\n`__context__`, and the traceback prints \"During handling of the above exception, another exception\noccurred\", which misreads as a second bug. `raise Domain(...) from e` sets `__cause__` and prints\n\"The above exception was the direct cause\", which is the honest story and keeps the vendor stack\ntrace for debugging. `from None` suppresses the context when the original is irrelevant noise.\n`e.add_note(\"request_id=...\")` (3.11) attaches context without changing the type.\n\n### Translate at the boundary, decide at the top\nWrap vendor SDK or HTTP errors into your hierarchy in the one adapter that talks to the vendor. Let\nexceptions propagate through the layers that cannot act on them; do not catch-log-reraise at every\nlevel, which produces the same stack trace five times. Decide at the layer that owns the policy: the\nretry loop catches `Transient`, the request handler maps `Permanent` to an HTTP status, the top-level\ncatches `LLMError` for the user-facing message. Keep `__init__` signatures compatible with pickling\n(pass the constructor arguments to `super().__init__`) if exceptions cross process boundaries.\n\n### Concurrent failures\nWhen several tasks fail at once (an `asyncio.TaskGroup` fanning out tool calls), there is no single\nexception to raise. `ExceptionGroup` (3.11) bundles them and `except* Transient` selects the matching\nmembers while re-raising the rest, which keeps the retry/no-retry split correct even for parallel work.","example":"A small hierarchy, translation of HTTP failures at the adapter, and a retry loop that only retries\nwhat is marked retryable. The error-body shape is illustrative; map your provider's codes the same way.\n\n```python\nimport time, httpx\n\nclass LLMError(Exception): ...\nclass Transient(LLMError): ...                      # safe to retry\nclass Permanent(LLMError): ...                      # do not retry\nclass Recoverable(LLMError): ...                    # retry only after changing the request\n\nclass RateLimited(Transient):\n    def __init__(self, retry_after: float):\n        super().__init__(retry_after)               # args == ctor args keeps it picklable\n        self.retry_after = retry_after\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        return f\"rate limited, retry after {self.retry_after:.1f}s\"\n\nclass ContextTooLong(Recoverable):\n    def __init__(self, tokens: int, limit: int):\n        super().__init__(tokens, limit)\n        self.tokens, self.limit = tokens, limit\n\ndef call_provider(client: httpx.Client, prompt: str) -> str:\n    try:\n        r = client.post(\"/v1/complete\", json={\"prompt\": prompt}, timeout=30)\n        r.raise_for_status()\n    except httpx.TransportError as e:               # timeouts, refused or reset connections\n        raise Transient(f\"transport error: {e!r}\") from e   # keep the cause for logs\n    except httpx.HTTPStatusError as e:\n        status = e.response.status_code\n        if status == 429:\n            ra = e.response.headers.get(\"retry-after\", \"2\")      # may also be an HTTP date\n            raise RateLimited(float(ra) if ra.isdigit() else 2.0) from e\n        if status >= 500:                           # check before parsing: 5xx bodies are often HTML\n            raise Transient(f\"provider returned {status}\") from e\n        is_json = \"json\" in e.response.headers.get(\"content-type\", \"\")\n        body = e.response.json() if is_json else {}\n        if body.get(\"error\", {}).get(\"code\") == \"context_length_exceeded\":\n            raise ContextTooLong(body[\"error\"][\"tokens\"], body[\"error\"][\"limit\"]) from e\n        raise Permanent(f\"provider returned {status}: {body}\") from e   # 400/401/403: stop\n    return r.json()[\"text\"]\n\ndef answer(client: httpx.Client, prompt: str, attempts: int = 3) -> str:\n    last: LLMError | None = None\n    for n in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            return call_provider(client, prompt)\n        except RateLimited as e:                    # subclass handler before its base\n            last = e; time.sleep(min(e.retry_after, 30))   # cap a hostile or huge hint\n        except Transient as e:\n            last = e; time.sleep(2 ** n)\n        except ContextTooLong as e:                 # recoverable: shrink, then try again\n            last = e; prompt = prompt[: len(prompt) * e.limit * 9 // (10 * e.tokens)]\n    raise Permanent(f\"gave up after {attempts} attempts\") from last   # Permanent: propagates\n```\n\nTrace one failure through it. The provider answers 429 with `retry-after: 7`. The adapter turns the\n`HTTPStatusError` into `RateLimited(7.0)`, chained to the original so the vendor trace survives.\n`answer` matches `except RateLimited` first (it is listed before its base), sleeps seven seconds and\nloops. A 401 instead becomes `Permanent`, which no handler in `answer` names, so it propagates\nimmediately to the request handler with zero retries spent. A `KeyError` from a changed response\nbody (`r.json()[\"text\"]`) is not an `LLMError` at all, so it escapes on the first attempt as the bug\nit is. The handlers never inspect a status code or a message string; the classification happened\nonce, in the adapter.","tradeoffs":["A policy-based tree lets callers catch exactly what they can handle, but every class must map to a distinct decision; a hierarchy mirroring the vendor's twenty error codes is coupling in disguise.","Translating at the adapter keeps the application vendor-neutral, at the cost of a mapping that must be maintained as providers add codes; unmapped errors should land in `Permanent`, not vanish.","Carrying structured data on exceptions (`retry_after`, `limit`) makes handlers precise, but non-trivial constructors must stay pickle-compatible if the exception ever crosses a process pool.","Exception groups model parallel failures faithfully, but `except*` semantics (partial matching, re-raising the remainder) are harder to read than a sequential loop; use them only where fan-out genuinely happens."],"pitfalls":["`except Exception:` around the provider call with a retry; the symptom is a `KeyError` in your own parser appearing in logs five times, each with a paid request behind it.","A bare `except:` or `except BaseException:`; the symptom is Ctrl-C being ignored and async tasks that refuse to cancel, because `CancelledError` and `KeyboardInterrupt` were swallowed.","Listing `except Transient` before `except RateLimited`; the leaf handler is unreachable and the `retry_after` hint is ignored, so you hammer a rate-limited endpoint with exponential backoff instead.","Raising a domain error without `from e`; the traceback says \"during handling of the above exception, another exception occurred\", and the on-call engineer chases a phantom second bug.","Using `assert` to reject bad model output or tool arguments; asserts are removed under `python -O`, so the symptom is validation that works in tests and silently vanishes in an optimised deployment. Raise a real exception.","Matching on `str(e)` from an SDK; it works until a minor SDK release rewords the message and every rate limit becomes a permanent failure overnight.","Logging with `logger.exception` at every layer and re-raising; the symptom is one failure producing a dozen stack traces, which trains people to ignore logs.","A custom `__init__` that takes keyword-only arguments but passes a formatted string to `super()`; the symptom is `TypeError` when a worker process tries to unpickle the exception."],"when":"Introduce a hierarchy as soon as a subsystem has more than one failure policy, which for anything\nthat calls a model or an external API is immediately. Keep it to one base plus policy branches plus\ndata-carrying leaves; add a class only when a handler would treat it differently. Translate at the\nadapter, propagate through the middle, decide at the top. Measure by counting retries per error type\nand by checking that no handler in the codebase catches `Exception` around a paid call. In\nday-to-day work this is the shape of every provider client wrapper; in interviews, the question is\n\"how do you decide what to retry\", and the answer is \"by exception class, with the mapping done once\nwhere the vendor error enters the system\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the adapter that maps SDK and HTTP errors into `Transient`/`Permanent` leaves and the retry loop that catches only `Transient`, with backoff hints read from the exception.","architect":"Owns the shared hierarchy across services so retry, fallback and circuit-breaker policies key off the same classes, and decides which failures reach the user as which status.","fde":"Extends the mapping for a customer's gateway or proxy that rewrites provider errors, so a 429 hidden inside a 502 is still classified as transient."},"check":{"q":"A colleague wraps every provider call in `except Exception as e: log(e); retry()` with three\nattempts and reports that reliability improved. What is wrong with that picture, and how would you\nrestructure it?","a":"The improvement is partly fake: transient failures were already retryable, while the policy\nalso retries permanent ones (bad API key, content refusal, context overflow) and your own bugs,\ntripling cost and latency on exactly the calls that cannot succeed, and hiding `KeyError`-style bugs\nbehind eventual success or a generic failure. The log line at that layer also duplicates whatever the\ntop level logs. Restructure with a small hierarchy under `LLMError`: `Transient` for timeouts, 429s\nand 5xx with a `retry_after` attribute, `Permanent` for 4xx auth and policy errors, and a recoverable\n`ContextTooLong` carrying the limit. Translate vendor exceptions into it once in the adapter with\n`raise ... from e`, retry only `Transient`, shrink on `ContextTooLong`, and let everything else\npropagate to a single top-level handler that logs once with the full chain and maps to a status."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does `except Exception:` not catch `KeyboardInterrupt` or `asyncio.CancelledError`?","options":["Those are warnings, not exceptions, and are handled by the `warnings` module","They derive from `BaseException` directly, so a handler for recoverable errors cannot accidentally block shutdown or cancellation","They are raised in a different thread than the handler","They are only catchable with `except*` because they arrive as exception groups"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The split between `BaseException` and `Exception` is deliberate: \"any error I might recover from\" must not include interrupts and cancellations. Catching `BaseException` or using a bare `except` does swallow them and breaks Ctrl-C and task cancellation."},{"q":"What does `raise ContextTooLong(...) from e` change compared with a plain `raise ContextTooLong(...)` inside an `except` block?","options":["It discards the original exception to keep the traceback short","It converts `e` into an instance of `ContextTooLong`","It sets `__cause__` so the traceback shows the original as the direct cause instead of an unrelated error that occurred during handling","It makes the new exception retryable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both forms keep the original reachable, but implicit chaining prints \"during handling ... another exception occurred\", which reads as two bugs. Explicit `from e` records intent and keeps the vendor trace for debugging."},{"q":"Which principle should organise a custom exception tree for an LLM client?","options":["One class per vendor error code so nothing is lost","One class per module that can raise, mirroring the package layout","Branches by handling policy (retry, recover, stop) with leaves that carry the data handlers need","A flat list of classes all inheriting directly from `Exception`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Handlers catch by class, so the tree should let a retry loop say `except Transient` and a request handler say `except Permanent`. Mirroring vendor codes or modules forces every caller to know details that belong in the adapter."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/async-await","sources":["Selivanov, 2015 — PEP 492: Coroutines with async and await syntax","Official docs: asyncio"],"why":"An LLM request takes on the order of seconds (illustrative; it varies with model and output\nlength). A retrieval-augmented agent might issue an embedding call, several vector-store queries,\na few tool calls and two or three model calls per user turn, and a batch evaluation might issue\nthousands of independent calls. Nearly all of that time is spent waiting on a socket, with the CPU\nidle. Sequential code serializes the waits: twenty independent one-second calls take twenty\nseconds.\n\nThe classic answer is threads: one per in-flight call. It works, and `concurrent.futures` makes it\neasy, but it scales badly for this shape of work. Each OS thread carries its own stack and kernel\nscheduling state, so thousands of in-flight requests mean thousands of threads; shared state needs\nlocks; and the small CPU portions (JSON parsing, prompt assembly) contend on the GIL. Streaming\nresponses, where tokens arrive over seconds, would pin a thread per stream for the whole duration.\n\n`async`/`await` takes a different route: a single thread, a queue of coroutines, and explicit pause\npoints. When a coroutine awaits a network response it yields control; the loop runs another\ncoroutine; when the socket becomes readable the first one resumes. Ten thousand waiting coroutines\nare ten thousand small Python objects, not ten thousand threads. The design is cooperative *because*\nthat is what makes it cheap: there is no preemption, no kernel involvement, and no lock needed to\nprotect state between two `await`s. The price is discipline: anything that blocks without awaiting\nfreezes every other coroutine.","how":"### Coroutines, awaitables and the loop\nCalling an `async def` function does not run it; it returns a coroutine object, a suspended\ncomputation. `await coro` runs it until it either finishes (the `await` expression evaluates to the\nreturn value) or hits an `await` on something not yet ready, at which point control returns to the\nevent loop. Under the hood a coroutine is built on the generator machinery: `await x` is roughly\n`yield from x.__await__()`, and the coroutine's frame is kept alive between resumptions. Things you\ncan await are coroutines, `Task`s, `Future`s and any object implementing `__await__`.\n\nThe event loop (`asyncio.run(main())` creates one and runs it to completion) keeps a queue of ready\ncallbacks and asks the operating system's I/O multiplexer (epoll, kqueue, IOCP) which sockets are\nready. A network read registers interest in a socket and returns a `Future`; when the OS reports\nreadiness, the loop resolves the Future and reschedules the coroutine awaiting it. The loop runs\none callback at a time, which is why there is no data race between two `await` points and also why\na long computation inside a coroutine blocks every other one.\n\n### A trace: two calls on one thread\nSay tasks A and B each send a request and await a reply that takes 1 s. At t=0 the loop runs A\nuntil it awaits the socket read; A's frame is parked with a pending Future and control returns to\nthe loop. The loop runs B the same way. Now nothing is ready, so the loop sleeps inside\n`epoll`/`kqueue` with no CPU used. At t≈1 s both sockets become readable; the loop resolves both\nFutures and resumes A, then B, each from exactly the line after its `await`. Total ≈1 s, not 2 s,\nand at no moment did two pieces of Python run at once. That last fact is both the safety property\n(no torn updates between awaits) and the hazard: if A had computed for 1 s instead of awaiting, B\nwould not even have *sent* its request until A finished.\n\n### Function colouring\n`await` is legal only inside `async def`, so a function that needs to await something must itself\nbecome async, and so must its callers, up to `asyncio.run` or a framework that owns the loop. This\n\"colouring\" is the cost of making suspension points explicit: you can see every place your code\ncan be interleaved, *because* each one is spelled `await`. The practical consequences are that an\nasync SDK cannot be called from plain sync code without starting a loop, a sync helper cannot\nawait, and libraries often ship both a sync and an async client (`OpenAI` and `AsyncOpenAI`,\n`httpx.Client` and `httpx.AsyncClient`) rather than one that works in both worlds.\n\n### Concurrency: tasks, gather, groups\n`await a(); await b()` is sequential: `b` starts after `a` finishes. To overlap them, wrap each in a\n`Task` with `asyncio.create_task(coro)`, which schedules it immediately, and await the tasks later.\n`asyncio.gather(*coros)` does this for a list and returns results in order;\n`return_exceptions=True` makes failures appear as values instead of raising the first.\n`asyncio.TaskGroup` (3.11) is the structured form: tasks started inside `async with` are all\nawaited at the block's end, and if one fails the others are cancelled and the failures are raised\ntogether as an `ExceptionGroup`. Prefer it for fan-out where a partial result is useless.\n\n### Limits and timeouts\nUnbounded fan-out against a provider turns into a rate-limit storm. `asyncio.Semaphore(n)` with\n`async with sem:` around the call caps in-flight requests at `n`, which is the knob that matches\nyour provider's concurrency allowance. It bounds concurrency, not rate: five slots with half-second\ncalls still allow about ten requests per second, so a requests- or tokens-per-minute quota needs a\ntoken-bucket limiter alongside it. `async with asyncio.timeout(seconds):` (3.11; earlier,\n`asyncio.wait_for`) cancels the awaited work when the budget expires and raises `TimeoutError`,\nwhich is how you stop a hung stream from holding a slot forever.\n\n### Async iteration and context managers\n`async for chunk in stream:` consumes an async generator or iterator, awaiting each item; this is\nthe natural shape for token streaming, where each chunk arrives from the socket at its own time.\n`async with client:` runs `__aenter__`/`__aexit__` that themselves await, which is how an\n`httpx.AsyncClient` or an SDK client opens and closes its connection pool.\n\n### What blocks the loop\n`time.sleep`, `requests.get`, a synchronous database driver, a synchronous SDK client, heavy\n`json.loads` on a huge payload, a CPU-bound embedding computation: none of these await, so the loop\ncannot switch away. The symptom is global: every request's latency rises together, and a \"fast\"\nendpoint becomes slow because a different endpoint blocked. Fix by using async libraries, by moving\nblocking I/O to a thread with `await asyncio.to_thread(fn, ...)`, and by moving CPU work to a\nprocess pool. The GIL, threads versus processes and cancellation semantics each have their own\nlesson in this module; this one is about the syntax and the cooperative model they build on.","example":"Twenty simulated model calls with a concurrency cap of five, a per-call timeout, references kept to\nevery task, and an async generator standing in for a token stream. `asyncio.sleep` plays the role\nof network latency.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, random, time\n\nMAX_INFLIGHT = 5\nin_flight = peak = 0\n\nasync def llm_call(prompt: str, sem: asyncio.Semaphore) -> str:\n    global in_flight, peak\n    async with sem:                                    # at most MAX_INFLIGHT past this line\n        in_flight += 1; peak = max(peak, in_flight)    # safe: no await between these two lines\n        try:\n            async with asyncio.timeout(2.0):           # per-call budget; raises TimeoutError\n                await asyncio.sleep(random.uniform(0.2, 0.6))   # stands in for the network wait\n            return f\"answer to {prompt!r}\"\n        finally:\n            in_flight -= 1\n\nasync def fan_out(prompts: list[str]) -> list[str | BaseException]:\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(MAX_INFLIGHT)\n    tasks = [asyncio.create_task(llm_call(p, sem)) for p in prompts]   # keep references\n    return await asyncio.gather(*tasks, return_exceptions=True)\n\nasync def stream_tokens(text: str):\n    for tok in text.split():\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.02)                      # a real client awaits each chunk here\n        yield tok\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    results = await fan_out([f\"q{i}\" for i in range(20)])\n    print(f\"{len(results)} results in {time.perf_counter() - t0:.2f}s, peak in-flight={peak}\")\n    # roughly: 20 results in ~1.7-1.9s, peak in-flight=5  (sequential ~8s)\n    async for tok in stream_tokens(\"tokens arrive as the model produces them\"):\n        print(tok, end=\" \", flush=True)\n    print()\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nReplace `await asyncio.sleep(...)` with `time.sleep(...)` and the run takes the full sequential\ntime with `peak in-flight=1`: the blocking call never yields, so no other coroutine can start.","tradeoffs":["One thread handles thousands of concurrent waits cheaply, but a single blocking call freezes all of them; the model flips to threads or processes when you cannot avoid blocking libraries.","No preemption means no data races between `await`s and no locks for simple state, yet any state touched across an `await` can be interleaved and needs the same care as threaded code.","Fan-out with `gather` collapses wall-clock time, but without a semaphore it collapses into provider rate limits and retries that cost more than the sequential version.","Async SDKs and drivers are required end to end; a stack with one synchronous dependency in the request path gains little and complicates debugging, so sometimes a thread pool is the honest choice.","Structured concurrency (`TaskGroup`) gives clean cancellation and error aggregation, but it forces all-or-nothing semantics; when partial results are valuable, `gather` with `return_exceptions=True` fits better."],"pitfalls":["Calling a coroutine without `await` (`client.chat(...)` instead of `await client.chat(...)`); nothing runs, and the only hint is a `RuntimeWarning: coroutine ... was never awaited`, emitted when the unused coroutine object is garbage-collected (often right away, sometimes much later).","Using a synchronous SDK client or `requests` inside an async endpoint; the symptom is every endpoint's p99 latency rising together under modest load.","Creating tasks with `create_task` and discarding the result; the loop holds only a weak reference, so the task can be garbage-collected mid-execution and silently disappear.","Firing `gather` over thousands of provider calls with no semaphore; the symptom is a burst of 429 responses followed by a retry storm that can end up slower than running sequentially.","Reading shared state, awaiting, then writing based on what you read (`if budget > cost: await call(); budget -= cost`); every task passes the check before any of them subtracts, and the symptom is a token budget or quota overspent by roughly the concurrency factor. Hold an `asyncio.Lock` across the check-and-act, or reserve before awaiting.","Calling `asyncio.run()` from inside a running loop (notebooks, some frameworks); it raises `RuntimeError`, and the fix is to `await` directly or use the framework's loop.","Catching `BaseException` or bare `except:` around awaits; it swallows `CancelledError` and produces tasks that cannot be cancelled and shutdowns that hang."],"when":"Use `async`/`await` when the work is I/O-bound and concurrent: fan-out model calls, parallel tool\nexecution, streaming tokens to a client, serving many simultaneous chats from one process. Do not\nuse it for CPU-bound work (tokenization of large corpora, local embedding computation) or when your\ncritical dependencies are synchronous; use threads or processes there, or wrap the blocking parts\nin `to_thread`. Measure event-loop lag (how late scheduled callbacks run) and in-flight counts; a\nrising lag with low CPU points at a blocking call. Day to day, this is the shape of every FastAPI\nendpoint, agent loop and evaluation harness you will write. In interviews, be ready to explain what\n`await` actually does, why one `time.sleep` breaks everything, and how you bound concurrency\nagainst a rate limit.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes agent loops and eval harnesses with `create_task`, `gather`, semaphores and timeouts, and keeps every call in the request path on an async client.","architect":"Decides which services run on the event loop versus thread or process pools, and sets concurrency caps and rate limiters per provider so fan-out stays inside negotiated quotas.","fde":"Finds the one synchronous call (a legacy auth client, a sync database driver) stalling a customer's async service and moves it to `to_thread` or an async driver."},"check":{"q":"A FastAPI service with async endpoints handles 50 requests per second comfortably, but after\na developer adds a feature that calls a legacy sync HTTP client, latency on *every* endpoint rises\nunder load, with the CPU mostly idle. Explain the mechanism and how you would fix and verify it.","a":"The sync client blocks the event-loop thread for the whole request round trip. During that\ntime no other coroutine can run, so every in-flight request, including unrelated endpoints, waits.\nCPU stays idle because the thread is blocked on a socket, not computing. The fix is to make the\ncall non-blocking: switch to an async client such as `httpx.AsyncClient`, or wrap the legacy call\nin `await asyncio.to_thread(...)` so it runs in a worker thread while the loop continues. Verify by\nmeasuring event-loop lag or by load-testing one endpoint while the slow one is exercised; after the\nfix the unrelated endpoint's latency should no longer move."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when a coroutine hits `await` on a network response that is not yet ready?","options":["The thread blocks until the response arrives","The coroutine suspends and the event loop runs other ready coroutines until the socket is readable","A new thread is spawned to wait for the response","The response is polled in a busy loop until it arrives"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`await` yields control to the loop, which uses the OS I/O multiplexer to learn when the socket is ready and then resumes the coroutine. No thread blocks and no busy-waiting occurs."},{"q":"Why does a single `time.sleep(1)` inside one coroutine delay every other request in the process?","options":["`time.sleep` acquires the GIL exclusively","asyncio runs each coroutine on its own thread and `time.sleep` joins them","The loop is cooperative and single-threaded; a call that never awaits never yields, so nothing else can run","`time.sleep` cancels pending tasks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Coroutines only switch at `await`. A blocking call holds the one thread the loop uses, so all scheduled work waits. `await asyncio.sleep(1)` yields instead and the problem disappears."},{"q":"What is the purpose of wrapping each provider call in `async with asyncio.Semaphore(5)`?","options":["To make the calls run in parallel on five CPU cores","To retry each call up to five times","To cap the number of in-flight calls at five so fan-out cannot flood the provider all at once","To guarantee results are returned in order"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A semaphore bounds concurrency: the sixth call waits until one of the five completes. `gather` alone would start all calls at once and trigger 429 responses. Note it limits calls in flight, not requests per minute; a requests- or tokens-per-minute quota needs a rate limiter on top."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/asyncio","sources":["Official docs: asyncio","PEP 3156 — Asynchronous IO Support Rebooted: the \"asyncio\" Module","PEP 492 — Coroutines with async and await syntax"],"why":"Take a chat backend that calls a model with a 3-second median response and streams it back. A\nsynchronous handler holds its thread for those 3 seconds doing nothing but waiting on a socket. Serving\n1,000 concurrent chats that way needs 1,000 threads: each costs a kernel object, a reserved stack\n(the platform default ranges from 512 KB on macOS to 8 MB on typical Linux, mostly virtual address space\nrather than resident memory), and scheduler time, and under the GIL\nthey cannot even run Python in parallel. Most of that machinery exists only to wait.\n\nThe naive fix, a bigger thread pool, moves the ceiling but not the shape of the cost. The sharper\nobservation is that a waiting handler needs almost no state: a socket to watch and the point in the\ncode to resume from. The operating system already offers a cheap way to watch many sockets at once\n(`epoll` on Linux, `kqueue` on macOS, IOCP on Windows). What Python lacked was a way to suspend a\nfunction mid-body, keep its locals, and resume it when the socket is ready. Generators had that\nability since `yield`; PEP 492 gave it dedicated syntax (`async def` / `await`), and asyncio is the\nscheduler that connects those suspended functions to the OS readiness notifications. Callback-style\nframeworks solved the same problem earlier, but the code read as a graph of callbacks; coroutines keep\nit reading top to bottom while behaving the same way underneath.","how":"### The three objects\n- A **coroutine** is what calling an `async def` function returns: a suspended frame that has not\n  started. It runs only when something drives it.\n- A **Future** is a one-shot result holder with callbacks: \"when this is set, call these\". Socket\n  readiness, timers and thread results all surface as futures.\n- A **Task** wraps a coroutine in a future and drives it. Each time the coroutine `await`s something\n  that is not ready, the task registers a callback on that future and returns control to the loop.\n  When the future resolves, the callback reschedules the task, which resumes the coroutine exactly\n  where it stopped.\n\n### One iteration of the loop\nThe event loop is a single-threaded `while True`:\n1. Compute how long until the earliest timer (`asyncio.sleep`, timeouts).\n2. Ask the OS selector for sockets that are readable or writable, waiting at most that long. This is\n   the only place the thread blocks, and it blocks on behalf of *all* tasks at once.\n3. For each ready socket or expired timer, queue its handler. Running the handler reads the bytes and\n   resolves the corresponding future, and resolving a future schedules the waiting task's step callback\n   with `call_soon`, so it lands on the ready queue too.\n4. Drain the callbacks that were ready when this iteration began, in FIFO order, running each task\n   until its next `await`. Anything they schedule waits for the next iteration, *because* otherwise a\n   task that keeps rescheduling itself could starve the selector and every timer.\n\nStep 4 is why asyncio is called *cooperative*: a task runs until it voluntarily yields at an `await`\non a not-yet-ready future. Awaiting an already-complete future, or a plain coroutine that never touches\nI/O, does not yield at all; `await asyncio.sleep(0)` is the explicit \"let others run\" hint.\n\n### Design choices and their reasons\n- **Single thread** *because* it removes data races between awaits: between two `await`s, no other\n  task can touch your state. It does not remove races *across* an await (read, await, write), which is\n  what `asyncio.Lock` is for. The single thread is also why a blocking call (`time.sleep`,\n  `requests.get`, a CPU-heavy loop) freezes every task; there is no other thread to keep the loop turning.\n- **Explicit `await` points** *because* they make interleaving visible in the source. A sync-looking\n  line can never secretly switch tasks, which is the property that makes most asyncio code reasonable\n  without locks.\n- **`create_task` schedules rather than runs** *because* the creator should continue; the new task\n  starts on a later loop iteration. `asyncio.gather(*coros)` wraps each coroutine in a task and\n  returns their results in order; `asyncio.TaskGroup` (3.11+) does the same with structured\n  cancellation.\n- **Blocking work is pushed off the thread** with `asyncio.to_thread(fn, *args)` (a\n  `ThreadPoolExecutor` under the hood) or `loop.run_in_executor(process_pool, fn)`, and the result\n  comes back as a future the loop can await. The loop stays responsive; the work is still serialized by\n  the GIL if it is pure Python.\n\n### Coordinating many tasks\nThe primitives differ in what they give back and when, which is the real choice:\n- `gather` returns all results in input order once every child is done; use it when you need them all.\n- `as_completed` yields results in finish order, so a UI or a pipeline can act on the fastest answer\n  first instead of waiting for the slowest.\n- `asyncio.wait(..., return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED)` returns `(done, pending)` sets and leaves the\n  pending tasks running, which is the building block for \"take the first good answer, cancel the rest\".\n- `TaskGroup` is `gather` with structured cleanup: if one child fails, the rest are cancelled.\n- `asyncio.Queue(maxsize=N)` with a fixed number of worker tasks bounds both concurrency and memory,\n  *because* producers block on `put` when consumers fall behind (backpressure), whereas `gather` over\n  10,000 coroutines creates 10,000 tasks up front.\n\n### Measuring loop health\nThe number that tells you whether the loop is healthy is **loop lag**: schedule a callback for time *t*\nand record when it actually runs. A task doing `await asyncio.sleep(0.5)` in a loop and logging\n`actual_elapsed − 0.5` measures it directly. Lag near zero means tasks are yielding; lag that tracks\nyour slowest synchronous section means something is blocking, and every request on that loop pays it.\n\n### Entry and exit\n`asyncio.run(main())` creates a fresh loop, runs `main` as a task, then cancels any tasks still\npending, finalizes async generators, and closes the loop. It cannot be called from inside a running\nloop (Jupyter already runs one; use `await main()` there). Debug mode (`PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1` or\n`asyncio.run(..., debug=True)`) adds the creation traceback to \"coroutine was never awaited\" warnings\nand logs callbacks that hog the loop longer than `loop.slow_callback_duration` (100 ms by default),\nwhich is the first tool for finding accidental blocking.","example":"Twenty chat completions fanned out concurrently, sharing one HTTP connection pool, with a blocking\ntokenizer step moved to a thread so it cannot stall the loop. Point `API` at any OpenAI-compatible\nserver (a local vLLM or Ollama instance works).\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\nimport httpx\n\nAPI = \"http://localhost:8000/v1/chat/completions\"\nHEADERS = {\"Authorization\": \"Bearer local\"}\n\nasync def ask(client: httpx.AsyncClient, question: str) -> str:\n    body = {\"model\": \"small-chat\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]}\n    r = await client.post(API, headers=HEADERS, json=body, timeout=60)   # the await is where we yield\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    return r.json()[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"][\"content\"]\n\ndef count_tokens(text: str) -> int:\n    time.sleep(0.05)                      # pretend this is a slow, blocking tokenizer\n    return len(text.split())\n\nasync def main(questions: list[str]) -> None:\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:          # one connection pool for every call\n        t0 = time.perf_counter()\n        answers = await asyncio.gather(*(ask(client, q) for q in questions))\n        # blocking work goes to a thread so the loop keeps serving other tasks meanwhile\n        counts = await asyncio.gather(*(asyncio.to_thread(count_tokens, a) for a in answers))\n        print(f\"{len(questions)} calls in {time.perf_counter() - t0:.2f}s, {sum(counts)} tokens\")\n\nasyncio.run(main([f\"Summarise item {i} in one line.\" for i in range(20)]))\n```\n\nWalk the timeline. `gather` wraps the twenty coroutines in tasks; on the next loop iteration each runs\nuntil `client.post` needs the socket, registers interest and yields, so all twenty requests are in\nflight within a few milliseconds. The loop then blocks once in the selector for all of them. If one\ncall takes 1.5 s, sequential code would take about 20 × 1.5 = 30 s; this takes about the slowest single\ncall, roughly 1.5 s plus scheduling overhead. Two hidden caps can erase that: the server's own\nparallelism (a local server configured for four parallel requests queues the other sixteen), and the\nclient's pool, since `httpx` allows 100 connections by default and request 101 waits for a free one.\n\nThe tokenizer step shows the other half of the contract. Twenty `time.sleep(0.05)` calls made directly\nin the coroutine would run back to back on the loop thread: one full second in which no other task on\nthe loop, such as another user's stream in a server, gets a single step. Through `to_thread` they run\nin the default executor (`min(32, cpu_count + 4)` threads, so all twenty at once on an 8-core machine),\nand because `time.sleep` releases the GIL they overlap to roughly 50 ms. Swap the sleep for a\npure-Python CPU loop and the threads keep the loop responsive but no longer overlap, because the GIL\nserialises them; that is the point where a process pool is the right tool.","tradeoffs":["Thousands of concurrent waits cost kilobytes each instead of a thread apiece, but the moment the work is CPU-bound Python the single loop thread becomes the bottleneck and threads or processes win.","Explicit await points make interleaving auditable, but \"function colour\" is contagious: an async call chain cannot call a blocking library without `to_thread`, so library choice (httpx vs requests, an async database driver vs a sync one) is dictated by the loop.","Cooperative scheduling has no preemption overhead and needs no locks for most state, but one misbehaving task (a tight loop, a sync HTTP call) degrades every request on the loop, where a thread pool would only lose one worker.","A traceback shows the await chain inside one task but not which task spawned it, and profilers see interleaved task steps under the loop's own frames, which is harder to read than threaded code until you learn asyncio debug mode and named tasks."],"pitfalls":["Calling a blocking function inside a coroutine (`time.sleep`, the sync OpenAI client, `pandas.read_csv` on a large file). Symptom: p99 latency climbs under load while CPU sits idle, and debug mode logs \"Executing <Task ...> took 2.3 seconds\".","Forgetting to `await` a coroutine. Nothing runs; the symptom is `RuntimeWarning: coroutine 'x' was never awaited` and a feature that silently does nothing.","Fire-and-forget `asyncio.create_task(...)` without keeping a reference. The loop holds tasks weakly, so the task can be garbage-collected mid-flight; the symptom is background work that sometimes vanishes. Store tasks in a set and discard on completion, or use a `TaskGroup`.","Calling `asyncio.run` from code that is already inside a loop (a FastAPI handler, a notebook). Symptom: `RuntimeError: asyncio.run() cannot be called from a running event loop`.","Treating `gather` as a limiter. Fanning out 5,000 embedding calls at once floods the provider and your own sockets; the symptom is a wave of 429s and connection errors. Bound it with a `Semaphore`."],"when":"Reach for asyncio when the program's time is dominated by waiting on many independent network\noperations: LLM and embedding calls, tool and MCP servers, vector stores, streaming responses to many\nclients. That describes most of an AI service, which is why FastAPI, httpx, the OpenAI and Anthropic\nSDKs and the MCP SDK all expose async APIs. Do not reach for it to speed up CPU-bound code; it will\nnot, and it makes that code harder to run. Measure event-loop lag (time between scheduling a callback\nand it running) and the number of pending tasks; both tell you whether the loop is healthy. In\ninterviews, expect to explain what happens at an `await`, why a blocking call hurts every request, and\nwhen you would move work to a thread or process instead.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async handlers and clients, fans out model and tool calls with `gather` or `TaskGroup`, and keeps blocking work off the loop with `to_thread`.","architect":"Decides which services are async-native and where the sync/async boundaries sit, so one blocking dependency cannot stall a whole worker process.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"slow under load but CPU is idle\" by finding the blocking call on their loop, often a sync SDK inside an async route."},"check":{"q":"A FastAPI service declares its chat route `async def`, and inside it calls the provider with a\nsynchronous SDK client. At ten concurrent users p99 latency is about thirty seconds while CPU sits at\nfive percent. Explain the mechanism and give two fixes with their trade-offs.","a":"The route runs on the event loop thread, and the sync client blocks that thread for the whole\nprovider round-trip, so every other request on the worker waits in line: with ten users and a\nthree-second call, the last one waits around thirty seconds, which matches the symptom, and CPU is idle\nbecause the thread is parked on a socket. Fix one: switch to the SDK's async client so the call becomes\nan `await` and the loop keeps serving others; this is the right default but requires async-compatible\ncode throughout the call chain. Fix two: keep the sync client but call it via\n`await asyncio.to_thread(...)`, or declare the route with plain `def` so the framework runs it in its\nthread pool; this is a smaller change, but the pool is bounded (AnyIO, which FastAPI uses, defaults to\n40 threads), so concurrency caps at its size and each call still costs a thread. To confirm the\ndiagnosis before fixing, turn on asyncio debug mode or a loop-lag probe: lag of about three seconds per\nrequest is the fingerprint."},"quiz":[{"q":"A coroutine on the event loop calls `time.sleep(2)`. What happens to the other tasks?","options":["They continue, because the loop runs each task in its own thread","They all stall for two seconds, because the one loop thread is blocked and cannot resume anyone","Only tasks waiting on the same socket stall","The loop detects the block and migrates the call to a thread automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"asyncio is single-threaded and cooperative. A blocking call never returns control to the loop, so no ready callback or timer can run until it finishes. Only `asyncio.sleep` or `to_thread` keep the loop free while waiting."},{"q":"Why should you keep a reference to a task created with `asyncio.create_task`?","options":["Tasks cannot start until they are awaited by someone","The result is discarded unless a reference exists","The loop keeps only weak references, so an unreferenced task can be garbage-collected before it finishes","`create_task` returns a coroutine, not a task, until it is referenced"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The task starts on a later loop iteration regardless, but the event loop does not own it. Without a strong reference elsewhere, garbage collection may destroy it mid-execution, which is why the docs recommend storing tasks in a collection or using a TaskGroup."},{"q":"Which workload gains the most from moving to asyncio?","options":["Re-ranking 10,000 candidates with a pure-Python scoring loop","Calling an embeddings API for 2,000 chunks with a concurrency cap of 32","Parsing a 5 GB JSONL file into pandas","Training a small model with PyTorch on a GPU"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The embeddings fan-out is I/O-bound: each call waits on the network, so one thread can keep 32 requests in flight. The other options are CPU- or GPU-bound and would not benefit; they would block the loop instead."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","sources":["Official docs: asyncio (Coroutines and Tasks, Task Groups, Timeouts)","PEP 654 — Exception Groups and except*"],"why":"An agent turn fans out: three tool calls and a retrieval query run concurrently, and the user then\ncloses the browser tab, or the 30-second deadline passes, or one tool returns a fatal error that\nmakes the others pointless. In every case the remaining work must stop, and stopping it badly has\nreal costs: an LLM stream that keeps generating (and, depending on the provider, keeps billing), a\nconnection that never goes back to the pool, a half-written row in a checkpoint store.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail. Killing a thread or coroutine from outside at an arbitrary instruction\nwould leave locks held and sockets half-written, which is why Python offers no safe way to kill a\nthread. Polling a `stop` flag only works if every piece of code checks it, and library code you call\nnever will. Ignoring errors in background tasks is worse: a task that raised but was never awaited\nloses its exception until garbage collection logs \"Task exception was never retrieved\", long after\nthe request returned a wrong answer.\n\nasyncio's answer is cooperative cancellation delivered as an exception. Because a coroutine can only\nbe suspended at an `await`, that is the only place it can be interrupted, so cancellation arrives at a\nknown point and travels up the stack through ordinary `try/finally` and `async with` cleanup. Error\npropagation then needs rules for what happens to siblings when one child fails, which is what\nstructured concurrency (`TaskGroup`) supplies.","how":"### Cancellation is a request, delivered at an await\nStep by step, `task.cancel()` does three things:\n1. If the task is suspended on a future, it cancels *that* future. If the task was awaiting another\n   task (`await other_task`), the inner task is cancelled too, so the request travels **down** the\n   await chain to whatever is actually waiting on I/O.\n2. Cancelling the future wakes the task on the next loop iteration, exactly as a result would.\n3. When the task resumes, it throws `CancelledError` into the coroutine at the suspended `await`, and\n   the exception then travels **up** the call stack.\n\nIf the task is running or ready rather than suspended, only a flag is set, and the throw happens at\nits next suspension point. Code between awaits runs to completion; a CPU loop with no await\ncannot be cancelled at all. The exception unwinds the coroutine like any other: `finally` blocks run,\n`async with` exits run (the `httpx` client closes the stream, the lock is released). Since Python 3.8\n`CancelledError` subclasses `BaseException`, not `Exception`, *because* the common\n`except Exception:` retry or logging block used to catch it by accident and keep the task alive.\n\nThe contract: you may catch `CancelledError` to clean up or log, but you must re-raise it. A task\nthat swallows it reports as completed, and whoever cancelled it (a timeout, a TaskGroup, the server\nshutting down) believes the cancellation failed or never happened.\n\n### Timeouts are cancellation plus translation\n`asyncio.timeout(delay)` (3.11+) schedules a cancel of the current task at the deadline. When the\nresulting `CancelledError` reaches the `async with` exit, the context manager recognises it as its own\nand converts it into `TimeoutError`. The older `asyncio.wait_for` is built on `asyncio.timeout` since\n3.12; before that it ran the awaitable as a separate inner task and cancelled that task instead, with\nthe same cancel-then-translate shape. That is why a swallowed\ncancellation inside a timeout block makes the deadline silently not fire. Python 3.11 added\n`Task.cancelling()` and `Task.uncancel()` so these constructs can tell their own cancellation apart\nfrom one requested from outside.\n\n### Where a task's exception goes\nAn exception raised inside a task is stored on the task, not raised anywhere. It surfaces only when\nsomeone awaits the task (the exception is re-raised in the awaiter, with its original traceback) or\ncalls `task.result()` / `task.exception()`. If nobody ever does, the loop logs \"Task exception was\nnever retrieved\" when the task object is garbage-collected, which may be minutes later or never. So\nevery task needs an owner: something that awaits it, a TaskGroup, or a done-callback that logs the\nerror. `asyncio.wait` follows the same rule: it never raises the children's exceptions, it hands you\n`done` tasks whose results you must still check.\n\n### Propagation: gather versus TaskGroup\n`asyncio.gather(*aws)` with the default `return_exceptions=False` propagates the *first* exception\nto the awaiting caller, but does not cancel the other children: they keep running, orphaned. With\n`return_exceptions=True` it returns exceptions as list items, so you must inspect every result.\nCancelling the gather itself does cancel all children.\n\n`asyncio.TaskGroup` (3.11+) implements structured concurrency: no child outlives the `async with`\nblock. When any child raises a non-cancellation exception, the group cancels all remaining children\n(and the body of the `async with`, if it is still running), waits for them to finish unwinding, then\nraises an `ExceptionGroup` holding every child failure. Children that ended by cancellation are not\nreported as failures, *because* the group itself caused them.\nYou handle those with `except*`, which matches by type inside the group, *because* concurrent code\ncan genuinely fail in several ways at once and a single exception would hide the others.\n\n### Shielding\n`asyncio.shield(aw)` protects an inner awaitable from a cancellation aimed at the outer task. It is\nneeded precisely because of step 1 above: without it, cancelling the outer task would cancel the inner\none it awaits. `shield` inserts a separate future between them, so the outer `await` still raises\n`CancelledError` while the inner work continues. Keep a reference to the inner task, or it can be\ngarbage-collected once nobody awaits it. Use it narrowly, for work that\nmust complete even when the requester is gone, such as committing an audit record.","example":"```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def call(name: str, delay: float, fail: bool = False) -> str:\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(delay)            # stands in for an HTTP call\n        if fail:\n            raise ValueError(f\"{name}: malformed tool output\")\n        return f\"{name}: ok\"\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        print(f\"{name}: cancelled, closing stream\")\n        raise                                 # re-raise, always\n    finally:\n        print(f\"{name}: connection released\")\n\nasync def agent_turn() -> None:\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.timeout(2.0):      # overall turn deadline\n            async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:\n                tg.create_task(call(\"retriever\", 0.5))\n                tg.create_task(call(\"calculator\", 0.2, fail=True))\n                tg.create_task(call(\"llm\", 5.0))\n    except* ValueError as eg:\n        for exc in eg.exceptions:\n            print(\"tool failed:\", exc)\n    except* TimeoutError:\n        print(\"turn deadline exceeded\")\n\nasyncio.run(agent_turn())\n```\nAt 0.2 s the calculator raises. The TaskGroup cancels the retriever and the LLM call; each prints\n\"cancelled\" and \"connection released\" as `CancelledError` unwinds it; then the group raises an\n`ExceptionGroup` that `except* ValueError` handles. Change `fail=True` to `False` and the 2-second\ntimeout fires instead: the timeout cancels `agent_turn`, the group passes that cancellation to its\nchildren, the LLM call unwinds, and the caller sees `TimeoutError`. Deleting the `raise` in the child\nwould not break this particular run, because the timeout belongs to `agent_turn`, not the child; but\nthe cancelled child would then report success, and the same swallow in the task that owns a timeout\nmakes the block exit normally so no `TimeoutError` is ever raised.","tradeoffs":["TaskGroup's fail-fast semantics stop wasted work and guarantee no orphans, but one flaky optional tool cancels the whole fan-out; when partial results are acceptable, catch errors inside each child.","`gather(return_exceptions=True)` keeps every result and never cancels siblings, at the cost of manual inspection; it flips to the better choice when children are independent and best-effort.","Cooperative cancellation keeps cleanup safe and predictable, but it cannot interrupt CPU-bound or blocking code; such work must run in a thread or process where cancellation only abandons the wait.","`shield` guarantees critical work finishes, but that work then outlives the request and can pile up during an incident; it is only safe when the shielded work is short and bounded."],"pitfalls":["A bare `except:` or `except BaseException:` without re-raise (or `except Exception` on Python 3.7 and earlier) swallows cancellation; the symptom is timeouts that never fire and shutdowns that hang.","Fire-and-forget `asyncio.create_task(...)` without keeping a reference or awaiting it; the symptom is \"Task exception was never retrieved\" in logs and occasionally tasks garbage-collected mid-flight.","Using `gather` and assuming a failure stops the other calls; the symptom is token spend and tool side effects continuing after the request already returned an error.","Awaiting inside `finally` during cancellation without a bound; a slow cleanup call can itself hang, so shutdown stalls until the process manager kills it.","Expecting cancel to stop a blocking `requests.get` or a tight loop; the symptom is a task that stays \"cancelling\" until the blocking call returns on its own."],"when":"You meet this whenever code starts more than one task or has a deadline, which in an AI backend is\nalmost always: per-request timeouts on model calls, client disconnects on streaming endpoints,\nparallel tool calls, and graceful shutdown. Default to `TaskGroup` for fan-out where all results\nmatter and `asyncio.timeout` for deadlines; use `gather(return_exceptions=True)` for best-effort\nbatches. Every coroutine that holds a resource should release it in `finally` or `async with`. Test\nit: write a test that cancels mid-call and asserts the connection pool and locks are back to idle.\nIn interviews, expect to explain why `CancelledError` is a `BaseException` and how TaskGroup differs\nfrom gather when one child fails.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes children that re-raise `CancelledError`, wraps fan-out in `TaskGroup`, and tests that a client disconnect actually closes the upstream LLM stream.","architect":"Sets the deadline budget across layers so inner timeouts are shorter than outer ones, and decides which side effects are shielded versus abandoned on cancel.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's hanging worker or runaway token bill by finding the swallowed cancellation or orphaned gather in their integration code."},"check":{"q":"An agent runs five tool calls with `asyncio.gather`. One raises; the endpoint returns 500, yet\nlogs show the other four tools finishing later and one writing to a CRM. Explain and fix.","a":"`gather` without `return_exceptions` propagates the first exception to the caller but does not\ncancel the remaining children, so they keep running as orphans and complete their side effects after\nthe response was sent. Switching to `asyncio.TaskGroup` cancels siblings when one fails and waits for\nthem to unwind before raising an `ExceptionGroup`, so nothing outlives the request. Each tool must\nre-raise `CancelledError` and release resources in `finally`. Because cancellation only lands at an\nawait, a CRM write already sent cannot be undone, so consequential tools also need idempotency keys\nor a compensating action."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does `CancelledError` inherit from `BaseException` rather than `Exception` in modern Python?","options":["So that it cannot be caught by any handler at all","So generic `except Exception` blocks for retries and logging do not swallow cancellation by accident","Because it is raised by the interpreter rather than by user code","So that it propagates across threads as well as tasks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Broad `except Exception` handlers are common in retry and logging code; when cancellation was an `Exception` they absorbed it and kept the task alive. It can still be caught explicitly, and must then be re-raised."},{"q":"A child inside `asyncio.TaskGroup` raises `ValueError` while two siblings are awaiting network calls. What happens?","options":["The ValueError propagates immediately and the siblings continue in the background","The siblings are killed instantly without running their finally blocks","The siblings are cancelled, the group waits for them to unwind, then raises an ExceptionGroup","The group retries the failed child once before failing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TaskGroup guarantees that no child outlives the block: it cancels the others, waits for their cleanup to run, and reports all failures together. The first option describes `gather`, not TaskGroup."},{"q":"A coroutine running `async with asyncio.timeout(5):` catches `CancelledError` and returns `None`. What is the effect?","options":["The timeout raises TimeoutError as normal after cleanup","The event loop logs a warning and re-raises automatically","Nothing changes, because timeouts do not use cancellation","The block exits normally, so the expected TimeoutError is never raised and the deadline is silently lost"],"answer":3,"explanation":"`asyncio.timeout` works by cancelling the task and converting the arriving `CancelledError` into `TimeoutError` at block exit; swallowing the exception means there is nothing to convert."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","sources":["Official docs: asyncio (Synchronization Primitives)"],"why":"A common belief is that single-threaded async code cannot race. It is half true: between two awaits a\ncoroutine runs uninterrupted, so `self.count += 1` on its own is safe in asyncio, unlike in threaded\ncode. But real logic spans awaits. \"If the auth token is expired, fetch a new one and store it\" reads\nstate, awaits a network call, then writes. While it waits, a hundred other requests run the same check,\nsee the same expired token, and each starts its own refresh. That is a thundering herd against the\nauth server, and if refreshes invalidate earlier tokens, some requests end up holding revoked ones.\n\nThe second forcing problem is resource limits. `gather` over 5,000 prompts starts 5,000 HTTP requests\nimmediately. The provider answers most with 429s, the client's connection pool queues the rest, memory\nfills with pending responses, and timeouts fire on requests that never left the process. You need a\ngate that lets N coroutines through and makes the rest wait.\n\nNaive fixes fail. A boolean `refreshing` flag is itself check-then-act and needs a way to wait for\nthe refresh to finish, which is a lock with extra steps. A `threading.Lock` blocks the whole event loop\nwhen contended, stalling every coroutine instead of just the waiter. asyncio's primitives exist because\nwaiting must itself be an `await` that yields to the loop.","how":"### Where races come from\nIn asyncio a race needs three things: shared mutable state, a read and a dependent write, and an\n`await` between them. Every `await` is a point where the loop may run any other ready coroutine. So\nthe audit question for a coroutine is: \"what shared state did I read before this await, and do I still\nrely on it after?\"\n\nThe interleaving for the token refresh, with two requests A and B:\n\n```\nA: token expired? yes          B: (not yet run)\nA: await refresh()  -> yields\n                               B: token expired? yes   (A has not written yet)\n                               B: await refresh()  -> yields\nA: store token_1\n                               B: store token_2        (token_1 now revoked)\n```\n\nNothing here is a torn write or a corrupted object, which is what threads give you; it is a stale\ndecision. That is why asyncio races are logic bugs that pass unit tests (which run one coroutine at a\ntime) and appear only under concurrent load.\n\n### asyncio.Lock\nA lock is owned by at most one coroutine. `async with lock:` awaits `acquire()`; if the lock is held,\nthe coroutine is parked in a FIFO queue of waiters and the loop runs other work. On release the next\nwaiter is woken. Waiting costs nothing but a queued future, *because* no thread is blocked. The\nstandard pattern for a shared cache is **double-checked locking**: check without the lock on the fast\npath, take the lock on a miss, then check again inside, *because* another coroutine may have refreshed\nthe value while you waited for the lock. The lock is not reentrant: acquiring it again from the\ncoroutine that holds it deadlocks. An uncontended `acquire()` returns without suspending, so a lock\ncosts almost nothing until there is contention. Because `async with` releases in its exit, a\ncoroutine cancelled while holding the lock still releases it, and one cancelled while *waiting* is\nremoved from the queue.\n\n### Single-flight: share the in-flight work instead of locking around it\nAn alternative to the lock for \"compute once, many waiters\" is to store the in-flight task itself:\nthe first caller on a miss creates `self._refresh = asyncio.create_task(fetch())`, and every caller,\nfirst or later, does `await self._refresh`. All waiters get the same result, or the same exception, from\none network call. It needs care with failure (clear the stored task when it raises, or every later\ncaller re-reads the cached error) and with cancellation (one caller being cancelled should not cancel\nthe shared task, which is what `asyncio.shield` around the await is for).\n\n### asyncio.Semaphore\nA semaphore holds a counter initialised to N. `acquire()` decrements it, or waits if it is zero;\n`release()` increments it and wakes a waiter. `async with sem:` around an LLM call therefore keeps at\nmost N calls in flight no matter how many tasks exist. `BoundedSemaphore` additionally raises if you\nrelease more times than you acquired, which catches bookkeeping bugs. A semaphore caps *concurrency*.\nThroughput is concurrency divided by latency (Little's law), so if latency rises, the same semaphore\nyields fewer requests per second. The same law sizes it: to sustain λ requests per second against\nlatency W seconds you need N ≈ λ × W slots, so 20 requests/s at 1.5 s latency needs about 30. Fewer and\nyou cannot reach the target; many more and you only queue inside the provider instead of inside your\nprocess. A provider limit expressed in requests or tokens per minute needs a rate limiter, such as a\ntoken bucket, often combined with a semaphore.\n\n### The other primitives\n`asyncio.Event` lets many coroutines wait until one sets a flag, useful for \"model warmed up\" or\n\"shutdown started\". `asyncio.Condition` combines a lock with wait/notify for more complex predicates.\n`asyncio.Queue(maxsize=N)` is often the best primitive of all: a bounded producer/consumer queue\ngives you concurrency limits and backpressure without explicit locking.\n\n### What they do not cover\nThese primitives coordinate coroutines on one event loop. They are not thread-safe and do nothing\nacross processes or replicas. Two Uvicorn workers each holding their own `Semaphore(8)` allow 16\nconcurrent calls; a cross-replica limit needs a shared store such as Redis or a gateway.","example":"```python\nimport asyncio, time\n\nclass TokenCache:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._token: str | None = None\n        self._expires = 0.0\n        self._lock = asyncio.Lock()\n        self.refreshes = 0\n\n    def _valid(self) -> bool:\n        return self._token is not None and time.monotonic() < self._expires\n\n    async def get(self) -> str:\n        if self._valid():\n            return self._token            # fast path, no lock\n        async with self._lock:\n            if not self._valid():         # re-check: someone may have refreshed\n                self.refreshes += 1\n                await asyncio.sleep(0.1)  # stands in for the auth call\n                self._token, self._expires = \"tok\", time.monotonic() + 300\n            return self._token\n\nasync def call_llm(cache: TokenCache, sem: asyncio.Semaphore, i: int) -> int:\n    async with sem:                       # at most 8 requests in flight\n        await cache.get()\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.05)         # stands in for the model call\n        return i\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    cache, sem = TokenCache(), asyncio.Semaphore(8)\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(call_llm(cache, sem, i) for i in range(100)))\n    print(len(results), \"calls,\", cache.refreshes, \"token refresh\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\nThis prints `100 calls, 1 token refresh`. Remove the lock and the first eight coroutines through the\nsemaphore all see no token, all await the auth call, and you get eight refreshes. Raise the semaphore\nto 100 and you get 100. The bug scales with your concurrency setting, which is why it often appears\nonly under load.","tradeoffs":["A lock makes a critical section correct, but serialises it; if the section contains a slow await, throughput collapses to one call at a time, so keep only the minimum inside the lock.","A tight semaphore protects the provider and your memory, but leaves capacity unused; when the provider quota is generous and latency is high, a larger limit gives more throughput.","In-process primitives are fast and simple, but are invisible to other workers and replicas; once you scale horizontally, limits must move to a shared store or an API gateway.","A bounded `asyncio.Queue` with fixed workers is easier to reason about than many tasks behind a semaphore, at the cost of more structure; it wins when work arrives continuously rather than in batches."],"pitfalls":["Assuming single-threaded means race-free; the symptom is duplicate refreshes, duplicate writes or double-spent budgets that only show up under concurrent load.","Creating a primitive at module import time and using it from different event loops, as tests often do; since 3.10 a primitive binds to the loop on which it first has to wait and raises `RuntimeError` (\"bound to a different event loop\") when it must wait on another loop, so the failure appears only under contention and looks flaky.","Using a semaphore to enforce a requests-per-minute quota; the symptom is 429s returning when provider latency drops, because the same concurrency then produces more requests per minute.","Re-acquiring the same `asyncio.Lock` in a nested call; the coroutine waits on itself forever and the request hangs with no error.","Holding a lock or semaphore across a call with no timeout; one hung upstream request starves every waiter behind it."],"when":"Reach for a lock when a coroutine reads shared state, awaits, then writes based on what it read:\ntoken and client caches, lazy model or index loading, per-conversation state in an agent, budget\ncounters. Reach for a semaphore (or a bounded queue) whenever you fan out to an external dependency\nwith finite capacity: LLM providers, embedding APIs, vector stores, scrapers. Do not add locks around\ncode with no await inside; it is already atomic with respect to other coroutines. Measure in-flight\nrequests, 429 rate and queue wait time; if wait time dominates latency, the limit is too tight or the\ndependency is saturated. In interviews, be ready to explain why asyncio can race at all.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wraps provider calls in a semaphore sized from the quota, and uses double-checked locking for shared clients and token caches.","architect":"Decides where concurrency limits live, per process, per replica or in a shared gateway, so the total stays inside provider quotas as the fleet scales.","fde":"Tunes a customer's batch-ingestion concurrency against their own API quota and explains why doubling workers doubled their 429s instead of their throughput."},"check":{"q":"A team wraps OpenAI calls in `asyncio.Semaphore(50)` per worker, runs 4 Uvicorn workers, and still\ngets 429s on a 3,000 requests-per-minute quota. Explain and propose a fix.","a":"The semaphore is per process, so the fleet allows 200 concurrent calls, and it limits concurrency\nrather than rate. By Little's law, 200 in flight at 2 seconds average latency is about 6,000 requests per\nminute (illustrative numbers), double the quota, and if latency drops the rate rises further. The fix is\na rate limiter based on requests and tokens per minute, shared across workers through Redis or enforced\nby a gateway, with the semaphore kept only to bound memory and connections. Retries on 429 should honour\n`Retry-After` with jittered backoff so they do not amplify the burst."},"quiz":[{"q":"In single-threaded asyncio code, when can two coroutines interfere with each other's view of shared state?","options":["Never, because only one coroutine runs at a time","Only when the GIL is released by a C extension","When one reads the state, awaits something, and then acts on what it read","Only when the shared state is a list or dict rather than an int"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The event loop switches coroutines only at awaits, so a read and a dependent write separated by an await can interleave with another coroutine doing the same. Code with no await in between is atomic."},{"q":"Why does the token cache re-check validity after acquiring the lock?","options":["Because asyncio.Lock does not guarantee mutual exclusion","Because another coroutine may have refreshed the token while this one waited for the lock","Because the clock may have moved backwards during the await","Because the fast-path check is not allowed to read private attributes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Several coroutines can miss on the fast path at once; they queue on the lock. The first refreshes, and without the second check every waiter behind it would refresh again on entry."},{"q":"A semaphore of 10 wraps an API with 1-second latency. Latency improves to 0.25 seconds. What happens to request rate?","options":["It stays at 10 requests per second because the semaphore is unchanged","It rises to about 40 requests per second because the semaphore bounds concurrency, not rate","It falls, because shorter requests release the semaphore less often","It becomes unbounded because the semaphore stops blocking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Throughput equals concurrency divided by latency. Ten slots each finishing in 0.25 seconds allow about 40 requests per second, which is why semaphores cannot enforce per-minute quotas."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","sources":["Official docs: asyncio, threading, multiprocessing, concurrent.futures","PEP 703 — Making the Global Interpreter Lock Optional in CPython"],"why":"AI backends have two very different kinds of work. Most wall-clock time goes to waiting: on a model\nprovider, an embedding API, a vector database, an S3 download. Some work is pure CPU: parsing PDFs,\nchunking text, tokenising, JSON-schema validation of large outputs, local re-ranking. Python gives you\nthree concurrency models, and each fits only some of this.\n\nThe naive approach is to pick one for everything. \"Everything async\" falls over on the first\nsynchronous library (a vendor SDK, a PDF parser, a database driver without async support): it blocks\nthe event loop, and every concurrent request stalls behind it. \"Everything threads\" works for I/O but\ndoes not speed up pure-Python CPU work, because in standard CPython the GIL lets only one thread run\nPython bytecode at a time, and thousands of threads for thousands of in-flight requests are heavy.\n\"Everything multiprocessing\" gives true CPU parallelism, but each process has its own memory, model\ncopies and connection pools, and every argument and result is pickled across a pipe, which can cost\nmore than the work itself.\n\nThe decision exists because the right answer depends on the bottleneck, and getting it wrong is\nexpensive. Usually the result is not a crash but a service whose throughput does not move however\nmany workers you add.","how":"### asyncio: one thread, many waits\nAn event loop multiplexes sockets and resumes coroutines when their I/O is ready. A suspended\ncoroutine costs a small heap object, so tens of thousands of concurrent LLM streams are feasible on\none thread. Switching happens only at `await`, which makes races easier to reason about. The cost is\n\"function colouring\": every layer must be async, and any blocking call freezes the loop. It gives no\nCPU parallelism at all.\n\n### Threads: preemptive, shared memory, GIL-limited\nOS threads are scheduled by the kernel and share the process's memory. In standard CPython a thread\nholds the GIL while running bytecode and releases it on blocking I/O and inside C extensions that\nchoose to release it (NumPy on large arrays, many PyTorch ops, hashlib, compression). So threads give\nreal concurrency for I/O and for GIL-releasing native code, but pure-Python CPU work runs one thread\nat a time. Each thread reserves its own stack and the OS schedules it, so the practical count is\nhundreds to low thousands, not tens of thousands. Switching can happen between almost any two\nbytecodes, so shared state needs `threading.Lock`.\n\n### Processes: true parallelism, no shared heap\n`multiprocessing` and `ProcessPoolExecutor` start separate interpreters, each with its own GIL, so\nCPU-bound Python scales across cores. The price: arguments and results are pickled (lambdas and open\nclients cannot be sent), each worker pays startup and import cost, and memory is multiplied by the\nnumber of workers. The start method matters. `fork` copies the parent quickly but is unsafe if the\nparent has threads or CUDA state; `spawn`, the default on macOS and Windows, starts clean but\nre-imports your modules (since 3.14 the Linux default is `forkserver`, which has the same import\nrequirements), so worker entry points must be importable and guarded by\n`if __name__ == \"__main__\":`. Heavy per-worker state (a tokenizer, a parser model) belongs in the\npool's `initializer=` function, which runs once per worker, *because* passing it with every task would\npickle it every time. The test for whether a process pool pays off is a ratio: per-task compute time\nagainst the time to pickle the arguments and result (`len(pickle.dumps(arg))` and a quick timing tell\nyou). Batch small items into chunks (`chunksize=` on `map`) until compute dominates.\n\n### A decision procedure\n1. Measure first: wall time, process CPU time and per-core utilisation for a representative run.\n2. CPU well below one core while latency is high: you are waiting. Use asyncio if the libraries are\n   async; threads if they are blocking. Either way, bound concurrency to what the dependency allows.\n3. One core pinned and adding threads changes nothing: pure-Python CPU work under the GIL. Use\n   processes, or move the hot loop into native code (NumPy, Polars, a compiled library).\n4. Several cores busy with threads: the hot path is in GIL-releasing native code, and threads are the\n   cheapest parallelism you have, sharing memory and model weights.\n5. The work mixes both: keep one async core and push each kind of work to the executor that matches it.\n\n### Bridges between them\n`await asyncio.to_thread(fn, *args)` (3.9+) runs a blocking call in the default thread pool without\nblocking the loop. `loop.run_in_executor(process_pool, fn, *args)` sends CPU work to processes and\nreturns an awaitable. This is the usual shape: async at the edges for I/O fan-out, executors for\neverything else. The free-threaded CPython build introduced by PEP 703 removes the GIL in an optional\nbuild, which may change the thread-versus-process answer for CPU work; check its status and your\ndependencies' support for your Python version before relying on it.","example":"A worked scenario, with illustrative numbers. An ingestion job must process 2,000 PDFs: download each\nfrom S3 (about 0.3 s, network), extract text with a pure-Python parser (about 0.5 s CPU), then embed\nchunks via an API (about 0.4 s, network). On an 8-core machine:\n\n- **Sequential:** 2,000 × 1.2 s = 2,400 s, about 40 minutes.\n- **All asyncio, parser called directly:** downloads and embeddings overlap, but every parse blocks the\n  loop. Parsing alone is 2,000 × 0.5 s = 1,000 s on one core, so the job takes at least ~17 minutes, and\n  during each parse no downloads or embeddings make progress.\n- **Threads for everything:** I/O overlaps well, but parses still serialise on the GIL: the same\n  ~1,000 s floor, plus contention.\n- **Async I/O plus a process pool of 8 for parsing:** parse time becomes roughly 1,000 s ÷ 8 ≈ 125 s if\n  it scales cleanly. With downloads and embeddings bounded by a semaphore and overlapped, the job is\n  near the parse floor, a couple of minutes, provided pickling the PDF bytes in and the extracted text\n  back is cheap relative to 0.5 s of work.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def handle(pdf_key, pool, sem):                   # pool: ProcessPoolExecutor(8)\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n    async with sem:\n        raw = await s3_get(pdf_key)                     # async I/O\n    text = await loop.run_in_executor(pool, parse, raw) # CPU in a process\n    async with sem:\n        return await embed(chunk(text))                 # async I/O\n```","tradeoffs":["asyncio gives the most concurrency per byte of memory, but demands async libraries throughout; one synchronous dependency in the hot path makes threads the pragmatic choice.","Threads are the easiest way to parallelise blocking SDK calls, but shared state needs locks and pure-Python CPU work gains nothing; that flips when the CPU work is in GIL-releasing native code.","Processes deliver real CPU parallelism, at the cost of pickling, startup and memory per worker; they stop paying off when each task is so small that serialisation dominates the compute.","Separate server workers (Uvicorn/Gunicorn processes) scale a web tier across cores without code changes, but multiply model memory and connection pools per worker, which matters with local models."],"pitfalls":["Calling a synchronous SDK or `requests` inside `async def`; the symptom is p99 latency rising for all requests on a worker while CPU stays low.","Adding threads to speed up pure-Python parsing or tokenisation; CPU sits at about one core and wall time barely changes.","Passing large objects (documents, NumPy arrays, model weights) to a process pool per task; profiling shows most time in pickling and worker memory spikes.","Using `fork` after starting threads or initialising CUDA in the parent; the symptom is workers that deadlock intermittently on a lock some parent thread held at fork time, or PyTorch's \"Cannot re-initialize CUDA in forked subprocess\" error.","Loading a local model at import time in a module that `spawn` re-imports; each worker loads its own copy and the machine runs out of memory."],"when":"Start by measuring where time goes: if CPU is low while latency is high, you are I/O-bound; if one\ncore is pinned, you are CPU-bound in Python. For an API server calling LLMs and vector stores, use\nasyncio and async clients such as `httpx` or the official async SDKs. Wrap unavoidable blocking calls\nin `asyncio.to_thread`. For heavy parsing, chunking or local inference preprocessing, use a process\npool, or move the work to a separate worker service. For GPU inference, the work runs in native code\nand a dedicated serving engine usually beats rolling your own concurrency. In interviews, justify the\nchoice from the bottleneck, not from preference.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps the event loop free of blocking calls, uses `to_thread` and process pools as bridges, and profiles before adding workers.","architect":"Splits I/O-bound request handling from CPU-bound batch work into separately scaled services, and decides worker counts against memory budgets.","fde":"Finds the synchronous call blocking a customer's async service, or the thread pool that cannot speed up their parser, and fixes it in their codebase."},"check":{"q":"A team's chunking step (pure-Python regex and string splitting, about 2 ms per document) is too slow.\nThey wrap it in a 16-thread pool: no change, and `top` shows the process at about 100% CPU. They switch to a\n`ProcessPoolExecutor(16)` submitting one document per task, and it gets slower. Explain both results and\nwhat you would do.","a":"The threads changed nothing because the work is pure-Python bytecode: under the GIL only one thread\nruns at a time, and \"100% CPU\" means one core, the fingerprint of GIL-bound work. The process pool removed\nthe GIL limit but introduced a per-task cost paid serially in the parent: it pickles every document,\npushes it through a pipe, tracks a future per task, and unpickles every list of chunks that comes back.\nSixteen workers each finishing a task every 2 ms demand thousands of tasks per second from that one\nparent thread, so the parent becomes the bottleneck, and when documents are large the per-task\nserialisation alone can exceed the 2 ms of work it is shipping. The fix keeps\nprocesses but changes the grain: send batches of a few hundred documents per task (`map` with\n`chunksize`, or explicit batches) so compute dominates, initialise any heavy objects once per worker,\nand size the pool to physical cores. Then measure throughput against batch size, because the optimum is\nempirical. If the hot loop can be expressed as vectorised Polars string operations, that may beat both."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which workload is the best fit for a `ProcessPoolExecutor` rather than threads or asyncio?","options":["Calling an LLM API 5,000 times with async-capable clients","Running large NumPy matrix multiplications that release the GIL","Parsing thousands of documents with a pure-Python parser","Streaming tokens from a model provider to many websocket clients"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pure-Python CPU work is serialised by the GIL in threads and blocks the loop in asyncio, so only separate processes run it in parallel. NumPy releasing the GIL makes threads viable for the second option."},{"q":"What does `await asyncio.to_thread(fn)` achieve inside a coroutine?","options":["It makes `fn` run in parallel with other Python bytecode regardless of the GIL","It runs the blocking `fn` in a worker thread so the event loop keeps serving other coroutines","It converts `fn` into a coroutine that yields at each line","It runs `fn` in a separate process to avoid the GIL"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The event loop stays responsive because the blocking call waits in another thread. It does not bypass the GIL, so it helps for blocking I/O but not for pure-Python CPU work."},{"q":"Why can a process pool make a job slower than running it sequentially?","options":["Processes share one GIL, so they contend more than threads","The operating system limits processes to one core each by default","Process pools cannot run more than one task per worker","Each task's arguments and results are pickled and copied, which can cost more than tiny tasks"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Processes have separate memory, so data crosses a pipe as pickled bytes. When per-task compute is small or the payload is large, serialisation and startup overhead outweigh the parallel speed-up."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/gil","sources":["PEP 703 — Making the Global Interpreter Lock Optional in CPython","PEP 684 — A Per-Interpreter GIL","Official docs: Python/C API (Thread State and the Global Interpreter Lock)"],"why":"Every CPython object carries a reference count that is incremented and decremented constantly, on\nnearly every assignment, call and attribute access. If two threads update the same count at once\nwithout coordination, the count goes wrong, and the object is either freed while still in use (a\ncrash) or never freed (a leak). The same is true of the interpreter's internal structures: dicts\nbacking modules and classes, the allocator's free lists, the import machinery.\n\nThe alternatives are expensive. Fine-grained locks on every object, or atomic instructions on every\nrefcount change, slow single-threaded code, which was and is the common case. They also force every C\nextension author to reason about concurrency. One global lock made the interpreter and its huge C\nextension ecosystem (NumPy, database drivers, compression) easy to write correctly, and kept\nsingle-threaded Python fast. The cost was paid later, when multi-core machines became standard: a\nCPU-bound Python program using threads still uses roughly one core.\n\nFor AI engineers the GIL matters because their workloads straddle the line. Calling LLM APIs is\nI/O-bound and threads or asyncio handle it fine. Tokenising a corpus, parsing documents or running\na pure-Python scoring loop is CPU-bound, and adding threads there gives almost nothing. Knowing which\nside of the line a workload sits on decides whether to use threads, processes or native code.","how":"### Holding and handing over\nA thread must hold the GIL to run bytecode or touch Python objects. A running thread gives it up in\ntwo ways. **Voluntarily** around blocking operations: socket reads, file I/O, `time.sleep`, waiting on\na lock. CPython's I/O code releases the GIL before the system call and reacquires it after, *because*\nthere is no reason to block other threads while the kernel waits. **On request**: if another thread\nhas waited longer than the switch interval (`sys.getswitchinterval()`, 5 ms by default), it sets a\nflag, and the running thread drops the GIL at its next check point in the eval loop so the waiter can\nrun. That forced handover is what keeps threads fair, and it is also why CPU-bound threads add\nswitching overhead rather than speed.\n\n### The hidden cost: CPU threads slow down I/O threads\nThe handover rule has a consequence that bites async services. When an I/O thread's socket becomes\nready, the kernel wakes it, but it still has to reacquire the GIL before it can run any Python. If a\nCPU-bound thread holds the GIL, the I/O thread waits until the running thread reaches a check point\nand yields, which is bounded by roughly the switch interval each time it needs the lock back. An\nevent-loop thread needs the GIL for every task step, so running pure-Python CPU work in\n`asyncio.to_thread` beside the loop keeps the loop *unblocked* but not *unaffected*: each step can be\ndelayed by milliseconds, and streaming responses get visibly jittery. The work belongs in a process.\n\n### Native code can opt out\nC, C++ and Rust extensions can release the GIL while working on their own memory, as long as they\ntouch no Python objects. In C this is the `Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS` / `Py_END_ALLOW_THREADS` pair around\nthe native work, and the extension must reacquire the GIL before it builds the Python result. NumPy does this for many operations on large arrays, PyTorch for most tensor\nops, `hashlib` for large inputs, and compression libraries for large buffers. So \"Python is\nsingle-core\" is wrong for these libraries: a thread pool running NumPy-heavy or PyTorch CPU work can\nuse several cores. Whether a given call releases the GIL is a property of that library and function,\nso check or measure rather than assume.\n\n### What the GIL does not give you\nThe GIL protects the interpreter's integrity, not your invariants. `counter += 1` compiles to\nseveral bytecodes (load, add, store), and a switch can occur between them, so two threads can lose an\nupdate. Check-then-act on a dict across threads needs a `threading.Lock`. Whether you can reproduce\nthe race depends on the Python version and timing, which makes it more dangerous, not less.\n\nThe GIL does make some single operations on built-in types atomic as a side effect: one\n`list.append` or one `d[key] = value` cannot be torn. Code that relies on this \"works\" by accident of\nimplementation. It is why `queue.Queue` and explicit locks are the documented tools, and why the\nfree-threaded build had to add per-object locking inside built-in containers to keep such single\noperations safe.\n\n### Ways around it, and its future\nProcesses (`multiprocessing`, `ProcessPoolExecutor`) each have their own interpreter and GIL. PEP 684\n(Python 3.12) gave each sub-interpreter its own GIL, enabling parallelism within one process with\nisolated state; in 3.12 this was reachable only through the C API, and a Python-level\n`concurrent.interpreters` module followed in 3.14 (PEP 734). PEP 703 introduced an optional\nfree-threaded CPython build with no GIL, using biased reference counting and other techniques\n(experimental in 3.13, officially supported but still a separate build in 3.14); extensions must be\nbuilt and marked compatible with it, and it has some single-threaded overhead. Its support status changes by release, so check the docs for\nyour version and your dependencies before planning around it.","example":"```python\nimport hashlib, time\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor, ThreadPoolExecutor\n\ndef py_work(n: int) -> int:            # pure Python: holds the GIL throughout\n    total = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        total += i * i % 7\n    return total\n\ndef c_work(data: bytes) -> str:        # hashlib releases the GIL on large inputs\n    return hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()\n\ndef timed(label, run):\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    run()\n    print(f\"{label:<20}{time.perf_counter() - t:6.2f}s\")\n\ndef pool(cls, fn, args):\n    with cls(max_workers=4) as ex:\n        list(ex.map(fn, args))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    nums, blobs = [5_000_000] * 4, [b\"x\" * 200_000_000] * 4\n    timed(\"python, sequential\", lambda: [py_work(n) for n in nums])\n    timed(\"python, 4 threads\", lambda: pool(ThreadPoolExecutor, py_work, nums))\n    timed(\"python, 4 procs\", lambda: pool(ProcessPoolExecutor, py_work, nums))\n    timed(\"sha256, sequential\", lambda: [c_work(b) for b in blobs])\n    timed(\"sha256, 4 threads\", lambda: pool(ThreadPoolExecutor, c_work, blobs))\n```\nOn a standard (GIL) CPython on a multi-core laptop, the pattern is: four threads of pure Python take\nabout the same time as sequential, four processes take roughly a third of it (less than 4× because of\nprocess startup), and four threads hashing large buffers run about 4× faster than sequential, because\n`hashlib` releases the GIL. Absolute times depend on the machine; the ratios are the lesson.","tradeoffs":["The GIL keeps single-threaded code and C extensions simple and fast, but caps pure-Python CPU work at one core per process; the trade flips when your hot loop lives in GIL-releasing native code.","Processes sidestep the GIL completely, at the cost of separate memory, pickled arguments and slower startup; they win when each task carries far more compute than data.","Moving a hot loop into NumPy, a compiled extension or a Rust library removes both the GIL limit and interpreter overhead, but adds a build and maintenance burden worth paying only for real hot spots.","A free-threaded build allows true thread parallelism for Python code, but trades some single-thread speed and depends on every compiled dependency supporting it, so it suits measured, controlled deployments."],"pitfalls":["Adding threads to speed up a pure-Python tokenising or parsing loop; the symptom is CPU pinned near 100% of one core and no wall-clock improvement.","Assuming the GIL makes shared counters or dicts thread-safe; the symptom is occasional lost updates or inconsistent state that disappears when you add logging.","Blaming the GIL for a slow I/O-bound service; if CPU is low and latency high, the problem is waiting or blocking calls, and the GIL is not the bottleneck.","Assuming a library releases the GIL because it is written in C; some hold it for the whole call, and profiling shows threads queuing instead of running in parallel.","Installing a free-threaded build and importing an extension not marked compatible with it; CPython then re-enables the GIL at runtime (with a warning), threads stop scaling, and `sys._is_gil_enabled()` (3.13+) returns `True` where you expected `False`."],"when":"You do not use the GIL; you design around it. When a Python service is slow, first ask whether it is\nwaiting (I/O) or computing (CPU). For I/O, threads or asyncio are fine and the GIL is irrelevant. For\nCPU-bound pure Python, use processes, vectorise with NumPy or Polars, or move the hot loop to native\ncode. For CPU work inside NumPy or PyTorch, threads can work, and you can measure it with a quick timing\nlike the example. The quickest diagnostic is the ratio of process CPU time to wall time with N threads:\nabout 1.0 means the GIL is serialising you, close to N means the work releases it. A sampling profiler\nsuch as py-spy shows which thread holds the GIL when that ratio surprises you. Track the free-threaded build's status if you run CPU-heavy Python at scale. In\ninterviews, explain why the GIL exists (refcounting), when it bites (CPU-bound bytecode), and why it does\nnot make code thread-safe.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Profiles before parallelising, uses processes or vectorised libraries for CPU-bound preprocessing, and still guards shared state across threads with locks.","architect":"Plans process-level scaling for CPU-heavy stages and pushes heavy compute into native libraries or separate services instead of fighting the GIL in one process.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their sixteen-thread ingestion script uses one core, and fixes it with a process pool or a vectorised rewrite on their hardware."},"check":{"q":"A colleague's embedding-prep script runs a pure-Python text-cleaning function over 1M documents with a\n32-thread pool on a 32-core machine and sees no speed-up. They propose 64 threads. What do you say?","a":"The cleaning function is pure-Python CPU work, so in standard CPython the GIL lets only one thread\nexecute bytecode at a time; more threads only add switching overhead, which is why 32 threads barely beat\none. The fix is a `ProcessPoolExecutor` with roughly one worker per core, sending documents in chunks so\npickling is amortised, or moving the hot loop into native code: Polars string expressions or a compiled\nlibrary. Fewer, larger `re` calls also cut per-document interpreter overhead, but the `re` module holds the\nGIL, so regex work still does not parallelise across threads. I would time a small sample sequentially and with processes to confirm the gain before running\nthe full million. Threads would be right only if the work were I/O or GIL-releasing native code."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did CPython adopt a single global lock rather than fine-grained locks?","options":["Because operating systems in the 1990s did not support threads","Because reference counting and C extensions needed protection, and one lock kept single-threaded code fast and simple","Because a global lock makes all user code thread-safe automatically","Because fine-grained locks are impossible to implement in C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every object's refcount changes constantly; protecting each one individually would slow the common single-threaded case and complicate every extension. The GIL protects interpreter state, not user invariants."},{"q":"A thread pool running large NumPy matrix multiplications uses several cores. Why is that possible under the GIL?","options":["NumPy spawns hidden subprocesses for each call","The GIL is only enforced for I/O operations","NumPy releases the GIL inside its native code while it works on its own buffers","Threads automatically bypass the GIL after the switch interval"],"answer":2,"explanation":"C extensions may drop the GIL while not touching Python objects, so NumPy's compiled kernels run in parallel across threads. The switch interval only governs handover between threads running bytecode."},{"q":"Two threads each run `counter += 1` a million times on a shared int. What can happen in standard CPython?","options":["The result is always exactly two million because of the GIL","The program raises a RuntimeError for concurrent modification","The result is always one million because the threads serialise","Updates can be lost, because the increment spans several bytecodes and a switch can occur between them"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The GIL makes each bytecode safe, not each statement. Load, add and store are separate steps, so an interleaving can overwrite another thread's increment; whether it shows up depends on version and timing."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/generators","sources":["PEP 255 — Simple Generators","PEP 380 — Syntax for Delegating to a Subgenerator","PEP 525 — Asynchronous Generators","Official docs: Python language reference (yield expressions)"],"why":"AI work is full of sequences too large or too slow to materialise: a corpus to chunk and embed, an eval\nset of 50,000 prompts, rows from a vector store export, server-sent events from a model. The naive\napproach builds lists: read every line, parse every document, chunk everything, then embed. Memory grows\nwith the dataset, the first embedding call waits until the entire corpus is parsed, and a crash at item\n900,000 loses all the work in flight.\n\nThe classic alternative is a hand-written iterator class with `__iter__`, `__next__` and explicit state\nfields for \"which file, which line, which chunk offset\". It works, but the control flow that would be a\nsimple nested loop gets turned inside out into a state machine, which is error-prone and hard to read.\n\nGenerators let you write the natural loop and have the interpreter keep the state for you. The function's\nframe is suspended at `yield` and resumed on the next request, so local variables, the open file and the\nloop position survive between items. The consumer pulls items as fast as it can use them, which gives\nstreaming, early results and bounded memory for free.","how":"### The protocol\nCalling a function that contains `yield` runs none of its body; it returns a generator object, which is\nan iterator. Each `next()` resumes execution until the next `yield`, which hands a value out and freezes\nthe frame. When the function returns, the generator raises `StopIteration` (carrying the return value),\nand `for` loops treat that as the end. A generator is single-pass: once exhausted, iterating it again\nyields nothing, with no error.\n\nMechanically, the generator object owns a frame: the local variables, the evaluation stack and the\nposition of the last executed instruction. An ordinary call creates a frame and discards it on\nreturn; a generator keeps its frame alive between `next()` calls, which is the whole trick. You can\nwatch the lifecycle with `inspect.getgeneratorstate(g)`: `GEN_CREATED` before the first `next()`,\n`GEN_RUNNING` while executing, `GEN_SUSPENDED` at a `yield`, `GEN_CLOSED` once finished. A generator\ncannot be resumed while it is running, so calling `next()` on one already executing, for example from\na second thread, raises `ValueError: generator already executing`.\n\n### Pipelines compose lazily\nGenerators chain: `chunks(read_jsonl(path))` builds a pipeline, but no work happens until the consumer\nasks for the first item. Then one line is read, parsed, chunked and passed down. Memory is bounded by\nwhat each stage holds, not by the dataset, *because* items flow one at a time. The flow is\n**pull-based**: the consumer's `next()` propagates up the chain, so a slow consumer (an embedding API\nat its rate limit) automatically slows reading, and nothing piles up between stages. That is\nbackpressure without any queue. Generator expressions,\n`(f(x) for x in xs)`, are the inline form; `itertools` (`islice`, `chain`, and `batched` from 3.12)\nsupplies the standard pipeline stages.\n\n### Two-way communication and delegation\n`yield` is an expression: `gen.send(value)` resumes the generator and makes the paused `yield` evaluate\nto that value; `gen.throw(exc)` raises an exception at that point; `gen.close()` raises `GeneratorExit`\nthere so `finally` blocks run. `yield from sub` (PEP 380) delegates to a sub-generator, passing sends and\nthrows through and evaluating to its return value, *because* refactoring a generator into helpers would\notherwise need hand-written forwarding. This send-and-suspend machinery is the ancestor of coroutines:\n`async`/`await` grew out of generator-based coroutines.\n\n### Cleanup and async generators\nA generator that holds a resource (an open file inside `with`) releases it when it finishes, when\n`close()` is called, or when it is garbage-collected, since `close()` runs the `finally`. If a consumer\nbreaks out early and keeps a reference, the resource stays open until that happens. `async def` with\n`yield` makes an async generator (PEP 525), consumed with `async for`; its `finally` may need to await,\nso early exit should be handled with `contextlib.aclosing()` (3.10+) to close it deterministically\nrather than leaving cleanup to the event loop's finaliser.","example":"```python\nimport itertools, json\nfrom collections.abc import Iterator\n\ndef read_jsonl(path: str) -> Iterator[dict]:\n    with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:      # stays open while iterated\n        for line_no, line in enumerate(f, 1):\n            if not line.strip():\n                continue\n            try:\n                yield json.loads(line)\n            except json.JSONDecodeError:\n                print(f\"skipping malformed line {line_no}\")\n\ndef chunks(docs: Iterator[dict], size: int = 800) -> Iterator[tuple[str, str]]:\n    for doc in docs:\n        text = doc[\"text\"]\n        for start in range(0, len(text), size):\n            yield doc[\"id\"], text[start:start + size]\n\ndef embed_corpus(path: str, batch_size: int = 64) -> int:\n    sent = 0\n    for batch in itertools.batched(chunks(read_jsonl(path)), batch_size):  # 3.12+\n        # vectors = embed_api([text for _, text in batch])  -> upsert by id\n        sent += len(batch)\n    return sent\n```\nIllustrative sizes: for a 4 GB JSONL corpus, the list-based version holds the parsed corpus plus every\nchunk in memory before the first API call. This pipeline holds one line, one parsed document and one\nbatch of 64 × 800 characters (about 50 KB of text) at a time, and the first batch is ready to send within\nmilliseconds of starting, instead of after the whole corpus is parsed. A malformed line is skipped and logged rather than killing a long job.\n\nTo see the laziness, put a `print` in each stage and run it on a two-document file with 2,000-character\ntexts. The output interleaves: read doc 1, chunk 1a, chunk 1b, chunk 1c, read doc 2, chunk 2a, and so on,\nwith the batch closing once 64 chunks (or the end of input) arrive. A list pipeline would print every\n\"read\" before the first \"chunk\". The interleaving is the mechanism: `batched` pulls a chunk, `chunks`\nneeds a document, `read_jsonl` resumes inside its `for` over the file, reads one line, and suspends\nagain at `yield`, with the file still open and the line counter intact.","tradeoffs":["Lazy pipelines keep memory flat and deliver first results early, but each item is produced once; if you need random access, `len()` or several passes, materialise to a list or reopen the source.","Generators make streaming code read like a plain loop, but errors surface at consumption time, not at the call, so a traceback may point at the consumer far from the faulty stage.","Per-item generator overhead is negligible for I/O-bound work, but for numeric hot loops over millions of small values, vectorised NumPy or Polars operations are far faster than yielding Python objects.","Async generators expose network streams naturally, at the cost of trickier cleanup when consumers stop early; for complex flows a bounded `asyncio.Queue` between tasks can be clearer."],"pitfalls":["Iterating the same generator twice, for example once to count and once to process; the second pass silently yields nothing and an eval reports zero results.","Calling a generator function and expecting side effects; nothing runs until it is iterated, so a \"write records\" generator that is never consumed writes nothing.","Breaking out of an async generator that wraps an HTTP stream without `aclosing`; the symptom is connection-pool exhaustion or \"unclosed\" warnings under load.","Wrapping a generator call in `try/except` expecting to catch its errors; exceptions are raised later during iteration, outside the handler, so the guard never triggers.","Converting the pipeline to a list for debugging (`list(gen)`) and leaving it in; memory use returns to dataset-sized and the job slows on large inputs.","Calling `next(it)` inside a generator on an iterator that may be exhausted; since PEP 479 the stray `StopIteration` is converted to `RuntimeError: generator raised StopIteration` instead of silently ending the outer generator, so use `next(it, None)` or catch it explicitly.","Sharing one generator between threads or DataLoader-style workers; the symptom is `ValueError: generator already executing` or duplicated and missing items, so give each worker its own source or put a `queue.Queue` between them."],"when":"Use generators whenever you process sequences that are large, slow to arrive or potentially unbounded:\nreading JSONL and Parquet exports row by row, chunking documents for embedding, batching requests,\npaginating through an API, and streaming model output to a client. FastAPI's `StreamingResponse` and most\nSDK streaming interfaces accept or return (async) iterators. Prefer a list when the data is small and you\nneed it more than once. Measure peak memory before and after converting a list pipeline. In interviews,\nexpect to explain laziness, single-pass behaviour, `yield from`, and how async generators relate to\ntoken streaming.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes ingestion and eval pipelines as generator stages with batching, and wraps streaming responses in async generators closed with `aclosing`.","architect":"Designs data and streaming paths so memory stays bounded by batch size, not dataset size, and so backpressure flows from the consumer to the source.","fde":"Rewrites a customer's notebook that loads a whole export into memory into a streaming script that runs on their modest VM without crashing."},"check":{"q":"An eval script does `cases = (load(x) for x in paths)`, logs `sum(1 for _ in cases)` cases, then runs\nthe model over `cases`. It reports 500 cases loaded but zero scored. Explain and fix it.","a":"`cases` is a generator expression, a single-pass iterator. Counting consumes it completely, so when\nthe scoring loop iterates it the generator is already exhausted and yields nothing, silently. The fix depends\non size: if the cases fit in memory, materialise once with `cases = [load(x) for x in paths]` and use\n`len(cases)`; if not, count while scoring in the same pass, or rebuild the generator from `paths` for each\npass. A good answer also notes that generators raise no error on reuse, so a test asserting the scored\ncount equals the loaded count would have caught this."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when you call a function whose body contains `yield`?","options":["The body runs until the first `yield` and returns that value","None of the body runs; a generator object is returned that runs the body on iteration","The whole body runs eagerly and the yielded values are collected into a list","It raises TypeError unless called inside a for loop"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The presence of `yield` makes the function a generator function. Calling it only creates the generator; execution starts on the first `next()` and pauses at each `yield`."},{"q":"Why does a generator pipeline over a large JSONL file keep memory roughly constant?","options":["Because Python compresses generator state automatically","Because generators store their outputs on disk instead of in memory","Because the file is memory-mapped by the generator","Because each stage holds only the current item or batch, pulled on demand by the consumer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Items flow one at a time from the source through each stage, so peak memory depends on batch size and per-item size, not on how many items the file contains."},{"q":"What is the safest way to stop consuming an async generator that wraps an HTTP stream early?","options":["Delete the variable and rely on reference counting to close it immediately","Iterate it inside `contextlib.aclosing()` so its cleanup is awaited when the block exits","Raise StopIteration inside the async for loop body","Call `next()` on it until it is exhausted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Async generator cleanup may need to await, which garbage collection cannot do synchronously. `aclosing` awaits `aclose()` deterministically, releasing the connection at a known point."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/decorators","sources":["PEP 318 — Decorators for Functions and Methods","PEP 3129 — Class Decorators","Official docs: functools (wraps, cache, lru_cache)"],"why":"An AI service has dozens of functions that call models, tools and stores, and almost all of them need the\nsame surrounding behaviour: retry on 429 and timeouts with backoff, record latency and token counts,\nopen a trace span, check permissions, cache identical calls. Written inline, every function starts with the\nsame fifteen lines of try/except/sleep/log, the copies drift apart, and one forgotten retry is an outage.\n\nThe naive alternatives are worse. Helper calls at the top and bottom of each function (`start = timer();\n...; log(start)`) still repeat in every function, and they miss early returns and exceptions unless each\nis wrapped in `try/finally`. Base-class inheritance forces unrelated functions into class hierarchies.\nHigher-order wrapping by hand, `fetch = with_retry(fetch)`, works, but it is separated from the definition,\neasy to forget and hard to see.\n\nPython functions are first-class objects, so a function can take another function and return a new one\nthat adds behaviour around the call. The `@` syntax (PEP 318) puts that wrapping on the definition\nitself, where readers see it. Frameworks lean on the same mechanism for registration: `@app.get(\"/chat\")`\nin FastAPI, `@mcp.tool()` in the MCP SDK and the tool decorators in agent frameworks record your function\nand read its signature and docstring to build a schema.","how":"### Desugaring\n```\n@timed\ndef embed(texts): ...\n```\nis exactly `embed = timed(embed)`, executed once, at definition time (usually import time). `timed`\ntypically defines an inner `wrapper(*args, **kwargs)` that does work before and after calling the\noriginal, and returns `wrapper`. The wrapper is a closure: it keeps a reference to the original function\nin its enclosing scope, which is how it can call it later.\n\n### Preserve identity with functools.wraps\nThe returned wrapper has its own `__name__`, `__doc__` and signature, so without care every decorated\nfunction looks like `wrapper(*args, **kwargs)` to logs, debuggers and, critically, to frameworks that\nbuild tool or API schemas from the signature and docstring. `@functools.wraps(fn)` copies the name,\ndocstring, module and annotations, and sets `__wrapped__` so `inspect.signature` reports the original\nsignature.\n\n### Decorators with arguments are factories\n`@retry(attempts=3)` calls `retry(attempts=3)` first, which returns the real decorator, which then\nreceives the function. That is three levels of nesting: factory, decorator, wrapper. Class decorators\n(PEP 3129) work the same way on classes; `@dataclass` is one.\n\n### Async functions need async wrappers\nCalling an `async def` function returns a coroutine without running it. A plain sync wrapper around it\nmeasures how long it took to *create* the coroutine (microseconds) and its `try/except` never sees the\nreal errors, which happen later at `await`. A decorator meant for both must check\n`inspect.iscoroutinefunction(fn)` and return an `async def` wrapper that awaits the call.\n\n### Typing: runtime metadata is not static types\n`wraps` fixes what `inspect` sees at runtime, but a type checker only sees the wrapper's declared\ntype. Annotate a decorator as `Callable[..., Any] -> Callable[..., Any]` and every decorated function\nloses its parameter types in mypy and the IDE. `ParamSpec` (PEP 612, 3.10+) carries them through:\n`def deco(fn: Callable[P, Awaitable[R]]) -> Callable[P, Awaitable[R]]`, with the wrapper taking\n`*args: P.args, **kwargs: P.kwargs`. A decorator that changes the signature (injecting a client\nargument, say) needs `Concatenate` to say so.\n\n### Class-based decorators and methods\nAny callable works as a decorator, including a class whose `__call__` wraps the function; that is\nconvenient when the decorator holds state such as counters or a cache. The catch is method binding.\nPlain functions are descriptors, so `obj.method` binds `self` automatically; an instance of your class\nis not, so decorating a method with it silently drops `self` unless the class implements `__get__`.\nFunction-based wrappers avoid this because the wrapper is itself a function.\n\n### Stacking order\n```\n@traced\n@retry(attempts=3)\ndef call(): ...\n```\nequals `call = traced(retry(attempts=3)(call))`. Decorators apply bottom-up, so at call time the\noutermost one, `traced`, runs first: here one span covers all three attempts. Swap them and each attempt\ngets its own span. Order is a design decision, not style. Registration decorators make this sharper:\n`@mcp.tool()` or `@app.get(...)` registers whatever object it receives, so it must sit on top. Put it\nbelow `@retry` and the framework registers the un-retried function, while the retrying wrapper is bound\nto a module name nobody calls.","example":"```python\nimport asyncio, functools, inspect, random, time\nfrom collections.abc import Awaitable, Callable\nfrom typing import ParamSpec, TypeVar\n\nP, R = ParamSpec(\"P\"), TypeVar(\"R\")\n\nclass Transient(Exception): ...\n\ndef retry(attempts: int = 3, base_delay: float = 0.5):\n    def decorator(fn: Callable[P, Awaitable[R]]) -> Callable[P, Awaitable[R]]:\n        if not inspect.iscoroutinefunction(fn):\n            raise TypeError(\"retry() here supports async functions only\")\n\n        @functools.wraps(fn)\n        async def wrapper(*args: P.args, **kwargs: P.kwargs) -> R:\n            for attempt in range(1, attempts + 1):\n                start = time.perf_counter()\n                try:\n                    return await fn(*args, **kwargs)\n                except Transient as exc:\n                    ms = (time.perf_counter() - start) * 1000\n                    print(f\"{fn.__name__} attempt {attempt} failed after {ms:.0f} ms: {exc}\")\n                    if attempt == attempts:\n                        raise\n                    delay = base_delay * 2 ** (attempt - 1)\n                    await asyncio.sleep(delay * random.uniform(0.5, 1.5))  # jitter\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\n@retry(attempts=4, base_delay=0.1)\nasync def complete(prompt: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Call the model provider.\"\"\"\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.05)\n    if random.random() < 0.5:\n        raise Transient(\"429 rate limited\")\n    return f\"answer to {prompt!r}\"\n\nprint(complete.__name__, complete.__doc__)     # complete Call the model provider.\nprint(asyncio.run(complete(\"hi\")))\n```\nWith 4 attempts and a 0.1 s base delay, the waits between failures are about 0.1, 0.2 and 0.4 s with\n±50% jitter, so a call that never succeeds gives up after roughly 0.7 s of backoff plus four call\ndurations (0.2 s here). With a 50% failure rate, all four attempts fail with probability 0.5⁴ = 1/16,\nso about 6% of calls still raise; retries change the error rate, they do not remove it. Trace what ran\nwhen: `retry(...)` executed once at import and returned `decorator`; `decorator(complete)` also ran once\nat import and rebound the name `complete` to `wrapper`; only `wrapper`'s body runs per call. The jitter\nmatters because many clients failing on the same 429 would otherwise retry in lockstep and hit the\nprovider together again. In production you would usually use `tenacity` rather than hand-roll this.","tradeoffs":["Decorators remove duplicated cross-cutting code and make it visible at the definition, but hide control flow; when behaviour depends on per-call context, an explicit wrapper or context manager is clearer.","Registration decorators make tool and route definitions concise, but run at import time, so import order and module side effects start to matter; that bites in large packages and test setups.","`functools.lru_cache` and `cache` give instant memoisation, but key on arguments and live for the process; they are wrong for unhashable inputs, per-user data or anything that must expire.","Generic decorators for both sync and async functions are convenient, at the cost of branching and harder typing; separate decorators are simpler when a codebase is all-async."],"pitfalls":["Omitting `functools.wraps`; the symptom is every tool in an agent's schema named `wrapper` with no description, or FastAPI dependencies resolving the wrong parameters.","Wrapping an `async def` with a sync wrapper; latency metrics show microseconds and retries never fire, because the error only appears when the coroutine is awaited elsewhere.","Applying `lru_cache` or `cache` to a method; the cache holds a reference to `self` in every key, so instances stay alive while cached (forever under `cache`), which shows up as steadily growing memory.","Stacking retry outside a timeout when you meant per-attempt timeouts, or the reverse; traces show either one timeout killing all retries or retries continuing far past the deadline.","Doing expensive work in the decorator body rather than in the wrapper; it runs once at import, which slows startup or freezes a value that should be read per call.","Placing a framework's registration decorator below your own wrappers; the framework registers the bare function, and the symptom is tools or routes that work but never retry, trace or check auth.","Retrying non-idempotent calls (a tool that sends an email or writes a CRM row) with a generic retry decorator; a timeout after the side effect happened shows up as duplicate emails or rows."],"when":"Use a decorator when the same behaviour wraps many functions and does not depend on their internals:\nretries, timeouts, tracing spans, metrics, auth checks, caching, and registration with a framework. Prefer\nestablished libraries (tenacity for retries, OpenTelemetry or your tracing SDK's decorator, `functools.cache`)\nover hand-rolled versions. Avoid decorators for logic that varies per call or that the reader must see to\nunderstand the function. When reviewing code, check that every wrapper uses `wraps`, handles async\ncorrectly and is stacked in the intended order. In interviews, expect to write a parameterised decorator\nand explain the desugaring.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async-aware decorators with `functools.wraps` for retries and tracing, and uses framework decorators to register tools and routes.","architect":"Standardises the cross-cutting layer, one retry policy and one tracing decorator, so every model and tool call is observable and resilient by default.","fde":"Adds tracing or retry decorators to a customer's existing functions without rewriting them, quickly instrumenting a pilot to find where latency goes."},"check":{"q":"A team adds `@timed` (sync wrapper, no `wraps`) to their async tool functions and registers them with an\nagent framework. Latency dashboards show 0.01 ms per tool and the model starts calling tools with wrong\narguments. Explain both symptoms.","a":"The sync wrapper calls the async function, which only creates a coroutine and returns immediately, so\nthe timer measures coroutine creation, not the awaited work; the real latency happens later, if at all, when\nthe framework awaits the returned coroutine. Frameworks that decide how to call a tool with\n`inspect.iscoroutinefunction` now see a sync function, and may never await the result. Without `functools.wraps`, the wrapper exposes its own name,\nmissing docstring and `(*args, **kwargs)` signature, so the framework generates tool schemas with no\nparameter names or descriptions, and the model guesses the arguments. The fix is an `async def` wrapper\nthat awaits the call, decorated with `functools.wraps(fn)` so name, docstring and signature survive."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is `@a` stacked above `@b` above `def f` equivalent to?","options":["`f = b(a(f))`, so `b` runs first on each call","`f = a(b(f))`, so `a`'s wrapper runs first on each call","`f = a(f); f = b(f)` with `b` outermost","It depends on which decorator was imported first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Decorators apply bottom-up at definition time, so the top decorator wraps everything below it and its wrapper is entered first at call time. That determines, for example, whether a span covers all retries."},{"q":"Why does an agent framework that reads function signatures need decorated tools to use `functools.wraps`?","options":["Because `wraps` makes the function run faster","Because frameworks reject any function that has been decorated","Because otherwise the framework sees the wrapper's generic name, docstring and `*args, **kwargs` signature","Because `wraps` converts the function into a coroutine automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tool schemas are built from name, docstring and parameters. `wraps` copies metadata and sets `__wrapped__`, so `inspect.signature` reports the original parameters instead of the wrapper's."},{"q":"A sync timing decorator wraps an `async def` model call. What does it actually measure?","options":["The full network round-trip of the model call","Nothing, because Python refuses to decorate coroutines","The time until the first token arrives","Only the time to create the coroutine object, since the call runs later when it is awaited"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Calling an async function returns a coroutine without executing its body. Timing, retries and exception handling must happen in an async wrapper that awaits the call."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","sources":["PEP 343 — The \"with\" Statement","PEP 492 — Coroutines with async and await syntax (async with)","Official docs: contextlib"],"why":"Resources are acquired on the happy path and leaked on the unhappy one. A streaming LLM call opens a\nconnection; the client disconnects, a parser raises on malformed JSON, or a timeout cancels the task,\nand the line that closes the stream never runs. Each leak is small, but an AI backend does this\nthousands of times an hour: the connection pool fills, new requests wait for a free connection, and\nlatency climbs until the service looks down. GPU code has the same shape: forget to restore a mode or\nrelease a buffer and memory creeps up.\n\nThe naive fixes fall short. Calling `close()` at the end of the function misses every early return and\nexception. `try/finally` is correct, but it must be repeated at every call site, nested for each\nadditional resource, and written correctly every time. Relying on `__del__` or garbage collection gives\nno timing guarantee (reference cycles delay it, other interpreters do not refcount, and async cleanup\ncannot run from a finaliser at all).\n\nPEP 343 packaged `try/finally` into the resource itself. The object that knows how to acquire also knows\nhow to release, and the `with` statement guarantees the release runs. Callers write one line and cannot\nget the cleanup wrong.","how":"### The protocol\n`with expr as x:` evaluates `expr` to a manager, calls `manager.__enter__()` and binds its return value to\n`x`, runs the block, then always calls `manager.__exit__(exc_type, exc, tb)`. On a normal exit all three\nare `None`. On an exception they describe it, and if `__exit__` returns a truthy value the exception is\n**suppressed**, *because* some managers (`contextlib.suppress`) exist precisely to do that; everyone else\nmust return `None` or `False`. Multiple managers in one statement exit in reverse order of entry, so\ndependencies unwind correctly.\n\nWritten out, `with mgr as x: body` is approximately what PEP 343 specifies:\n\n```\nenter, exit = type(mgr).__enter__, type(mgr).__exit__   # looked up on the type, once\nx = enter(mgr)                    # if this raises, exit is never called\nok = True\ntry:\n    body\nexcept BaseException as e:\n    ok = False\n    if not exit(mgr, type(e), e, e.__traceback__):\n        raise                     # falsy return: propagate the original\nfinally:\n    if ok:\n        exit(mgr, None, None, None)   # normal exit, including return/break\n```\n\nThree consequences follow from that expansion. First, `__exit__` runs only if `__enter__` succeeded,\n*because* the protocol cannot know how far a failed setup got; a manager that acquires two things in\n`__enter__` must clean up the first itself if the second fails. Second, `except BaseException` means\n`KeyboardInterrupt` and `CancelledError` trigger cleanup too. Third, if `__exit__` itself raises, that\nnew exception replaces the original (which survives only as `__context__` in the traceback), so a\ncleanup that can fail should log and swallow its own error rather than mask the real one.\n\n### async with\n`async with` calls `await __aenter__()` and `await __aexit__(...)`, *because* closing a network\nresource is itself I/O: flushing a stream, returning a connection, sending a close frame. `httpx.AsyncClient`,\nasync DB sessions and `asyncio.Lock` are all async context managers. Cancellation arrives as\n`CancelledError` and passes through `__aexit__` like any exception, which is why `async with` is the\nreliable way to release resources in cancellable code. Keep exit code short and bounded, because it can\nitself be interrupted during shutdown.\n\n### contextlib: managers without classes\n`@contextmanager` turns a generator into a manager: code before `yield` is setup, the yielded value is\nbound by `as`, code after is teardown. The exception from the block is thrown in at the `yield`, so\nteardown must sit in `finally` or it is skipped on error. `@asynccontextmanager` is the async version, and\nit is what FastAPI's `lifespan` expects. `ExitStack`/`AsyncExitStack` manage a number of resources known\nonly at runtime and unwind whatever was entered if a later setup step fails, which is exactly the\npartial-setup problem from the expansion above. Smaller helpers: `closing()` for objects with only\n`close()`, `suppress()`, `nullcontext()` for optional managers, and `aclosing()` for async generators.\nA `@contextmanager` object is single-use (it wraps one generator, so entering it twice raises), its\ngenerator must yield exactly once, and it doubles as a decorator: `@timed_span()` above a function\nwraps every call in the block.\n\n### When there is no block to hang it on\nSome lifetimes do not fit a lexical block: an object handed to a caller, a cache entry, a worker that\nlives until shutdown. Then the owner of that lifetime (the app's lifespan, the pool, the cache's\neviction hook) must call close explicitly. `weakref.finalize(obj, cleanup)` is a better last-resort\nsafety net than `__del__`, since it runs at most once and also at interpreter exit, but it still runs\non garbage-collection timing, so treat it as leak insurance, not as the cleanup plan.\n\n### Context managers that are not about I/O\n`torch.no_grad()` and `torch.inference_mode()` switch autograd off for a block and restore it after.\n`decimal.localcontext()` and temporary environment patches in tests follow the same pattern: change state,\nguarantee restoration. The principle generalises to anything with a \"before\" and a mandatory \"after\".","example":"```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom contextlib import AsyncExitStack, asynccontextmanager\n\nclass Client:                              # stands in for httpx.AsyncClient, a DB pool\n    def __init__(self, name: str): self.name = name\n    async def aclose(self): print(f\"{self.name}: closed\")\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def open_client(name: str):\n    client = Client(name)\n    print(f\"{name}: opened\")\n    try:\n        yield client\n    finally:\n        await client.aclose()              # runs on success, error or cancellation\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app=None):              # FastAPI calls lifespan(app) with this shape\n    async with AsyncExitStack() as stack:\n        llm = await stack.enter_async_context(open_client(\"llm-http\"))\n        db = await stack.enter_async_context(open_client(\"vector-db\"))\n        yield {\"llm\": llm, \"db\": db}\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    try:\n        async with lifespan() as deps:\n            print(\"serving with\", sorted(deps))\n            raise RuntimeError(\"crash mid-request\")\n    except RuntimeError as exc:\n        print(\"propagated:\", exc)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\nOutput: `llm-http: opened`, `vector-db: opened`, `serving with ['db', 'llm']`, then `vector-db: closed`\nand `llm-http: closed` in reverse order, then `propagated: crash mid-request`. The error is not\nsuppressed, both clients are closed, and the dependency opened last is closed first. The clients are\ncreated once per process, not once per request, which is the point of putting them in a lifespan.","tradeoffs":["`with` guarantees cleanup at a lexically visible point, but ties resource lifetime to a block; when a resource must outlive the function (a pool shared by all requests), move it to an app-level lifespan.","`@contextmanager` is far shorter than a class, but forgets cleanup on error if you omit `try/finally`; a class with `__exit__` is clearer when the manager carries state or is reused often.","`ExitStack` handles dynamic resource sets, at the cost of less obvious control flow; for two or three fixed resources a single multi-item `with` statement is easier to read.","Suppressing exceptions in `__exit__` can turn a crash into graceful degradation, but hides real bugs unless the manager suppresses only specific, expected exception types."],"pitfalls":["Creating an `httpx.AsyncClient` inside each request with `async with`; correct cleanup but no connection reuse, which shows up as high latency from repeated TLS handshakes.","Returning a lazy stream or generator from inside a `with` block; the resource closes on return, and the consumer later fails with \"stream closed\" or reads nothing.","Writing `@contextmanager` teardown after `yield` without `finally`; it runs in tests on the happy path and silently does not run when the block raises.","An `__exit__` that returns `True` by accident (e.g. returning the result of a cleanup call); exceptions vanish and callers see `None` results with no traceback.","Relying on `__del__` to close connections; the symptom is \"unclosed client session\" warnings and pool exhaustion that varies with garbage-collection timing.","Acquiring a second resource in `__enter__` after the first without guarding it; when the second fails, `__exit__` never runs, and the first leaks only on that rare setup-failure path, which shows up as slowly climbing open connections after upstream outages.","A cleanup step in `__exit__` or `finally` that can raise (closing an already-broken stream); the traceback reports the cleanup error and the real cause is buried under \"During handling of the above exception\"."],"when":"Use a context manager for anything with an acquire-and-release pair: clients and connection pools, streams,\nDB sessions and transactions, locks and semaphores, temp files and directories, tracing spans, and\ntemporary mode changes such as `torch.inference_mode()`. Prefer the library's own manager over manual\n`close()` calls. Give long-lived resources a lifespan-scoped manager and short-lived ones a block-scoped\nmanager. Measure by watching open connections, pool wait time and file descriptors under load and after\nerror injection; if they climb after errors, a path is skipping cleanup. In interviews, expect to explain\n`__exit__`'s arguments and return value and write a `@contextmanager` with a correct `finally`.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wraps every client, stream, session and lock in `with`/`async with`, and writes `@asynccontextmanager` helpers with cleanup in `finally`.","architect":"Defines resource lifetimes, process-scoped pools in a lifespan versus per-request sessions, so connection counts and shutdown behaviour are predictable.","fde":"Traces a customer's \"connection pool exhausted\" incident to a stream opened without a context manager on an error path, and fixes it under their load."},"check":{"q":"A helper does `with httpx.stream(\"POST\", url, json=body) as r: return r.iter_lines()`, and callers loop over\nthe result. It fails in production with errors about a closed stream. Why, and how should it be written?","a":"`return` leaves the `with` block, so `__exit__` runs immediately and closes the response before\nanyone iterates; `iter_lines()` is lazy, so the caller iterates a closed stream. The context manager must\nstay open for as long as the data is consumed. Either make the helper a generator that `yield`s lines\ninside the `with` block, so the stream closes when iteration ends, with consumers using `closing()` or\n`aclosing()` for early exit, or have the helper itself be a context manager that yields the response so\nthe caller controls its lifetime with `with`."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens if an `__exit__` method returns `True` while the block raised an exception?","options":["The exception is re-raised after cleanup completes","The exception is suppressed and execution continues after the `with` statement","Python raises a TypeError because `__exit__` must return None","The block is retried once before the exception propagates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A truthy return from `__exit__` tells the interpreter the exception was handled. That is how `contextlib.suppress` works, and why accidental truthy returns hide bugs."},{"q":"Why does an async HTTP client use `async with` rather than plain `with`?","options":["Because plain `with` cannot be used inside an `async def` function","Because `async with` runs the block in a separate thread","Because opening and closing it involve I/O that must be awaited without blocking the event loop","Because `async with` suppresses CancelledError during cleanup"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Releasing connections and closing streams involve network I/O, so `__aenter__`/`__aexit__` are coroutines. Ordinary `with` remains usable inside async code for purely synchronous resources."},{"q":"A `@contextmanager` function has setup, `yield conn`, then `conn.close()` with no try/finally. When does the connection leak?","options":["Never, because the decorator always runs code after the yield","Only when the generator is garbage-collected before the block starts","Only when the block returns early with `return`","When the block raises, because the exception is thrown in at the yield and skips the close"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The exception from the block is raised inside the generator at the `yield`, so code after it runs only if it is in `finally`. An early `return` exits normally and does run it."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","sources":["Fowler, 2004 — Inversion of Control Containers and the Dependency Injection pattern","Cockburn, 2005 — Hexagonal Architecture (Ports and Adapters)","Official docs: typing.Protocol"],"why":"AI applications are mostly glue around external services that are slow, expensive, non-deterministic\nand owned by someone else. The first version of such code looks the same in every codebase: a\nmodule-level `client = OpenAI()` and a `client.chat.completions.create(...)` call inside whichever\nfunction needs an answer. It works, and then three things go wrong at once.\n\nTesting: every unit test of that function now needs an API key and a network, costs money, takes\nseconds, and returns a different string each run, so the test either hits production or gets skipped.\nSwapping: when the team wants to evaluate Anthropic, a self-hosted vLLM endpoint or a cheaper model\nfor one feature, the vendor's request and response shapes are scattered through forty files.\nConfiguration: the module reads `OPENAI_API_KEY` at import time, so importing it from a script that\nonly needed a helper function either crashes or silently talks to the wrong environment.\n\nThe naive fixes each fail. `unittest.mock.patch(\"app.summarize.client\")` works until a refactor\nmoves the module, because the test is coupled to an internal path rather than a contract, and each\npatch re-implements a slice of the vendor response object. An environment flag such as\n`if os.getenv(\"FAKE_LLM\")` puts test branches in production code. A global registry\n(`get_llm_client()`) hides the dependency again, just one hop away: nothing in the function signature\nsays it needs a model.\n\nDependency injection flips the direction: the code that needs a completer is given one. The interface\nboundary says what is needed, in terms your code owns, so the domain never imports the vendor. One\ncomposition root does the wiring. The cost is explicitness; the payoff is that the dependency graph is\nvisible, testable and changeable in one place.","how":"### Move 1: name the dependency as an interface you own\nDeclare a `Protocol` with exactly the methods your use case calls:\n`Completer.complete(system, user, *, max_tokens) -> Completion`. Use `typing.Protocol` rather than an\nabstract base class *because* protocols are structural: the vendor adapter and the test fake satisfy\nit by having the right method, without inheriting from anything in your package, and mypy checks the\nmatch statically. The protocol lives next to the domain code, so the dependency arrow points from the\nadapter toward your code, never the other way round.\n\n### Move 2: translate at the edge\nThe adapter turns the vendor's response into your own `Completion` dataclass (text, token counts,\nfinish reason). This matters more than it looks: if `openai.types.chat.ChatCompletion` objects flow\nthrough your services, the vendor is still a dependency of everything that touches them, and a\nprovider switch becomes a rewrite. The boundary is only as strong as the types that cross it. The\nadapter is the one file per vendor that imports the SDK, handles its exception types, and maps its\nstatus codes into your error hierarchy: `openai.RateLimitError` becomes your `ProviderRateLimited`, so\nthe retry policy upstream is written once against your exception and works for every vendor. This is\nthe dependency inversion principle in practice: the high-level policy and the low-level SDK both\ndepend on an abstraction that the high-level side owns.\n\n### Move 3: inject at construction, wire at the root\nConstructor injection, `Summarizer(completer)`, is the default in Python *because* it makes the\ndependency part of the signature: a reader, a type checker and a test author all see it. The\ncomposition root (`main()`, an app factory, a FastAPI lifespan) is the single place that reads\nsettings, instantiates real clients and connects them. Tests build the same graph with fakes. Because\neverything else receives its collaborators, nothing else reads environment variables or creates\nnetwork clients, which is what removes import-time side effects.\n\n### Not every dependency needs a class\nWhen the dependency is a single operation, inject a callable instead of a Protocol:\n`def __init__(self, now: Callable[[], datetime] = datetime.now)` or\n`embed: Callable[[list[str]], list[list[float]]]`. This is still dependency injection, *because* the\ncaller decides what runs; it is just the smallest possible interface. Promote it to a Protocol when a\nsecond method appears or when the call needs named, typed options. One trap: a default argument is\nevaluated once, when the `def` runs at import time, so `completer: Completer = OpenAICompleter(OpenAI())`\nquietly reintroduces an import-time client. Defaults are safe only for cheap, side-effect-free values\nsuch as `datetime.now` itself.\n\n### Sync or async is part of the contract\n`def complete(...)` and `async def complete(...)` are different interfaces: a caller of the first gets\na `Completion`, a caller of the second gets a coroutine. Decide at the port, *because* an async service\ncalling a sync adapter blocks the event loop on every network call, and a sync service handed an async\nadapter receives an un-awaited coroutine that mypy will flag but the runtime will not. If both are\nneeded, define two ports (`Completer`, `AsyncCompleter`) rather than one that tries to be both.\n\n### Why Python rarely needs a container\nJava-style containers reflect on constructors to auto-wire graphs. Python has first-class functions,\nclosures, `functools.partial` and dataclasses, which cover almost every wiring need in a dozen lines.\nFastAPI's `Depends` is a request-scoped injector that handles the per-request lifetime for you.\nReach for a container library (`dependency-injector`, `punq`, `lagom`) only when the graph has dozens\nof services with mixed lifetimes (singleton HTTP clients and loaded models versus per-request\nsessions) and the hand-written root has become the hardest file to read.\n\n### Where to draw boundaries\nAt I/O and non-determinism: LLM and embedding providers, vector stores, databases, HTTP, filesystem,\nthe clock, randomness. Not around pure logic: a `PromptBuilder` that renders a dataclass into a string\nneeds no interface, just a direct test. The working rule: if you would want to replace it in a test or\nat deployment time, it is a port; otherwise it is just code. Keep ports narrow, the methods a use case\nactually calls, and widen them deliberately (add `stream()` when a use case streams). A port that\nmirrors the vendor SDK's fifteen methods is the SDK with extra steps, and nobody can write a fake\nfor it.\n\n### Lifetimes\nShared objects (an `httpx.AsyncClient` with its connection pool, a loaded sentence-transformer) are\ncreated once at the root and passed in; per-request objects (a bound logger with `request_id`, a DB\nsession) are created by the request handler. Constructor injection makes sharing cheap because the\nsame instance is simply passed to several consumers.\n\n### Test doubles at the boundary\nA hand-written fake that returns scripted answers and records calls tests business logic quickly and\ndeterministically. The real adapter is covered by a small contract test against the real API or a\nrecorded response, so the fake and reality cannot drift unnoticed. Keep fakes dumb: a fake that\nre-implements vendor logic will pass tests the real thing fails.","example":"A domain service, a vendor adapter, a fake and a composition root. Note that `Summarizer` and the\n`Completer` protocol import nothing from any SDK, the vendor import is deferred into the root, and the\ntest needs no network.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Completion:\n    text: str\n    input_tokens: int\n    output_tokens: int\n\nclass Completer(Protocol):                       # the port: your vocabulary, your types\n    def complete(self, system: str, user: str, *, max_tokens: int) -> Completion: ...\n\nclass OpenAICompleter:                           # the adapter: the only file that imports the SDK\n    def __init__(self, client, model: str) -> None:\n        self._client, self._model = client, model\n    def complete(self, system, user, *, max_tokens):\n        r = self._client.chat.completions.create(\n            model=self._model, max_tokens=max_tokens,\n            messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": system}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user}])\n        return Completion(r.choices[0].message.content or \"\",\n                          r.usage.prompt_tokens, r.usage.completion_tokens)\n\nclass FakeCompleter:                             # test double: scripted answers, records calls\n    def __init__(self, answers: list[str]) -> None:\n        self.answers, self.calls = list(answers), []\n    def complete(self, system, user, *, max_tokens):\n        self.calls.append((system, user, max_tokens))\n        return Completion(self.answers.pop(0), 10, 5)\n\nclass Summarizer:                                # domain service: knows nothing about vendors\n    def __init__(self, completer: Completer, max_tokens: int = 200) -> None:\n        self._c, self._max = completer, max_tokens\n    def summarize(self, doc: str) -> str:\n        return self._c.complete(\"Summarize in two sentences.\", doc, max_tokens=self._max).text\n\ndef build_summarizer() -> Summarizer:            # composition root: the one place real things are chosen\n    from openai import OpenAI\n    return Summarizer(OpenAICompleter(OpenAI(), model=\"gpt-4o-mini\"))\n\ndef test_summarizer_passes_document_through():\n    fake = FakeCompleter([\"Short summary.\"])\n    assert Summarizer(fake).summarize(\"long text\") == \"Short summary.\"\n    assert fake.calls[0][1] == \"long text\" and fake.calls[0][2] == 200\n```","tradeoffs":["You gain testability and swappability but pay an extra file and an extra hop per boundary; this flips for a 200-line script with one call site, where calling the SDK directly is the right choice.","Owning the types that cross the boundary frees you from the vendor, but a lowest-common-denominator port hides provider-specific features (tool-call formats, prompt caching controls, logprobs); when you deliberately depend on those, widen the port on purpose rather than pretending neutrality.","Fakes make unit tests fast and deterministic, but a fake can drift from the real adapter, so you must keep a thin integration or contract test that exercises the real adapter against the real API or a recorded response.","Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit, but a class taking eight collaborators is a sign the class does too much, not that injection is wrong; split it or group related settings.","A container automates wiring across a large graph with mixed lifetimes, but it hides the graph behind decorators and configuration; below a few dozen services the hand-written root is clearer."],"pitfalls":["Creating clients at module import time (`client = OpenAI()` at the top of a file); the symptom is tests failing on import with a missing API key, or a CLI `--help` that takes seconds and needs production credentials.","Letting vendor response objects cross into domain code; the symptom is `response.choices[0].message.content` appearing in business logic and a provider switch touching dozens of files.","Patch-heavy tests (`@patch(\"app.services.summarize.openai.OpenAI\")` stacked three deep); the symptom is thirty tests breaking when a module is renamed, and each test re-implementing a slice of the vendor's response shape.","A port with a single implementation that mirrors the SDK surface; the symptom is that nobody can write a fake for it, so tests mock the port's internals instead.","Fakes that are too clever (parsing the prompt and generating plausible answers); the symptom is green tests for behaviour the real provider does not exhibit.","An async service whose port is sync (or an adapter that wraps an async SDK in `asyncio.run`); the symptom is request latency that grows with concurrency because each LLM call blocks the event loop, or `RuntimeError: asyncio.run() cannot be called from a running event loop`.","Threading individual config values (`api_key`, `model`, `temperature`) through five layers; the symptom is signatures that change whenever a setting is added. Inject the configured adapter instead."],"when":"Draw a boundary wherever your code meets something slow, paid, non-deterministic or owned by a third\nparty: model providers, embedding services, vector stores, databases, HTTP, time and randomness. Do not\ndraw one around pure logic. Measure the health of the design by two questions: can the core use case\nbe exercised in a test with no network and no credentials, and how many files would a provider switch\ntouch? In day-to-day work this shows up as the adapter you write when adding a provider, the fake you\nhand to a test, and the one composition root you edit when configuration changes. In interviews the\nexpected depth is explaining why Protocols beat ABCs here, why the boundary's types must be yours, and\nwhy a composition root exists at all.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the `Completer` and `VectorStore` protocols, one adapter per provider, dumb fakes for tests, and keeps the SDK import inside the adapter and the composition root.","architect":"Owns where boundaries sit and what crosses them: which provider features are exposed through the port, how adapters report errors, and which objects are shared versus per-request.","fde":"Swaps a customer's mandated provider, on-prem endpoint or internal vector database in by writing one adapter against the existing port, without touching the delivered business logic."},"check":{"q":"A service class imports both `openai` and `anthropic` and switches between them on a config\nstring inside its methods. The team says this is already \"provider-agnostic\". What is wrong, and how\nwould you restructure it?","a":"The service still depends on both SDKs: any test imports both, any new provider edits the\nservice, and vendor response objects are handled inline in domain code. Provider choice belongs in the\ncomposition root, not in the service. Define a `Completer` protocol in your own types, write one\nadapter per vendor that imports its SDK and translates responses into your `Completion` dataclass,\nand have the service take a `Completer` in its constructor. The root reads the config string once and\nconstructs the matching adapter. Tests pass a fake; adding a vendor adds one file; the service never\nchanges. A strong answer also mentions keeping the port narrow and a contract test per adapter so the\nfakes cannot drift from reality."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is `typing.Protocol` usually preferred over an abstract base class for a port such as `Completer`?","options":["Protocols are checked at runtime, so a bad adapter fails at construction time","Protocols are structural, so vendor adapters and test fakes satisfy them without inheriting from your package","Abstract base classes cannot have more than one concrete implementation","Protocols are faster because they skip method resolution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A class satisfies a Protocol by having the right methods, checked statically by mypy, so adapters stay decoupled from your code. Protocols are not enforced at runtime (`@runtime_checkable` only checks that method names exist, not signatures), and ABCs support many implementations; the difference is nominal versus structural typing."},{"q":"Which of these should NOT get its own interface boundary in a typical RAG service?","options":["The vector store client used for retrieval","The system clock used by a rate limiter","A pure function that renders a dataclass into a prompt string","The embedding model that turns chunks into vectors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Boundaries belong at I/O and non-determinism, where you want to substitute a fake in tests or a different implementation at deployment. Pure, deterministic logic is tested directly; wrapping it in a Protocol adds indirection with nothing to swap."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of a composition root?","options":["To cache expensive objects so they are constructed only once per process","To validate configuration before the application starts","To be the single place that chooses concrete implementations and wires them into the object graph","To provide a global registry that any module can query for a dependency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The root is where real adapters are instantiated and handed to the services that need them, so nothing else reads settings or builds clients. A queryable global registry is the service-locator anti-pattern, which hides dependencies instead of making them explicit."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/package-structure-and-import-design","sources":["Official docs: Python import system (Python Language Reference)","PEP 420 — Implicit Namespace Packages","PEP 562 — Module __getattr__ and __dir__","Official docs: Python Packaging User Guide (src layout vs flat layout)"],"why":"AI codebases usually start as one notebook, then a `utils.py`, then forty modules where everything imports\neverything. Three failures follow. First, **circular imports**: `agent.py` imports `tools.py`, which imports\n`agent.py` for a type, and one day Python raises `ImportError: cannot import name 'Agent' from partially\ninitialized module`. Second, **slow, fragile startup**: importing your package pulls in `torch`,\n`transformers` and three vendor SDKs, so a CLI that only lists prompts takes seconds to start, and a\nmodule-level `client = OpenAI()` fails at import time when `OPENAI_API_KEY` is missing, which also breaks\ntest collection. Third, **tests that pass for the wrong reason**: with a flat layout, `pytest` imports the\npackage from the working directory rather than from what `pip install` would ship, so a missing file in the\nwheel or a wrong package-data declaration is invisible until production.\n\nThe naive fixes make each symptom go away locally and leave the cause. `sys.path.insert(0, \"..\")` at the\ntop of a test or a `PYTHONPATH=.` in someone's shell makes imports resolve on one machine and differently\nin CI, Docker and a teammate's laptop, because the import result now depends on where the process was\nstarted. Wrapping a failing import in `try/except ImportError` hides a cycle until a different import\norder triggers it. \"Just be careful\" does not scale because nothing enforces it. Import structure has to\nbe designed, given a direction, and checked by a tool, the same way you would design service boundaries.","how":"### What an import actually does\n`import pkg.mod` looks in `sys.modules`; if absent, it walks `sys.path` for `pkg`, creates the module object,\n**inserts it into `sys.modules` before executing its body**, then runs the module top to bottom. Two\nconsequences drive every rule below. A module's body runs once per process, at first import, so anything at\ntop level (network clients, file reads, model loading, `logging.basicConfig`) is an import-time side effect.\nAnd because the half-built module is already registered, a cycle does not loop forever: the second module\ngets the partially initialized first one, and `from a import X` fails if `X` has not been defined yet.\n\nThe two import forms bind different things. `import a` binds the module object, so a cycle using it\nsurvives as long as `a.X` is only looked up later, at call time. `from a import X` copies the current value\nof `a.X` into the importing module's namespace *now*, which is why it fails mid-cycle, and also why\n`mock.patch(\"a.X\")` does not affect a module `b` that already did `from a import X`: `b` holds its own\nreference, so you must patch `b.X`, the name where it is *used*.\n\n### Where `sys.path[0]` comes from\n`python path/to/script.py` puts the script's own directory first on `sys.path`; `python -m pkg.mod` puts\nthe current directory there and imports `pkg.mod` as part of its package. So running\n`python src/myapp/entrypoints/cli.py` makes `entrypoints/` the top of the search path: sibling files become\ntop-level modules, relative imports fail with \"attempted relative import with no known parent package\", and\na sibling named like a library shadows it. Run code as `python -m myapp.entrypoints.cli`, or declare a\nconsole script under `[project.scripts]` in `pyproject.toml`, *because* both resolve imports through the\ninstalled package rather than through wherever the file happens to sit.\n\n### src layout\nPut code in `src/myapp/` with `tests/` beside `src/`, and install it in editable mode (`uv sync`, or\n`pip install -e .`). Because the project root no longer contains an importable `myapp` directory, tests can\nonly import the package through the installation, so an undeclared dependency or a module that is not\nactually part of the package shows up instead of being silently picked up from the working directory. One\ncaveat: an editable install still points back at `src/`, so files that exist on disk but are missing from the\nwheel (prompt templates, package data) are only caught when CI builds the wheel and runs the tests against a\nnon-editable install of it. A flat layout (`myapp/` at the root) is fine for scripts and small tools, but it\nlets the current directory shadow the installed package.\n\n### Layering and direction\nPick layers and allow imports only downward:\n- `domain/` — pure types and logic (Pydantic models, prompt templates, scoring). Imports nothing from the app.\n- `services/` — orchestration (the RAG pipeline, the agent loop). Depends on domain and on *interfaces*\n  (Protocols) for LLMs, vector stores and tools.\n- `adapters/` — concrete clients (OpenAI, Anthropic, pgvector, Redis). Implement those Protocols.\n- `entrypoints/` — FastAPI app, CLI, workers. The composition root that wires adapters into services.\n\nThe direction is chosen *because* the parts that change most (vendor SDKs) should be the parts nothing else\ndepends on. A tool such as `import-linter` can turn this into a CI check with \"layers\" and \"forbidden\"\ncontracts, so a stray `from myapp.adapters.openai import ...` inside `domain/` fails the build.\n\n### Breaking cycles properly\nA cycle is a design signal: two modules share a concept that belongs in a third. Move the shared types down\ninto `domain/`. If the only reason for the import is a type annotation, guard it with\n`if TYPE_CHECKING:` and use string or postponed annotations, because type checkers read it but the runtime\nnever executes it. Function-local imports also break cycles, but use them deliberately, not as a habit.\n\n### Public surface and lazy imports\n`__init__.py` defines what callers see. Re-exporting a small API there (`from .client import Client`, plus\n`__all__`) lets you reorganize internals without breaking users, but re-exporting everything makes\n`import myapp` load everything. For heavy optional dependencies, import inside the function that needs them,\nor use a module-level `__getattr__` (PEP 562) to defer loading until first attribute access.\n`python -X importtime -c \"import myapp\"` prints per-module import cost, so you can see what to defer.\n\n### Regular vs namespace packages\nA directory with `__init__.py` is a regular package. Since PEP 420, a directory *without* one is still\nimportable as a namespace package, which exists so that several separately installed distributions can\ncontribute subpackages to one name (`acme.llm` and `acme.rag` shipped independently). Use that on purpose\nonly. An accidental namespace package, a forgotten `__init__.py`, usually still imports in development,\nbut setuptools' `find_packages()` skips directories without `__init__.py`, so the subpackage can silently\nbe left out of the wheel, and some tools resolve module names differently for it. In a single application\npackage, give every package directory an `__init__.py`.\n\n### Absolute vs relative imports\nAbsolute imports (`from myapp.domain import Query`) are explicit and survive copying a module elsewhere;\nexplicit relative imports (`from .models import Query`) are shorter and survive renaming the top package.\nEither is fine; pick one style and let `ruff` enforce it. Implicit relative imports do not exist in Python 3.","example":"A layered layout plus two techniques: a type-only import that removes a runtime cycle between the agent and\nits tool registry, and an embedding adapter that defers the expensive `torch` import until the first call, so\nimporting the package (or collecting tests) stays fast and side-effect free.\n\n```python\n# src/myapp/\n#   domain/models.py        <- pure Pydantic models, no app imports\n#   services/agent.py       <- depends on domain + Protocols\n#   adapters/embeddings.py  <- imports torch / sentence-transformers lazily\n#   entrypoints/cli.py      <- composition root\n\n# services/agent.py\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Protocol\nfrom myapp.domain.models import Message\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:                      # seen by mypy/pyright, never executed at runtime\n    from myapp.services.tools import ToolRegistry\n\nclass LLM(Protocol):\n    def complete(self, messages: list[Message]) -> Message: ...\n\nclass Agent:\n    def __init__(self, llm: LLM, tools: ToolRegistry) -> None:\n        self.llm, self.tools = llm, tools\n\n# adapters/embeddings.py\nclass LocalEmbedder:\n    def __init__(self, model_name: str) -> None:\n        self.model_name, self._model = model_name, None   # nothing heavy at import\n\n    def embed(self, texts: list[str]) -> list[list[float]]:\n        if self._model is None:\n            from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer  # pays torch import on first use\n            self._model = SentenceTransformer(self.model_name)\n        return self._model.encode(texts).tolist()\n\n# Measure: python -X importtime -c \"import myapp.entrypoints.cli\" 2> imports.log\n```","tradeoffs":["Strict layering makes adapters swappable and services testable with fakes, but adds indirection that feels like ceremony in a 500-line prototype; adopt it once a second adapter or a test suite appears.","Lazy imports cut startup time and make optional extras optional, but move `ImportError` from startup to first use; for a long-running server, eager import at boot can be safer because it fails fast.","A rich `__init__.py` gives users a tidy API, but couples import cost to the whole package; it flips toward minimal `__init__` files once the package contains heavy or optional dependencies.","Splitting into many small packages (a monorepo with workspaces) isolates dependencies cleanly, but costs versioning and release overhead that a single package with clear layers usually does not need."],"pitfalls":["Creating clients or loading models at module top level; the symptom is tests failing at collection with a missing API key, or a CLI `--help` that takes several seconds.","Fixing a circular import by moving the import inside a function without fixing the shared concept; the symptom is the same cycle reappearing in a new form a few weeks later.","Naming a module after a dependency (`openai.py`, `logging.py`, `datasets.py`) inside a directory on `sys.path`; the symptom is `AttributeError: module 'openai' has no attribute 'OpenAI'` because your file shadowed the library.","Patching where a name is defined rather than where it is looked up (`patch(\"myapp.adapters.llm.OpenAI\")` when `services/rag.py` did `from myapp.adapters.llm import OpenAI`); the symptom is a test that still makes real network calls even though the patch \"is applied\".","Running modules as files (`python src/myapp/cli.py`) instead of with `-m` or a console script; the symptom is \"attempted relative import with no known parent package\", or imports that work from one directory and fail from another.","Using a flat layout and never building the wheel in CI; the symptom is `ModuleNotFoundError` or missing prompt templates only after deployment, because package data was never declared."],"when":"Apply these rules from the moment code leaves the notebook and gets a `pyproject.toml`. Use a src layout for\nanything that is installed, deployed or shared; keep a flat layout for one-off scripts. Introduce explicit\nlayers when you have more than one implementation of an external dependency, or when tests start needing to\npatch module globals to avoid network calls. Measure import time when startup matters (CLIs, serverless cold\nstarts, worker autoscaling). In code review, the question to ask is \"which way does this import point?\"; in\ninterviews, explaining why a cycle signals a misplaced concept, and how `sys.modules` makes a cycle fail\nthe way it does, shows real understanding.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps vendor SDK imports in adapters, guards type-only imports, and runs `-X importtime` when a worker's cold start creeps up.","architect":"Defines the layer contract and enforces it in CI, so the dependency direction survives team growth and vendor swaps.","fde":"Restructures a customer's notebook-grown repo into an installable package that their platform team can deploy, test and own after handover."},"check":{"q":"Your FastAPI service imports `myapp`, and `myapp/__init__.py` re-exports everything. Cold starts on\nyour serverless platform take several seconds and unit tests fail without an `OPENAI_API_KEY`. What do you\nchange and why?","a":"Both symptoms come from import-time work. Re-exporting everything means `import myapp` executes every\nmodule, including ones that import `torch` and ones that construct SDK clients at top level. I would shrink\n`__init__.py` to a small public API, move client construction into the composition root (the FastAPI lifespan\nor a factory), and inject clients into services. Heavy libraries move behind function-local or PEP 562 lazy\nimports. Then `-X importtime` confirms the cold-start gain, and tests run with fakes because nothing touches\nthe network or environment at import time."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a circular import often fail with \"cannot import name X from partially initialized module\"?","options":["Python detects the cycle and refuses to load either module","The first module is registered in sys.modules before its body finishes, so the second gets it half-built","Relative imports are not allowed inside packages that import each other","The bytecode cache is invalidated when two modules reference each other"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Python inserts a module into sys.modules before executing it. The cyclic import returns that partially executed module, so names defined later in its body do not exist yet; Python does not refuse cycles outright."},{"q":"What is the main reason to prefer a src layout for a deployable package?","options":["It makes imports run faster at startup","It is required by pyproject.toml build backends","Tests import the installed package instead of the working-directory copy, exposing packaging mistakes","It prevents circular imports between subpackages"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With src/, the repository root has no importable package directory, so tests use what installation provides (pair it with a test run against the built wheel to catch missing package data). It does not affect import speed or cycles, and build backends support flat layouts too."},{"q":"A type annotation is the only reason module A imports module B, and this creates a cycle. Best fix?","options":["Merge A and B into one module","Replace the import with importlib.import_module at call time","Import B under `if TYPE_CHECKING:` and use postponed or string annotations","Catch the ImportError and set the name to None"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TYPE_CHECKING is False at runtime, so the import exists only for type checkers and the runtime cycle disappears. Merging modules or dynamic imports work but are heavier changes than the cause needs."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/tenacity","sources":["Official docs: tenacity","Brooker, 2015 — Exponential Backoff And Jitter (AWS Architecture Blog)"],"why":"A production AI service makes thousands of remote calls: chat completions, embeddings, vector-store queries,\ntool APIs. Some fraction fail for reasons that go away on their own: rate limits, overloaded upstreams,\ndropped connections. Without retries, each of those becomes a user-facing error or a dead agent run.\n\nThe naive retry, `for i in range(3): try: ... except Exception: time.sleep(1)`, fails in four ways. It\nretries **everything**, including a 400 for a malformed request or a 401 for a bad key, wasting time and\nquota on errors that will never succeed. It waits a **fixed** interval, so when a provider rate-limits a\nfleet of workers, they all come back at the same instant and get limited again (the thundering herd). It\nhas **no total deadline**, so three attempts with long timeouts can hold a request open past the caller's\nown timeout. And it is **invisible**: no log says a call needed four attempts, so a degrading dependency\nlooks healthy until it falls over. Writing this correctly every time, in sync and async variants, is the\ngap tenacity fills.","how":"A retry policy in tenacity is built from four composable pieces passed to `@retry` (or to the `Retrying` /\n`AsyncRetrying` objects):\n\n### stop: when to give up\n`stop_after_attempt(5)`, `stop_after_delay(30)` (seconds since the first attempt), combined with `|`\n(\"whichever comes first\"). A time-based stop matters *because* the caller has its own deadline; attempt\ncounts alone do not bound latency. Note that stop conditions are evaluated only after an attempt fails, so\n`stop_after_delay` is a soft budget: an attempt that starts at 29 s still runs to completion (or to its own\ntimeout), and the true worst case is roughly the delay plus one wait plus one attempt timeout. Recent releases\nadd `stop_before_delay`, which also refuses an attempt that would begin past the budget; either way, keep the\nper-attempt timeout small relative to the budget. **The default is never stop**, so a bare `@retry` loops\nforever.\n\n### wait: how long between attempts\n`wait_fixed`, `wait_random`, `wait_exponential(multiplier=, min=, max=)`, and jittered variants such as\n`wait_random_exponential` (a random wait up to an exponentially growing cap) and `wait_exponential_jitter`.\nWaits can be added together, e.g. `wait_fixed(1) + wait_random(0, 2)`. Exponential growth backs off from an\noverloaded server; jitter spreads clients out so their retries do not arrive in synchronized waves. The\ndefault is no wait at all.\n\nThe arithmetic is worth doing once. `wait_exponential(multiplier=m, max=c)` waits `min(m · 2^(n−1), c)` after\nthe *n*-th failed attempt, so with `m=1` the waits are 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 s. `wait_random_exponential` uses the\nsame curve as an upper bound and draws uniformly below it (the \"full jitter\" scheme from Brooker's analysis):\nthe waits after attempts 1 to 4 fall in [0, 1], [0, 2], [0, 4] and [0, 8] s, about 7.5 s in expectation\nbefore the fifth attempt instead of exactly 15 s. Two hundred workers rate-limited in the same second\ntherefore return spread over seconds rather than as one spike. The cost is that one unlucky client can\ndraw near-zero waits and retry almost immediately; the cap and the attempt limit bound how much harm that does.\n\n### retry: which outcomes count as failures\n`retry_if_exception_type((RateLimitError, APITimeoutError))`, `retry_if_exception(predicate)`, or\n`retry_if_result(lambda r: r is None)` for \"successful\" responses that are actually bad, combined with `|`\nand `&`. This is where you encode the classification: retry 429, 5xx and timeouts; never retry 400, 401,\n403 or validation errors. The default retries on any exception, which is exactly the classification bug above.\n\n### hooks and the final failure\n`before_sleep=before_sleep_log(logger, logging.WARNING)` logs each retry with the attempt number and wait.\nWhen the stop condition fires, tenacity raises `RetryError` wrapping the last attempt; with `reraise=True`\nit re-raises the original exception instead, which is usually what callers and error handlers expect.\nEvery callback receives a `RetryCallState` (attempt number, elapsed time, `outcome`), so a custom wait can,\nfor example, honor a server's `Retry-After` header. Any callable that takes the state and returns seconds\nis a valid `wait`:\n\n```python\nfallback = wait_random_exponential(multiplier=1, max=20)\ndef wait_retry_after(state):\n    exc = state.outcome.exception()\n    hdr = getattr(getattr(exc, \"response\", None), \"headers\", {}).get(\"retry-after\")\n    return min(float(hdr), 60) if hdr and hdr.isdigit() else fallback(state)\n```\n\nThe server knows its own rate window, so its hint beats any guess; the `min` cap stops a hostile or buggy\nheader from parking a worker for an hour, and the `isdigit` check skips the HTTP-date form of the header.\nDecorated functions expose `.retry.statistics` and `.retry_with(...)` to override the policy per call site.\n\n### Async and code blocks\nDecorating an `async def` works directly: tenacity detects the coroutine and sleeps with the event loop\nrather than blocking it. For retrying a block instead of a function, iterate:\n`for attempt in Retrying(...): with attempt: ...`.\n\n### Alternatives\n`backoff` is a similar decorator library; `stamina` is a thin, opinionated wrapper over tenacity with safer\ndefaults; `urllib3.Retry` and httpx transports retry at the HTTP layer only. Vendor SDKs (OpenAI,\nAnthropic) already retry some errors internally through a `max_retries` setting, which you must account for\nbefore adding your own layer.","example":"A policy for a chat-completion call: retry only transient errors, exponential backoff with jitter, a\n30-second retry budget, a log line per retry, and the original exception re-raised at the end. The SDK's own\nretries are switched off so attempts do not multiply. Because the budget is checked between attempts, the\nworst case is a failure just under 30 s, plus one wait (at most 8 s, since five attempts allow only four\nwaits with windows of 1, 2, 4 and 8 s), plus one 20 s attempt timeout: just under a minute. If a caller\nneeds a strict ceiling, shrink the per-attempt timeout or enforce an outer deadline.\n\n```python\nimport logging\nfrom openai import OpenAI, RateLimitError, APITimeoutError, APIConnectionError, InternalServerError\nfrom tenacity import (retry, stop_after_attempt, stop_after_delay, wait_random_exponential,\n                      retry_if_exception_type, before_sleep_log)\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"llm\")\nclient = OpenAI(max_retries=0, timeout=20.0)   # one retry layer only: ours\n\nTRANSIENT = (RateLimitError, APITimeoutError, APIConnectionError, InternalServerError)\n\n@retry(\n    retry=retry_if_exception_type(TRANSIENT),            # 400/401/422 fail immediately\n    wait=wait_random_exponential(multiplier=1, max=20),  # jittered, capped backoff\n    stop=stop_after_attempt(5) | stop_after_delay(30),   # whichever comes first\n    before_sleep=before_sleep_log(log, logging.WARNING),\n    reraise=True,                                        # caller sees RateLimitError, not RetryError\n)\ndef summarize(text: str) -> str:\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Summarize:\\n{text}\"}],\n    )\n    return r.choices[0].message.content\n\n# Per-call override, e.g. a batch job that can wait longer:\n# summarize.retry_with(stop=stop_after_attempt(10))(doc)\n```","tradeoffs":["Retries convert transient failures into latency instead of errors, which is right for background jobs but can blow a user-facing p99; tighten `stop_after_delay` when a person is waiting.","Jittered exponential backoff protects a struggling upstream, but makes worst-case latency less predictable than a fixed wait; predictability wins only when there is a single client, not a fleet.","Retrying in your code gives one place for policy and logging, but duplicates what SDKs and HTTP transports already do; it pays off when you disable the inner layers or retry at a higher level (a whole tool call).","Per-call retries handle blips, but under a sustained outage they multiply load; beyond a few attempts a circuit breaker that fails fast is the better tool."],"pitfalls":["Using bare `@retry` with no arguments; the symptom is a request that hangs forever, spinning with no wait, against an endpoint returning a permanent error.","Retrying non-idempotent operations such as \"send email\" or \"create ticket\" without an idempotency key; the symptom is duplicate side effects after a timeout that actually succeeded upstream.","Stacking retries in the SDK, your function and the agent loop; with 3 layers of 3 attempts the symptom is up to 27 upstream calls per user action and rate limits that get worse under load.","Forgetting `reraise=True`; the symptom is error handlers that catch `RateLimitError` never firing because the exception arriving is a `tenacity.RetryError`.","Retrying a bad LLM *result* (invalid JSON, failed schema) with the identical prompt at temperature 0; the symptom is every attempt failing the same way, because the same input tends to reproduce the same output. Feed the validation error back into the next attempt, as Instructor does, or change something.","Retrying on `Exception` and thereby on your own bugs; the symptom is a `KeyError` in response parsing that costs five paid API calls before surfacing."],"when":"Reach for tenacity whenever code calls something remote that can fail transiently and the operation is safe\nto repeat: LLM and embedding calls, vector-store queries, idempotent tool APIs, downloads. Prefer the SDK's\nbuilt-in retries when they already cover your error classes and you only need to tune a count; use tenacity\nwhen you need custom classification (retry on a bad *result*, such as invalid JSON), a time budget, logging,\nor retries around a multi-step block. Do not use it to paper over permanent errors or sustained outages.\nMeasure attempts per call and retry rate per dependency; a rising retry rate is an early warning that a\nprovider is degrading.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the stop/wait/retry policy per dependency, disables duplicate SDK retries, and adds `before_sleep` logging so retries show up in traces.","architect":"Owns the retry budget across layers, which operations are idempotent, and where circuit breakers take over from retries.","fde":"Tunes backoff to the customer's actual provider quota and gateway timeouts so retries fit inside their proxy's deadline instead of being cut off mid-attempt."},"check":{"q":"An agent's tool calls a customer's ticketing API through a function decorated with\n`@retry(stop=stop_after_attempt(3))`. Customers report duplicate tickets. What is happening and how do you fix it?","a":"Creating a ticket is not idempotent. When a request times out after the server has already created\nthe ticket, tenacity retries on the exception and creates a second one; the policy also retries on any\nexception with no wait. Fixes, in order: send an idempotency key (or a client-generated ticket ID) so the\nserver deduplicates; restrict `retry=` to errors that guarantee the request was not processed, such as\nconnection refused or explicit 429/503; add jittered backoff and a time budget; and check whether the HTTP\nclient or SDK also retries, so attempts are not multiplied across layers."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `@retry` with no arguments do when the wrapped function keeps raising?","options":["Retries three times with exponential backoff, then re-raises","Retries indefinitely with no wait between attempts","Retries once, then raises RetryError","Retries only on network-related exceptions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"tenacity's defaults are stop-never, no wait, and retry on any exception. That is why every real policy sets stop, wait and retry explicitly; the \"three attempts with backoff\" behaviour must be configured."},{"q":"Why add jitter to exponential backoff when many workers call the same LLM API?","options":["Jitter reduces the total number of attempts each worker makes","Jitter is required for tenacity to work with asyncio","It desynchronizes clients so retries do not arrive together and trigger the rate limit again","It makes the latency of each retry predictable for SLOs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without jitter, workers limited at the same moment back off by identical amounts and return in a wave. Randomizing the wait spreads the load; it makes individual latency less predictable, not more."},{"q":"Your error handler catches `openai.RateLimitError`, but after retries are exhausted it never fires. Likely cause?","options":["The SDK swallows rate-limit errors after retries","tenacity converts all exceptions to TimeoutError","Without reraise=True, tenacity raises RetryError wrapping the last attempt instead of the original exception","before_sleep_log suppresses the final exception"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When the stop condition fires, tenacity raises RetryError by default. Setting reraise=True re-raises the last underlying exception, so existing except clauses for the SDK's error types keep working."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/logging-structlog","sources":["Official docs: Python logging (Logging HOWTO and Logging Cookbook)","Official docs: structlog"],"why":"`print()` and f-string log lines work until there are many concurrent requests. An LLM service interleaves\nhundreds of in-flight calls in one process; a line like `\"call failed after 3.2s\"` is useless when you cannot\ntell which user, conversation, model or prompt version it belongs to. Prose logs also cannot be aggregated:\n\"p95 latency by model\" or \"token spend by tenant\" needs fields, and parsing those back out of free text with\nregexes is fragile and slow.\n\nThe stdlib `logging` module solves routing (who emits, at what level, to where) but not structure. You can\npass `extra={...}`, yet every formatter must know which keys to print, and there is no natural way to attach\na request ID once and have every downstream line carry it, especially across `await` points. structlog fills\nthat gap: context is bound once, every event is a dict, and the final rendering (JSON for machines, coloured\nconsole for humans) is a configuration choice rather than something baked into every call site.","how":"### stdlib logging: the routing layer\n- **Loggers** are named and hierarchical (`myapp.rag.retriever` is a child of `myapp`). Use\n  `logging.getLogger(__name__)` so names mirror modules.\n- **Levels** (`DEBUG < INFO < WARNING < ERROR < CRITICAL`) are checked on the logger and again on each handler.\n- **Handlers** send records somewhere (stderr, file, syslog); **formatters** turn a record into text.\n- **Propagation**: a record goes to its logger's handlers and then up to ancestors, ending at the root. This\n  is why a library should never call `basicConfig` or attach handlers: configuration belongs to the\n  application, and a library adds only a `NullHandler` so it stays silent unless the app opts in.\n- Use `log.info(\"retrieved %d chunks\", n)`, not an f-string, *because* formatting is deferred and skipped\n  entirely when the level is disabled; `log.exception(...)` inside `except` attaches the traceback.\n\n### structlog: events and processors\nA structlog call such as `log.info(\"llm_call\", model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", tokens_in=812)` creates an **event\ndict**. That dict passes through a **processor chain**, an ordered list of functions\n`(logger, method_name, event_dict) -> event_dict`, then a final **renderer**. Typical processors:\n`merge_contextvars` (pull in request-scoped context), `add_log_level`, `TimeStamper(fmt=\"iso\")`,\n`format_exc_info` or `dict_tracebacks` (structured exceptions), a custom redaction step, then\n`JSONRenderer()` in production or `ConsoleRenderer()` locally. The pipeline design means policy (timestamps,\nredaction, sampling) lives in one place instead of at every call site. A processor is just a function, so\nredaction and sampling look like this:\n\n```python\nSENSITIVE = {\"api_key\", \"authorization\", \"prompt\", \"completion\"}\ndef redact(logger, method_name, event_dict):\n    for key in SENSITIVE & event_dict.keys():\n        event_dict[key] = \"[redacted]\"\n    return event_dict\n\ndef sample_debug(logger, method_name, event_dict):\n    if method_name == \"debug\" and random.random() > 0.01:\n        raise structlog.DropEvent          # silently discards this event\n    return event_dict\n```\n\nOrder matters *because* each processor sees only what earlier ones produced: redaction must run after\n`merge_contextvars` (or a key bound in context escapes it) and before the renderer (after which there is\nonly a string). Every processor runs on every emitted event, so keep them cheap.\n\n### Context binding\n`log = log.bind(conversation_id=cid)` returns a new logger carrying that field; bound loggers are immutable,\nso binding in one function cannot leak into another. For request-wide context, `structlog.contextvars`\n(`bind_contextvars`, `clear_contextvars`) stores fields in a `contextvars.ContextVar`. Context variables are\ncopied into each asyncio task, so a request ID bound in FastAPI middleware appears on every line emitted\nwhile handling that request, even across `await`s, without passing it explicitly. Clear it at the start of\neach request so a reused worker does not carry stale values. The copy is one-way and happens at task\ncreation: a value bound inside a child task is invisible to its parent, and work handed to a thread with\n`loop.run_in_executor` does not inherit the context at all (`asyncio.to_thread` does copy it), so lines\nlogged from that thread lose their `request_id`.\n\n### Handlers can block the event loop\nA stdlib handler writes synchronously inside the logging call. Writing to stdout or stderr in a container\nis normally cheap, but a file on a slow disk or a network handler (syslog, HTTP) stalls every coroutine in\nthe process while it writes. The stdlib answer is `QueueHandler` on the hot path plus a `QueueListener`\nthread that owns the slow handlers, *because* enqueueing is fast and the I/O moves off the event loop. In\ncontainers the usual pattern sidesteps this: write JSON to stdout and let the platform's log agent ship it.\n\n### Level discipline\nLevels are only useful if they mean the same thing everywhere: DEBUG for detail you would enable while\ninvestigating, INFO for one summary event per meaningful unit of work (a request, an LLM call, a tool\ncall), WARNING for something that recovered (a retry, a fallback model), ERROR for a failed unit of work\nsomeone may need to look at. Alerting is then built on ERROR rate and on fields, not on message text.\n\n### Integrating the two\nThird-party libraries (httpx, openai, uvicorn) log through stdlib. structlog can either use stdlib loggers as\nits backend or render stdlib records via `structlog.stdlib.ProcessorFormatter`, so library and application\nlines come out in one JSON format. `make_filtering_bound_logger(logging.INFO)` gives a fast level filter\nwhen structlog is used standalone, and `cache_logger_on_first_use=True` avoids rebuilding loggers on every call.\n\n### Logs vs traces vs metrics\nLogs are discrete events; traces (OpenTelemetry spans) carry timing and parent-child structure across\nservices; metrics are cheap aggregates. Put the trace ID in every log event so the three can be joined, and\ndo not use logs as your only latency metric.","example":"Production-style configuration: JSON output, request context via contextvars, and one structured event per\nLLM call carrying the fields you will later aggregate on. The prompt itself is not logged, only its hash.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, logging, time, uuid\nimport structlog\n\nstructlog.configure(\n    processors=[\n        structlog.contextvars.merge_contextvars,     # request-scoped fields\n        structlog.processors.add_log_level,\n        structlog.processors.TimeStamper(fmt=\"iso\"),\n        structlog.processors.dict_tracebacks,        # exceptions as structured data\n        structlog.processors.JSONRenderer(),\n    ],\n    wrapper_class=structlog.make_filtering_bound_logger(logging.INFO),\n    cache_logger_on_first_use=True,\n)\nlog = structlog.get_logger()\n\ndef handle_request(user_id: str, prompt: str) -> None:\n    structlog.contextvars.clear_contextvars()\n    structlog.contextvars.bind_contextvars(request_id=str(uuid.uuid4()), user_id=user_id)\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    # ... call the model here ...\n    log.info(\"llm_call\", model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", prompt_version=\"summarize-v3\",\n             prompt_sha=hashlib.sha256(prompt.encode()).hexdigest()[:12],\n             tokens_in=812, tokens_out=164, latency_ms=round((time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000))\n\nhandle_request(\"u_42\", \"Summarize this contract ...\")\n# {\"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini\", \"prompt_version\": \"summarize-v3\", \"prompt_sha\": \"…\", \"tokens_in\": 812,\n#  \"tokens_out\": 164, \"latency_ms\": 0, \"event\": \"llm_call\", \"request_id\": \"…\", \"user_id\": \"u_42\",\n#  \"level\": \"info\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-…Z\"}\n# (keys follow processor order: call kwargs, then contextvars, level, timestamp; pass\n#  JSONRenderer(sort_keys=True) if your pipeline wants a stable alphabetical order)\n```","tradeoffs":["JSON logs are queryable and aggregate cleanly, but are hard to read in a terminal; render with `ConsoleRenderer` in development and switch on an environment flag rather than choosing one forever.","Logging full prompts and completions makes debugging easy, but stores user data and secrets in a system with broad access and long retention; it flips to acceptable only with redaction, sampling and a short retention policy, or by sending payloads to a dedicated LLM tracing store instead.","Rich context on every line costs bytes and ingestion fees at high volume; sample DEBUG and per-chunk streaming events, keep one summary event per call at INFO.","structlog adds a dependency and a configuration step over plain stdlib; for a small script or a library, stdlib logging with `extra=` is enough and keeps you dependency-free."],"pitfalls":["Calling `logging.basicConfig` or adding handlers inside a library; the symptom is duplicated lines or log formats changing depending on import order in the host application.","Binding request context in a module-level global instead of contextvars; the symptom is log lines from one user's request carrying another user's `request_id` under concurrency.","Logging an exception with `log.error(str(e))`; the symptom is incidents where you know *that* something failed but have no traceback, because only `log.exception` or `exc_info=True` captures it.","Logging raw prompts that contain API keys, PII or retrieved documents; the symptom is a security review finding customer data in a log index that half the company can query.","Offloading blocking work with `loop.run_in_executor` and logging inside it; the symptom is a subset of lines, exactly those from the thread pool, missing `request_id` and impossible to join to their request.","Emitting a log line per streamed token; the symptom is log volume and cost scaling with output length and the useful summary line drowning in noise."],"when":"Use stdlib logging everywhere as the routing backbone, and add structlog in any service that handles\nconcurrent requests or whose logs feed a search or analytics backend; that is nearly every deployed AI\nservice. Define a small event vocabulary early (`llm_call`, `retrieval`, `tool_call`, `guardrail_block`)\nwith consistent field names, because dashboards and alerts are built on them. Measure log volume per\nrequest and check that every line carries a request or trace ID. In interviews, explaining propagation,\nwhy libraries do not configure logging, and how contextvars keep async context straight is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures the processor chain, binds request and conversation IDs in middleware, and emits one structured event per model or tool call with tokens and latency.","architect":"Sets the logging contract: field names, what may never be logged, retention, and how logs join traces and cost metrics across services.","fde":"Adapts the output format and redaction rules to the customer's log pipeline and compliance rules, often their SIEM or a mandated JSON schema."},"check":{"q":"Under load, your FastAPI service's logs sometimes show a request's events tagged with another user's\nID. You store the current user in a module-level dict that middleware updates. Why does this happen and\nwhat is the fix?","a":"A module-level dict is shared by every concurrent request in the process. With async handlers,\nrequest A sets the user, awaits the model, request B overwrites the value, and A's later log lines read B's\nuser. The fix is request-scoped storage: `structlog.contextvars.bind_contextvars` (backed by a\n`ContextVar`), which asyncio copies per task, so each request sees its own values across awaits. Also clear\ncontextvars at request start so a reused worker does not inherit stale fields, and add a test that runs\nconcurrent requests and asserts each line's ID matches."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a library add only a NullHandler rather than configure logging itself?","options":["NullHandler makes the library's logging faster than a StreamHandler","Handler and format configuration belong to the application; libraries that configure logging cause duplicated or reformatted output","Libraries are not allowed to use getLogger(__name__)","Records from libraries do not propagate to the root logger"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Library records propagate to the application's handlers, so the app decides where and how they appear. A library that attaches handlers or calls basicConfig overrides that choice and often duplicates lines."},{"q":"What does a structlog processor chain receive and return?","options":["A formatted string, returning a formatted string","A stdlib LogRecord, returning a LogRecord","The event dict, returning a (possibly modified) event dict, with a renderer at the end","A list of handlers, returning the handler to use"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each processor takes the logger, method name and event dict and returns an event dict; the final renderer turns it into JSON or console text. That is what makes redaction and timestamps one-place policy."},{"q":"Why does `log.info(\"retrieved %d chunks\", n)` beat `log.info(f\"retrieved {n} chunks\")` in stdlib logging?","options":["f-strings are not supported by logging handlers","The %-style call writes JSON automatically","Formatting is deferred and skipped when the level is disabled, and the template stays constant for grouping","The f-string version is not thread-safe"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The f-string is built before logging checks the level, wasting work for disabled levels, and it produces a unique message per value, which defeats grouping by message template in log tools."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","sources":["Official docs: Python profile/cProfile and tracemalloc modules","Official docs: py-spy","Official docs: memray","Official docs: Scalene"],"why":"\"The pipeline is slow\" and \"the worker gets OOM-killed after a few hours\" are the two most common\nperformance tickets in an AI codebase, and intuition is a poor guide to either. Engineers rewrite a loop in\nNumPy when the real cost was a tokenizer re-created per request; they add a cache when the real cost was\nwaiting sequentially on twelve embedding calls; they blame \"a memory leak in the model\" when an unbounded\n`lru_cache` or a conversation list that is never trimmed is holding every request's text.\n\nThe naive approach, sprinkling `time.time()` prints and watching `top`, fails for structural reasons. Prints\nonly measure code you already suspect. `top` reports resident memory (RSS) for the whole process, which\nmixes the Python heap, native library allocations, memory-mapped model weights and allocator fragmentation,\nso it can tell you *that* memory grows but never *where*. A profiler attributes cost to code systematically,\nincluding code you did not think to suspect.","how":"### First question: CPU-bound or waiting?\nCompare wall time with CPU time (`time.perf_counter()` vs `time.process_time()`). If CPU time is a small\nfraction of wall time, the process is waiting on I/O, locks or a GPU, and a CPU profiler will mostly show\ntime inside the event loop's selector. The fix there is concurrency, batching or caching, and the right\nmeasurement is a trace with spans per remote call, not a function profile.\n\n### Deterministic profiling: cProfile\n`cProfile` (stdlib) hooks every function call and return, recording call counts, total time inside each\nfunction (`tottime`) and time including callees (`cumtime`). It is exact about call counts, which is how\nyou discover \"this tokenizer was constructed 10,000 times\". The cost is overhead on every call, which\ninflates functions that make many small calls, and it attributes poorly across `await` points and threads.\nRead results with `pstats` sorted by `cumulative` to find the expensive subtree, then by `tottime` to find\nthe hot leaf; `snakeviz` renders them visually.\n\n### Sampling profilers: py-spy, Scalene\nA sampling profiler interrupts at intervals and records the current stack, so overhead stays low and\nroughly constant, *because* cost depends on the sampling rate rather than on how many calls you make.\n`py-spy` reads the target process's memory from outside, so it can attach to a running production worker\n(`py-spy top --pid`, `py-spy record -o flame.svg --pid`, `py-spy dump` for a hung process) without code\nchanges; `--idle` includes waiting threads and `--native` includes C extension frames. Scalene separates\ntime spent in Python from time in native code and also reports memory and copy volume per line, which\nanswers \"is my NumPy code actually vectorized?\". `line_profiler` (`@profile` plus `kernprof -l`) gives\nper-line timings for one function once you know which function matters.\n\n### Python heap memory: tracemalloc\n`tracemalloc` (stdlib) records a traceback for every allocation made through Python's allocator. Take a\nsnapshot, run the workload, take another, and `compare_to` shows which source lines' live allocations grew.\nThat is the right tool for leaks in Python objects: growing lists, dicts, caches and closures. Note what it\nanswers: *where* the surviving bytes were allocated, not *who is keeping them alive*. The second question is\nthe one you fix. Take one leaked object (say, a string from the growing line) and walk its referrers with\n`gc.get_referrers(obj)`, or draw the chain back to a module global with `objgraph.show_backrefs`; the\nretainer is typically a module-level list, a cache, a closure, or a bound-method callback registered on a\nlong-lived object. tracemalloc itself slows the\nprogram, and it cannot see allocations that native libraries make with their own allocators unless the\nlibrary reports them explicitly. NumPy is the notable exception: it registers its array data buffers with\ntracemalloc (via `PyTraceMalloc_Track`), so large arrays do show up; PyTorch CPU tensors, Hugging Face\n`tokenizers` (Rust) and most C/C++ parsers do not.\n\n### Native and GPU memory\n`memray` intercepts the allocator calls themselves (`malloc`, `mmap` and friends), so it sees native\nallocations as well (C extensions, PyTorch CPU tensors, tokenizers), records to a file and\nproduces flame graphs of what was live at peak (`memray run`, then `memray flamegraph`). For PyTorch on GPU,\nuse `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()`, `max_memory_allocated()` and `memory_summary()`; RSS and tracemalloc\nknow nothing about VRAM, and the caching allocator keeps freed blocks reserved, so `nvidia-smi` shows more\nthan tensors actually use. Timing GPU work has its own trap: CUDA kernels launch asynchronously, so\n`perf_counter()` around `model(x)` measures how long it took to *queue* the work. Call\n`torch.cuda.synchronize()` before reading the clock (or use `torch.cuda.Event` timers or `torch.profiler`),\notherwise the cost shows up later, at whatever line first needs a result on the CPU, such as `.item()`.\n\n### Measuring a fix\nA profile tells you where to look; a benchmark tells you whether the change helped. For a function, use\n`timeit` with several repeats and compare the minimum, *because* noise from the OS and other processes only\never adds time. For a service, compare latency percentiles (p50 and p95) and throughput under the same load\nscript before and after, with the process warmed up, since a single request mixes in one-off costs.\n\n### Why RSS may not go down\nCPython's small-object allocator groups objects into arenas and can only return an arena to the OS when\nevery object in it is freed, and native allocators have similar behaviour. A process that peaked at 6 GB and\nfreed most objects may still report a high RSS. Growth that plateaus is usually fragmentation or a warm\ncache; growth that never stops under steady load is a leak.","example":"Find a memory leak and a hot spot in a RAG worker. tracemalloc compares two snapshots around a burst of\nrequests; cProfile then shows where the CPU time goes.\n\n```python\nimport cProfile, pstats, tracemalloc\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nHISTORY: list[str] = []                          # the bug: grows forever\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)                         # second bug: unbounded cache keyed by full text\ndef normalize(text: str) -> str:\n    return \" \".join(text.lower().split())\n\ndef handle(i: int) -> int:\n    doc = f\"Retrieved chunk {i} \" * 200\n    HISTORY.append(doc)                          # kept \"for debugging\"\n    return len(normalize(doc).split())\n\ntracemalloc.start(10)                            # keep 10 frames per allocation\nbefore = tracemalloc.take_snapshot()\nfor i in range(5_000):\n    handle(i)\nafter = tracemalloc.take_snapshot()\nfor stat in after.compare_to(before, \"lineno\")[:3]:\n    print(stat)    # top lines by growth: the `doc = ...` line (strings kept alive by HISTORY),\n                   # normalize's join (cached results) and the call site (lru_cache entries)\ntracemalloc.stop()\n\nprof = cProfile.Profile()\nprof.enable()\nfor i in range(5_000, 10_000):\n    handle(i)\nprof.disable()\npstats.Stats(prof).sort_stats(\"cumulative\").print_stats(5)\n# Fix: bound the history (collections.deque(maxlen=100)) and give lru_cache a maxsize,\n# then re-run the snapshot diff and confirm growth is flat across bursts.\n```","tradeoffs":["Deterministic profilers give exact call counts but distort timings of call-heavy code; sampling profilers keep timings honest and are safe in production, but miss rare short calls and give no exact counts.","tracemalloc pinpoints Python-object leaks to a line, but slows the program noticeably and misses native memory; switch to memray once RSS grows while tracemalloc's totals stay flat.","Profiling in production (py-spy attach) shows real behaviour under real traffic, but needs `ptrace`-style permissions that hardened containers often deny; locally reproduced load is easier but may not trigger the real hot path.","Micro-optimizing a hot Python function can halve its time, but if wall time is dominated by remote calls the user sees no difference; batching or parallelizing those calls is the larger lever then."],"pitfalls":["Profiling an I/O-bound async service with cProfile and \"optimizing\" whatever tops the list; the symptom is that the selector or event loop dominates the profile and latency does not improve after the change.","Treating high RSS after a peak as a leak; the symptom is memory that plateaus under steady load rather than climbing, which points to fragmentation or a cache, not unbounded growth.","Measuring once, on a cold process; the symptom is profiles dominated by imports, model loading and JIT or CUDA initialization that never recur in steady state.","Timing GPU code without `torch.cuda.synchronize()`; the symptom is a forward pass that \"takes 0.2 ms\" while an innocent `.item()` or `.cpu()` call a few lines later appears to take hundreds of milliseconds.","Stopping at tracemalloc's allocation line and never finding the retainer; the symptom is a fix to the allocating code (a smaller string, a copy avoided) that shrinks each leaked object but leaves memory climbing at the same slope.","Reading `nvidia-smi` as tensor usage; the symptom is apparent \"GPU leaks\" that are actually the PyTorch caching allocator holding reserved but free blocks.","Leaving tracemalloc or a deterministic profiler enabled in a production build; the symptom is a large, otherwise unexplained latency regression."],"when":"Profile before optimizing, and whenever a latency SLO, a cost target or an OOM is in play. Start with the\ncheapest discriminating measurement: wall vs CPU time, then a sampling profile, then a deterministic or\nline profile of the function that matters. For memory, watch RSS over time under steady load first; if it\nclimbs, use tracemalloc snapshot diffs for Python objects and memray for native buffers, and the CUDA memory\nAPIs for GPU. Measure after every fix to confirm it moved the number you cared about. In interviews,\ndistinguishing CPU-bound from waiting, and Python heap from native and GPU memory, is the core skill.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs py-spy on a slow worker, uses tracemalloc diffs to find the unbounded cache, and verifies each fix with a before-and-after measurement.","architect":"Decides where profiling hooks and memory limits live, and sets the capacity model from measured per-request CPU, memory and GPU cost rather than estimates.","fde":"Diagnoses performance inside the customer's environment, often without root access, so relies on sampling, logs of wall vs CPU time and reproducible load scripts."},"check":{"q":"A document-ingestion worker's RSS climbs from 1.5 GB to 9 GB over six hours and the pod is\nOOM-killed. tracemalloc shows the Python heap stable at about 300 MB. How do you proceed?","a":"A stable traced heap with growing RSS means the growth is outside what tracemalloc sees: native\nbuffers (PDF parsers, PyTorch tensors, tokenizers; NumPy arrays would have shown up because NumPy reports\nthem to tracemalloc), memory-mapped files, or fragmentation. I would run the worker under\nmemray, or attach it in a staging replica, and look at what is live at the high-water mark, checking for\nnative objects kept alive by Python references such as a cache of parsed documents or arrays. I would also\ncheck whether growth plateaus (fragmentation, possibly mitigated by recycling workers after N jobs) or is\nunbounded (a real leak). Then fix, rerun the same workload, and confirm RSS stays flat."},"quiz":[{"q":"An async RAG endpoint's cProfile output is dominated by the event loop's select call. What does that indicate?","options":["The event loop implementation is inefficient and should be replaced","The process spends most wall time waiting on I/O, so CPU optimization will not help much","cProfile cannot run on async code at all","There is a memory leak in the selector"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Time in the selector is time spent waiting for sockets. The levers are concurrency, batching and caching of remote calls, measured with traces; replacing the loop rarely moves waiting-dominated latency."},{"q":"Why can py-spy safely profile a live production process when cProfile usually is not used there?","options":["py-spy only records memory, not CPU","cProfile requires a debug build of Python","py-spy samples stacks from outside the process at low constant overhead, while cProfile instruments every call","py-spy rewrites bytecode to remove its own overhead"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sampling cost depends on the sampling rate, not on how many calls the program makes, and py-spy reads the target's memory externally, so no code change or restart is needed."},{"q":"RSS keeps growing but tracemalloc's traced total is flat. Most likely explanation?","options":["tracemalloc is reporting incorrectly and should be restarted","Python's garbage collector is disabled","The leak is in a Python list that tracemalloc ignores","Memory is allocated by native code or the allocator is fragmented, outside what tracemalloc traces"],"answer":3,"explanation":"tracemalloc sees allocations through Python's allocator. Native libraries using their own allocators, mmaps and fragmentation all raise RSS invisibly to it, which is when a native-aware tool like memray helps."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","sources":["Official docs: pip","Official docs: Poetry","Official docs: uv (Astral)","PEP 621 — Storing project metadata in pyproject.toml"],"why":"A Python project needs four things: a list of what it depends on, an exact resolved set of versions that\nsatisfies all constraints, an isolated environment holding those versions, and a way to build and publish\nitself. pip historically did only the install step. It has a real resolver (it backtracks to satisfy\nconstraints), but out of the box it does not record the result in a lockfile, does not create environments,\nand does not distinguish \"what I asked for\" from \"what I ended up with\". (A standard lockfile format,\n`pylock.toml`, was only accepted as PEP 751 in 2025, and pip's `pip lock` command that writes it is still\nmarked experimental; most teams therefore still get locking from pip-tools, Poetry or uv.)\n\nThe naive workflow, `pip install` things into a venv and `pip freeze > requirements.txt`, mixes those\nconcerns. The freeze captures whatever happens to be installed, including leftovers from experiments, for\none OS and one Python version, with no record of which packages were direct requirements. Upgrading one\nlibrary means hand-editing pins and hoping the set is still consistent. Tools such as pip-tools, Poetry and\nuv exist to separate *intent* (ranges in `pyproject.toml`) from *resolution* (an exact lockfile), and to make\nrecreating the environment one deterministic command.","how":"### What every tool is doing underneath\nAll three run the same pipeline, and knowing it explains most failures:\n1. **Collect constraints.** Your direct requirements (`openai>=1.40`) plus, recursively, each candidate\n   version's own declared dependencies and environment markers (`; sys_platform == \"linux\"`).\n2. **Resolve.** Choose one version per package so every constraint holds at once. This is a search\n   problem, NP-hard in general, so resolvers backtrack: pick the newest candidate, follow its\n   dependencies, and on a conflict undo choices and try older versions. pip uses the `resolvelib`\n   backtracking resolver; Poetry and uv both use PubGrub-style solvers, which record *why* a conflict\n   happened so they can skip whole families of doomed versions and print an explanation chain.\n3. **Pick artifacts.** For each chosen version, find a wheel whose tags match the interpreter and platform\n   (a filename such as `...-cp312-cp312-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.whl` encodes Python ABI and OS), else fall\n   back to the source distribution and build it locally.\n4. **Install.** Unpack into the environment's `site-packages` and write the record of what was installed.\n\nTwo consequences follow. Resolution is slow when metadata is expensive to get: an sdist with a dynamic\n`setup.py` has to be *built* just to learn its dependencies, which is why a resolve can stall on one old\npackage. And the answer depends on the platform, *because* markers and available wheels differ between a\nMac laptop and a Linux container, which is the root of \"the lock works for me but not in CI\".\n\n### pip (+ venv, + pip-tools)\n`python -m venv .venv` creates an isolated interpreter environment; `pip install -r requirements.txt` resolves\nand installs into it from PyPI or another index. pip prefers wheels (pre-built archives) and falls back to\nbuilding from source distributions, which is where compiler and CUDA errors come from. pip-tools adds\n`pip-compile`, which turns a short `requirements.in` (or `pyproject.toml`) into a fully pinned, optionally\nhashed `requirements.txt`, and `pip-sync`, which makes the environment match it exactly. This is the minimal,\nwidely understood baseline, and every other tool can export to it.\n\n### Poetry\nPoetry is a project manager: `poetry add httpx` updates `pyproject.toml`, re-resolves and writes\n`poetry.lock`; `poetry install` creates or reuses a virtual environment and installs exactly what the lock\nsays; dependency groups separate dev and test tools; `poetry build` and `poetry publish` handle packaging.\nRecent major versions also read standard PEP 621 `[project]` metadata rather than only its own\n`[tool.poetry]` tables. Poetry's strength is a mature, integrated workflow for libraries and applications;\nits historical weaknesses were resolution speed on large graphs and friction with non-PyPI wheel indexes.\n\n### uv\nuv, from Astral, is written in Rust and covers the whole toolchain:\n- **Project workflow:** `uv init`, `uv add`, `uv lock` (writes `uv.lock`), `uv sync` (makes `.venv` match the\n  lock), `uv run <cmd>` (syncs if needed, then runs inside the environment). In CI, `uv sync --locked` fails if\n  the lock is out of date with `pyproject.toml`, and `--frozen` uses the lock without checking.\n- **Universal lockfile:** `uv.lock` records resolutions for all platforms and Python versions the project\n  supports, so one lock serves macOS laptops and Linux containers.\n- **Python versions:** `uv python install 3.12` downloads interpreters, replacing pyenv for many teams.\n- **pip-compatible interface:** `uv pip install`, `uv pip compile`, `uv pip sync` work as drop-ins for\n  existing requirements-based projects, which makes adoption incremental.\n- **Tools and scripts:** `uvx ruff` runs a CLI tool in a throwaway environment; `uv run script.py` honors\n  inline script metadata (PEP 723) so a one-file script can declare its own dependencies.\n- **Indexes:** per-package index pinning (`[tool.uv.sources]`) handles the classic AI pain of installing\n  `torch` from a CUDA-specific index while everything else comes from PyPI.\n\nuv is fast *because* of a parallel resolver and downloader, a global cache whose files are cloned\n(copy-on-write, the default on macOS), hard-linked (the default on Linux) or copied into environments instead\nof being re-downloaded and re-unpacked, and reading wheel metadata without fetching whole wheels\nwhere indexes allow. The practical effect is that recreating an environment becomes cheap enough to do on\nevery CI run and in every Docker build.","example":"The same project, three ways. The scenario: a FastAPI RAG service that needs `openai`, `httpx` and\n`torch` (CPU wheels in CI), plus `pytest` as a dev dependency.\n\n- **pip + pip-tools:** keep `requirements.in` with `openai>=1.40`, `httpx`, `torch`; run\n  `pip-compile --generate-hashes requirements.in` to get a pinned, hashed `requirements.txt`; a separate\n  `dev.in` with `-c requirements.txt` and `pytest`. CI runs `python -m venv .venv` then\n  `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt`. Two files per environment, and a Mac developer who\n  needs different wheels may need a separately compiled file.\n- **Poetry:** `poetry add openai httpx torch` and `poetry add --group dev pytest`; commit `pyproject.toml` and\n  `poetry.lock`; CI runs `poetry install`. Pointing torch at the PyTorch CPU index needs a configured source\n  and an explicit source on that dependency.\n- **uv:** `uv init`, `uv add openai httpx torch`, `uv add --dev pytest`, then route only `torch` to the\n  PyTorch CPU index:\n\n  ```toml\n  [[tool.uv.index]]\n  name = \"pytorch-cpu\"\n  url = \"https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu\"\n  explicit = true            # used only by packages that name it, never as a general fallback\n\n  [tool.uv.sources]\n  torch = { index = \"pytorch-cpu\" }\n  ```\n\n  `explicit = true` matters *because* an index that is searched for every package lets a same-named\n  package on it shadow the PyPI one, the dependency-confusion pattern. Commit `pyproject.toml` and\n  `uv.lock`. CI runs `uv sync --locked` then `uv run pytest`. The Docker build copies the\n  two files first and runs `uv sync --locked --no-dev`, so the dependency layer is cached until the lock\n  changes.\n\nIn all three, the rule is the same: humans edit ranges, a tool writes the exact lock, and CI and production\ninstall only from the lock.\n\nWhen resolution fails, read the explanation rather than loosening pins at random. An illustrative case:\nthe service needs `pydantic>=2`, and an older internal helper library declares `pydantic<2`. No version\nassignment satisfies both, so a PubGrub-style resolver reports the chain (\"because helper==0.3 depends on\npydantic<2 and you require pydantic>=2, helper==0.3 cannot be used...\"). The fixes are, in order of\npreference: upgrade the helper to a release that supports pydantic 2, drop it, or, as a last resort, use\nan override and own the risk of running it against a version it never declared support for.","tradeoffs":["pip with pip-tools is the most portable and universally understood option, but you assemble venv, pinning, syncing and Python versions yourself; it wins when an organization's tooling is already built around requirements files.","Poetry gives an integrated, mature workflow with good publishing support, but is slower on large graphs and adds its own conventions; it remains a reasonable choice for teams already standardized on it.","uv is dramatically faster and replaces several tools, but is younger, and new features have evolved quickly; pin the uv version in CI so a tool upgrade does not change lock behaviour under you.","Resolving to the newest allowed versions is right for applications, but a library's declared lower bounds are then never tested; for libraries, run CI once with uv's `--resolution lowest-direct` so `httpx>=0.23` means what it claims.","A universal lockfile serves every platform from one file, but resolves for platforms you may never deploy to, which can surface conflicts (often around CUDA wheels) that a single-platform lock would never hit."],"pitfalls":["Installing into the system or base conda interpreter instead of a project environment; the symptom is a package upgrade for one project breaking another, permission errors on `pip install`, or (on distro and Homebrew Pythons that adopt PEP 668) pip refusing with `externally-managed-environment`.","Committing `pyproject.toml` but not the lockfile for an application; the symptom is CI and production resolving newer transitive versions than developers tested.","Mixing tools in one project (running `pip install` inside a uv- or Poetry-managed venv); the symptom is an environment that drifts from the lock and gets silently reset or reports a different set on the next sync.","Adding an extra index with pip's `--extra-index-url` for one private package; the symptom is the public index and the private one being treated as equals, so a public package with the same name (and a higher version) can be installed in place of yours.","Letting the default index provide `torch`; the symptom is a multi-gigabyte CUDA wheel in a CPU-only image, or a CPU-only build on a GPU host, discovered as slow images or `torch.cuda.is_available()` being False."],"when":"For a new application or service, uv is a strong default: one tool for Python versions, environments,\nlocking and running, fast enough for CI and Docker. Keep Poetry where a team already uses it well; migrating\nbuys speed but costs churn. Use pip with pip-tools, or uv's pip interface, when your deployment platform or\norganization expects requirements files. Whatever the tool, measure success by one property: a fresh clone\nplus one command reproduces the exact environment CI tested. In interviews, explain the separation of\nintent from resolution rather than tool preferences.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Uses `uv add`/`uv sync` daily, keeps dev tools in a dev group, and configures the torch index so local and CI environments get the right wheels.","architect":"Chooses one toolchain across repos and defines how locks are updated, reviewed and consumed in CI and container builds.","fde":"Works inside whatever the customer mandates, often pip against a private mirror, and exports a pinned requirements file from uv or Poetry when that is the only accepted format."},"check":{"q":"Your service's CI installs with `pip install -r requirements.txt` generated by `pip freeze` on a\ndeveloper's Mac. Linux builds fail intermittently and images contain unexpected packages. What would you\nchange, and why does it fix both symptoms?","a":"A freeze captures one machine's whole environment: experiment leftovers, macOS-specific packages and\nmarkers, and no distinction between direct and transitive dependencies. I would declare direct dependencies\nwith ranges in `pyproject.toml`, generate a lock with a resolver (uv lock, Poetry, or pip-compile run for\nthe target platform), commit it, and have CI install only from it (`uv sync --locked`). The lock contains\nonly what the declared dependencies need, so stray packages disappear, and it is resolved for Linux, so\nplatform mismatches stop."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `uv sync --locked` do in CI that plain `uv sync` does not?","options":["It skips installing dev dependencies","It fails if uv.lock is out of date with pyproject.toml instead of silently re-locking","It installs packages globally instead of into .venv","It upgrades every dependency to the newest allowed version"],"answer":1,"explanation":"--locked asserts the lockfile already matches the declared dependencies, so a developer who edited pyproject.toml without re-locking gets a failing build rather than CI resolving a new, untested set."},{"q":"Why is `pip freeze > requirements.txt` a poor lockfile for a deployed service?","options":["pip freeze cannot output version numbers","pip refuses to install files generated by freeze","It captures the whole local environment for one platform, without separating direct from transitive dependencies","It always includes hashes that break installation on other machines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A freeze records whatever is installed, including unrelated packages, resolved on one OS and Python version. A resolver-generated lock is derived from declared requirements and can target the deploy platform."},{"q":"A main reason uv installs are fast on repeated builds is that it:","options":["Skips dependency resolution entirely","Downloads only source distributions and compiles in parallel","Reuses a global cache, linking or copying files into environments instead of re-downloading them","Installs packages without metadata"],"answer":2,"explanation":"uv still resolves, but caches packages globally and materializes them into each environment cheaply, alongside parallel downloads. Building from source would be slower, not faster."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","sources":["Official docs: pip (secure installs and hash-checking mode)","Official docs: uv (locking and syncing)","PEP 440 — Version Identification and Dependency Specification","PEP 751 — A file format to record Python dependencies for installation reproducibility"],"why":"A typical AI service declares perhaps fifteen direct dependencies and installs well over a hundred. Each\ndirect dependency allows a range of versions of its own dependencies, and new releases appear daily. With\nonly ranges, `pip install` on Tuesday and on Friday can produce different environments from the same\nsource tree. The failure is rarely loud at install time; it appears later as a changed default, a removed\nkeyword argument or a new validation rule, in a package nobody on the team chose directly.\n\nThe naive responses both fail. Pinning nothing (\"always get the latest fixes\") means every build is a new,\nuntested combination, so a green CI run on Monday says nothing about Friday's deploy. Pinning everything by\nhand in `requirements.txt` freezes direct dependencies but misses transitive ones unless you freeze the whole\nenvironment, and then upgrades become painful manual surgery, so teams stop upgrading and accumulate\nvulnerable versions. A lockfile, generated by a resolver from declared intent, gives you exactness *and*\ncheap, reviewable upgrades.","how":"### Two layers: constraints and resolution\nIn `pyproject.toml` you declare intent with PEP 440 specifiers: `openai>=1.40,<2`, `httpx>=0.27`. A resolver\n(uv, Poetry, pip-compile) searches for one version of every package in the transitive graph that satisfies\nall constraints at once, then writes the result. Keeping the two layers separate is the point: humans reason\nabout compatibility in ranges; machines record the exact solution.\n\n### Reading specifiers precisely\n- `>=1.40,<2` is the common application-side range: newer minors allowed, next major excluded.\n- `~=1.4.2` is the *compatible release* operator and means `>=1.4.2, ==1.4.*`; `~=1.4` means\n  `>=1.4, ==1.*`. The number of components you write decides how much can float, which is easy to misread.\n- `==1.4.*` is a prefix match; `==1.4.2` is an exact pin; `!=1.4.3` excludes one known-bad release.\n- Pre-releases (`2.0.0rc1`) are skipped by default unless a specifier names one or you opt in.\n\nNone of this encodes semantic versioning, *because* PEP 440 defines ordering, not compatibility promises.\nMany core AI-stack packages are still on `0.x`, where a minor bump is allowed to break, as the example\nbelow shows; a range is a bet about the maintainer's behaviour, and the lock is what makes that bet testable.\nThe project's `requires-python` is a constraint too: a universal lock must find versions that work across\nthe whole declared Python range, so declaring `>=3.9` when you only run 3.12 can force older releases or\nsplit resolutions for some packages.\n\n### What a lockfile records\n- **Exact versions** for every package, direct and transitive.\n- **Hashes** of the distribution files. With hash checking (`pip install --require-hashes`, or tools that\n  verify lock hashes automatically), an install fails if a file's bytes differ from what was locked,\n  *because* a version number alone does not prove you got the same artifact: a compromised mirror or index\n  could serve different bytes under the same name.\n- **Markers and platforms.** Some packages differ by OS, CPU architecture or Python version. A\n  *universal* lock (uv.lock, poetry.lock) records resolutions for all supported environments; a\n  platform-specific one (a pip-compile output run on Linux) is only valid where it was resolved.\n- **Sources.** Which index each package came from, which matters for torch CUDA wheels and private mirrors.\n\nPEP 751 defines a standard `pylock.toml` format so that different tools can produce and consume the same\nlockfile; tool-specific formats remain common.\n\n### Applications vs libraries\nAn application is the end of the dependency chain, so it should lock exactly. A library is installed\nalongside other people's dependencies; if it pinned `httpx==0.27.2`, any application also needing a newer\nhttpx could not install both. Libraries therefore declare the widest range they are actually tested with.\nUpper bounds (`<2`) on libraries are debated: they prevent breakage from a future major version but cause\nunsatisfiable resolutions when that version ships and the library is not updated. Lower bounds should reflect\nthe oldest version you actually test.\n\n### Upgrading on purpose\nPinning is safe only if upgrades keep happening. Bots (Dependabot, Renovate) or a scheduled job run\n`uv lock --upgrade` (or `--upgrade-package openai` for one package), open a pull request with the lock diff,\nand let CI and the eval suite decide. Small, frequent upgrades are cheaper to debug than a year of drift\nlanding at once, and they keep security fixes flowing.\n\n### What a lock does not pin\nThe interpreter version, OS libraries (glibc, CUDA drivers), the base container image and model weights are\noutside the Python lock. Reproducibility needs those pinned too: a `requires-python` range plus a\n`.python-version`, an image digest, and model revisions or checksums.","example":"A worked failure and its fix. A RAG service declares `openai>=1.0` and `httpx` with no lockfile. The image is\nrebuilt for an unrelated config change; the resolver picks up a new minor release of the HTTP client in which\na long-deprecated constructor argument was removed. The SDK version that was also resolved still passes that\nargument, so every request fails at client construction with a `TypeError` about an unexpected keyword. Code,\nprompts and tests are unchanged; only the environment moved. (A real instance of this pattern hit older\nOpenAI Python SDK releases when httpx 0.28 removed its `proxies` argument.)\n\nWith a lockfile the sequence is different. The rebuild installs exactly the locked versions, so production is\nunaffected. Separately, the weekly upgrade job runs `uv lock --upgrade` and opens a pull request whose diff shows\nthe HTTP client moving one minor version. CI runs the unit tests and a small integration test that constructs\nthe real SDK client, which fails with the same `TypeError`, so the upgrade is held back. The fix is to also\nupgrade the SDK to a release that supports the new client, or to add a temporary upper bound (`httpx<0.28`)\nwith a comment and a ticket to remove it. The failure happened in a pull request instead of in production,\nand the cause was visible as a two-line lock diff rather than reconstructed from logs.","tradeoffs":["Exact locks make builds reproducible and incidents bisectable, but you stop receiving fixes automatically; that is only safe when an automated upgrade process actually runs and its PRs get merged.","Hash checking defends against tampered or substituted artifacts, but breaks when the lock lacks a hash for the wheel another platform needs, when an internal mirror rebuilds or re-packages files (new bytes, new hash), or for direct URL and VCS dependencies pip cannot hash-check; in those cases verify at the mirror or pin by commit instead.","A universal lock serves every developer OS and CI image from one file, but may fail to resolve packages that only exist for some platforms; a Linux-only lock is simpler when deployment is the only target.","Tight upper bounds in a library protect users from untested majors, but cause unresolvable conflicts in applications; for libraries, testing against new releases quickly usually beats capping."],"pitfalls":["Not committing the lockfile, or adding it to `.gitignore`; the symptom is \"works locally, fails in CI\" with different transitive versions in each environment.","Installing with `pip install .` in Docker while the lock sits unused in the repo; the symptom is images that contain versions which appear nowhere in the lockfile.","Pinning exact versions in a published library's dependencies; the symptom is user bug reports saying your package cannot be installed alongside another common package.","Locking but never upgrading; the symptom is a security scan listing many known vulnerabilities whose fixes shipped months ago, and an eventual upgrade that changes dozens of packages at once.","Running `uv lock` (or `poetry lock`) locally with a different tool version than CI uses; the symptom is lock diffs that rewrite many lines (format revision, metadata) with no dependency change, burying the one real change in review noise. Pin the tool version alongside the lock.","Reading `~=1.4` as \"1.4.x only\"; the symptom is a 1.9 release appearing in an upgrade PR that someone assumed the range excluded. Write `~=1.4.0` or `>=1.4,<1.5` when you mean patch releases only.","Treating the Python lock as full reproducibility; the symptom is identical locks giving different results because the base image, CUDA runtime or model revision changed underneath."],"when":"Lock every application, service, notebook environment that feeds production, and evaluation harness, because\neval results are only comparable when the environment is fixed. Do not lock a library's published\ndependencies; lock its development environment for CI instead. Install with a check that the lock matches\nthe declared dependencies (`uv sync --locked`, `poetry install` with a check that the lock is current) and\nwith hash verification where your tooling allows. Measure lock age and the number of packages behind\nlatest, and schedule upgrades. In interviews, the expected answer separates application from library policy\nand explains what hashes protect against.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Commits the lock, reads lock diffs in upgrade PRs, and adds a narrowly scoped temporary bound with a ticket when an upstream release breaks something.","architect":"Defines organization-wide policy: locking for deployables, upgrade cadence, hash verification, approved indexes, and how base images and model revisions are pinned alongside.","fde":"Reproduces the customer's exact environment from their lock or image when debugging, and ships locked, mirror-compatible requirements that pass their artifact review."},"check":{"q":"You maintain both an internal Python library used by several services and one of those services.\nHow should version constraints differ between them, and what goes wrong if you use the service's policy for\nthe library?","a":"The service is a leaf: it locks every package exactly, with hashes, and installs only from the lock,\nbecause reproducibility of what runs matters most. The library is installed alongside other services'\ndependencies, so it declares ranges covering versions it is tested against, perhaps with conservative bounds,\nand locks only its own CI environment. If the library pinned exact versions, every consuming service would\nbe forced onto those versions, and any service needing a different version of a shared package (an SDK,\npydantic, httpx) would hit an unresolvable conflict."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does hash checking in a lockfile protect against that exact version pins alone do not?","options":["A dependency releasing a new minor version","Receiving different file contents under the same package name and version","Resolution conflicts between transitive dependencies","Packages that require a newer Python version"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A version number names a release; a hash identifies the exact bytes. If an index or mirror serves a tampered or substituted file, the hash no longer matches and installation fails instead of proceeding."},{"q":"Why should a widely used library avoid exact pins in its declared dependencies?","options":["Exact pins slow down import time","PyPI rejects packages with exact pins","Exact pins disable hash checking for users","Applications installing it with other packages may be unable to find a version set that satisfies everyone"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Every package in an environment must agree on one version of each dependency. A library's exact pins become everyone's constraints, causing unresolvable conflicts; applications are the right place to lock."},{"q":"Two builds from the same commit and the same lockfile give different model outputs. Which is a plausible cause?","options":["The lockfile hashes were ignored, so packages differed","Something outside the Python lock changed, such as the base image, CUDA runtime or model revision","Lockfiles do not pin transitive dependencies","The resolver re-ran and chose different versions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A lock pins Python packages, including transitive ones, but not the interpreter build, system libraries, drivers or model weights. Those need their own pins, such as image digests and model revisions."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","sources":["Official docs: pip-audit (PyPA)","Official docs: Bandit (PyCQA)","Official docs: OSV (Open Source Vulnerabilities) database"],"why":"An AI service is mostly third-party code: an LLM SDK, an HTTP stack, a web framework, tokenizers, parsers for\nPDFs and HTML, often torch and transformers. Vulnerabilities are disclosed in packages like these regularly,\nand an exact lockfile means you keep running the vulnerable version until someone notices. Nobody reads\nadvisories for a hundred transitive packages by hand, so without automation the gap between \"fix published\"\nand \"fix deployed\" is unbounded.\n\nYour own code adds a second class of risk that AI work makes more common. Model artifacts are loaded with\npickle-based formats that execute code on load; LLM output is tempting to pass to `eval`, a shell or SQL;\nprototype code hard-codes API keys; `yaml.load` and `trust_remote_code=True` slip in from tutorials. Code\nreview catches some of this, inconsistently. The naive approach, \"we will review carefully and update\ndependencies when we remember\", fails because both problems scale with code volume and dependency count,\nwhile attention does not. Cheap, deterministic scanners on every pull request close the obvious holes.","how":"### pip-audit: known vulnerabilities in dependencies\npip-audit, maintained under PyPA, takes a set of installed or declared packages (the current environment,\n`-r requirements.txt`, or a project directory), resolves exact versions, and queries a vulnerability service\n(PyPI's advisory data by default, or OSV with `-s osv`) for advisories affecting each version. It reports the\npackage, version, advisory ID and the fixed versions, exits non-zero when anything is found (so CI fails), and\ncan emit JSON or CycloneDX SBOM output. `--fix` upgrades to fixed versions where possible; `--ignore-vuln ID`\nrecords a deliberate, reviewed exception.\n\nThe design depends on exact versions, *because* advisories are expressed as affected version ranges. That is\nwhy auditing a lockfile or exported pinned requirements is meaningful, while auditing loose ranges in\n`pyproject.toml` is not: the answer depends on what would be installed. Auditing a lockfile is also how you\nfind vulnerable transitive packages you never chose directly.\n\n### Triage: what to do with a finding\nA finding is the start of a decision, not the decision. A useful order:\n1. **Is a fixed version available?** If yes, upgrade *through the lock*: `uv lock --upgrade-package jinja2`\n   (or the Poetry or pip-compile equivalent), then sync. `pip-audit --fix` upgrades the environment it\n   is pointed at, which in a locked project just creates drift that the next sync erases.\n2. **Is it transitive?** If the vulnerable package is pulled in by another dependency whose declared range\n   excludes the fixed version, the real fix is upgrading the parent. If that is not yet possible, a\n   constraint or override that forces the fixed version is a stopgap you must test, *because* you are\n   now running the parent against a version it did not declare support for.\n3. **Is the vulnerable path reachable?** Read the advisory: a flaw in a template sandbox does not matter if\n   you never render untrusted templates, while one in the HTTP parser of your public endpoint does.\n   Unreachable is a reason to *defer* with a written justification, not to ignore forever.\n4. **Record the exception.** `--ignore-vuln ID` with the reason and an expiry or review date next to it, so\n   the suppression is visible in review and revisited when a fix ships.\n\n### Bandit: risky patterns in your code\nBandit parses each Python file into an abstract syntax tree and runs plugins that match known-dangerous\nconstructs, each with a test ID, severity and confidence. Examples: `B101` assert used (asserts vanish under\n`python -O`, so they must not enforce security), `B301` pickle deserialization, `B307` `eval`, `B506`\nunsafe `yaml.load`, `B602` subprocess with `shell=True`, `B105` hard-coded password strings, `B608` SQL built\nby string formatting. Run `bandit -r src -ll` to report medium severity and above, remembering that some\nuseful checks are rated low (B105 and B101 among them) and disappear under that floor. B105 is also a\nname heuristic: it fires on literals assigned to names containing words such as `password`, `secret` or\n`token`, so `API_KEY = \"sk-...\"` slips past it, which is one more reason to run a real secret scanner. Configure skips per\nproject (passing the config file explicitly), and suppress a reviewed line with `# nosec B602` plus a\nreason. Ruff implements many of the same checks as its `S` rules (from flake8-bandit), which lets teams run\nthem inside the linter they already have.\n\nBandit is fast and predictable *because* it matches syntax rather than tracking data flow. The cost is that\nit cannot tell whether the string reaching `subprocess` came from a constant or from a user, so it produces\nfalse positives, and it misses vulnerabilities that do not have a recognizable syntactic shape.\n\n### What neither tool covers\n- **Malicious or typosquatted packages** that have no CVE: mitigated by locks with hashes, approved\n  indexes or mirrors, and reviewing new dependencies.\n- **Logic and authorization bugs**, prompt injection, data leakage between tenants: design and review issues.\n- **Secrets already committed**: dedicated secret scanners (gitleaks, trufflehog) search history, which\n  Bandit's string heuristics do not.\n- **Model artifacts**: prefer safetensors over pickle-based checkpoints, pin model revisions, and avoid\n  `trust_remote_code=True` for unreviewed repositories.\n- **Reachability**: a vulnerable function may be present but never called; tools such as OSV-Scanner,\n  Dependabot alerts, Safety or Snyk differ in databases and in triage features, so choose by workflow.","example":"Code a Bandit run would flag, the safer equivalent, and the CI commands. The scenario is an agent that loads\na classifier, runs a shell tool and stores results.\n\n```python\nimport pickle, sqlite3, subprocess, shlex\nimport yaml\n\nAPI_TOKEN = \"sk-live-123\"                                # B105 (low severity): hard-coded secret -> env/secrets\nclf = pickle.load(open(\"router.pkl\", \"rb\"))              # B301: executes code from an untrusted file\ncfg = yaml.load(open(\"agent.yaml\"), Loader=yaml.Loader)  # B506: full loader can build arbitrary objects\n\ndef run_tool(cmd_from_llm: str) -> str:                  # B602: model output reaches a shell\n    return subprocess.run(cmd_from_llm, shell=True, capture_output=True, text=True).stdout\n\ndef save(db: sqlite3.Connection, user: str, answer: str) -> None:\n    db.execute(f\"INSERT INTO answers VALUES ('{user}', '{answer}')\")   # B608: SQL injection\n\n# Safer versions\nALLOWED = {\"ls\", \"wc\"}\ndef run_tool_safe(cmd_from_llm: str) -> str:\n    argv = shlex.split(cmd_from_llm)                     # no shell: no ;, |, $(...) expansion\n    if not argv or argv[0] not in ALLOWED:               # arguments (paths) still need validating\n        raise PermissionError(f\"tool not allowed: {argv[:1]}\")\n    return subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10).stdout\n\ndef save_safe(db: sqlite3.Connection, user: str, answer: str) -> None:\n    db.execute(\"INSERT INTO answers VALUES (?, ?)\", (user, answer))\n\ncfg_safe = yaml.safe_load(open(\"agent.yaml\"))\n# Weights: store as safetensors; for any pickle-based checkpoint, load only from trusted, pinned sources.\n\n# CI (fails the build on findings):\n#   uv export --format requirements-txt --no-hashes --no-emit-project > req.txt\n#   pip-audit -r req.txt --no-deps --disable-pip   # export is already fully resolved; skip pip install\n#   bandit -r src -ll                              # medium+ only: hides B105/B101, so run -l periodically\n```","tradeoffs":["Failing CI on any pip-audit finding keeps dependencies current, but blocks unrelated work when an advisory has no fix yet; allow time-boxed, reviewed `--ignore-vuln` entries rather than disabling the check.","Bandit catches common dangerous calls with near-zero setup, but its syntax-only view yields false positives; tune with severity thresholds and justified `nosec` comments, or move to a data-flow tool such as Semgrep or CodeQL when signal-to-noise becomes the bottleneck.","Running scanners on every pull request catches problems early, but new advisories appear for code that has not changed; also schedule a daily or weekly audit of the deployed lockfile.","Using Ruff's `S` rules avoids another tool, but may not match Bandit's full plugin set and configuration; pick one and keep the rule list explicit."],"pitfalls":["Auditing `pyproject.toml` ranges or the developer's environment instead of the lock that ships; the symptom is a clean report while the production image contains a flagged version.","Blanket `# nosec` or skipping whole test IDs to make the build green; the symptom is a security review finding `shell=True` on model output that the scanner had reported months earlier.","Treating a clean pip-audit report as \"no supply-chain risk\"; the symptom is surprise when a malicious lookalike package with no CVE is found in the dependency tree.","Assuming Bandit understands context; the symptom is the team learning to ignore its output because findings in tests and constant strings drown real ones, which a config excluding tests and a severity floor prevents.","Running `pip-audit --fix` in a project managed by a lockfile; the symptom is a green local audit followed by the same finding in CI, because the fix changed the environment but never reached the lock.","Loading pickle-based model files from a public hub because \"it is just weights\"; the symptom is arbitrary code execution on the inference host, which no dependency scanner would report."],"when":"Add both checks to CI as soon as a project has a lockfile and code that will run with real data or\ncredentials; for AI services that is usually the first deployment. Run pip-audit against the exact lock on\nevery PR and on a schedule, and Bandit (or Ruff `S` rules) on source directories with tests excluded or\ntreated leniently. Track open findings, time from advisory to fix deployed, and the count of suppressions.\nIn regulated customer environments, the JSON or CycloneDX output often becomes audit evidence. In interviews,\nthe strong answer explains what each tool can and cannot see rather than listing tools.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Fixes or justifies each finding, replaces pickle, `eval` and `shell=True` patterns, and keeps suppressions narrow with a reason next to them.","architect":"Sets the policy: which scanners gate merges, severity thresholds, exception expiry, approved indexes and model-artifact formats.","fde":"Produces SBOMs and audit reports in the format the customer's security team requires, and adapts scanning to their private mirrors and air-gapped build systems."},"check":{"q":"Your service passes pip-audit and Bandit with zero findings. A security reviewer still blocks the\nlaunch, citing the agent's ability to download and load community fine-tuned models. Is the reviewer right?","a":"Yes. pip-audit only knows published advisories for Python packages, and Bandit only matches syntax in\nour source; neither inspects model artifacts. Pickle-based checkpoints, and repositories loaded with\n`trust_remote_code=True`, execute code chosen by whoever published the model, so loading community models is\nremote code execution by design unless constrained. Mitigations: accept only safetensors, pin model revisions\nor checksums, allowlist sources, scan or review artifacts, and run loading in a sandbox with minimal\ncredentials. Clean scanner output narrows risk; it does not certify the system."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should pip-audit run against the lockfile rather than the version ranges in pyproject.toml?","options":["pip-audit cannot parse pyproject.toml files","Advisories apply to specific versions, so only the exact resolved set says what is actually vulnerable","Ranges are always newer than the lockfile and therefore safe","The lockfile contains the vulnerability database"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vulnerability data is expressed as affected version ranges. A range like httpx>=0.27 could install many versions; the lock records exactly what ships, including transitive packages, so the audit is meaningful."},{"q":"What kind of problem is Bandit structurally unable to detect well?","options":["subprocess calls with shell=True","use of pickle.load","whether a value reaching a dangerous call came from untrusted input across functions","hard-coded password-like string literals"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bandit matches patterns in each file's syntax tree; it does not track data flow. It flags the dangerous call itself, but cannot tell a constant from user-controlled input reaching it, hence false positives."},{"q":"A team wants to defend against a typosquatted package with no CVE. Which measure helps most?","options":["Running pip-audit more frequently","Lowering Bandit's severity threshold","Reviewing new dependencies and installing only from a lockfile with hashes from an approved index","Adding nosec comments to import statements"],"answer":2,"explanation":"pip-audit only reports known advisories, and Bandit scans your code, not packages. Controlling what enters the lock, and verifying hashes from trusted sources, is what stops an unknown malicious package."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pytest","sources":["Official docs: pytest","Okken, 2022 — Python Testing with pytest (2nd ed.)"],"why":"Python's standard `unittest` is a port of JUnit. Every test lives in a `TestCase` subclass, every\nassertion is a method (`assertEqual`, `assertIn`, `assertRaises`, dozens more), and setup is\n`setUp`/`tearDown` per class with no way to compose shared resources. A failing `assertEqual(a, b)`\nprints `3 != 4` and nothing about where 3 came from. Writing a plain `assert a == b` instead gives you\neven less: Python's own `AssertionError` carries no message at all.\n\nThe cost of that friction is not aesthetic. AI code is full of small pure functions that need many\ncases: a chunker with boundary conditions, a prompt builder that must escape user text, a parser for\nmodel output that may or may not be fenced JSON, a tool dispatcher that must reject malformed\narguments, a retry wrapper that must stop after N attempts. Each needs a table of inputs and expected\noutputs, and each needs some shared, expensive setup: a tokenizer loaded once, a fake LLM, a temporary\ndirectory, a container. If the table costs a class per row and the setup costs a method per class,\npeople write three tests where they needed thirty.\n\npytest's design answers both problems. It rewrites `assert` so a bare comparison yields a useful\nfailure, and it treats setup as dependency injection: a test declares what it needs as parameters,\nand the runner builds and tears it down with the right lifetime. Everything else, from async support\nto parallelism, arrives as plugins through the same hook mechanism.","how":"### Discovery and collection\n`pytest` walks `testpaths` (or the current directory), imports files matching `test_*.py` or\n`*_test.py`, and collects functions prefixed `test_` and methods of classes prefixed `Test` that have\nno `__init__`. It also collects `unittest.TestCase` subclasses, which is how a legacy suite migrates\nincrementally. Collection happens before any test runs, so a syntax error anywhere fails the whole\nsession early, and `--collect-only` shows the exact test ids that would run.\n\n### Assertion rewriting\nThe useful failure messages come from an import hook. When pytest imports a test module (and\n`conftest.py` files, and any package registered with `pytest.register_assert_rewrite`), it rewrites the\nmodule's AST so each `assert expr` becomes code that evaluates the sub-expressions, keeps their values,\nand builds a message on failure. That is why `assert len(chunks) == n` fails with\n`assert 4 == 3` and ` +  where 4 = len([...])`. It is done at import time rather than with tracing\n*because* tracing would slow every test and could not see inside a single expression; it is limited to\ntest modules *because* changing bytecode in production code would be a surprising side effect. The\nrewritten modules are cached as `.pyc` files tagged with the pytest version.\n\n### Fixtures\nA fixture is a function decorated with `@pytest.fixture`. A test that names it as a parameter gets its\nreturn value; pytest resolves the dependency graph (fixtures can depend on fixtures) per test. Scope\ncontrols lifetime: `function` (default, rebuilt per test), `class`, `module`, `package`, `session`. A\n`yield` inside the fixture splits setup from teardown, and teardown runs even when the test fails.\nFixtures declared in a `conftest.py` are visible to every test in that directory tree without\nimports, which is how a project shares its `fake_llm` or `db` fixture. Built-ins cover the common\nneeds: `tmp_path` (fresh directory), `monkeypatch` (undoable attribute and env-var changes), `capsys`\nand `caplog` (captured stdout and log records), `request` (introspection of the calling test).\nThe rule that a higher-scoped fixture cannot depend on a lower-scoped one exists *because* a\nsession-wide object cannot be rebuilt once per test; violating it raises `ScopeMismatch` at setup.\n\nThree more rules explain most fixture surprises. Teardown runs in reverse order of setup, *because* a\nfixture's dependencies must still exist while it cleans up (the client closes before the server it\ntalks to stops). `autouse=True` applies a fixture to every test in its scope without being named,\nwhich suits \"always block real network calls\" or \"always reset settings\", and also makes it invisible\nto the reader of a test. And a fixture with the same name in a nearer `conftest.py` (or in the test\nmodule) shadows the outer one, so `tests/integration/conftest.py` can replace the root `llm` fake with\na real client for that directory alone.\n\n### Parametrization\n`@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"raw,expected\", cases, ids=[...])` expands one function into one test per\nrow, each with its own id such as `test_parse[fenced-json]`. Stacking two decorators yields the\nCartesian product. `pytest.param(..., marks=pytest.mark.xfail)` marks a single row. Fixtures can be\nparametrized too (`params=`), which runs every dependent test once per parameter: a `store` fixture\nparametrized over `[\"memory\", \"pgvector\"]` runs the whole store suite against both backends.\n\n### Markers and selection\n`@pytest.mark.skip`, `skipif`, and `xfail(strict=True)` document known gaps; custom markers such as\n`integration` or `e2e` are registered in configuration and selected with `-m \"not e2e\"`. The\n`--strict-markers` option turns a typo in a marker name into an error, because an unregistered marker\notherwise produces only a `PytestUnknownMarkWarning` that is easy to miss in the summary, and the\nmisspelled test is not matched by `-m` so it runs in the wrong lane. `-k` selects by substring or\nboolean expression over test names, `-x` stops at the first failure, `--lf` reruns only the last\nfailures, `--durations=10` lists the slowest tests.\n\n### Configuration and plugins\nSettings live under `[tool.pytest.ini_options]` in `pyproject.toml`: `testpaths`, `addopts`,\n`markers`, `filterwarnings`. Plugins are ordinary packages that implement `pytest_*` hook functions\nand register an entry point; `conftest.py` can implement the same hooks locally. The ones an AI\nengineer meets weekly: `pytest-asyncio` (coroutine tests), `pytest-xdist` (`-n auto` distributes tests\nacross one worker process per CPU core), `pytest-cov` (coverage), `pytest-timeout` (kill hung tests),\n`pytest-mock` (the `mocker` fixture), and Hypothesis, which registers its own settings and database\nhandling. xdist is the one that changes fixture semantics: each worker is a separate process with its\nown session, so a \"session\" fixture that starts a container runs once *per worker*, and any test that\nrelies on another test's side effects breaks because the two may land on different workers.","example":"A chunker test file showing a session-scoped fixture from `conftest.py`, parametrized cases with\nreadable ids, and `pytest.raises`. The chunker here counts whitespace tokens so the arithmetic in the\nids is checkable by hand.\n\n```python\n# tests/conftest.py\nimport pytest\nfrom myapp.chunking import Chunker\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef chunker():                              # built once per run: construction is the slow part\n    return Chunker(max_tokens=50, overlap=5)\n\n# tests/test_chunking.py\nimport pytest\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"text,n_chunks\", [\n    (\"\", 0),\n    (\"short\", 1),\n    (\"word \" * 120, 3),                     # windows 0-49, 45-94, 90-119 -> three chunks\n], ids=[\"empty\", \"single\", \"overlapping\"])\ndef test_chunk_count(chunker, text, n_chunks):\n    assert len(chunker.split(text)) == n_chunks\n\ndef test_every_chunk_respects_limit(chunker):\n    chunks = chunker.split(\"word \" * 1000)\n    assert all(chunker.count_tokens(c) <= 50 for c in chunks)\n\ndef test_overlap_is_shared_tokens(chunker):\n    text = \" \".join(f\"w{i}\" for i in range(120))   # distinct tokens, so overlap is observable\n    first, second = chunker.split(text)[:2]\n    assert first.split()[-5:] == second.split()[:5] == [\"w45\", \"w46\", \"w47\", \"w48\", \"w49\"]\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"bad\", [None, 42, b\"bytes\"])\ndef test_rejects_non_text(chunker, bad):\n    with pytest.raises(TypeError):\n        chunker.split(bad)\n```\n\nRun `pytest -q tests/test_chunking.py` and a wrong overlap implementation fails as\n`test_chunk_count[overlapping]` with `assert 4 == 3` followed by `where 4 = len([...])`, which tells\nyou the case and the value without a debugger. The overlap test uses distinct tokens on purpose: with\n`\"word \" * 120` every window looks identical and the assertion would pass even with zero overlap.\n`pytest -k overlap` runs just the two test ids that contain the word: `test_chunk_count[overlapping]`\nand `test_overlap_is_shared_tokens`.","tradeoffs":["Fixture injection by name is concise, but the implicit wiring through `conftest.py` hides where a value comes from; in a large suite you pay with `--fixtures` lookups and the occasional surprise override from a nearer `conftest.py`.","Assertion rewriting makes bare `assert` informative, but it only applies to modules pytest imports; helper assertions in a non-test package get the plain `AssertionError` unless you register them.","Session-scoped fixtures save minutes when the resource is a loaded model or a container, but any mutable state they hold leaks between tests; the saving flips into flakiness the moment one test writes to the shared object.","Plugins give you parallelism, coverage and async support for free, but each adds configuration interactions (xdist and session fixtures, asyncio loop scopes) that you must understand when a test passes alone and fails in the suite.","`autouse` fixtures guarantee a safety net (no real network, clean settings) in every test, but hide setup from the reader; keep them for invariants the whole suite must obey, and name a fixture explicitly whenever only some tests need it."],"pitfalls":["Running tests in the wrong lane because a marker was misspelled; the symptom is an `e2e` test hitting a real API on every pull request. `--strict-markers` turns this into a collection error.","Tests that pass in isolation and fail together, usually a shared fixture mutated by an earlier test; `pytest-randomly` shuffles the order and prints its seed, `--randomly-seed=<n>` replays that order, and running the suspected pair alone confirms the dependence.","Asserting on floating-point results with `==`; embedding similarities and loss values differ in the last bits across BLAS builds. The fix is `pytest.approx(expected, rel=1e-6)`.","Catching too broadly with `pytest.raises(Exception)`, so a `TypeError` from a typo passes as the intended `ValueError`; use the specific class and `match=` on the message.","Leaving `print` debugging in tests and wondering where it went: pytest captures stdout and only shows it for failures. `-s` disables capture; `capsys` asserts on it."],"when":"Use pytest for every Python test, including the unittest-style classes you inherit; there is no\nscenario in modern Python where a different runner is the better default. What you decide is how to\nstructure the suite: which fixtures live in the root `conftest.py` (fake LLM, settings override,\ntemporary directory layout) versus a package-local one, which resources deserve session scope, and\nwhich markers split fast unit tests from slower integration and model-calling lanes. Measure the\nsuite's wall time with `--durations` and keep the pull-request lane under a few minutes, because a\nslow suite is one people stop running. In interviews, the expected depth is being able to explain\nassertion rewriting, fixture scopes and the `conftest.py` lookup, and to sketch a parametrized test\nfor a parser of model output on the spot.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes parametrized tests for chunkers, prompt builders and output parsers, keeps a `fake_llm` fixture in `conftest.py`, and splits lanes with markers so unit tests stay fast.","architect":"Sets the suite's shape: which dependencies are allowed in the unit lane, the marker taxonomy, time budgets per lane, and the plugins the whole organisation standardises on.","fde":"Lands inside a customer repo with an unfamiliar suite and uses `--collect-only`, `--fixtures` and `-k` to find the tests covering the integration they are changing before touching code."},"check":{"q":"A colleague argues that `unittest` is \"good enough\" and pytest is just syntax sugar. Using the\nmechanics rather than taste, what does pytest provide that changes how a test suite is built?","a":"Two mechanisms, not sugar. First, assertion rewriting: pytest rewrites the AST of `assert`\nin test modules at import time so a bare comparison reports the intermediate values, which removes\nthe need for a dozen assertion methods and makes failures diagnosable without a debugger. Second,\nfixtures as dependency injection with scopes: a test names what it needs, pytest resolves a graph of\nfixtures with function, module or session lifetimes and runs teardown after `yield`, so expensive\nresources (a loaded tokenizer, a container) are built once and composed rather than duplicated in\n`setUp` methods. Add parametrization, which turns a table of cases into individually identifiable\ntests, and a hook system that lets plugins own concerns like async loops and parallel workers. Those\nchange the economics of writing tests, which is why suites built on pytest have more cases."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can pytest print the values inside a failing plain `assert` statement?","options":["Python's `AssertionError` always carries the evaluated sub-expressions","pytest rewrites the `assert` statements of test modules at import time into code that records intermediate values","pytest runs every test under `sys.settrace` and reconstructs the expression from the trace","The values come from the fixture that produced them, which pytest tracks by name"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Plain Python raises a bare `AssertionError`. pytest's import hook rewrites the AST of `assert` in collected test modules so the failure message includes each operand; tracing would be far too slow and could not see inside a single expression."},{"q":"A session-scoped fixture declares a dependency on a function-scoped fixture. What happens?","options":["It works; the function-scoped fixture is rebuilt for each test and injected into the shared one","It works but pytest emits a warning and caches the first value","pytest raises a `ScopeMismatch` error at setup time","The session fixture is silently downgraded to function scope"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A session-wide object is built once and cannot be rebuilt per test, so it cannot depend on something that changes per test. pytest refuses the graph with `ScopeMismatch` rather than guessing a lifetime."},{"q":"A test function carries two stacked `parametrize` decorators with 3 and 4 cases. How many tests run?","options":["4, because the inner decorator takes precedence","7, one per case across both decorators","12, the Cartesian product of the two case lists","It fails at collection because stacked parametrization is not allowed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each `parametrize` multiplies the existing set of test instances by its own cases, so stacking produces every combination. Use a single decorator with explicit tuples when only specific pairs are meaningful."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pytest-asyncio","sources":["Official docs: pytest-asyncio","Official docs: Python asyncio"],"why":"AI services are mostly I/O: a request fans out to an LLM, a vector store, a reranker and a database,\nusually through `httpx.AsyncClient`, async database drivers and `asyncio.gather`. The interesting bugs\nlive in that async code: a semaphore that does not actually cap concurrency, a timeout that leaves the\nunderlying request running, a cancelled stream that never closes its connection, a retry that swallows\n`CancelledError`. You cannot test those properties by calling the sync wrappers, because the bug only\nexists while the event loop is scheduling tasks.\n\nThe naive approach is to write `def test_x(): asyncio.run(main())` in every test. It works for one\ncoroutine, but it creates and destroys a loop per call, gives you no way to write async setup and\nteardown that the test shares (an async client opened before the test and closed after it), and\ncannot share an expensive async resource such as a connection pool across tests. Writing `async def\ntest_x()` without a plugin is worse: pytest has no idea how to run it. Older pytest versions warned\nand skipped such tests; recent versions fail them. Either way, the plugin is the piece that turns a\ncoroutine into a test run.","how":"### What the plugin hooks into\npytest-asyncio registers hooks that recognise coroutine test functions and async fixtures. For a test\nit treats as async, it obtains an event loop for the configured scope, then drives the coroutine to\ncompletion on that loop and reports the outcome as an ordinary pass, fail or error. Async fixtures\n(plain `async def` returning a value, or an async generator using `yield`) are wrapped the same way:\nthe part before `yield` runs on the loop as setup, the part after runs on the same loop as teardown,\nbecause closing an async client on a different loop than the one it was opened on fails.\n\n### Strict vs auto mode\nThe plugin has two modes, set with `asyncio_mode` in pytest config.\n- **strict** (the default): only tests marked `@pytest.mark.asyncio` run as asyncio tests, and async\n  fixtures must be declared with `@pytest_asyncio.fixture`. It exists because a project may use\n  several async frameworks (asyncio, trio via anyio) and the plugin should not claim tests it does not\n  own.\n- **auto**: every `async def` test is treated as an asyncio test and plain `@pytest.fixture` works on\n  async functions. It removes boilerplate in a project that is asyncio-only.\n\n### Loop scope\nEach async test runs on a loop with a scope: function (a fresh loop per test, the default), class,\nmodule, package or session. A fresh loop isolates tests, because leftover tasks and callbacks from\none test cannot leak into the next. A wider loop is needed when an async fixture with a wide scope (a\nsession-wide database pool) must be used by tests, because those tests have to run on the same loop\nthe pool was created on. Recent versions express this with `loop_scope` arguments on the marker and\non `pytest_asyncio.fixture`, plus a default-fixture-loop-scope config key; older versions used an\noverridable `event_loop` fixture, which has been deprecated and removed. Check the docs for the\nversion you have pinned, because this API changed more than once.\n\n### What it does not do\nThree misconceptions are worth killing early. First, async tests do not run concurrently with each\nother: pytest still executes one test at a time, and the plugin runs each test's coroutine to\ncompletion before the next starts, so a slow async suite is not sped up by being async (use\npytest-xdist for that). Concurrency exists only *inside* a test, among the tasks that test creates.\nSecond, the plugin gives you no fake clock: `asyncio.sleep(10)` really waits ten seconds, which is\nwhy timeout and retry tests either use tiny delays or inject the delay or clock as a parameter.\nThird, it does not clean up after you: tasks a test spawned and never awaited are still pending when\nthe loop closes, which surfaces as warnings or as errors attributed to a later test. To run on uvloop\nor another loop implementation, use the plugin's documented mechanism for your pinned version (an\nevent-loop policy fixture in recent releases; Python 3.14 deprecates loop policies, so expect this to\nmove again) rather than constructing and swapping loop objects yourself.\n\n### The alternative\nanyio ships its own pytest plugin (`@pytest.mark.anyio`) that can run the same test under asyncio and\ntrio. If your code is written against anyio (as Starlette and httpx internals are), that plugin is a\nreasonable alternative. Pick one per suite: with pytest-asyncio in auto mode both plugins can try to\nclaim the same coroutine tests, and the resulting errors are confusing to debug.","example":"A fan-out helper must never exceed a concurrency limit, and a timeout must leave no request in\nflight. Both properties only exist while the loop is running, so they need real async tests. A fake\nLLM records peak concurrency; the async fixture checks in teardown that nothing leaked.\n\n```python\n# pyproject.toml: [tool.pytest.ini_options] asyncio_mode = \"strict\"\nimport asyncio\nimport pytest\nimport pytest_asyncio\n\nclass FakeLLM:\n    def __init__(self, delay: float):\n        self.delay, self.in_flight, self.peak = delay, 0, 0\n    async def complete(self, prompt: str) -> str:\n        self.in_flight += 1\n        self.peak = max(self.peak, self.in_flight)\n        try:\n            await asyncio.sleep(self.delay)\n            return prompt.upper()\n        finally:\n            self.in_flight -= 1          # runs on success and on cancellation\n\nasync def fan_out(llm, prompts, limit: int):\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(limit)\n    async def one(p):\n        async with sem:\n            return await llm.complete(p)\n    return await asyncio.gather(*(one(p) for p in prompts))\n\n@pytest_asyncio.fixture\nasync def llm():\n    client = FakeLLM(delay=0.01)\n    yield client\n    assert client.in_flight == 0          # teardown, same loop as the test\n\n@pytest.mark.asyncio\nasync def test_fan_out_respects_limit(llm):\n    out = await fan_out(llm, [f\"p{i}\" for i in range(20)], limit=4)\n    assert out == [f\"P{i}\" for i in range(20)]   # gather preserves order\n    assert llm.peak == 4\n\n@pytest.mark.asyncio\nasync def test_timeout_cancels_inner_call(llm):\n    llm.delay = 10\n    with pytest.raises(TimeoutError):\n        async with asyncio.timeout(0.05):        # Python 3.11+\n            await llm.complete(\"x\")\n    assert llm.in_flight == 0\n```\n\nThe suite runs in well under a second because the delays are tiny; a real limit bug shows up as\n`peak == 20`, and a timeout that does not cancel shows up as `in_flight == 1`.","tradeoffs":["Function-scoped loops isolate every test from leaked tasks, but force expensive async resources to be rebuilt per test; widen the loop scope only for the fixtures that are genuinely costly.","Auto mode removes markers and special fixture decorators, but claims every coroutine test, so it fits asyncio-only projects and breaks once anyio or trio tests appear in the same suite.","Real `asyncio.sleep` delays keep tests faithful to production timing, but make suites slow and timing-sensitive; injecting a clock or tiny delays flips that once the suite grows past a few minutes.","Testing through the real event loop catches scheduling bugs that sync wrappers hide, at the price of tests that are harder to read and debug; if the property under test does not depend on scheduling (a prompt builder that happens to be `async`), extract it and test it synchronously.","Session-scoped loops let one connection pool serve the whole suite, but one test's leaked task or closed client now poisons every later test; that trade flips to function scope as soon as failures start appearing only in particular test orders."],"pitfalls":["A session-scoped async fixture (pool, client) used by function-loop tests; the symptom is `RuntimeError` mentioning a Future or Task \"attached to a different loop\", often only in CI ordering.","Forgetting the marker or the `pytest_asyncio.fixture` decorator in strict mode. A missing marker makes recent pytest fail the test as an unsupported `async def` (older pytest warned and skipped it); a missing fixture decorator hands the test an un-awaited coroutine or async generator object instead of the value, so it fails on the first attribute access or with a \"never awaited\" warning.","Calling blocking code (`time.sleep`, sync HTTP, sync DB drivers) inside an async test or fixture; the test passes but serialises everything, so concurrency assertions are silently meaningless.","Spawning tasks with `asyncio.create_task` and never awaiting or cancelling them; the symptom is \"Task was destroyed but it is pending\" warnings and assertions failing in a later, unrelated test.","Copying an `event_loop` fixture override from an old blog post into a pinned recent version; on 0.2x releases the symptom is a deprecation warning, and on 1.x the plugin no longer consults that fixture at all, so your custom loop or policy silently stops applying."],"when":"Use pytest-asyncio whenever the unit under test is a coroutine and the property you care about\ndepends on scheduling: concurrency caps, timeouts, cancellation, streaming, ordering of `gather`\nresults, backpressure. Pure logic that happens to sit inside async code is better extracted into a\nsync function and tested without a loop. Pin the plugin version and set `asyncio_mode` and the default\nfixture loop scope explicitly in config, so behaviour does not change on upgrade. For FastAPI apps,\npair it with an async HTTP client against the ASGI app. In interviews, being able to explain the \"attached\nto a different loop\" error from first principles is a strong signal you understand both asyncio and\npytest fixtures.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async tests for fan-out, retry and streaming code, choosing loop scopes so shared pools work without leaking state between tests.","architect":"Sets the project-wide async testing convention: one async framework, pinned plugin, explicit mode and loop scope, and a rule that blocking calls in async code fail lint or review.","fde":"Reproduces a customer's intermittent timeout or hung stream as a small async test with a fake slow dependency, so the fix can be shown working before redeploying."},"check":{"q":"Your suite has a session-scoped async fixture that opens an `httpx.AsyncClient`. Tests pass\nwhen run one at a time but fail in CI with \"attached to a different loop\". What is happening and how\ndo you fix it?","a":"The client lives for the whole session, but each test by default runs on its own\nfunction-scoped loop that is closed when the test ends. The client's pooled connections are bound to\nwhichever loop was running when they were opened, typically the first test that made a request. The\nnext test pulls those keep-alive connections from the pool and awaits I/O tied to a dead loop. Run\nalone, only one test loop ever exists, so nothing is shared across loops and it passes. Fixes: run the\nfixture and the tests that use it on the same wider loop (`loop_scope=\"session\"` on both\n`pytest_asyncio.fixture` and the `asyncio` marker), or make the fixture function-scoped and accept\nthe cost, or create the client inside each test. The principle is that async resources are bound to\nthe loop they were made on, so fixture lifetime and loop lifetime must match."},"quiz":[{"q":"In pytest-asyncio's strict mode, what happens to an unmarked `async def` test?","options":["It is run automatically on a fresh event loop","The plugin does not claim it, so it is not run as an asyncio test","It runs on the session event loop instead of a function-scoped one","It is converted into a synchronous test by wrapping it in asyncio.run"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Strict mode only treats tests with `@pytest.mark.asyncio` as asyncio tests, so multiple async plugins can coexist; an unclaimed coroutine test is then failed (or, on older pytest, skipped) by pytest itself. Automatic claiming of every coroutine test is what auto mode does."},{"q":"Why does an async generator fixture run its teardown on the same loop as its setup?","options":["Because pytest requires all fixtures to share one thread","Because a new loop would rerun the setup code","Because async resources such as clients and pools are bound to the loop they were created on","Because teardown code is not allowed to await anything"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Closing a connection or awaiting a future from another loop raises errors, so setup, the test and teardown must all execute on one loop. Thread sharing is a separate concern from loop binding."},{"q":"A concurrency-limit test passes, yet production exceeds the limit. Which test flaw best explains it?","options":["The test used a function-scoped event loop","The fake dependency blocked with time.sleep, so calls never overlapped and peak concurrency stayed low","The test used auto mode instead of strict mode","The fixture was declared with pytest_asyncio.fixture"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A blocking sleep stops the loop from switching tasks, so the test never produced concurrent calls to count. Loop scope and plugin mode affect which loop runs the test, not whether calls overlap."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","sources":["Meszaros, 2007 — xUnit Test Patterns","Fowler, 2007 — Mocks Aren't Stubs","Official docs: unittest.mock","Official docs: pytest"],"why":"Code around a model talks to things you cannot afford to call in a unit test. A real LLM call costs\nmoney, takes hundreds of milliseconds to seconds, returns different text on each run, rate-limits you\nin CI, and needs a secret the CI runner may not have. A vector database needs a running server. A\npayment or ticketing API has side effects. If every test of your retry logic, prompt assembly or tool\ndispatch goes through those, the suite is slow, flaky and expensive, and people stop running it.\n\nYou also cannot easily make the real dependency misbehave on demand. The branches that matter most,\n\"the model returned an empty string\", \"the API timed out on the second attempt\", \"the tool returned\nmalformed JSON\", are exactly the ones a live service rarely produces when you want it to.\n\nThe naive fix, patching everything with `MagicMock` until the test goes green, trades one problem for\nanother. A bare mock accepts any attribute and any call, so a test can pass while the code calls a\nmethod that does not exist on the real client. And tests that assert on every internal call break on\nevery refactor even when behaviour is unchanged. The vocabulary of test doubles exists to pick the\nsmallest stand-in that verifies what you actually care about.","how":"### The kinds of double\nMeszaros's taxonomy, which most of the industry uses loosely:\n- **Dummy:** passed only to fill a parameter; never used. `client=None` when the path under test does\n  not touch the client.\n- **Stub:** returns canned answers. \"Whatever you are asked, reply `'{\"label\": \"spam\"}'`.\" Used to drive\n  the code down a branch. You assert on the code's output, not on the stub.\n- **Spy:** a stub that also records how it was called, so the test can check afterwards (\"was the\n  second attempt sent with a lower temperature?\").\n- **Mock:** pre-programmed with expectations about calls; the test fails if the interactions differ.\n  In Python, `unittest.mock.Mock` is technically a spy you assert on after the fact.\n- **Fake:** a working, simplified implementation: an in-memory vector store that really ranks by\n  cosine similarity, a dict-backed cache with real TTL logic, a model server that speaks the provider's\n  HTTP API. Fakes are more code, but they make tests read like usage and can be shared across suites.\n\nStrictly, a \"fake LLM\" that pops scripted replies off a list is a stub that also records calls (a\nspy); it has no behaviour of its own. Teams call it a fake anyway, and the label matters less than the\nquestion it answers: does this double *compute* anything, or only replay what the test told it?\n\nThe distinction matters because it decides what the test verifies. Stubs and fakes support\n**state verification**: run the code, check the result. Mocks and spies support **behaviour\nverification**: check the calls. State verification survives refactoring; behaviour verification is\nright only when the call *is* the behaviour (an email was sent, a tool was invoked with these\narguments, a charge was not made twice).\n\n### unittest.mock mechanics\n- `Mock` / `MagicMock` create attributes on access and record every call in `call_args_list`.\n  `MagicMock` also supports dunder methods (`len`, iteration, context managers). `AsyncMock` returns\n  awaitables, for async clients.\n- `return_value` and `side_effect` script behaviour; `side_effect` can be an exception (raised on every\n  call), a callable, or an iterable consumed one item per call in which any exception item is raised.\n  `side_effect=[TimeoutError(), \"ok\"]` is how you simulate \"timeout, then success\".\n- **Spec it.** `Mock(spec_set=RealClass)` rejects attributes the real class lacks (and assignment of\n  new ones); `create_autospec(RealClass, instance=True)` goes further and also checks call signatures\n  of the methods. Either matters because an unspecced mock will happily accept `client.compelte(...)`\n  and the typo ships.\n- `patch(\"pkg.module.name\")` replaces a name *where it is looked up*, not where it is defined. If\n  `app/summarize.py` does `from app.llm import call_llm`, you patch `app.summarize.call_llm`;\n  patching `app.llm.call_llm` changes nothing the code under test sees. The reason is mechanical:\n  `from x import y` copies a reference into the importing module's namespace at import time, and\n  `patch` only rebinds one namespace entry. `patch.object(obj, \"attr\")` avoids the string path entirely.\n- `Mock(wraps=real)` is Python's true spy: calls go through to the real object and are recorded, which\n  suits \"did the retry wrapper call the real tokenizer exactly once?\"\n\n### Fixtures\nA pytest fixture is a function whose return value (or `yield`ed value) is injected into any test that\nnames it as a parameter. Code after `yield` is teardown, and runs even if the test fails. Scope\n(`function`, `class`, `module`, `package`, `session`) controls how often it is rebuilt: wide scope for\nexpensive immutable things (a loaded tokenizer), function scope for anything a test can mutate.\nFixtures in `conftest.py` are shared by every test below that directory. A **factory fixture**\nreturns a function, so each test can build its own variant (\"an LLM that replies with these three\nstrings\"). Built-ins do common jobs: `monkeypatch` sets env vars and attributes and undoes them,\n`tmp_path` gives an isolated directory.\n\n### Prefer injection over patching\nIf a function takes its client as a parameter (or a constructor does), the test passes a fake and\nneeds no patching at all. Patching is the tool for code you cannot restructure; dependency injection\nis what makes doubles cheap.\n\n### Where to put the seam\nFor an LLM call there are three places to cut, and each decides what the test still exercises:\n1. **Your own interface** (`LLMClient.complete`): a fake replaces everything below it. Fastest and\n   simplest, but the SDK, request building, retries and response parsing are not tested at all.\n2. **The HTTP transport**: libraries such as `respx` (for httpx) or recorded cassettes (vcrpy,\n   pytest-recording) intercept requests below the SDK. The real SDK serialises your request and\n   parses a realistic response, so a wrong field name or a changed response shape fails here.\n3. **A fake server process**: the code under test talks to a real socket. This is the integration\n   level, covered in the integration-testing lesson.\nCut as high as the test allows, because higher seams are faster and less brittle; move the seam down\nonly for the code whose correctness depends on what is below it, such as response parsing.","example":"A summarizer retries once when the model returns empty output and truncates to a limit read from the\nenvironment. The tests use three kinds of double: a scripted stub-with-spy (what most teams call a fake\nLLM) for state checks, an autospecced mock for one interaction that matters, and `monkeypatch` for\nconfiguration.\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport pytest\nfrom unittest.mock import create_autospec\n\nclass LLMClient:                                   # the real client's interface\n    def complete(self, messages: list[dict], *, model: str) -> str: ...\n\ndef summarize(text: str, *, client, model: str = \"large\") -> str:\n    limit = int(os.environ.get(\"SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS\", \"500\"))\n    for _ in range(2):\n        out = client.complete([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Summarize: {text}\"}], model=model)\n        if out.strip():\n            return out[:limit]\n    raise RuntimeError(\"model returned empty output twice\")\n\nclass FakeLLM:                                     # stub + spy: scripted replies, records calls\n    def __init__(self, replies): self.replies, self.calls = list(replies), []\n    def complete(self, messages, *, model):\n        self.calls.append((messages, model))\n        return self.replies.pop(0)\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef make_llm():                                    # factory fixture\n    return lambda *replies: FakeLLM(replies)\n\ndef test_retries_once_on_empty(make_llm):\n    llm = make_llm(\"\", \"a summary\")\n    assert summarize(\"long text\", client=llm) == \"a summary\"\n    assert len(llm.calls) == 2\n\ndef test_gives_up_after_two_empties(make_llm):\n    with pytest.raises(RuntimeError):\n        summarize(\"x\", client=make_llm(\"\", \"  \"))\n\ndef test_forwards_model_name():\n    llm = create_autospec(LLMClient, instance=True)    # rejects calls the real class would reject\n    llm.complete.return_value = \"ok\"\n    summarize(\"text\", client=llm, model=\"small\")\n    assert llm.complete.call_args.kwargs[\"model\"] == \"small\"\n\ndef test_truncates_to_env_limit(monkeypatch, make_llm):\n    monkeypatch.setenv(\"SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS\", \"5\")       # undone after the test\n    assert summarize(\"x\", client=make_llm(\"abcdefgh\")) == \"abcde\"\n```\n\nNone of these tests hit the network, and none breaks if `summarize` changes how it builds its prompt.","tradeoffs":["Fakes make tests readable and refactor-proof, but are code you must maintain and can drift from the real service; pair them with a contract or integration test once drift has bitten you.","Mocks verify interactions precisely, but couple tests to implementation; they flip from liability to asset only when the interaction itself is the requirement, such as \"never call the payment API twice\".","Wide fixture scopes make suites fast, but let one test's mutation leak into another; use them only for objects nothing mutates, and function scope for everything else.","Patching lets you test code you cannot restructure, but hides the dependency; when you own the code, injecting the collaborator removes the need for patching entirely."],"pitfalls":["Unspecced `MagicMock` standing in for a client; the symptom is a green test while production raises `AttributeError` or `TypeError` because the method name or signature is wrong.","Patching the name where it is defined rather than where it is imported; the symptom is a test that still makes real network calls or fails with a missing API key despite the patch.","Asserting on every call and argument; the symptom is dozens of tests failing after a refactor that changed no behaviour, so the team learns to update assertions without reading them.","Stubbing the LLM with an idealised reply (perfect JSON, no fences, no preamble); the symptom is a parser that passes every test and fails on the first real response wrapped in markdown fences.","Mutating a session-scoped fixture inside a test; the symptom is tests that pass alone and fail depending on order, or only under parallel runs.","Using a plain `Mock` for an async client; awaiting its result raises `TypeError: object Mock can't be used in 'await' expression`, or, worse, code that never awaits passes silently. Use `AsyncMock` or autospec, which creates async methods for coroutine functions."],"when":"Use doubles at the boundary of your process: model APIs, HTTP services, databases, clocks and\nrandomness. Do not double your own pure functions or value objects; call them. Default to fakes and\nstubs with state assertions, reach for spec'd mocks when the call is the requirement, and record real\nmodel responses as stub data so your stubs look like what models actually return, including their\nmess. Every double is a claim about the real dependency, so back the important ones with a contract\nor integration test. In interviews, expect \"how do you test code that calls an LLM?\"; a good answer\nseparates deterministic logic tested with doubles from model quality measured with evals.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds a shared fake LLM client and factory fixtures so every test can script model replies, failures and timeouts without network access or keys.","architect":"Defines client interfaces and injection points so doubles are cheap, and decides which boundaries also need contract or integration tests to keep fakes honest.","fde":"Captures real responses from the customer's systems (sanitised) and turns them into stub data, so a failure seen on site becomes a fast local regression test."},"check":{"q":"A teammate's tests for an agent's tool dispatcher mock the LLM, the tool registry and every tool,\nthen assert the exact sequence of calls. They break weekly. What would you change, and what would you\nkeep?","a":"Those tests verify implementation, not behaviour, so any refactor of call order breaks them. I\nwould replace the mocked LLM with a scripted fake that emits specific tool-call messages, use real tool\nregistry code, and use fake tools that record what they received. Tests then assert outcomes: the right\ntool ran with validated arguments, malformed arguments were rejected, the final answer included the\ntool result. I would keep interaction assertions only where the call is the requirement, such as a\ndestructive tool never running without approval, and spec every remaining mock against the real class."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which double is best described as a working, simplified implementation?","options":["A stub that always returns the same canned response","A fake, such as an in-memory vector store that really ranks documents by similarity","A mock configured with expected calls","A dummy passed only to satisfy a parameter"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fake actually behaves like the dependency, just simplified. A stub only returns canned answers and does not model behaviour, which is why fakes read more like real usage in tests."},{"q":"`app/agent.py` does `from app.llm import call_llm`. Which patch target replaces it for code in agent.py?","options":["app.llm.call_llm","app.agent.call_llm","call_llm","app.llm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The import copied a reference into app.agent's namespace, so that is where the name is looked up. Patching app.llm.call_llm rebinds the original module's name, which agent.py no longer consults."},{"q":"Why prefer `create_autospec(RealClient, instance=True)` over a bare `MagicMock()` for a client?","options":["It runs faster than MagicMock","It automatically records real responses from the API","It rejects attributes and call signatures the real class does not have","It makes the test independent of the client's interface"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An autospecced double mirrors the real interface, so a typo or wrong argument fails the test. A bare MagicMock accepts anything, letting interface mistakes pass tests and fail in production."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/property-based-testing","sources":["Claessen & Hughes, 2000 — QuickCheck: A Lightweight Tool for Random Testing of Haskell Programs","MacIver et al., 2019 — Hypothesis: A new approach to property-based testing","Official docs: Hypothesis"],"why":"Example-based tests check the cases their author imagined. The author of a text chunker writes tests\nfor \"a paragraph\", \"exactly one chunk long\" and \"three chunks\", and the chunker passes. Then\nproduction hits an empty document, a string of combining characters, a chunk size of 1, or an overlap\nequal to the chunk size minus one, and it crashes or silently drops text. The bug was always there; the\nexample set simply never visited that part of the input space.\n\nAI glue code is dense with exactly this kind of function: tokenisation-adjacent splitting, prompt\ntemplating that must escape user text, parsers for model output, JSON round-trips for tool arguments,\ndeduplication, score normalisation, pagination over retrieval results. Their input spaces are huge and\ntheir correctness conditions are easy to state but hard to enumerate.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"generate random inputs in a loop\", finds some bugs but has two problems.\nUniform random data rarely hits the boundaries that matter (an empty list, zero, the maximum value, a\nlone surrogate), and when it does find a failure the input is a 1,400-character random string from\nwhich you must reverse-engineer the bug by hand. Property-based testing fixes both: generators are\nbiased toward edge cases, and failures are automatically shrunk to the smallest input that still fails.","how":"### Properties, not examples\nA property is a universally quantified claim about your code: for all valid inputs *x*, some predicate\nholds. Common shapes, roughly in order of how often they pay off:\n- **Round-trip:** `decode(encode(x)) == x` (JSON, serialisers, tokenizer encode/decode where lossless).\n- **Invariant:** the output always satisfies a condition (every chunk length ≤ size; scores in [0, 1];\n  output sorted; no duplicate IDs).\n- **Idempotence:** `f(f(x)) == f(x)` (normalisation, deduplication, cleaning).\n- **Oracle / differential:** a fast implementation agrees with a slow obviously-correct one, or the new\n  version agrees with the old one.\n- **Metamorphic:** a known change to the input produces a known change to the output (reordering\n  documents does not change the set retrieved).\n\n### Strategies\nA strategy describes how to generate values: `st.integers(0, 100)`, `st.text()`, `st.lists(...)`,\n`st.dictionaries(...)`, combined with `.map`, `.filter`, `st.one_of`, `st.recursive` and `st.builds` (call\na constructor with generated arguments). `st.from_type` derives a strategy from type hints, which works\nwith dataclasses and many annotated classes. `st.data()` lets a test draw values interactively, so one\nparameter can depend on another (an overlap smaller than the chunk size). Strategies deliberately\nover-sample edge cases: empty collections, boundary integers, unusual Unicode.\n\n### Running, shrinking and remembering\n`@given(...)` turns a test into a loop. By default Hypothesis runs about 100 examples per test (tunable\nwith `@settings(max_examples=...)`) and flags examples that run too long via a deadline setting. When an\nexample fails, Hypothesis shrinks it: it repeatedly tries simpler versions of the input and keeps any\nthat still fail. Unlike classic QuickCheck, which shrinks values by type-specific rules, Hypothesis\nshrinks the underlying sequence of random choices that produced the value. That is a design choice with\na real payoff: shrinking works automatically for any composed strategy, including ones with `.map` and\n`.filter`, without you writing a shrinker.\n\nFailing examples are saved in a local database (the `.hypothesis/` directory) and replayed first on the\nnext run, so a bug, once found, is checked every time until fixed. `@example(...)` pins a specific input\npermanently, which is how you turn a found counterexample into a documented regression case.\n`assume(cond)` discards inputs that do not meet a precondition; use it sparingly, because heavy\nfiltering makes generation slow and Hypothesis will report a health-check failure. The cheaper fix is\nalmost always to construct valid inputs directly (draw `size` first, then `overlap` below it) rather\nthan generate freely and throw most away.\n\n### Profiles: different budgets for different lanes\nThe example count is a dial between thoroughness and suite time, so set it per environment rather\nthan per test. `settings.register_profile(\"ci\", max_examples=1000)` and\n`settings.register_profile(\"dev\", max_examples=50)` in `conftest.py`, then select one with\n`--hypothesis-profile=ci` or `settings.load_profile(...)` from an environment variable. A CI profile\ncan also set `derandomize=True`, which makes the generated examples a fixed function of the test, so\na red build is reproducible and never caused by an unlucky draw; the price is that CI explores the\nsame inputs every run, which is why some teams keep a randomised nightly job alongside it.\n\n### Stateful testing\nFor objects with operations (a cache, a conversation memory, a rate limiter), the bug is often in a\n*sequence* of calls rather than in any single input. `RuleBasedStateMachine` lets you declare each\noperation as a `@rule` with its own argument strategies and each invariant as an `@invariant`.\nHypothesis then generates random programs (put, put, evict, get, ...), runs each against the real\nobject and a deliberately trivial model (a plain `dict` standing in for an LRU cache), and checks\nafter every step that the two agree. When they diverge, it shrinks the *program*, so a 40-step\nfailure is typically reported as the three or four calls that actually matter, such as \"insert at\ncapacity, read the oldest key, insert again\".","example":"A chunker for retrieval with overlap. A few properties pin down its contract: no chunk exceeds `size`,\nonly the last chunk may be short, consecutive chunks really share `overlap` characters, and dropping\neach later chunk's overlap rejoins the original text exactly. An earlier version used\n`range(0, len(text), step)`; Hypothesis failed it immediately and shrank the counterexample to\n`text=''`, where that version returned no chunks at all.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom hypothesis import given, strategies as st\n\ndef chunk(text: str, size: int, overlap: int) -> list[str]:\n    if not 0 <= overlap < size:\n        raise ValueError(\"need 0 <= overlap < size\")\n    step = size - overlap\n    return [text[i:i + size] for i in range(0, max(len(text) - overlap, 1), step)]\n\n@given(text=st.text(max_size=2000), size=st.integers(1, 200), data=st.data())\ndef test_chunk_properties(text, size, data):\n    overlap = data.draw(st.integers(0, size - 1), label=\"overlap\")\n    chunks = chunk(text, size, overlap)\n    assert chunks, \"always at least one chunk\"\n    assert all(len(c) <= size for c in chunks)\n    assert all(len(c) == size for c in chunks[:-1])           # only the last chunk may be short\n    assert all(a[len(a) - overlap:] == b[:overlap] for a, b in zip(chunks, chunks[1:]))\n    assert chunks[0] + \"\".join(c[overlap:] for c in chunks[1:]) == text\n\njson_values = st.recursive(\n    st.none() | st.booleans() | st.integers() | st.text(),        # no floats: NaN != NaN\n    lambda inner: st.lists(inner) | st.dictionaries(st.text(), inner),\n)\n\n@given(st.dictionaries(st.text(min_size=1), json_values))\ndef test_tool_args_round_trip(args):\n    assert json.loads(json.dumps(args)) == args\n\ndef test_empty_text_regression():          # the shrunk counterexample, pinned as a plain test\n    assert chunk(\"\", size=4, overlap=1) == [\"\"]\n```\n\nThe chunker test encodes the specification in five assertions. The full-length and shared-overlap\nchecks matter: without them a chunker that emitted one character per chunk would still rejoin\ncorrectly and pass whenever `overlap` is 0. No hand-written example set would have covered size 1,\noverlap `size - 1`, empty text and non-ASCII text all at once. The round-trip test excludes floats on\npurpose, because `NaN` breaks equality, which is itself the kind of fact property tests force you to\ndecide explicitly.","tradeoffs":["Properties cover far more of the input space than examples, but are harder to write; for code whose correctness you cannot state as a rule, a handful of good examples remains the better investment.","More examples find rarer bugs, but slow the suite; run the default count locally and a larger count in a nightly or CI profile, and flip that balance once the suite becomes a bottleneck.","Generated inputs explore edge cases you did not imagine, but random exploration means a new failure can surface on an unrelated commit; the example database and pinned `@example`s keep that manageable.","Restating the implementation as the property is easy but proves nothing; an independent oracle or invariant costs more thought and is the only version that actually catches bugs."],"pitfalls":["Writing a property that re-implements the function under test; the symptom is a test that has never failed, even when you deliberately break the code.","Overusing `filter` or `assume` to carve out valid inputs; the symptom is slow tests and Hypothesis health-check errors about too much data being filtered out.","Generating floats for equality-based properties without thinking about NaN, infinities and rounding; the symptom is spurious failures on `nan != nan` or tiny representation differences.","Using a function-scoped pytest fixture (such as `tmp_path`) inside a `@given` test and expecting it fresh per example; it is created once per *test function* and shared by all generated examples, so state written by one example leaks into the next. Hypothesis raises a health-check error for this; create the resource inside the test body instead.","Calling a real LLM or network service inside `@given`; the symptom is a test that makes a hundred paid, slow, non-deterministic calls per run and fails on flaky responses, not on bugs.","Deleting the `.hypothesis/` database in CI without pinning found failures; the symptom is a bug that reproduced once and then mysteriously stops reproducing until it hits production."],"when":"Reach for property-based testing on deterministic code with a large input space and an easy-to-state\nrule: parsers for model output, chunkers and splitters, serialisers, prompt template escaping, ID and\ndeduplication logic, ranking and score normalisation, and anything stateful like caches and memories.\nIt is not the tool for judging model quality; the model's outputs are not deterministic and their\n\"correctness\" is a graded judgement, which is what evals and metamorphic evaluation address. Use it\nalongside a few readable examples, which serve as documentation. In interviews, being able to name the\nproperty for a given function (round-trip, invariant, idempotence, oracle) is the skill being tested.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes Hypothesis properties for parsers, chunkers and tool-argument serialisation, and pins every shrunk counterexample as a permanent regression test.","architect":"Requires property tests on boundary code that turns untrusted text into structured data, because that is where malformed inputs enter the system.","fde":"Turns a customer's odd documents (encodings, empty pages, enormous tables) into strategies that generate similar inputs, proving the pipeline handles the whole class."},"check":{"q":"You must test a function that extracts a JSON object from a model reply that may include markdown\nfences and surrounding prose. What properties would you write, and what would you not use Hypothesis for?","a":"Generate an arbitrary JSON-compatible dict, serialise it, then wrap it with generated prose\nbefore and after, optionally inside a fenced block; the property is that extraction returns the\noriginal dict. A second property: text with no JSON object raises a specific parse error rather than\nreturning garbage. A third: extraction is unchanged by leading and trailing whitespace. Generated\nprose should be filtered so it cannot itself contain braces, or the property must define which object\nwins. I would not use Hypothesis to decide whether the model produces good JSON in the first place;\nthat is an eval over real prompts, and replies would be recorded examples, not generated ones."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does Hypothesis shrinking do after a property fails?","options":["Reruns the failing input until it passes, to detect flakiness","Searches for a simpler input that still fails and reports the minimal one","Reduces max_examples so the test runs faster next time","Removes the failing input from future runs to avoid noise"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shrinking turns a large random failure into a small, readable counterexample such as an empty string. Failing inputs are saved and replayed first next time, not removed."},{"q":"Which is the strongest property for a JSON serialiser of tool arguments?","options":["The output is a non-empty string","The output is shorter than ten kilobytes","Deserialising the output returns a value equal to the original input","Serialising twice produces the same string"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The round-trip property checks that no information is lost or corrupted. Non-empty output and determinism can hold while values are silently mangled, so they are much weaker checks."},{"q":"Why can Hypothesis shrink values built with `.map` and `.filter` without a custom shrinker?","options":["It requires every strategy to define its own shrink function","It only shrinks integers and strings, not composed values","It shrinks the underlying sequence of random choices, then regenerates the value from it","It stores every generated value and picks the smallest one"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shrinking the choice sequence works for any strategy composition automatically. Type-based shrinking, as in classic QuickCheck, needs a shrinker per type and struggles with mapped values."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/contract-testing","sources":["Robinson, 2006 — Consumer-Driven Contracts: A Service Evolution Pattern","Official docs: Pact","Official docs: Schemathesis"],"why":"An AI system is a set of services talking over interfaces: an agent service calls a retrieval service,\na tool gateway, a model gateway and customer APIs; tools exchange JSON with the model; an MCP server\nexposes schemas to clients. Each side is tested in isolation with doubles. The agent's tests stub the\nretrieval service's response as `{\"hits\": [{\"doc_id\", \"score\", \"text\"}]}`, and they pass. Then the\nretrieval team renames `text` to `content` in a release. Both test suites stay green, because each\nside's tests only check that side against its own assumptions, and the break appears in production as\nthe agent quietly answering without context.\n\nThe two obvious fixes both fail at scale. Shared end-to-end environments that run every service\ntogether do catch the rename, but they are slow, flaky, expensive to keep in sync, and they tell you\nsomething broke long after the commit that broke it. Writing an API spec document and asking teams\nto \"follow it\" does not work either, because nothing executes the spec against the code. The provider\nalso cannot know which fields consumers actually use, so every change feels risky, and APIs ossify.\n\nContract testing makes the agreement executable and splits it in two: each consumer's expectations\nbecome a file, and the provider runs that file against its real code on every build. A breaking change\nthen fails the provider's CI, in the commit that caused it, naming the consumer it would break.","how":"### Consumer-driven contracts\n1. **Consumer test.** The consumer's test talks to a mock provider that is configured with expected\n   interactions: \"given the index contains a refunds document, `POST /search` with this body returns\n   200 and a list of hits each having a string `doc_id`, a number `score` and a string `text`.\" The\n   consumer code runs for real against that mock, so the expectations are exactly what the code uses.\n2. **Contract file.** The run writes the interactions to a contract (a pact file in Pact's terms).\n   Matchers express \"a string\" or \"a list of at least one like this\" rather than exact values, because\n   the consumer depends on shape, not on today's data.\n3. **Provider verification.** The provider's CI fetches every consumer's contract, puts its real\n   application into each named **provider state** (seeds the test index), replays each request, and\n   checks the response matches. Extra fields are fine; missing or retyped ones fail.\n4. **Broker and deploy gate.** Contracts are published to a shared store (the Pact Broker or similar)\n   tagged by version and environment, so the provider verifies against what is actually deployed and a\n   \"can I deploy\" check blocks releasing a version that breaks a live consumer.\n\nThe design choice that matters is direction: the consumer states only what it uses, so the provider\nlearns exactly which fields are load-bearing and is free to change everything else. The same\nmechanism covers asynchronous messages, where there is no request to replay: the consumer records the\nevent payload it expects (a `document.indexed` event on a queue), and the provider's verification\ncalls the producing code and checks the message it would publish.\n\n### Evolving a contract without a flag day\nContracts make breaking changes visible; they do not make them free. Renaming `text` to `content`\nsafely is an expand-and-contract sequence, and the broker's version matrix is what tells you when\neach step is allowed:\n1. **Expand.** The provider returns both `text` and `content`. Every existing contract still passes,\n   because extra fields are allowed.\n2. **Migrate.** Each consumer switches to `content` and publishes a new contract that no longer\n   mentions `text`.\n3. **Contract.** The provider removes `text`. Verification now passes only if *every deployed*\n   consumer version has stopped relying on it, so the \"can I deploy\" check blocks the removal until\n   the last old consumer has actually been redeployed, not merely merged.\nThe order matters because services deploy independently: at any moment several versions of each are\nlive, and the contract that must hold is the one between the versions actually running together.\n\n### Provider-driven, schema-based contracts\nThe alternative is that the provider publishes a schema (OpenAPI, JSON Schema, protobuf) and both\nsides test against it. Schemathesis generates requests from an OpenAPI document and checks the real\nservice's responses conform, which is property-based testing applied to an API. Schema checks catch\n\"the service does not do what its spec says\"; they do not tell you which consumers break when the spec\nchanges, which is what consumer-driven contracts add.\n\n### Contracts you cannot verify\nYou cannot run provider verification against a model vendor's API. For third-party providers the\npattern is: validate every response at your boundary with strict models, keep recorded real responses\nas consumer test fixtures, pin SDK versions, and run a small scheduled canary against the live API\nthat validates real responses against your models, so a vendor-side change fails a check before\nusers notice. Tool schemas and structured-output schemas shown to a model are contracts too, and they\nare verified the same way: by validating what actually comes back.","example":"An agent service consumes a retrieval service. The consumer models only the fields it reads and\npublishes one interaction; the provider's test replays it against the real app in a seeded state.\nThis hand-rolled version shows the mechanism that Pact automates with matchers and a broker.\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict\n\nclass Hit(BaseModel):                      # consumer side: only fields the agent reads\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"ignore\", strict=True)   # strict: \"0.9\" is not a float\n    doc_id: str\n    score: float\n    text: str\n\nclass SearchResponse(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"ignore\", strict=True)\n    hits: list[Hit]\n\nCONTRACT = {                               # published by the consumer's CI\n    \"consumer\": \"agent-service\", \"provider\": \"retrieval-service\",\n    \"interactions\": [{\n        \"description\": \"search returns scored hits\",\n        \"provider_state\": \"index contains doc d1 about refunds\",\n        \"request\": {\"method\": \"POST\", \"path\": \"/search\", \"json\": {\"query\": \"refund policy\", \"k\": 3}},\n        \"response\": {\"status\": 200, \"schema\": SearchResponse},  # shipped as model_json_schema()\n    }],\n}\n\n# provider side, in the retrieval service's own test suite\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\nfrom retrieval.app import app, seed_state  # the provider's real code\n\ndef test_retrieval_honours_agent_contract():\n    client = TestClient(app)\n    for it in CONTRACT[\"interactions\"]:\n        seed_state(it[\"provider_state\"])\n        req = it[\"request\"]\n        r = client.request(req[\"method\"], req[\"path\"], json=req[\"json\"])\n        assert r.status_code == it[\"response\"][\"status\"]\n        body = it[\"response\"][\"schema\"].model_validate_json(r.content)  # missing/retyped field raises\n        assert body.hits, \"provider state promised at least one hit\"\n```\n\nOn the consumer side, the matching test runs the agent's real retrieval adapter against a stub\nserver that returns a response built from the same interaction, so the contract cannot list a field\nthe adapter does not parse, or omit one it does. That pairing is what Pact's mock provider automates.\n\nIf the retrieval team renames `text` to `content`, the provider test fails in their pull request, while adding\na new `chunk_id` field passes because the consumer ignores extras. `strict=True` matters: Pydantic's\ndefault lax mode would coerce a `score` that became the string `\"0.9\"` back into a float, and the\nretype would pass verification while breaking any consumer that does not coerce. In a real setup the\nconsumer publishes the JSON Schema (or Pact matchers) rather than a Python class.","tradeoffs":["Contracts catch interface breaks in seconds per build, but only interface breaks; a provider that returns the right shape with the wrong documents still passes, which integration tests or evals cover.","Consumer-driven contracts tell providers exactly which fields matter, but need a broker, versioning discipline and buy-in from both teams; with one team owning both sides, a shared schema is often enough.","Schema-first contracts are simple to adopt and serve many unknown consumers, but cannot report which consumer a change breaks; they flip to the better choice for public APIs with external clients.","Loose matchers (type, not value) keep contracts stable, but can admit semantically wrong data; tighten them only where a value is genuinely part of the agreement, such as an enum of error codes."],"pitfalls":["Contracts that pin exact values from the consumer's fixtures; the symptom is provider verification failing whenever test data changes, until teams stop trusting and then stop running it.","Consumers recording fields they do not actually read; the symptom is a provider that cannot remove or rename anything, because every field appears to be load-bearing.","Missing provider states, so verification runs against an empty database; the symptom is contracts that only ever verify the \"no results\" path while the populated path breaks unnoticed.","Treating a vendor LLM API as stable because your mocks are; the symptom is a production parse error after an SDK or response-format change that no test of yours could have seen.","Removing a field as soon as consumers' main branches stop using it; the symptom is an outage in the window before the last consumer is redeployed, which a deploy gate on deployed versions prevents.","Verifying against the main-branch contract instead of the deployed one; the symptom is a provider release that passes CI and still breaks the consumer version currently running in production."],"when":"Use consumer-driven contracts when separately deployed services are owned by different teams and\ninterface breaks have actually reached production; that is when the broker and process pay for\nthemselves. Use schema-based checks (OpenAPI plus Schemathesis, or shared Pydantic models) when one team\nowns both sides or the API is public. For third-party model and SaaS APIs, use boundary validation plus\na live canary. Do not use contracts as a substitute for integration tests of behaviour. In interviews,\ncontract testing is the expected answer to \"how do you stop mocks drifting from the real service?\"","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes consumer tests that record only the fields the code reads, and provider tests with explicit states, so contract failures point at real breaks.","architect":"Decides where contracts sit (service APIs, tool schemas, MCP surfaces, event payloads) and wires a deploy gate so breaking changes cannot ship to a live consumer.","fde":"Captures the customer system's real request and response shapes as a contract early, so an upstream change in their API fails a check instead of a live integration."},"check":{"q":"Your agent calls an internal tool service owned by another team and a public LLM API. Mocks on\nboth sides keep passing while production breaks. Design the testing for each boundary.","a":"For the internal tool service, I would use consumer-driven contracts: the agent's tests run\nagainst a mock built from recorded interactions that use type matchers and name provider states; the\ncontract is published to a broker; the tool team's CI verifies it against their real service and a\ndeploy gate checks deployed versions. For the LLM API, I cannot run provider verification, so I would\nvalidate every response with strict models at the boundary, keep recorded real responses as fixtures,\npin the SDK, and run a scheduled canary that calls the live API and validates the shape. Neither\nreplaces evals of answer quality; contracts only guarantee the shape of the conversation."},"quiz":[{"q":"In consumer-driven contract testing, who verifies the contract against real code?","options":["The consumer, by calling the real provider in its test suite","The provider, by replaying the consumer's recorded interactions against its real application","A shared end-to-end environment that runs both services together","Nobody; the contract file is documentation reviewed by humans"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The consumer runs against a mock to produce the contract; the provider verifies it in its own build. That split is what removes the need for a shared environment to catch interface breaks."},{"q":"Why should consumer contracts use type matchers rather than exact values?","options":["Exact values make contract files too large to store","The consumer depends on response shape, so exact values cause failures whenever test data changes","Type matchers let the provider omit fields the consumer uses","Exact values cannot be serialised to JSON"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pinning values couples the contract to fixtures rather than to the interface, making verification brittle. Matchers still fail when a used field is missing or changes type."},{"q":"What can schema-based contract checks not tell a provider that consumer-driven contracts can?","options":["Whether responses match the published OpenAPI schema","Whether a field has the declared type","Which specific consumers would break if a field were removed","Whether the service handles malformed requests"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A schema describes what the provider offers, not what each consumer uses. Consumer-driven contracts record actual usage, so a removal fails against exactly the consumers that read that field."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","sources":["Cohn, 2009 — Succeeding with Agile","Official docs: Testcontainers","Official docs: pytest"],"why":"A RAG service can have perfect unit tests and still fail on its first real query. The fake vector store\nreturned exactly *k* results, but the real approximate index with a tenant filter returns fewer. The fake\ndatabase accepted 1,536-dimensional vectors into a column declared for 768. The SQL that the ORM\ngenerates differs from what the mocked repository implied. The app works, but the migration that\ncreates the index was never run in tests. The streaming endpoint works in isolation, but the reverse\nproxy buffers the stream. None of these are logic bugs; they are disagreements between your code and\nthe real systems it touches, and a double cannot reveal them because the double *is* your assumption.\n\nThe naive answer, \"test everything end to end against a staging environment\", fails in the opposite\ndirection. End-to-end runs are slow (minutes, not milliseconds), they depend on shared state that other\npeople change, they fail for reasons unrelated to your change (a network blip, a model rate limit, a\nredeploy), and when they fail they rarely say *where*. A suite built mainly from them becomes slow,\nflaky and ignored. The test pyramid is the compromise: many fast unit tests for logic, fewer integration\ntests at each real boundary, and a thin layer of end-to-end tests for the critical user journeys.","how":"### Where the lines are\n- **Unit:** one piece of your code, collaborators replaced by doubles, milliseconds per test.\n- **Integration:** your code plus one or more *real* collaborators you control in the test: a database\n  in a container, a real HTTP client against a local server, the real framework routing and\n  serialisation. Usually seconds per test, mostly spent on startup.\n- **End-to-end:** the assembled system, deployed roughly as in production, exercised only through its\n  public interface (HTTP API, UI, queue), checking a full journey: upload a document, ask a question,\n  get a cited answer.\n\n### Real dependencies, disposable\nTestcontainers (with a Python library) starts a throwaway Docker container from a pinned image for the\nduration of a fixture, waits until it is ready, and gives you its connection URL. You test against the\nsame engine and extensions as production rather than SQLite or an in-memory imitation, because dialect,\nindexing and extension behaviour are exactly what you are trying to verify. Docker Compose does the same\nfor a multi-service stack. Container startup is the expensive part, so scope the container per session\nand make each test isolated within it. Under pytest-xdist, \"per session\" means per worker process,\nso eight workers start eight databases; that is usually fine, and it is also why tests must not\nassume they share rows with any other test.\n\n### Isolation inside a shared dependency\nOptions, each with its own failure mode: wrap each test in a transaction and roll back (fast, but breaks if\nthe code commits or uses several connections); give each test a unique namespace, such as a tenant ID,\nschema or collection name (works across connections and services); or truncate tables between tests\n(simple, slower). Never rely on test order.\n\n### The model in the loop\nThe LLM is the dependency that breaks the usual playbook, because it is non-deterministic, paid and\nrate-limited. Choices for integration and e2e tests:\n1. **A fake model server** that speaks the provider's HTTP API and returns scripted replies. The full HTTP\n   path, SDK, retries and parsing run for real; only the model is fixed.\n2. **Record and replay**: capture real responses once (cassette libraries such as vcrpy do this for HTTP)\n   and replay them, refreshing deliberately.\n3. **Live calls**, kept few, run on a schedule or before release, asserting structure and invariants\n   (valid JSON, cited document IDs exist) rather than exact text. Judging answer *quality* belongs in the\n   eval suite, not the test suite.\n\n### End-to-end tests that are not flaky\nMost e2e flakiness is self-inflicted, and each cause has a mechanical fix:\n- **Starting before the system is ready.** Wait on a readiness endpoint that checks dependencies, not\n  on a fixed sleep, because startup time varies with the CI machine.\n- **Racing asynchronous work.** Ingestion in a RAG system is usually a background job, so \"upload,\n  then immediately ask\" sometimes runs before indexing finishes. Poll for the observable outcome (the\n  document's status is `indexed`) with a deadline, and fail with the last status seen, rather than\n  sleeping a guessed number of seconds that is either too short on a slow day or wasteful on every run.\n- **Shared data.** Create uniquely named tenants, users and documents per run, so concurrent runs and\n  leftovers from crashed runs cannot collide; clean up best-effort, but never depend on cleanup.\n- **Undiagnosable failures.** Attach the request IDs or trace IDs of every call the test made to the\n  failure message, so a red run points at the logs of the service that misbehaved.\nA test written this way fails only when the system is broken or genuinely slower than its budget,\nwhich is the only kind of failure worth paging a developer about.\n\n### Running them\nMark slow tests (`@pytest.mark.integration`, `@pytest.mark.e2e`), register the markers, and select them\nwith `-m` so the default local run stays fast while CI runs integration tests on every change and e2e\non merge or deploy.","example":"An integration test for a pgvector retriever, against the real extension in a container. The embedder is\na deterministic fake so results are reproducible; everything else (SQL, index, migrations, tenant\nfilter) is real. `PgRetriever` and `HashEmbedder` stand for the application's own code.\n\n```python\nimport uuid\nimport pytest\nfrom testcontainers.postgres import PostgresContainer\nfrom app.retriever import PgRetriever     # code under test: migrate(), add(), search()\nfrom app.embed import HashEmbedder        # deterministic fake: hashes text into an 8-dim vector\n\npytestmark = pytest.mark.integration      # run with: pytest -m integration\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef retriever():\n    with PostgresContainer(\"pgvector/pgvector:0.8.0-pg16\", driver=None) as pg:  # pin both versions\n        r = PgRetriever(pg.get_connection_url(), embedder=HashEmbedder(dim=8))\n        r.migrate()                       # the real migrations: extension, table, HNSW index\n        yield r\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef tenant():\n    return f\"t-{uuid.uuid4().hex[:8]}\"    # unique namespace per test: no cleanup, no cross-talk\n\ndef test_search_never_crosses_tenants(retriever, tenant):\n    retriever.add(tenant, \"d1\", \"refunds are issued within 14 days\")\n    retriever.add(tenant + \"-other\", \"d2\", \"refunds are issued within 14 days\")\n    hits = retriever.search(tenant, \"refund window\", k=5)\n    assert [h.doc_id for h in hits] == [\"d1\"]\n\ndef test_wrong_dimension_is_rejected_by_the_database(retriever, tenant):\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):       # adapter maps the DB error to a domain error\n        retriever.add_vector(tenant, \"d3\", [0.1] * 7)\n\ndef test_filtered_search_returns_all_matches(retriever, tenant):\n    for i in range(50):\n        retriever.add(tenant, f\"doc{i}\", f\"policy section {i}\")\n    assert len(retriever.search(tenant, \"policy\", k=20)) == 20\n```\n\nThe last test is the one doubles cannot write: with a shared approximate index and a selective tenant\nfilter, a naive query can return fewer than *k* rows, which is a property of the real engine and its\nsettings, not of your Python. pgvector's HNSW scan gathers a bounded candidate list (`hnsw.ef_search`,\n40 by default) and the `WHERE` filter is applied afterwards; version 0.8.0 added iterative index scans\nto keep searching when too few rows survive. To make the test meaningful, seed enough rows for other\ntenants that the planner actually uses the index; on a near-empty table Postgres may choose an exact\nsequential scan and the test passes for the wrong reason. Note the image tag pins pgvector as well as\nPostgres: a bare `pg16` tag moves when pgvector releases.","tradeoffs":["Real dependencies catch dialect, index and migration bugs that fakes hide, but add container startup time and a Docker requirement in CI; session-scoped containers keep that cost roughly constant.","End-to-end tests are the only check that the deployed whole works, but are slow, flaky and poor at locating faults; keep them to a few critical journeys and push everything else down a level.","Fake model servers make AI e2e tests deterministic and free, but test only your handling of replies you anticipated; scheduled live checks flip that, at the cost of money, rate limits and noise.","Transaction rollback isolation is very fast, but breaks once code commits or spans connections; unique namespaces per test are slower to set up and work everywhere."],"pitfalls":["Using SQLite or an in-memory imitation in place of Postgres with pgvector; the symptom is green tests and production errors on vector operators, JSON functions or index behaviour.","Tests sharing rows or collections; the symptom is a suite that passes serially and fails under parallel runs or in a different order, which teams misdiagnose as random flakiness.","Asserting exact LLM output text in end-to-end tests; the symptom is failures after any model or prompt change even though the system still works, until the tests are disabled.","Unpinned container image tags such as `latest`; the symptom is a suite that breaks overnight with no code change because the database or extension version moved.","Fixed `sleep` calls waiting for background ingestion or deployment; the symptom is a test that fails on busy CI runners and passes on rerun, while the suite's wall time grows by the sum of every guess.","Retrying flaky e2e tests until green; the symptom is a real intermittent bug (a race, a timeout too close to p99 latency) that the retry hides and production users then hit."],"when":"Write an integration test at every boundary where your code meets something you did not write and\ncannot fully fake: databases and vector stores, queues, caches, the web framework's request handling,\nand the HTTP layer to model providers via a fake server. Keep end-to-end tests to the handful of journeys\nthat would be a severity-one incident if broken, and run them on merge or deploy rather than on every\nsave. Measure suite time per lane and flake rate (the share of runs that fail and then pass on rerun with\nno code change), and fix or delete flaky tests quickly, because each one teaches the team to ignore red. In interviews, \"how would\nyou test a RAG service?\" expects this layering, plus a clear separation from evals that measure quality.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes container-backed integration tests with unique per-test namespaces, marks them, and keeps the default local test run fast.","architect":"Defines the test layering, what runs on every commit versus before deploy, and where the model is faked, replayed or called live, with an explicit budget for each.","fde":"Builds an end-to-end smoke test against the customer's environment (their SSO, network, data store) that proves the deployment works before handing it over."},"check":{"q":"Your RAG service's unit tests all pass, but after deploying, answers for some tenants have no\ncitations. How would you change the test suite so this class of failure is caught before deploy?","a":"Missing citations for some tenants smells like a real-engine behaviour: a tenant filter combined\nwith an approximate index returning fewer rows, a migration not applied, or a dimension mismatch. I would\nadd container-backed integration tests against the same Postgres and pgvector version, running the real\nmigrations, with per-test tenant namespaces and cases for selective filters and large tenants. Then one\nend-to-end test that uploads a document and asks a question through the public API against a fake model\nserver, asserting that the response cites an existing document ID. Model answer quality stays in evals;\nthese tests check the plumbing that the model depends on."},"quiz":[{"q":"What distinguishes an integration test from a unit test in this layering?","options":["Integration tests are written in a different framework","Integration tests run your code against at least one real collaborator instead of a double","Integration tests always go through the public UI","Integration tests cannot be run with pytest"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The defining feature is the real dependency, such as a containerised database. Driving the whole deployed system through its public interface describes end-to-end tests, not integration tests."},{"q":"Why is SQLite a poor stand-in for Postgres with pgvector in integration tests?","options":["SQLite is too slow for tests","SQLite cannot be started from pytest fixtures","The test exists to verify engine-specific behaviour, which a different engine does not have","SQLite does not support transactions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vector operators, index behaviour and SQL dialect are exactly what integration tests check, so a different engine silently skips the assumptions you meant to verify. SQLite is fast, not slow."},{"q":"Which approach keeps an AI end-to-end test deterministic while still exercising the HTTP path?","options":["Calling the live model with temperature zero and asserting exact text","Pointing the system at a fake model server that speaks the provider's API with scripted replies","Mocking the service's handler function inside the test process","Retrying the test until the model produces the expected answer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fake server keeps SDK, retries and parsing real while fixing the model's output. Temperature zero does not guarantee identical text, and mocking the handler is no longer end to end."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/mypy","sources":["Official docs: mypy","PEP 484 — Type Hints","PEP 561 — Distributing and Packaging Type Information"],"why":"Python's runtime does not enforce annotations. `def call_model(prompt: str) -> str` happily accepts a\nlist and returns `None`; you find out when a downstream `.strip()` raises `AttributeError` in production,\noften on a rare path: the model returned no content, an env var was unset, a tool call had no arguments.\nAI code is especially exposed because so much of it passes loosely shaped data between layers: provider\nresponses where `content` may be `None`, dicts of tool arguments, optional fields in config, results\nthat are either a value or an error.\n\nTests catch some of this, but only on the paths you test, and the `None` paths are exactly the ones\npeople forget. Runtime validation with Pydantic catches it at boundaries, but not inside your own code\nbetween boundaries. The naive alternative, \"we have annotations, that is enough\", gives you\ndocumentation that silently becomes wrong after the third refactor, because nothing checks it.\n\nA static checker turns annotations into a cheap, whole-program test that runs in seconds and covers\nevery path, including the ones no test exercises. It also makes refactoring safe: change a function's\nsignature and the checker lists every caller you broke.","how":"### Checking\nmypy parses each module, builds types for every definition from annotations and from inference\n(`x = []` followed by `x.append(1)` infers `list[int]`), and checks every expression against them.\nErrors carry a code in brackets (`[arg-type]`, `[union-attr]`, `[return-value]`), which you use for\ntargeted ignores and configuration.\n\n**Narrowing** is what makes optional types usable: after `if content is None: return`, mypy knows\n`content` is `str`. The same works for `isinstance`, comparisons against `Literal` values, and\n`TypeGuard`/`TypeIs` functions. Calling `assert_never(x)` in the final branch makes mypy prove a\nchain of checks is exhaustive, so adding a new literal value breaks the build where it is not handled.\n\n### Subtyping: nominal, structural, and variance\nBy default a value is compatible with a class if it is an instance of that class or a subclass\n(nominal typing). A `Protocol` switches to structural typing: any object with matching methods\nqualifies, without inheriting anything, which is how you type \"anything with an async `complete`\nmethod\" so a real client and a test fake both satisfy one interface. Generic containers carry a\nsubtlety that surprises most people: `list[int]` is *not* a `list[float]`, even though `int` is\naccepted where `float` is expected. The reason is soundness: a function taking `list[float]` may\nappend `0.5`, which would corrupt the caller's `list[int]`. Mutable containers are therefore\ninvariant, while read-only ones such as `Sequence` and `Mapping` (values) are covariant, so the usual\nfix is to accept `Sequence[float]` when the function only reads. `reveal_type(expr)` prints what mypy\ninferred at that point, and is the first tool when a message seems wrong.\n\n### Gradual typing\nAnything typed `Any` is compatible with everything, in both directions, so errors stop propagating\nthrough it. By default mypy does not check the bodies of functions that have no annotations at all,\nbecause adopting a checker on an untyped codebase would otherwise produce thousands of errors. Both\nchoices make adoption possible and both hide bugs, which is why `--strict` (and flags such as\n`disallow_untyped_defs`, `check_untyped_defs`, `warn_return_any`, `warn_unused_ignores`) exist.\n\n### Third-party types\nFor libraries, mypy uses inline hints if the package ships a `py.typed` marker (PEP 561), otherwise\nstub packages: the standard library's from typeshed (bundled), others from `types-*` packages. Untyped\nlibraries produce \"missing library stubs\" errors, silenced per module with `ignore_missing_imports`.\nSome libraries need plugins to be understood, such as Pydantic's mypy plugin for model constructors.\n\n### Configuration and speed\nConfigure in `pyproject.toml`; per-module overrides let you be strict in new code and lenient in legacy:\n\n```toml\n[tool.mypy]\nstrict = true\nplugins = [\"pydantic.mypy\"]\n\n[[tool.mypy.overrides]]\nmodule = [\"legacy.*\"]\ndisallow_untyped_defs = false\n\n[[tool.mypy.overrides]]\nmodule = [\"some_untyped_sdk.*\"]\nignore_missing_imports = true\n```\n\nmypy reads types from the packages installed in the environment it runs in, so it must run with the\nproject's real dependencies installed and the same target Python version (`python_version`) as\nproduction; run it in a bare CI image and every third-party import becomes a missing-stubs error or\nsilently `Any`. mypy caches results in `.mypy_cache` and rechecks only what changed; `dmypy` keeps a daemon running for\nfaster editor-style feedback. The main alternative is pyright (the engine behind Pylance), which is\nfaster and infers more aggressively, so the two disagree at the edges; newer Rust-based checkers such as\nAstral's ty and Meta's Pyrefly are much faster but younger, so check their current maturity and\ntyping-spec conformance before making one a gate. Pick one checker as the CI gate.","example":"Bugs a test suite can easily miss, each reported by mypy with its error code, plus one it misses. The comments show\nmypy's messages (paths and line numbers omitted).\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom typing import Literal, TypedDict, assert_never   # assert_never: Python 3.11+\n\nclass ToolCall(TypedDict):\n    name: Literal[\"search\", \"fetch_url\"]\n    arguments: dict[str, str]\n\ndef run(model: str, prompt: str) -> str: ...\n\ndef answer(prompt: str) -> str:\n    model = os.environ.get(\"MODEL\")          # str | None\n    return run(model, prompt)\n    # error: Argument 1 to \"run\" has incompatible type \"str | None\"; expected \"str\"  [arg-type]\n\ndef first_text(content: str | None) -> str:\n    return content.strip()\n    # error: Item \"None\" of \"str | None\" has no attribute \"strip\"  [union-attr]\n\ndef query_of(call: ToolCall) -> str:\n    return call[\"argumnets\"][\"query\"]\n    # error: TypedDict \"ToolCall\" has no key \"argumnets\"  [typeddict-item]\n\ndef route(call: ToolCall) -> str:\n    name = call[\"name\"]\n    if name == \"search\":\n        return \"search-service\"\n    elif name == \"fetch_url\":\n        return \"fetcher\"\n    else:\n        assert_never(name)   # adding \"run_sql\" to the Literal makes this line an error\n\ndef helper(resp):                  # unannotated: mypy skips this body by default\n    return first_text(resp) + 1    # str + int: reported only with check_untyped_defs (or strict)\n    # resp.choises would pass even then: an unannotated parameter is Any until you annotate it\n```\n\nThe fixes are one line each (`os.environ[\"MODEL\"]` or a default, an `is None` branch, the correct key),\nand the last function shows why `strict` matters: unannotated bodies are skipped by default, and even\n`check_untyped_defs` cannot see through parameters that are implicitly `Any`. Strict mode's\n`disallow_untyped_defs` forces the annotation that makes the checker useful there.","tradeoffs":["Strict mode catches the most bugs, but costs annotation effort and fights dynamic code; it flips from burden to bargain once a codebase is refactored often or shared by several teams.","Gradual typing lets you adopt mypy incrementally, but `Any` and unannotated functions leave holes that look checked; track the untyped share and ratchet strictness module by module.","Static checks cover every path at near-zero runtime cost, but say nothing about data from outside the process; model output and HTTP payloads still need runtime validation with Pydantic or similar.","pyright is faster and more aggressive at inference, while mypy is the long-standing reference with a plugin system; mixing both as CI gates mostly produces arguments about edge cases."],"pitfalls":["Sprinkling bare `# type: ignore` comments; the symptom is suppressed real errors that outlive their reason, which `warn_unused_ignores` and error-code-specific ignores make visible.","Typing provider responses or tool arguments as `dict[str, Any]`; the symptom is mypy passing code that reads misspelled keys, because `Any` turns off every check downstream of it.","Leaving functions unannotated and assuming they are checked; the symptom is obvious misuse of typed values in those bodies that mypy never reports until `check_untyped_defs` is on, and attribute typos on their implicitly `Any` parameters that stay invisible until the parameters are annotated.","Running mypy only in editors, not in CI; the symptom is a main branch with hundreds of errors that nobody can turn on as a gate without a week of cleanup.","Annotating a read-only parameter as `list[float]` or `dict[str, float]`; the symptom is `[arg-type]` errors at callers passing `list[int]`, \"fixed\" with casts or ignores, when `Sequence[float]` or `Mapping[str, float]` states the real requirement.","Ignoring missing stubs globally rather than per module; the symptom is a typo in an import or a wrongly named function from a library that no longer produces any error."],"when":"Run mypy (or one chosen alternative) on every service that will be maintained beyond a prototype, as a\nCI gate alongside ruff and pytest. New projects should start in strict mode; existing ones should start\nlenient, fix the error codes that find real bugs (`union-attr`, `arg-type`, `return-value`), and tighten\nper module. Pair it with `TypedDict`, `Literal` and Pydantic models at the points where untyped data\nenters, because types are only as good as the boundary that establishes them. In interviews, explain\ngradual typing and why `Any` is contagious, not just which flags you set.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Annotates provider and tool boundaries precisely, narrows optional values explicitly, and treats every `type: ignore` as a reviewed exception with an error code.","architect":"Sets the typing policy: which checker gates CI, strictness for new versus legacy modules, and how typed contracts flow between services and agent tools.","fde":"Adds typed adapters around a customer's loosely shaped API early, so changes in their payloads surface as type errors in the integration code rather than as runtime failures."},"check":{"q":"A team says \"we have type hints everywhere and mypy passes, yet we keep getting `NoneType has no\nattribute` errors in production.\" Give two plausible causes and how to confirm each.","a":"First, `Any` leakage: responses or JSON are typed `dict[str, Any]` or come from an untyped library,\nso attribute access on them is never checked. Confirm with `reveal_type` at the failing site or mypy's\nany-expression reports, and fix by modelling the data with `TypedDict` or Pydantic at the boundary.\nSecond, gradual-typing gaps: the failing code is in unannotated functions or under a lenient override,\nso its body is not checked; turn on `check_untyped_defs` or strict for that module and see what appears.\nA third possibility is that runtime data violates the declared types, such as a field the provider\ndocuments as present arriving as null, which only runtime validation catches."},"quiz":[{"q":"By default, what does mypy do with the body of a function that has no annotations?","options":["Infers all parameter types from call sites and checks the body fully","Treats it as dynamically typed and does not report errors inside the body","Refuses to check the module until annotations are added","Converts the parameters to object and reports every attribute access"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Skipping unannotated bodies makes gradual adoption possible; `check_untyped_defs` or strict mode changes that. mypy does not infer parameter types from call sites; unannotated parameters are treated as Any."},{"q":"Why does typing a model response as `dict[str, Any]` weaken mypy's checking so much?","options":["mypy cannot check dictionaries at all","It forces mypy into a slower checking mode","Values typed Any are compatible with everything, so errors downstream of them are not reported","It makes mypy treat the whole module as untyped"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`Any` switches off checks wherever it flows, so a misspelled key or wrong attribute passes silently. Modelling the response with TypedDict or Pydantic restores checking downstream."},{"q":"What does calling `assert_never(x)` in a final `else` branch let mypy verify?","options":["That x is never None at runtime","That every possible value of x's union or Literal type was handled by earlier branches","That the function never raises an exception","That the else branch is executed at least once in tests"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After narrowing, x in the final branch should have no remaining type; if a new Literal value is added and not handled, mypy reports an error at the assert_never call."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/ruff","sources":["Official docs: Ruff","Official docs: flake8-bugbear"],"why":"A typical Python project used to run five or six tools on every commit: flake8 for errors and style,\nseveral flake8 plugins (bugbear for likely bugs, comprehensions, simplify), isort for import order,\npyupgrade for modern syntax, Black for formatting, sometimes pylint and bandit. Each had its own config\nfile or section, its own version pins, its own ideas about line length, and its own parse of every file.\nOn a large repository the combination took long enough that people disabled the pre-commit hook and let\nCI find problems, which means finding them minutes later instead of while the code is still on screen.\n\nThis matters more than it sounds in AI code, because some lint rules catch real defects, not style.\nA blocking `time.sleep` or `requests.get` inside an `async def` stalls every concurrent request in the\nserver. An `asyncio.create_task` whose result is not stored can be garbage-collected mid-flight. A mutable\ndefault argument shares state across calls. SQL built with f-strings is an injection risk when a tenant\nID comes from a request. A blind `except Exception` swallows unexpected errors and hides\nfailures. All of these are cheap to detect statically and expensive to debug in production.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"code review will catch it\", does not scale: reviewers miss mechanical issues,\nand review time spent on import order is time not spent on design.","how":"### One parse, many rules\nRuff parses each file once into an AST and token stream and runs every enabled rule over that single\nrepresentation, in parallel across files, with a per-file cache so files unchanged since the last run\n(under the same settings) are skipped. Native Rust code and that architecture together explain the\nspeed: the old toolchain ran each Python tool as its own process and re-parsed each file once per tool. The maintainers publish benchmarks showing large speedups\nover flake8; treat any specific multiple as illustrative and measure on your own repository.\n\nThe same design sets the limit of what it can see. Rules work on one file at a time, with a semantic\nmodel of that file's scopes, bindings and imports (enough to know that `requests.get` refers to the\n`requests` package, or that a name is unused), but with no cross-module type inference. That is why\nRuff recognises a blocking call by its qualified name and cannot follow it through your own wrapper\nfunction in another module, and why the pylint checks that depend on whole-program inference are not\namong the ones it re-implements.\n\n### Rules are namespaced by origin\nEach rule has a code whose prefix names the tool it re-implements: `F` (pyflakes: unused imports,\nundefined names), `E`/`W` (pycodestyle), `I` (isort), `B` (bugbear), `UP` (pyupgrade), `S` (bandit\nsecurity checks), `ASYNC` (flake8-async), `PT` (pytest style), `SIM`, `C4`, plus `RUF` for Ruff's own\nrules. You enable by prefix (`select = [\"B\"]`) or single code; `extend-select` adds to the inherited\nselection instead of replacing it. Newly added rules start in **preview** and run only with\n`preview = true`, so upgrading Ruff does not silently add findings to your CI; rules graduate to\nstable in later releases. The default set is deliberately small,\ncovering pyflakes and a subset of pycodestyle errors, because a linter that floods a new project with\nwarnings gets switched off. `ruff rule B006` prints the rationale for any rule.\n\n### Fixes\nMany rules have autofixes, split into **safe** fixes (behaviour-preserving, applied by `ruff check\n--fix`) and **unsafe** ones (may change behaviour, such as removing an import with side effects),\napplied only with `--unsafe-fixes`. The split exists because a tool that silently changes semantics\ncannot be trusted in a pre-commit hook.\n\n### Formatter\n`ruff format` is designed as a near drop-in replacement for Black's style, so teams can switch without\nreformatting debates. Linting and formatting are separate commands; when the formatter owns layout,\ndisable lint rules that fight it (line length is the usual one).\n\n### Configuration\nEverything lives in `pyproject.toml` (or `ruff.toml`): `[tool.ruff]` for shared settings such as\n`target-version` and `line-length`, `[tool.ruff.lint]` for `select`, `ignore` and `per-file-ignores`.\nInline `# noqa: B006` suppresses one rule on one line, and `RUF100` flags `noqa` comments that no\nlonger suppress anything, so suppressions do not outlive the code they excused. What Ruff does not do is type checking: it does not know that a value may be\n`None`, which is mypy's or pyright's job, so the two run side by side.","example":"A configuration for an async AI service, then a snippet with the findings Ruff reports under it. The\ncomments name the rule codes; each is a real defect class, not cosmetics.\n\n```toml\n[tool.ruff]\ntarget-version = \"py312\"\nline-length = 100\n\n[tool.ruff.lint]\nselect = [\"E\", \"F\", \"I\", \"B\", \"UP\", \"ASYNC\", \"S\", \"RUF\"]\nignore = [\"E501\"]                          # the formatter owns line length\n\n[tool.ruff.lint.per-file-ignores]\n\"tests/**\" = [\"S101\"]                      # assert is the point of a test\n```\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json, time                 # F401 json unused; I001 import block unsorted (E401 too)\nfrom typing import List                    # UP035 deprecated typing alias import\nimport requests\n\nasync def log_done(n: int) -> None: ...\n\nasync def fetch_all(urls: List[str], seen: set = set()) -> list:   # UP006 List; B006 mutable default\n    results = []\n    for url in urls:\n        if url in seen:\n            continue\n        time.sleep(0.1)                    # ASYNC251 blocks the event loop for every request\n        r = requests.get(url, timeout=10)  # ASYNC210 blocking HTTP call inside async def\n        results.append(r.json())\n    asyncio.create_task(log_done(len(results)))   # RUF006 task reference not kept\n    return results\n\ndef docs_for(cur, tenant: str):\n    try:\n        cur.execute(f\"SELECT * FROM docs WHERE tenant = '{tenant}'\")   # S608 SQL from string\n    except Exception as e:                 # F841 e unused\n        raise RuntimeError(\"query failed\")   # B904 re-raise without `from e`\n```\n\n`ruff check --fix` resolves the import, `List` and some other findings safely; the async, SQL and\ndefault-argument findings need a human, because the fix is a design change (an async client, a\nparameterised query, a `None` default).\n\nIn a pre-commit hook, run `ruff check --fix` before `ruff format`, because a lint fix (removing an\nimport, rewriting a comprehension) can leave code the formatter would lay out differently. In CI, run\n`ruff check` and `ruff format --check`; neither writes files, so CI fails on findings instead of\nquietly fixing them in a throwaway checkout.","tradeoffs":["One fast tool replaces a fragmented chain and runs on every save, but you depend on Ruff's re-implementations; a niche flake8 plugin or pylint check it lacks still needs the original tool.","Selecting broad rule families catches more real bugs, but floods legacy code with findings; start with `F`, `B`, `ASYNC`, `S` and grow the selection once the backlog is cleared.","Autofix in pre-commit removes mechanical review churn, but unsafe fixes can change behaviour; keep unsafe fixes manual and reviewed, which flips only for rules you have audited.","Ruff's formatter matches Black closely enough to switch in place, but small output differences mean one large reformatting commit; do it once, then add that commit to the blame-ignore list."],"pitfalls":["Running only the default rules and assuming the code is checked; the symptom is blocking calls in async handlers and mutable defaults shipping, because `ASYNC` and `B` were never enabled.","Blanket `# noqa` without a code; the symptom is a line that silently suppresses every future finding, including the real bug someone adds to it later.","Different Ruff versions locally, in pre-commit and in CI; the symptom is findings that appear only in CI or fixes that flip back and forth between machines, so pin one version everywhere.","Treating a clean Ruff run as type safety; the symptom is `None` attribute errors in production in code Ruff passed, because Ruff does not reason about types.","Enabling both a line-length lint rule and the formatter with different limits; the symptom is findings the formatter can never fix, and contributors adding `noqa` comments to get past them."],"when":"Use Ruff in every Python project, from the first commit: it is cheap enough to run in the editor, in a\npre-commit hook and in CI with the same pinned version. Choose rule families deliberately: `F` and `E`\nfor correctness, `B` and `RUF` for likely bugs, `ASYNC` for any service built on asyncio, `S` for code\nthat touches SQL, subprocesses or secrets, `UP` to keep syntax current with your minimum Python. Run it\nnext to mypy, not instead of it, and next to dependency auditing, which it does not do. In interviews, a\ngood answer explains which rules catch production defects in async AI services, not just that Ruff is fast.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Fixes findings as they appear in the editor and knows why `ASYNC`, `B006` and `RUF006` matter in an event-loop service, rather than silencing them.","architect":"Owns the shared rule selection and pinned version across repositories, so every service enforces the same correctness and security baseline in CI.","fde":"Runs Ruff on a customer's existing Python integration code on day one to surface blocking calls, injection risks and swallowed errors before building on top of it."},"check":{"q":"An async FastAPI service for LLM calls has latency spikes under load, yet CPU is low. The team\nalready runs Ruff with its default rules. How could Ruff have helped, and what are its limits here?","a":"Low CPU with spikes under concurrency suggests the event loop is being blocked: a `time.sleep`,\na sync HTTP client or a sync database driver called inside `async def`. The default rule set does not\ninclude the flake8-async family, so enabling `ASYNC` would flag blocking sleeps and HTTP calls inside\ncoroutines, and `RUF006` would flag fire-and-forget tasks. The limits: Ruff only recognises known\nblocking calls by name; a sync call hidden behind your own helper, or a CPU-heavy function such as\ntokenising a huge document, is invisible to it. You confirm with asyncio debug mode or profiling."},"quiz":[{"q":"What mainly explains Ruff's speed advantage over the older multi-tool lint chain?","options":["It skips checking files larger than a fixed size","It parses each file once and runs all rules on that parse, in parallel, with caching","It only runs rules on lines changed in the last commit","It uses type inference to prune rules that cannot apply"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The old chain re-parsed every file once per tool. Ruff shares one parse across all rules, runs files in parallel and caches unchanged files; it does not do type inference."},{"q":"Which issue is Ruff not designed to catch?","options":["A blocking time.sleep call inside an async function","A mutable list used as a default argument","Calling .strip() on a value whose declared type is str | None","An SQL query assembled with an f-string"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Optional-type errors need a type checker such as mypy or pyright. The other three are covered by Ruff's ASYNC, bugbear and bandit-derived rule families when those are enabled."},{"q":"Why does Ruff separate safe fixes from unsafe fixes?","options":["Unsafe fixes are slower to compute","Safe fixes only apply to formatting, never to lint findings","Unsafe fixes may change program behaviour, so they are not applied by default","Unsafe fixes require an internet connection to download rule data"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A fix such as removing an import with side effects can alter runtime behaviour. Keeping such fixes opt-in lets `--fix` run automatically in hooks without silently changing semantics."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/python-dotenv","sources":["Official docs: python-dotenv","Wiggins, 2011 — The Twelve-Factor App"],"why":"An AI service needs a handful of values that differ per environment and must never be committed: the\nOpenAI or Anthropic API key, a vector-database URL, a Langfuse key, the model name you are trialling.\nThe Twelve-Factor convention is to read all of these from environment variables, because every\ndeployment target (Docker, Kubernetes, a PaaS, CI) knows how to inject them and none of them require\ncode changes.\n\nThe friction is on the developer laptop. Exporting eight variables in every new terminal is tedious, and\nthe workarounds are worse: hardcoding the key \"just for now\" ends in a leaked key in git history;\nputting it in `~/.zshrc` leaks one project's key into every other project and every subprocess; a\n`config.py` with real values gets committed by accident. What people want is a per-project file that\nbehaves like environment variables, is easy to edit, and is ignored by git.\n\npython-dotenv is that bridge. The code path stays `os.environ[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"]` in every environment;\nonly the *source* of the variable changes. Locally a file fills it in; in production the platform does,\nand the library steps aside.","how":"### Finding the file\n`load_dotenv()` with no arguments calls `find_dotenv()`, which starts in the directory of the calling\nmodule (or the current directory in an interactive session) and walks up parent directories until it\nfinds a `.env`. It walks *up* because scripts are often run from a subfolder (`scripts/`, `notebooks/`)\nwhile the file sits at the project root. You can pass an explicit path instead, and\n`find_dotenv(usecwd=True)` searches from the working directory. If nothing is found, `load_dotenv()`\nreturns `False` rather than raising, because \"no file\" is the normal production case. The walk is a\nconvenience with two sharp corners: it stops at the *first* `.env` going up, so in a monorepo a parent\ndirectory's file can be picked up by a child project; and when the calling code lives in an installed\npackage (under `site-packages`), the walk starts there and never reaches your project. Passing an\nexplicit path in the entry point removes both ambiguities.\n\n### Parsing\nEach line is `KEY=value`, optionally prefixed with `export ` so the same file can be `source`d by a\nshell. Comments start with `#`. Single-quoted values are literal; double-quoted values support escapes\nsuch as `\\n` and can span lines, which is how people store PEM keys. With interpolation on (the default\nfor `load_dotenv`), `${OTHER}` expands from earlier lines or the existing environment, so\n`DB_URL=postgres://${DB_USER}@localhost/app` works.\n\n### The precedence rule\n`load_dotenv(override=False)` is the default, and it is the key design choice: a variable already in\nthe process environment wins over the file. This is *because* the file is meant to be the fallback for\nthe laptop. In CI or production, the platform-injected value is authoritative, and a stray `.env` baked\ninto an image must not silently replace it. Pass `override=True` only when you deliberately want the\nfile to win.\n\nA consequence people miss: with `override=False`, *the first source to set a key wins*. If you load\nseveral files, load the most specific one first (`load_dotenv(\".env.local\")` then\n`load_dotenv(\".env\")`), the opposite of the \"later overrides earlier\" order most config systems use.\nInterpolation follows the same precedence, and `${VAR:-default}` supplies a fallback when neither the\nenvironment nor the file defines `VAR`.\n\n### Lifecycle\nLoading is a one-time copy into the process environment, not a live binding. Editing `.env` changes\nnothing in a running process; you must restart it. Anything that captured a value before\n`load_dotenv()` ran (a module-level `client = OpenAI()`, a `settings` object built at import) keeps\nwhat it saw at that moment, which is why the load belongs on the first lines of the entry point.\n\n### Two ways to use the values\n- `load_dotenv()` mutates `os.environ`, so every library that reads env vars (the OpenAI SDK reads\n  `OPENAI_API_KEY` on its own) sees the value, as do subprocesses you spawn.\n- `dotenv_values(\".env\")` returns a plain dict and touches nothing. This is better for tests and for\n  merging several files (`{**dotenv_values(\".env.shared\"), **dotenv_values(\".env.local\")}`), because\n  global mutable state is avoided.\n\nThere is also a CLI (`dotenv run -- python app.py`, from the `cli` extra) that loads the file and then\nexecs the command, useful for tools you cannot edit.\n\n### What it does not do\nEvery value is a string. `DEBUG=false` gives you the non-empty string `\"false\"`, which is truthy. There\nis no schema, no required-field check and no secret masking; that is the job of a settings layer such\nas pydantic-settings, which can itself read `.env` files.","example":"A small entry point that loads local config, then fails fast and explicitly on what is missing.\n\n```python\n# .env (gitignored)                     .env.example (committed, no real values)\n#   OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-local-dev           OPENAI_API_KEY=\n#   MODEL=gpt-4o-mini                     MODEL=gpt-4o-mini\n#   MAX_TOKENS=512                        MAX_TOKENS=512\n#   DEBUG=false                           DEBUG=false\nimport os\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv, dotenv_values\n\nfound = load_dotenv()          # walks up to find .env; existing env vars win\nprint(\"loaded .env:\", found)   # False in production, where the platform sets vars\n\nREQUIRED = [\"OPENAI_API_KEY\", \"MODEL\"]\nmissing = [k for k in REQUIRED if not os.environ.get(k)]\nif missing:\n    raise SystemExit(f\"missing config: {missing}; copy .env.example to .env\")\n\nmodel = os.environ[\"MODEL\"]\nmax_tokens = int(os.environ.get(\"MAX_TOKENS\", \"256\"))      # strings only: convert yourself\ndebug = os.environ.get(\"DEBUG\", \"false\").lower() in {\"1\", \"true\", \"yes\"}\nprint(model, max_tokens, debug)  # bool(\"false\") would have been True\n\n# Without touching os.environ (good in tests):\ncfg = dotenv_values(\".env.example\")\nassert set(REQUIRED) <= cfg.keys(), \"example file is out of date\"\n```\n\nRunning `MODEL=claude-test python app.py` prints `claude-test`, because the shell's variable was set\nbefore `load_dotenv()` ran and `override` defaults to `False`.","tradeoffs":["One code path (`os.environ`) for every environment, at the cost of a second, implicit source of truth that can drift from what production injects; a committed `.env.example` keeps the drift visible.","Zero dependencies beyond the library and trivial to adopt, but you hand-roll type conversion and required checks; once there are more than a few settings, pydantic-settings pays for itself.","Mutating `os.environ` makes values visible to third-party SDKs and subprocesses, which is convenient until a child process or crash dump exposes a key it never needed; `dotenv_values` avoids that.","A plaintext file on disk is fine for a dev key with low limits, but for production secrets a secrets manager with rotation and audit wins regardless of team size."],"pitfalls":["Committing `.env` because `.gitignore` was added later; the symptom is a provider's leaked-key alert or a secret scanner hit, and rotating the key is mandatory because git history keeps it.","Expecting the file to win over a stale exported variable; the symptom is \"I changed the key in .env and nothing changed\" because an old `export` in the shell takes precedence under `override=False`.","Calling `load_dotenv()` after importing a module that read `os.environ` at import time; the symptom is a `None` key even though the file is correct, fixed by loading first thing in the entry point.","Treating `\"false\"` or `\"0\"` as falsy; the symptom is debug mode or a feature flag stuck on.","Copying `.env` into a Docker image with `COPY . .`; the symptom is real keys visible in a pulled image layer, prevented with `.dockerignore`.","Relying on the upward search from a script in a nested repo or an installed package; the symptom is values that come from the wrong project's `.env` or `load_dotenv()` returning `False` on a machine where the file plainly exists. Log the return value and pass an explicit path."],"when":"Use python-dotenv for local development, notebooks, demos and small scripts where the alternative is\nexporting variables by hand. Call it once, at the top of the entry point, and never inside library code,\nwhich should only read the environment it is given. Commit a `.env.example` listing every key. Stop\nrelying on it when settings need types, validation, nesting or per-environment profiles: move to\npydantic-settings, which reads the same file. Do not use it as the production secret delivery\nmechanism; production should get variables from the platform or a secrets manager, and your code should\nwork identically when no `.env` exists. In interviews, the signal is knowing the override default and why.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Loads `.env` once in `main` or `conftest.py`, keeps `.env.example` current, and converts string values explicitly instead of trusting truthiness.","architect":"Sets the rule that `.env` is a local fallback only, production secrets come from a managed store, and environment precedence is documented.","fde":"In a customer VPC, ships a `.env.example` and runbook, and checks the customer's platform injects variables rather than mounting a plaintext file."},"check":{"q":"Your service reads `OPENAI_API_KEY` via python-dotenv. In staging it uses the wrong key even\nthough the Kubernetes secret is correct. What are the likely causes and how do you fix it?","a":"First check whether a `.env` file got into the image (no `.dockerignore`) and whether the code\ncalls `load_dotenv(override=True)`; together those make the baked file beat the injected secret. If\n`override` is the default `False`, the injected variable should win, so the next suspect is that the pod\ndoes not actually receive it (wrong secret key name or namespace) and the file supplies a fallback. Fix\nby excluding `.env` from images, keeping the default precedence, and failing fast at startup when a\nrequired variable is absent, so no silent fallback exists."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `load_dotenv()` do by default when `OPENAI_API_KEY` is already set in the shell?","options":["Replaces it with the value from `.env`","Leaves the existing value in place and only fills variables that are not already set","Raises an error about a conflicting definition","Keeps both values and joins them with a separator"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The default is `override=False`, so the process environment wins. That lets production inject real values while `.env` only fills gaps on a laptop; `override=True` reverses the precedence."},{"q":"A `.env` has `ENABLE_CACHE=false`, and the code does `if os.environ[\"ENABLE_CACHE\"]:`. What happens?","options":["The branch is skipped because python-dotenv parses booleans","A TypeError is raised because the value is not a bool","The branch runs, because the value is the non-empty string \"false\"","The variable is ignored because lowercase booleans are invalid"],"answer":2,"explanation":"python-dotenv only produces strings, and any non-empty string is truthy in Python. Convert explicitly or use a typed settings layer such as pydantic-settings that parses \"false\" into False."},{"q":"When is `dotenv_values()` a better choice than `load_dotenv()`?","options":["When you need third-party SDKs to pick up the key automatically","When you want the values as a dict without mutating global process state, such as in tests","When the file uses `export` prefixes that `load_dotenv` cannot parse","When running in production without a `.env` file present"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`dotenv_values` returns a dict and leaves `os.environ` untouched, which avoids leakage between tests and makes merging files explicit. SDKs that read the environment themselves need `load_dotenv` instead."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pydantic-settings","sources":["Official docs: pydantic-settings","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"Reading configuration with `os.environ.get` scattered through the code has three failure modes that\nbite AI services in particular. Everything is a string, so `TIMEOUT=\"30\"` must be converted by hand at\neach call site, and `\"false\"` is truthy. Nothing is checked up front, so a missing `VECTOR_DB_URL` or a\ntypo in `RERANK_TOP_K` is discovered only when the first request reaches that code path, possibly hours\nafter deploy. And there is no single list of what the service needs, so a new environment is configured\nby trial and error.\n\nThe naive fix, a hand-written `config.py` that reads and converts every variable, works until it has\nthirty fields, nested groups (retrieval, model, tracing), lists of allowed models and secrets that must\nnot appear in logs. At that point you are reimplementing a validation library. Pydantic already is one,\nso pydantic-settings reuses it: the same type coercion, constraints and error messages, aimed at the\nprocess environment. It was split out of Pydantic into its own package with Pydantic v2, so it is a\nseparate install (`pip install pydantic-settings`).","how":"### Declaring settings\nSubclass `BaseSettings` and annotate fields exactly as in a normal Pydantic model. Behaviour is set with\n`model_config = SettingsConfigDict(...)`: `env_prefix` (so `APP_MODEL` fills `model`, avoiding collisions\nwith other software's variables), `env_file` (one path or a tuple, later files winning), `case_sensitive`\n(off by default), `env_nested_delimiter` and `extra`.\n\n### Where values come from, in priority order\nWhen you instantiate `Settings()`, each field is resolved from the highest-priority source that has it:\n\n1. Keyword arguments passed to the constructor (used by tests).\n2. Environment variables.\n3. The dotenv file(s).\n4. Files in `secrets_dir`, one file per field, which is how Docker and Kubernetes secrets are mounted.\n5. Field defaults.\n\nThe ordering is deliberate: the platform's environment beats a local file, which matches python-dotenv's\ndefault and means a stale `.env` cannot override production. You can reorder or add sources (a vault,\na YAML file) by overriding the `settings_customise_sources` classmethod.\n\nMechanically, each source is just a callable that returns a dict of raw values for the fields it knows\nabout. pydantic-settings calls them, deep-merges the dicts so a higher-priority source overwrites a\nlower one key by key, and then runs one ordinary Pydantic validation over the merged dict. That design\nis why everything Pydantic can do (coercion, validators, nested models, aliases) works unchanged for\nconfig, and why a custom source only has to produce a dict. Note the asymmetry with python-dotenv:\nwithin `env_file=(\".env\", \".env.local\")` the *later* file wins, whereas repeated `load_dotenv` calls\nkeep the first value.\n\nWhen a variable's name is fixed by someone else (the OpenAI SDK's `OPENAI_API_KEY`, a platform's\n`DATABASE_URL`), give the field `validation_alias=\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"` (or `AliasChoices` for several\naccepted names) instead of bending your prefix convention.\n\n### Parsing beyond strings\nSimple types are coerced with Pydantic's normal rules, so `\"30\"` becomes `30.0` for a `float` field and\n`\"false\"` becomes `False` for a `bool`. Complex fields (`list`, `dict`, nested models) are parsed from a\nJSON string, so `APP_ALLOWED_MODELS='[\"a\",\"b\"]'` works. With `env_nested_delimiter=\"__\"`,\n`APP_RETRIEVAL__TOP_K=20` sets one field of a nested model without restating the rest. Constraints\n(`Field(gt=0)`, `Literal[\"dev\",\"prod\"]`) are enforced exactly as for API input, and a failure raises a\nsingle `ValidationError` listing every bad field at once, because one deploy should report all problems\nrather than one per restart.\n\n### Secrets\nType sensitive fields as `SecretStr`. The value is masked in `repr`, `str` and default serialization, so\n`print(settings)` or a crash log shows `**********`; you must call `.get_secret_value()` at the point of\nuse. This does not encrypt anything; it prevents the most common leak, which is logging a config object.\n\n### Where it lives at runtime\nConstruct settings once and share them: a cached `get_settings()` function, or an object built in the\nFastAPI lifespan and injected as a dependency. That gives you one validation at startup and an easy\noverride in tests (construct a `Settings(...)` with explicit kwargs and `_env_file=None`).","example":"A settings class for a RAG service, and what happens with real environment values.\n\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, SecretStr\nfrom pydantic_settings import BaseSettings, SettingsConfigDict\n\nclass Retrieval(BaseModel):\n    top_k: int = Field(8, ge=1, le=100)\n    rerank: bool = True\n\nclass Settings(BaseSettings):\n    model_config = SettingsConfigDict(\n        env_prefix=\"APP_\", env_file=\".env\", env_nested_delimiter=\"__\", extra=\"ignore\")\n    env: Literal[\"dev\", \"staging\", \"prod\"] = \"dev\"\n    openai_api_key: SecretStr                     # required: no default\n    model: str = \"gpt-4o-mini\"\n    request_timeout_s: float = Field(30.0, gt=0)\n    allowed_models: list[str] = [\"gpt-4o-mini\"]\n    retrieval: Retrieval = Retrieval()\n\n@lru_cache\ndef get_settings() -> Settings:\n    return Settings()\n\n# Environment:\n#   APP_OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-live-...      APP_ENV=prod\n#   APP_RETRIEVAL__TOP_K=20             APP_ALLOWED_MODELS='[\"gpt-4o-mini\",\"gpt-4o\"]'\ns = get_settings()\nprint(s.retrieval)          # top_k=20 rerank=True   (nested override, default kept)\nprint(s.openai_api_key)     # **********             (masked)\nclient_key = s.openai_api_key.get_secret_value()\n\n# In a test, ignore the developer's .env (real env vars still apply, so\n# kwargs or monkeypatch.delenv must cover anything the shell might set):\nt = Settings(_env_file=None, openai_api_key=\"test\", env=\"dev\")\n```\n\nIf staging sets `APP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_S=thirty` and `APP_ENV=production`, startup fails with one\n`ValidationError` reporting two errors: `request_timeout_s` is not a valid number and `env` is not one\nof `'dev', 'staging', 'prod'`. The pod crash-loops visibly instead of serving traffic with a broken\ntimeout.","tradeoffs":["Fail-fast validation turns silent misconfiguration into a crash at startup, which is right for services but annoying for scripts that only need one value; there, plain `os.environ` is simpler.","One typed object documents every setting, but it couples config to Pydantic's coercion rules, so lax coercion (`\"1\"` to `True`) can surprise; strict mode trades convenience for exactness.","JSON-in-env for lists and nested models keeps everything in variables, yet quoting JSON inside shell, YAML and Helm templates is error-prone; past a few nested fields a mounted config file is cleaner.","`SecretStr` masks accidental logging but adds `.get_secret_value()` noise, and it is not a substitute for a secrets manager with rotation; it only governs how the value is printed."],"pitfalls":["Instantiating `Settings()` at import time in a library module; the symptom is tests failing to import because a required key is missing, fixed by constructing lazily or in the entry point.","Forgetting `env_prefix` and naming a field `path` or `home`; the symptom is the field mysteriously filled from the system's own `PATH` or `HOME` variable.","Leaving `extra` at its default (`\"forbid\"`) when sharing a `.env` with other tools; the symptom is a validation error about unexpected keys from the dotenv file (environment variables never trigger it), fixed with `extra=\"ignore\"`.","Expecting a nested override without setting `env_nested_delimiter`; the symptom is `APP_RETRIEVAL__TOP_K` being silently ignored and the default used.","Caching settings with `lru_cache` and then changing env vars in a test; the symptom is old values, fixed with `get_settings.cache_clear()` or dependency overrides."],"when":"Reach for pydantic-settings in any long-running service or worker with more than a few settings,\nanything with secrets, and anything deployed to more than one environment. It is the default choice in\nFastAPI codebases because settings plug straight into dependency injection. Keep plain `os.environ` or\npython-dotenv for throwaway scripts and notebooks. Measure success by how config failures surface: they\nshould appear as startup errors naming the field, never as request-time `KeyError`s or wrong behaviour.\nIn interviews, explain the source precedence and why secrets are typed as `SecretStr`.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the `Settings` class, injects it through a cached dependency, and overrides it in tests with explicit kwargs instead of patching environment variables.","architect":"Decides which settings exist per environment, the prefix convention, which values come from a secrets store, and that services fail closed on invalid config.","fde":"Hands the customer a documented settings schema and `.env.example`, and reads the startup `ValidationError` to diagnose their environment in minutes."},"check":{"q":"Your platform team rotated the API key by updating a Kubernetes secret mounted as a file in\n`secrets_dir`. The pods restarted, but they still call the provider with the old key. An old ConfigMap\nalso sets `APP_OPENAI_API_KEY`. Explain what happened and two ways to fix it.","a":"Sources are merged by priority: init kwargs, environment, dotenv, secret files, defaults. The\nstale ConfigMap value arrives as an environment variable, which outranks the mounted secret file, so the\nrotation was silently shadowed; the restart re-read both and the env var won again. Fix one: delete the\nvariable from the ConfigMap so the secret file is the only source. Fix two, if the team wants files to\nbe authoritative by design: override `settings_customise_sources` to return the file-secret source\nahead of the environment source. Either way, add a startup log of *which source* supplied each secret\n(never the value), so the next shadowing is visible, and remember a running process never re-reads its\nsettings without a restart."},"quiz":[{"q":"`APP_MODEL` is set in the environment, `.env` sets `APP_MODEL` differently, and the field has a default. Which value wins?","options":["The `.env` value, because files are read last","The environment variable, because it outranks dotenv files and defaults","The default, because explicit defaults always win","Startup fails because of the conflicting definitions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Priority is constructor kwargs, then environment variables, then dotenv, then secret files, then defaults. This matches the deployment model: the platform's injected value beats a local file."},{"q":"Why type an API key as `SecretStr` rather than `str`?","options":["It encrypts the value in memory","It validates that the key has the provider's format","It masks the value when the settings object is printed, logged or serialized","It loads the value from a secrets manager automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`SecretStr` only changes representation, so logging the config shows asterisks. You still need a real secrets store and must call `.get_secret_value()` where the key is actually used."},{"q":"How would you set only `retrieval.top_k` from the environment for a nested `Retrieval` model?","options":["`APP_RETRIEVAL.TOP_K=20`, which works by default","Set `env_nested_delimiter=\"__\"` and use `APP_RETRIEVAL__TOP_K=20`","Nested models cannot be configured from the environment at all","Set `APP_TOP_K=20`, since nested fields are flattened automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without a configured delimiter, nested models are only settable as a whole JSON string. With `env_nested_delimiter`, a single nested field can be overridden while the others keep their defaults."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/json-jsonl","sources":["Bray, 2017 — RFC 8259: The JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) Data Interchange Format","Official docs: Python json module","Official docs: JSON Lines"],"why":"Almost everything an AI engineer moves around is a record with nested, loosely structured fields: a\nchat transcript with a variable number of messages, a tool call with arguments, a model response with\nmetadata. CSV cannot represent nesting without inventing an escaping scheme, and binary formats need a\nschema up front and are not readable in a terminal. JSON represents nested objects and arrays directly,\nevery language parses it, and LLMs produce it reliably enough that providers expose JSON modes.\n\nBut a single JSON document is a bad container for *many* records. A file holding one big array\n`[{...}, {...}, ...]` must be parsed in full before you can use the first record, cannot be appended to\nwithout rewriting the closing bracket, cannot be split across workers without parsing, and is corrupted\nas a whole if a writer crashes before the final `]`. With a million eval cases that means holding the\nwhole dataset in memory just to read one.\n\nJSONL fixes this with one rule: each line is a self-contained JSON value, separated by `\\n`. Appending\nis writing a line. Reading is iterating lines. Splitting is `split -l`. A crash damages at most the last\nline. Because standard JSON encoders escape newlines inside strings as `\\n`, any JSON value can be\nserialized onto one line, so the rule costs nothing in expressiveness.","how":"### JSON's data model\nRFC 8259 defines six types: object, array, string, number, `true`/`false`, `null`. There are no comments,\nno trailing commas, no dates, no bytes and no NaN or Infinity. Strings are Unicode and, for interchange,\nthe text must be UTF-8. Python's `json` maps these to `dict`, `list`, `str`, `int`/`float`, `bool` and\n`None`. Anything else (datetimes, `Decimal`, sets, most numpy scalars such as `np.int64` and\n`np.float32`) raises `TypeError` on `dumps` unless you convert it (`np.float64` slips through only\nbecause it subclasses Python `float`), typically with a `default=` function or by serializing a\nPydantic model with `model_dump_json()`.\n\n### The edges where Python's defaults bite\n- **NaN and Infinity.** `json.dumps(float(\"nan\"))` emits `NaN`, which is *not* valid JSON; JavaScript's\n  `JSON.parse` and strict parsers reject it. Pass `allow_nan=False` to fail at write time instead.\n- **Big integers.** Python round-trips any integer exactly, but parsers that use IEEE doubles (JavaScript)\n  lose precision above 2^53. IDs from Snowflake-style generators should travel as strings.\n- **Non-ASCII.** `ensure_ascii=True` is the default, so `café` is written as `caf\\u00e9`. That is valid,\n  but inflates files and hurts readability for multilingual data; `ensure_ascii=False` writes UTF-8, which\n  is correct as long as the file is opened with `encoding=\"utf-8\"`.\n- **Duplicate keys.** The RFC leaves them undefined; Python keeps the last one silently.\n\n### JSONL mechanics\nWrite: `f.write(json.dumps(obj) + \"\\n\")` per record, with no pretty-printing, because `indent=` inserts\nnewlines and breaks the one-record-per-line rule. Read: iterate the file object line by line and\n`json.loads` each, which keeps memory proportional to one record. Writers should treat the file as\nappend-only and flush per record or per batch; readers should report errors with line numbers so a bad\nrecord can be located, and decide explicitly whether to skip or stop. Compression composes cleanly:\n`gzip.open(path, \"wt\")` gives `.jsonl.gz` with the same line semantics.\n\nThree details decide whether the crash-isolation promise actually holds:\n- **What counts as a line.** Iterating a text file splits only on `\\n` (and `\\r\\n`), which is what\n  JSONL means. `str.splitlines()` also splits on characters such as U+2028 and U+0085, which\n  `json.dumps(..., ensure_ascii=False)` leaves unescaped inside strings, so reading a file via\n  `text.splitlines()` can cut a valid record in half. Iterate the file, or split on `\"\\n\"` explicitly.\n- **The torn last line.** A writer killed mid-write leaves a final line with no trailing newline that\n  usually fails to parse. A reader resuming a job should tolerate exactly that case (drop it, then\n  truncate or rewrite the file) and treat a bad line anywhere else as real corruption.\n- **One writer per file.** Several processes appending to the same file can interleave their bytes\n  when records are large or the file lives on network storage, producing lines that are two half\n  records. Give each worker its own shard (`results-00.jsonl`, `results-01.jsonl`) or funnel writes\n  through one process.\n\n### Where you meet it\nOpenAI-style fine-tuning datasets are JSONL with one `{\"messages\": [...]}` object per line; the OpenAI\nBatch API takes a JSONL file of requests, each with a `custom_id` so results can be matched back; eval\nharnesses store cases and results as JSONL; structured logs are usually one JSON object per line. For\nanalytics over millions of rows, JSONL is usually converted to a columnar format such as Parquet, since\nre-parsing text on every query is slow.","example":"Writing an eval set safely and reading it back as a stream, with failures pinned to a line.\n\n```python\nimport json, math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ncases = [\n    {\"id\": \"q1\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Refund policy?\\nAsk twice.\"}],\n     \"expected\": \"policy\", \"score\": 0.9},\n    {\"id\": \"q2\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Café opening hours\"}],\n     \"expected\": \"hours\", \"score\": math.nan},          # a bad value we must catch\n]\n\ndef write_jsonl(path: Path, rows) -> None:\n    with path.open(\"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        for row in rows:\n            # allow_nan=False refuses to emit the invalid token NaN\n            f.write(json.dumps(row, ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False) + \"\\n\")\n\ndef read_jsonl(path: Path):\n    with path.open(encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        for lineno, line in enumerate(f, 1):\n            if not line.strip():\n                continue\n            try:\n                yield json.loads(line)\n            except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{path}:{lineno}: {e.msg}\") from e\n\ncases[1][\"score\"] = None                               # fix: null is valid JSON\np = Path(\"eval.jsonl\")\nwrite_jsonl(p, cases)\nprint(p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").count(\"\\n\"))       # 2: the embedded newline was escaped\nfor case in read_jsonl(p):                             # one record in memory at a time\n    print(case[\"id\"], len(case[\"messages\"]))\n```\n\nWithout the fix, `write_jsonl` raises `ValueError: Out of range float values are not JSON compliant`\non the second record, at write time, instead of producing a file that a TypeScript dashboard later\nrefuses to parse.","tradeoffs":["JSONL streams and appends record by record, but loses the single-document guarantees: there is no enclosing schema, so a malformed middle line is only found when a reader reaches it.","Text JSON is human-readable and universal, yet it is several times larger and slower to parse than columnar binary formats; once you run repeated analytical queries over millions of rows, Parquet wins.","Schemaless records make it easy to add fields to eval or log data, but readers drift silently unless a Pydantic model or JSON Schema validates each line at the boundary.","`ensure_ascii=False` keeps multilingual data compact and readable, but any consumer that guesses the wrong encoding will mangle it; ASCII escapes are safer when you do not control the reader."],"pitfalls":["Using `json.dump(obj, f, indent=2)` in a JSONL writer; the symptom is a `JSONDecodeError` on the very first line (a lone `{` is not a complete value) because each record now spans many lines.","Serializing NaN from pandas or numpy metrics; the symptom is a JavaScript or Go consumer rejecting the file while Python reads it fine.","Calling `f.readlines()` or building `[json.loads(l) for l in f]` on a multi-gigabyte JSONL file; the symptom is memory climbing to several times the file size (parsed Python objects are far larger than their text) and the process being OOM-killed. (`json.load` on JSONL fails outright with \"Extra data\".)","Reading with `path.read_text().splitlines()`; the symptom is a `JSONDecodeError` (\"Unterminated string\") on a handful of records that contain U+2028 or similar characters, often scraped web text.","Opening files without `encoding=\"utf-8\"`; the symptom is `UnicodeDecodeError` or mojibake on Windows or in containers with a non-UTF-8 locale.","Passing numpy types into `json.dumps`; the symptom is `TypeError: Object of type float32 is not JSON serializable` deep inside an eval run."],"when":"Use JSON for single payloads: API requests and responses, tool arguments, config documents, a model's\nstructured output. Use JSONL whenever you have a collection of records that grows over time or is\nprocessed one at a time: eval datasets, fine-tuning data, batch-inference inputs and outputs, agent\ntraces and logs. Validate each line at ingestion with a Pydantic model so bad records fail with a line\nnumber. Move to Parquet or a database when you need column-wise queries, joins or typed schemas across\nmillions of rows. Interviewers often ask why JSONL rather than a JSON array; the answer is streaming,\nappending and crash isolation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes JSONL readers as generators with line-numbered errors, sets `allow_nan=False`, and validates every record against a Pydantic model before it enters an eval or training run.","architect":"Chooses where JSONL is the interchange format and where data is promoted to Parquet or a database, and defines the record schema and versioning for datasets.","fde":"Converts a customer's CSV or ticket export into a validated JSONL eval set, catching encoding and malformed-row problems before they poison results."},"check":{"q":"Your batch-inference job writes 2 million results to a single JSON array file, and a pod restart at\n90% leaves it unusable. Redesign it and explain why each choice helps.","a":"Write results as JSONL, one object per line, each carrying the request's `custom_id`. A restart\nthen damages at most the final partial line, and the reader can drop a last line that fails to parse. On\nresume, read the existing file, collect completed IDs and only submit the rest, which makes the job\nidempotent. Flush after each batch so durability matches progress. Downstream consumers can stream the\nfile without loading 2 million objects, and shards can be split by line for parallel scoring. If the\nresults feed analytics, convert the finished JSONL to Parquet."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is JSONL preferred over a single JSON array for a large, growing eval dataset?","options":["JSONL files are always smaller than equivalent JSON arrays","Each line parses independently, so the file can be streamed, appended to and split without parsing the whole thing","JSONL supports comments and trailing commas that JSON forbids","JSONL stores numbers in binary, which is faster to parse"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The size and syntax are essentially the same as JSON. The advantage is structural: one record per line means constant memory reads, appends without rewriting, line-based sharding and crash damage limited to one line."},{"q":"What does Python's `json.dumps({\"x\": float(\"nan\")})` produce by default?","options":["`{\"x\": null}`, because NaN maps to null","A `TypeError` because NaN is not serializable","`{\"x\": NaN}`, which is not valid JSON and strict parsers reject","`{\"x\": \"NaN\"}`, a string representation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Python defaults to `allow_nan=True` and emits the JavaScript-style token NaN, which RFC 8259 does not allow. Setting `allow_nan=False` raises a ValueError instead, catching the bad value at write time."},{"q":"A JSONL writer uses `json.dumps(record, indent=2)`. What breaks?","options":["Nothing; readers ignore whitespace inside JSON values","Only non-ASCII characters are affected","The file becomes valid JSON instead of JSONL","Each record spans several lines, so line-by-line readers fail to parse"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Indentation inserts newlines between keys. JSON parsers tolerate whitespace inside one document, but JSONL readers split on newlines first, so each fragment is an incomplete value and parsing fails."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/numpy","sources":["Harris et al., 2020 — Array programming with NumPy","Official docs: NumPy"],"why":"A Python list of floats is an array of pointers to separate heap objects, each boxed with a type and a\nreference count. Computing a dot product over it means, per element, two pointer chases, two type checks,\nan allocation for the result and an interpreter dispatch. For a retrieval prototype scoring a query\nagainst 100,000 embeddings of 768 dimensions, that is about 77 million multiply-adds through the\ninterpreter, which takes seconds to minutes in pure Python where the same work in compiled code takes\nmilliseconds (illustrative orders of magnitude; measure on your machine).\n\nThe memory cost is just as bad. A boxed Python float is far larger than its 8 bytes of payload, so lists\nof numbers use several times the memory of the raw values and scatter them across the heap, which ruins\nCPU cache behaviour.\n\nNumPy's answer is to store numbers unboxed in one buffer with a single dtype, and to expose operations\nthat act on the whole buffer. The loop still exists, but it runs in C (or in an optimized BLAS library\nfor matrix products) over contiguous memory. Nearly the whole scientific Python stack, pandas,\nscikit-learn, and the tensor interop in PyTorch, is built on or speaks this array model.","how":"### The ndarray: buffer, dtype, shape, strides\nAn array is a pointer to raw bytes plus metadata: `dtype` (for example `float32`, 4 bytes), `shape`\n(for example `(100000, 768)`) and `strides`, the number of bytes to step to move one position along each\naxis. For a C-ordered (row-major) float32 matrix of shape `(100000, 768)`, strides are `(3072, 4)`.\nElement `[i, j]` lives at `offset + i*3072 + j*4`. Memory is exactly `n × d × itemsize`:\n100,000 × 768 × 4 = 307,200,000 bytes, about 307 MB. In float64 it would be twice that, which is why\nembeddings are usually kept in float32.\n\n### Views versus copies\nBecause indexing is just arithmetic on strides, many operations return a *view* that shares the buffer:\nbasic slicing (`a[:10]`, `a[:, ::2]`), `reshape` when possible, and transpose (`a.T` just swaps the\nstrides). This is a design choice for speed: slicing a 307 MB array costs nothing. The consequence is\nthat writing into a view writes into the original. Fancy indexing with integer arrays or boolean masks\n(`a[[1, 5, 9]]`, `a[a > 0]`) must gather scattered elements, so it returns a copy.\n\n### Contiguity and layout\nViews are free because they change strides, not bytes, and that leaves many arrays *non-contiguous*:\n`a.T` of a C-ordered matrix is Fortran-ordered, `a[:, ::2]` skips every other element. NumPy's own\noperations accept any strides, but walking memory with a large stride wastes cache lines, so reducing\nalong the contiguous axis is faster than along the strided one. More importantly, compiled extensions\nthat take a raw pointer (FAISS, many C/CUDA kernels, some serialization paths) expect C-contiguous\nmemory of a specific dtype. Check `a.flags[\"C_CONTIGUOUS\"]` and convert once with\n`np.ascontiguousarray(a, dtype=np.float32)` at the boundary; that is the one place a copy is the\ncorrect choice. `reshape` returns a view only when the existing strides can express the new shape;\notherwise it silently copies, so after a transpose a reshape may cost a full copy.\n\n### Vectorization and ufuncs\nElement-wise operations (`+`, `np.exp`, comparisons) are *ufuncs*: compiled loops applied over every\nelement. Reductions (`sum`, `max`, `argmax`) take an `axis`, the dimension that collapses: `a.sum(axis=1)`\non `(N, d)` gives `(N,)`. Matrix products (`@`) dispatch to BLAS, which uses SIMD and multiple cores.\nMany of these release the GIL while they run, so threads can overlap NumPy work.\n\n### Broadcasting\nTo combine arrays of different shapes without copying, NumPy aligns shapes from the right; each pair of\ndimensions must be equal or one of them 1, and size-1 dimensions are stretched virtually (stride 0).\n`(N, d) / (N, 1)` divides each row by its own norm; `(N, d) - (d,)` subtracts a mean vector from every\nrow. This is powerful and also the top source of silent bugs: `(N,) - (N, 1)` broadcasts to an `(N, N)`\nmatrix rather than raising an error.\n\n### Dtypes and numerical behaviour\nInteger arrays have fixed width and wrap on overflow without an exception (a `uint8` pixel of 250 plus 10\nbecomes 4). Float32 has about 7 significant decimal digits, enough for similarity scores but risky for\nlong accumulations. NumPy's `float32` scalars are not Python floats, so they do not serialize to JSON\nuntil you call `.item()` or `.tolist()`.\n\nMixed dtypes promote to the wider one: `float32_array * float64_array` produces float64, so one\nfloat64 array (a weights vector built with `np.array([...])`, a result loaded from an older file) can\nsilently double the memory of everything downstream. A plain Python scalar does not force the upcast\n(`emb * 0.5` stays float32). A NumPy `float64` scalar, such as a value pulled out of another array,\ndoes under NumPy 2's promotion rules, whereas NumPy 1.x let it slide, so upgrades can change dtypes.\nCheck `result.dtype` at stage boundaries rather than assuming.\n\n### Randomness\nUse `rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)` and pass `rng` around rather than seeding a global state, because\nexplicit generators make experiments reproducible and do not interfere across libraries.","example":"Exact top-k cosine search over 100,000 embeddings, the core of every retrieval prototype.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\ndocs = rng.standard_normal((100_000, 768), dtype=np.float32)   # ~307 MB\nquery = rng.standard_normal(768, dtype=np.float32)\nprint(docs.nbytes, docs.strides)          # 307200000 (3072, 4)\n\n# Normalize rows: (N, 768) / (N, 1) broadcasts per row; /= writes into docs in place\ndocs /= np.linalg.norm(docs, axis=1, keepdims=True)\nquery /= np.linalg.norm(query)\n\nscores = docs @ query                     # (100000,) via one BLAS matrix-vector product\nk = 5\ntop = np.argpartition(-scores, k)[:k]     # O(N) selection of the k best, unordered\ntop = top[np.argsort(-scores[top])]       # sort only those k\nprint(top, scores[top].round(3))\n\n# Pitfall demo: forgetting keepdims gives shape (N,), which cannot broadcast against (N, 768)\nnorms = np.linalg.norm(docs, axis=1)      # (100000,)\n# docs / norms would raise: operands could not be broadcast together\nhead = docs[:10]                          # a view: head[:] = 0 would zero rows of docs\npicked = docs[[0, 5, 9]]                  # fancy indexing: a copy, safe to modify\nprint(np.shares_memory(head, docs), np.shares_memory(picked, docs))  # True False\n```\n\n`argpartition` avoids sorting 100,000 scores to find 5, and `keepdims=True` keeps the norm as\n`(N, 1)` so the division broadcasts per row. Past a few million vectors, exact search like this is\nreplaced by an approximate index.","tradeoffs":["Whole-array operations run at compiled speed, but every intermediate is a full temporary array, so memory can peak at several copies of your data; in-place operators and `out=` arguments help.","Views make slicing and transposes free, at the cost of aliasing: a mutation through a view changes the original, which is a correctness hazard in shared code paths.","float32 halves memory and speeds up BLAS compared with float64, but loses precision in long sums or ill-conditioned linear algebra, where float64 is worth the cost.","NumPy runs on CPU in a single process; once arrays exceed RAM or you need GPUs or autograd, PyTorch, memory-mapped arrays or a vector database take over."],"pitfalls":["Writing Python loops over array elements; the symptom is a NumPy script that is no faster than pure Python, revealed by a profiler showing time in the interpreter, not in NumPy calls.","Accidental broadcasting of `(N,)` against `(N, 1)`; the symptom is an unexpectedly huge array, a `MemoryError`, or a metric that looks plausible but is averaged over N² pairs.","Modifying a slice and corrupting the source array; the symptom is embeddings or features changing \"by themselves\" after an unrelated function ran.","Integer overflow in fixed-width dtypes; the symptom is negative counts or wrapped pixel values with no warning raised.","Default float64 from `np.array(list_of_floats)` for embeddings, or one float64 operand promoting a float32 pipeline; the symptom is double the expected memory and slower matrix products, visible in `arr.dtype` and `arr.nbytes`.","Passing a transposed or sliced array to a C extension that assumes C-contiguous float32; the symptom is an explicit \"array is not C-contiguous\" error or, with careless bindings, garbage results."],"when":"Use NumPy for anything numerical that fits in memory on one machine: embedding similarity, metric\ncomputation, feature arrays for scikit-learn, image and audio buffers, and as the interchange format\nbetween libraries. Think about shapes first: write the expected shape next to each line. Reach for\npandas or Polars when your data is tabular with named, mixed-type columns; for PyTorch when you need\nGPUs or gradients; and for a vector index when exact search over millions of vectors becomes too slow.\nMeasure with `nbytes`, a profiler and `%timeit` rather than guessing. Interviews often ask you to\nvectorize a loop or explain broadcasting.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Vectorizes scoring and metrics, tracks shapes and dtypes explicitly, and uses float32 and `argpartition` for retrieval prototypes.","architect":"Uses `n × d × bytes` arithmetic to size in-memory indexes and decides when exact NumPy search must give way to an approximate vector store.","fde":"Loads a customer's embedding dump with NumPy, checks norms, dtypes and NaNs, and runs exact search as a ground-truth baseline for their vector database."},"check":{"q":"You need cosine similarity between 50,000 queries and 1,000,000 documents, both 768-d float32.\nA colleague writes `Q @ D.T` in one line. What happens and what would you do?","a":"The result matrix is 50,000 × 1,000,000 float32 values, 200 GB, so it will not fit in memory, on\ntop of 3 GB for D and about 154 MB for Q. Normalize both once, then process queries in batches (say\n1,000 at a time, a 4 GB result per batch, or smaller), and for each batch take `argpartition` top-k per\nrow and keep only those indices and scores, discarding the full block. If this runs regularly, an\napproximate nearest-neighbour index is the right tool, with the exact batched computation kept as the\nrecall baseline."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which operation returns a view sharing memory with the original array `a` of shape (1000, 64)?","options":["`a[[0, 10, 20]]`","`a[a[:, 0] > 0]`","`a[:, ::2]`","`np.sort(a, axis=0)`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Basic slicing with step sizes only changes offset and strides, so it shares the buffer. Integer and boolean indexing must gather scattered rows into new memory, and sort returns a new array."},{"q":"`x` has shape (500,) and `y` has shape (500, 1). What is the shape of `x - y`?","options":["(500,)","(500, 1)","(500, 500)","An error, because the shapes differ"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Broadcasting aligns trailing dimensions: (1, 500) against (500, 1) stretches both to (500, 500). No error is raised, which is why this silent shape blow-up is a common bug in metric code."},{"q":"Why is `np.argpartition` preferred over `np.argsort` for top-5 retrieval over a million scores?","options":["It returns the results already sorted by score","It finds the k best in linear time without fully sorting the array","It runs on the GPU automatically","It handles NaN scores while argsort cannot"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A full sort is O(N log N) work for an answer that needs only k items. Partitioning is linear and leaves the top k unordered, so you sort just those k afterwards, which is cheap."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pandas","sources":["McKinney, 2010 — Data Structures for Statistical Computing in Python","Official docs: pandas"],"why":"NumPy arrays hold one dtype and address data by integer position. Real AI-engineering data is not like\nthat: an eval run produces rows with a case ID, model name, category string, pass/fail boolean, latency,\ntoken counts and a timestamp. You want to ask \"pass rate and p95 latency per model per category\", join\nin a price table, and filter to failures to read them. Doing that with lists of dicts means hand-written\nloops for every group-by and join, slow and easy to get wrong; doing it with raw NumPy means keeping\nparallel arrays aligned by hand.\n\nSQL answers these questions well, but loading data into a database for a ten-minute analysis is\nfriction, and SQL is awkward for the iterative, look-at-it-and-adjust workflow of a notebook. pandas\nbrought the data-frame model familiar from R into Python: named columns of different types, row labels,\nand a large vocabulary of vectorized operations, all in process with your other Python code. That is\nwhy so much of the ecosystem, from scikit-learn inputs to plotting libraries, accepts DataFrames.","how":"### Columns, dtypes and the index\nA DataFrame is essentially an ordered collection of columns (each a `Series`) sharing one `Index` of row\nlabels. Each column is backed by a NumPy array or an extension array, so numeric columns get NumPy speed.\nStrings were historically stored in `object` columns, arrays of pointers to Python objects, which are\nslow and memory-hungry; newer pandas versions offer dedicated string dtypes (pandas 3.0 makes a\nstring dtype the default for text) and, since pandas 2.0, optional Arrow-backed columns\n(`dtype_backend=\"pyarrow\"`) that store data in Arrow's columnar format.\n\nMissing values are a historical compromise: a float column uses `NaN`, so an integer column that gains\none missing value silently becomes `float64`. Nullable dtypes (`Int64`, `boolean`, `string`) use a\nseparate mask instead, keeping the type.\n\n### Alignment\nArithmetic between Series aligns on index *labels*, not positions. `a + b` matches rows with the same\nlabel and yields `NaN` where a label exists on only one side. This was designed for time series, where\naligning by date is what you want; for freshly filtered frames with gappy indexes it causes silent NaNs,\nwhich is why `reset_index(drop=True)` appears so often.\n\n### Vectorized operations and their escape hatch\nColumn expressions such as `df[\"cost\"] = df[\"tokens\"] / 1000 * df[\"price\"]` run as NumPy operations over\nwhole columns. `df.apply(f, axis=1)` calls a Python function per row, which brings back interpreter\nspeed, so it should be a last resort. String and datetime operations live under `.str` and `.dt`.\n\n### Group-by, joins, reshaping\n`groupby` implements split-apply-combine: split rows by key, apply an aggregation per group, combine\ninto a new frame. Named aggregation (`agg(pass_rate=(\"passed\", \"mean\"))`) keeps output columns explicit.\n`merge` performs SQL-style joins; its `validate=` argument checks the expected cardinality so a\nduplicate key cannot silently multiply rows. `pivot_table` and `melt` switch between wide and long shapes.\n\nUnder the hood, `groupby` first *factorizes* the key columns: one hash pass maps each distinct key to\nan integer group code. Built-in aggregations named by string (`\"mean\"`, `\"sum\"`, `\"size\"`) then run a\ncompiled loop that adds each row's value into its group's accumulator, so cost is roughly linear in rows\nwhatever the number of groups. A lambda or custom function instead gets called once per group with a\nSeries, which is fine for tens of groups and slow for a million. Two defaults matter: `dropna=True`\ndrops rows whose key is missing, and for categorical keys `observed=` decides whether empty\ncategories appear as rows.\n\n### Indexing, copies and views\nUse `.loc[row_mask, \"col\"]` for label-based selection and assignment and `.iloc` for positions. Chained\nindexing such as `df[mask][\"col\"] = x` may assign into a temporary copy and leave `df` unchanged, the\norigin of the `SettingWithCopyWarning`. pandas 3.0 made Copy-on-Write the default: every derived\nobject behaves as a copy and real copying is deferred until a write. That removes the ambiguity in one\ndirection: chained assignment *never* modifies `df`, and pandas emits a `ChainedAssignmentError`\nwarning instead. Code written for pandas 2.x that relied on chained assignment silently stops working.\n\n### Execution model\npandas is eager: each operation materializes a full result immediately, mostly on a single core. With\nintermediates, a common rule of thumb is to have several times the dataset's size available as RAM.\nData larger than memory needs chunked reading (`chunksize=`), a columnar engine such as Polars or DuckDB,\nor a database.","example":"Summarising an eval run: pass rate, tail latency and cost per model and category.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({          # in practice: pd.read_json(\"results.jsonl\", lines=True)\n    \"case_id\":    [1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3],\n    \"model\":      [\"small\", \"large\"] * 3,\n    \"category\":   [\"refund\", \"refund\", \"billing\", \"billing\", \"refund\", \"refund\"],\n    \"passed\":     [False, True, True, True, False, True],\n    \"latency_ms\": [420, 1310, 380, 1250, 450, 1400],\n    \"out_tokens\": [120, 180, 90, 150, 130, 200],\n})\nprice = pd.DataFrame({\"model\": [\"small\", \"large\"],\n                      \"usd_per_1k_out\": [0.0006, 0.01]})     # illustrative prices\n\ndf = df.merge(price, on=\"model\", how=\"left\", validate=\"many_to_one\")\ndf[\"cost_usd\"] = df[\"out_tokens\"] / 1000 * df[\"usd_per_1k_out\"]   # column math, no loop\n\nsummary = (df.groupby([\"model\", \"category\"])\n             .agg(pass_rate=(\"passed\", \"mean\"),\n                  p95_ms=(\"latency_ms\", lambda s: s.quantile(0.95)),\n                  cost_usd=(\"cost_usd\", \"sum\"),\n                  n=(\"case_id\", \"size\"))\n             .reset_index())\nprint(summary)\n\n# Wrong: chained indexing may write into a temporary copy\n# df[df[\"model\"] == \"small\"][\"passed\"] = True\ndf.loc[df[\"model\"] == \"small\", \"reviewed\"] = True             # right: one .loc call\nfailures = df.loc[~df[\"passed\"], [\"case_id\", \"model\", \"category\"]]\n```\n\nThe summary shows `small` passing 0.0 on refund (2 cases) and 1.0 on billing, while `large` passes\neverything; refund cost for `large` is (180 + 200) / 1000 × 0.01 = 0.0038 USD versus 0.00015 for\n`small`. That is the shape of a real model-choice discussion: the cheap model fails one category, so\nroute that category to the large one.","tradeoffs":["An enormous API and ecosystem make most tasks one line away, but the API has many ways to do the same thing and inconsistent historical defaults, so code quality varies wildly across a team.","Eager execution is easy to inspect cell by cell in a notebook, yet it materializes every intermediate; on data near RAM size, lazy engines such as Polars or DuckDB optimize and stream instead.","Index-label alignment is ideal for time series, but on ad-hoc tables it silently introduces NaNs, so many teams treat the index as a plain row number and join explicitly.","`object` dtype columns can hold anything, which tolerates messy data at the cost of speed and memory; converting to proper string, categorical or Arrow dtypes flips that once data grows."],"pitfalls":["Using `apply(axis=1)` or `iterrows()` for arithmetic; the symptom is a notebook cell taking minutes on a few million rows where the vectorized expression takes under a second.","Chained assignment like `df[mask][\"col\"] = x`; the symptom is `SettingWithCopyWarning` (pandas 2.x) or `ChainedAssignmentError` (pandas 3.x, Copy-on-Write) and a frame that is unchanged afterwards.","Merging on a key that is not unique on the side you assumed; the symptom is the row count growing after a join and inflated totals, caught by `validate=\"many_to_one\"`.","One missing value turning an ID column into `float64`; the symptom is IDs printed as `1234.0` and failed joins against integer keys.","Loading a multi-gigabyte CSV with default dtypes; the symptom is memory exhaustion, fixed by specifying dtypes, reading only needed columns, or switching to Parquet. `df.memory_usage(deep=True)` shows which columns (usually `object` strings) are responsible.","Grouping by a column with missing values under the default `dropna=True`; the symptom is per-group counts that sum to fewer rows than the frame, because cases with no category vanished from the report. Pass `dropna=False` or fill the key first."],"when":"Use pandas for exploratory analysis and reporting on data that fits comfortably in memory: eval results,\nannotation exports, token and cost logs, small training sets, and anything feeding scikit-learn or a\nplot. It is also the lingua franca for handing tables between libraries. Prefer Polars or DuckDB when\ndata approaches RAM size, when a pipeline runs repeatedly and speed matters, or when you want lazy,\nquery-optimized execution. Prefer a database when data is shared, updated concurrently or needs\ndurability. In interviews, expect questions on group-by, joins, vectorization and why chained\nassignment fails.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Loads JSONL eval and trace data into DataFrames, slices failures by category, and writes vectorized, `.loc`-based transformations that survive Copy-on-Write.","architect":"Decides where notebook-scale pandas analysis ends and production pipelines in Polars, Spark or SQL begin, and standardizes Parquet as the hand-off format.","fde":"Pulls a customer's CSV or ticket export into pandas on day one to profile volumes, categories and data quality before scoping the solution."},"check":{"q":"A notebook computes per-ticket cost with `df.apply(lambda r: price[r.model] * r.tokens, axis=1)`\nover 5 million rows and takes many minutes. Why is it slow and how do you fix it?","a":"`apply` with `axis=1` builds a Series for each row and calls the Python lambda 5 million times,\nso the work runs in the interpreter rather than in compiled column operations. Fix it by expressing the\nlookup as a join or a vectorized map: `df[\"unit\"] = df[\"model\"].map(price)` (or merge a price table),\nthen `df[\"cost\"] = df[\"unit\"] * df[\"tokens\"]`, both of which operate on whole columns. Also convert\n`model` to a categorical dtype if it repeats, and check the merge or map left no NaNs for unknown models."},"quiz":[{"q":"`a` has index [0, 1, 2] and `b` has index [1, 2, 3]. What does `a + b` return?","options":["An error, because the indexes differ","Three values computed by position","Four rows labelled 0 to 3, with NaN for labels present on only one side","Two rows, labels 1 and 2 only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"pandas aligns on labels and takes the union of the indexes, so labels 0 and 3 have no partner and become NaN. Positional addition requires `.to_numpy()` or resetting the indexes first."},{"q":"Why does `df[df.score < 0.5][\"flag\"] = True` often fail to modify `df`?","options":["Boolean masks are read-only in pandas","The first indexing step can return a new object, and the assignment goes into that temporary copy","The column must exist before assignment","pandas requires `inplace=True` for all assignments"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Chained indexing performs two separate operations; the second writes into whatever the first returned, which may be a copy (under pandas 3's Copy-on-Write it always behaves as one). A single `df.loc[mask, \"flag\"] = True` modifies the original frame."},{"q":"After a merge, an eval summary reports more rows than there were test cases. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The merge defaulted to an outer join","groupby dropped NaN keys","The join key was duplicated in the right-hand table, multiplying matching rows","Copy-on-Write duplicated the frame"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A many-to-many match repeats each left row once per matching right row. Passing `validate=\"many_to_one\"` raises an error when the right side's keys are not unique, catching the problem at the join."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/polars","sources":["Official docs: Polars","Official docs: Apache Arrow"],"why":"pandas was designed when a single core and a few hundred megabytes were the norm. Its execution model\nshows that: each operation runs eagerly, materializes a full intermediate result, mostly on one core,\nand string columns have historically been arrays of Python objects. Point it at 50 GB of agent traces to\ncompute daily token usage per model and you read every column of every file into memory, filter\nafterwards, and wait on one core while the others idle.\n\nTwo things are needed to do better. The first is a memory layout built for analytics: typed, contiguous\ncolumns with a proper string representation and real null handling, so scans and aggregations are\ncache-friendly and vectorizable. Apache Arrow defines exactly that. The second is a query engine rather\nthan a function library: if the library sees the whole computation before running it, it can skip\ncolumns that are never used, apply filters while reading the file instead of after, and split work\nacross threads. Databases have done this for decades; Polars brings it to a DataFrame API that lives in\na Python process, with no server to run.","how":"### Expressions, not row functions\nComputation is described with expressions: `pl.col(\"latency_ms\").quantile(0.95)`,\n`(pl.col(\"out_tokens\") / 1000 * pl.col(\"price\")).alias(\"cost\")`, `pl.when(cond).then(a).otherwise(b)`.\nAn expression is a description of a column computation, not a result. Contexts apply them:\n`select`, `with_columns`, `filter`, and `group_by(...).agg(...)`. Because expressions are declarative,\nPolars can run independent expressions in parallel and optimize them, which a Python lambda executed per\nrow would prevent. Python UDFs (`map_elements`) exist but drop you back to interpreter speed.\n\n### Eager and lazy\n`pl.read_parquet` returns a `DataFrame` immediately, like pandas. `pl.scan_parquet` (also `scan_csv`,\n`scan_ndjson`) returns a `LazyFrame`: each subsequent call adds a node to a logical plan and nothing is\nread until `.collect()`. Before executing, the optimizer applies rules such as:\n\n- **Projection pushdown:** only columns referenced anywhere in the plan are read from disk.\n- **Predicate pushdown:** filters move into the scan, so Parquet row groups whose statistics rule them\n  out can be skipped.\n- **Simplification and common-subexpression elimination**, so repeated expressions are computed once.\n\n`lf.explain()` prints the optimized plan, so you can verify what will be read.\n\n### Memory and parallelism\nColumns are Arrow-style buffers with a validity bitmap for nulls, so `null` is distinct from a float\n`NaN` and an integer column with missing values stays an integer column. The engine is multi-threaded\nin Rust, outside the GIL, so a group-by uses all cores without multiprocessing. A parallel group-by\nworks the way a distributed one does: threads hash rows into partitions, build partial aggregates\nindependently, and merge them. That is why output group order is not guaranteed unless you pass\n`maintain_order=True` (which costs speed), and why it matters for tests and reports that compare\nframes row by row. For data larger than RAM, Polars has a streaming engine that processes the plan in\nbatches, and `sink_parquet` writes a lazy result straight to disk without materializing it; how you\nenable streaming has changed across releases, so check the docs for your version.\n\n### No index, strict types\nThere is no row index and no label alignment: rows are positions, and joins are explicit. Schemas are\nstrict; mixing types in a column or comparing incompatible dtypes raises an error rather than silently\nbecoming `object`. This is a deliberate trade: fewer silent surprises, more errors up front.\n\n### Interop\nBecause both speak Arrow, conversion to and from pyarrow is typically zero-copy, and `to_pandas()`\nexists for libraries that only accept pandas. Many plotting and ML libraries accept Polars frames\ndirectly now, but not all.","example":"Daily token usage per model from a directory of trace files, read lazily.\n\n```python\nimport polars as pl\n\nlf = (\n    pl.scan_parquet(\"traces/*.parquet\")                    # builds a plan; reads nothing\n      .filter(pl.col(\"ts\") >= pl.datetime(2026, 10, 1))\n      .filter(pl.col(\"status\") == \"ok\")\n      .with_columns(\n          (pl.col(\"output_tokens\") / 1000 * pl.col(\"usd_per_1k_out\")).alias(\"cost_usd\"))\n      .group_by(\"model\", pl.col(\"ts\").dt.date().alias(\"day\"))\n      .agg(\n          pl.len().alias(\"calls\"),\n          pl.col(\"input_tokens\").sum(),\n          pl.col(\"output_tokens\").sum(),\n          pl.col(\"cost_usd\").sum(),\n          pl.col(\"latency_ms\").quantile(0.95).alias(\"p95_ms\"),\n      )\n      .sort(\"day\", \"model\")\n)\nprint(lf.explain())   # both filters appear inside the Parquet scan; only 7 columns are projected\ndaily = lf.collect()  # multi-threaded execution happens here\ndaily.write_parquet(\"daily_usage.parquet\")\n```\n\nSuppose the trace files hold 40 columns, including large prompt and completion text fields, and only\nrows from 1 October onward are needed. The plan reads 7 numeric or short columns and skips row groups whose `ts`\nstatistics fall before October, so the bytes read can be a small fraction of the dataset. The\nequivalent eager pandas code would load all 40 columns, text included, before filtering.","tradeoffs":["Lazy plans with pushdown and parallelism are fast on large data, but debugging means reading a query plan instead of inspecting intermediate frames; for small exploratory data, eager mode or pandas is just as quick to write.","Strict types and no index remove pandas' silent alignment and dtype drift, at the cost of more upfront casting and errors when ingesting messy JSON or CSV.","Faster and leaner than pandas on most analytical workloads, yet its ecosystem is younger, so some libraries still expect pandas and you pay a conversion at the boundary.","An in-process engine needs no server, but it has no concurrency control, durability or access management; once many users share the data, a warehouse or database wins."],"pitfalls":["Writing pandas habits in Polars, such as `map_elements` with Python lambdas; the symptom is Polars code running no faster than pandas and using one core.","Calling `.collect()` early and continuing eagerly; the symptom is memory spikes, because pushdown can only apply to the part of the plan built before collecting.","Confusing `null` and `NaN`; the symptom is `drop_nulls()` leaving NaN rows behind, or averages that come out NaN.","Assuming code copied from older blog posts still runs; the symptom is `AttributeError` or deprecation warnings because names such as `groupby` became `group_by` and other APIs evolved quickly.","Relying on `group_by` output order; the symptom is a test or a diff of a daily report that fails intermittently with the same data, because groups come back in a different order each run. Sort explicitly or pass `maintain_order=True`.","Reading wide JSONL with inferred schemas; the symptom is a type error deep in the file when a field that was always an integer suddenly holds a string, fixed by passing an explicit schema."],"when":"Reach for Polars when data is in the gigabytes, when a transformation runs on a schedule (token and\ncost rollups, eval aggregation, dataset deduplication and filtering before fine-tuning), or when pandas\nis running out of memory or time. Use lazy scans over Parquet whenever possible. Stay with pandas for\nsmall notebooks, for code that leans on pandas-only libraries, or when the team's skills make it the\ncheaper choice. Consider DuckDB if the team prefers SQL; it occupies similar ground. Measure wall time\nand peak memory on your real data before migrating a pipeline.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes lazy Polars pipelines over Parquet trace and eval data, checks `explain()` for pushdown, and converts to pandas only at library boundaries.","architect":"Chooses between pandas, Polars, DuckDB and a warehouse based on data size, run frequency and sharing needs, and standardizes Arrow and Parquet as the interchange layer.","fde":"Processes a customer's large log export on a single VM without provisioning a cluster, getting usage and quality numbers within hours of receiving access."},"check":{"q":"A pandas job reads 30 GB of Parquet traces, filters to one customer, and sums tokens per day. It\nOOMs on a 64 GB machine. Why might a Polars lazy rewrite succeed, and what would you verify?","a":"As written, pandas reads every column of every file into memory before filtering (its\n`read_parquet` accepts `columns=` and `filters=`, but the naive job uses neither), and decompression,\nPython string objects and intermediates add more, so 30 GB on disk can exceed 64 GB in RAM. A lazy Polars plan built with `scan_parquet` pushes the\ncustomer filter and the column selection into the scan, so only the few needed columns are read and\nrow groups for other customers can be skipped using Parquet statistics. The aggregation then runs in\nparallel. I would verify with `explain()` that the filter and projection are in the scan, measure peak\nmemory, and if data is not sorted or partitioned by customer, expect less skipping and consider the\nstreaming engine."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does projection pushdown do in a Polars lazy query over Parquet?","options":["It moves the group-by to run before filters","It reads only the columns the query actually references","It caches the result for later queries","It converts the plan into SQL for an external database"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The optimizer sees the whole plan, so it knows which columns are used and asks the Parquet reader for only those. Predicate pushdown is the related rule that moves filters into the scan."},{"q":"Why can a Polars group-by use all CPU cores without multiprocessing?","options":["It forks one Python process per core automatically","It uses asyncio to schedule groups concurrently","The engine runs in Rust threads outside the Python interpreter, so the GIL does not serialize the work","It sends the work to a local Spark cluster"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Polars executes expressions in native code with its own thread pool. Python only builds the plan, so the GIL is not held during the heavy computation and parallelism needs no extra processes."},{"q":"A teammate's Polars pipeline is no faster than the pandas version. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Polars is slower than pandas for CSV files in general","Lazy mode adds planning overhead that dominates every query","The machine has too few cores for Rust code","Row-wise Python functions via `map_elements` force interpreter-speed execution"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Expressions run in native, parallel code, but a Python UDF must be called per element under the GIL. Rewriting the logic as built-in expressions such as `when/then` or string methods restores the speedup."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pyarrow","sources":["Official docs: Apache Arrow","Official docs: Apache Parquet"],"why":"Every data tool used to have its own in-memory layout. Moving a table from a database client to pandas,\nthen to Spark, then to a model's data loader meant converting each value at every hop: row-by-row\nserialization, often through Python objects, that could cost more than the analysis itself. And on disk,\nCSV and JSONL store numbers as text with no types, so every read re-parses every byte and must read every\ncolumn even when the query needs two.\n\nArrow attacks the in-memory problem with a shared specification. If every library lays out a column the\nsame way, a table can be handed from C++ to Python to Rust by passing pointers to buffers. Parquet\nattacks the storage problem: a columnar file format with types, encodings, compression and statistics, so\na reader can skip columns and whole blocks of rows. The two are complementary: Parquet is optimized for\nbeing small on disk and selectively readable, Arrow for being fast to compute on in memory, and pyarrow\nconverts between them efficiently.","how":"### The Arrow memory layout\nAn Arrow array is a few contiguous buffers. A fixed-width column such as `int64` or `float32` is one\nvalues buffer plus an optional *validity bitmap*, one bit per row marking nulls, which is why missing\nvalues do not force a type change as NaN does in pandas. A variable-length column such as `string` adds an\n*offsets* buffer: row *i* is the bytes between `offsets[i]` and `offsets[i+1]` in one shared data buffer,\nso a million strings are three buffers rather than a million Python objects. Nested types (`list`,\n`struct`, `fixed_size_list`) compose these. A fixed-size list of 768 float32 values is a natural type for\nembeddings: one contiguous float buffer that converts to a NumPy `(n, 768)` array without copying.\n\n### Tables, batches and chunks\nA `RecordBatch` is a set of equal-length arrays with a `Schema`. A `Table` is a schema plus columns that\nare `ChunkedArray`s, sequences of arrays, *because* tables are built by concatenating batches from files\nor streams, and chunking avoids copying them into one buffer. Many operations work chunk by chunk;\n`combine_chunks()` makes a single contiguous array when a consumer needs one.\n\n### Sharing without copying\nWithin one process, the Arrow C Data Interface lets libraries exchange buffers by pointer, which is how\nPolars, DuckDB and pandas' Arrow-backed dtypes interoperate cheaply; in Python this is increasingly\nexposed through the Arrow PyCapsule protocol (`__arrow_c_stream__` and friends), so a library can accept\n\"anything Arrow-shaped\" without importing the producer. Zero-copy holds only while both sides keep the\nArrow layout. `table.to_pandas()` with default settings turns a string column into an `object` column of\nPython strings, which is a full copy and often several times larger; `to_pandas(types_mapper=pd.ArrowDtype)`\nkeeps Arrow-backed columns instead. Across processes, the Arrow IPC\nformat (also called Feather v2 on disk) writes the in-memory layout directly, so a file can be\nmemory-mapped and read with almost no decoding. Hugging Face `datasets` stores data as Arrow files and\nmemory-maps them, which is why a dataset larger than RAM can still be indexed randomly.\n\n### Parquet on disk\nA Parquet file is split into *row groups*, each holding a *column chunk* per column. Column chunks are\nencoded (dictionary encoding for repetitive values such as tenant IDs or model names, run-length and\nbit-packing for small integers) and then compressed (Snappy by default in pyarrow, Zstandard for a better\nratio). The footer stores the schema and per-column-chunk statistics such as min and max. A reader asked\nfor two columns with a filter reads the footer, skips row groups whose statistics cannot match, and\nfetches only the needed column chunks, which is why the same file serves cheap selective queries. The\n`pyarrow.dataset` module extends this to directories of files, including hive-style partitions such as\n`tenant=acme/`, which prune whole files.\n\n### Compute\n`pyarrow.compute` provides vectorized kernels (filters, arithmetic, string functions, aggregations) over\nArrow arrays. It is useful for light transformations, but for analytical queries you usually reach for\nPolars or DuckDB on top of Arrow.","example":"Storing chunk text and embeddings for a RAG corpus in Parquet with an explicit schema, then reading back\nonly what retrieval needs.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport pyarrow as pa\nimport pyarrow.parquet as pq\n\nn, d = 10_000, 384\nemb = np.random.default_rng(0).standard_normal((n, d), dtype=np.float32)  # 15.4 MB\n\nschema = pa.schema([\n    (\"doc_id\", pa.string()),\n    (\"tenant\", pa.string()),                       # Parquet dictionary-encodes it on disk\n    (\"chunk\", pa.large_string()),                  # 64-bit offsets: no 2 GB-per-array limit\n    (\"embedding\", pa.list_(pa.float32(), d)),      # fixed_size_list<float32>[384]\n])\ntable = pa.table({\n    \"doc_id\": [f\"doc-{i}\" for i in range(n)],\n    \"tenant\": [f\"t{i % 3}\" for i in range(n)],\n    \"chunk\": [f\"text of chunk {i}\" for i in range(n)],\n    \"embedding\": pa.FixedSizeListArray.from_arrays(pa.array(emb.ravel()), d),\n}, schema=schema)\n\npq.write_table(table, \"chunks.parquet\", compression=\"zstd\", row_group_size=2_000)\nprint(pq.ParquetFile(\"chunks.parquet\").metadata.num_row_groups)    # 5\n\nt = pq.read_table(\"chunks.parquet\", columns=[\"doc_id\", \"embedding\"],\n                  filters=[(\"tenant\", \"=\", \"t1\")])                 # chunk text never read\nvecs = t.column(\"embedding\").combine_chunks().flatten().to_numpy().reshape(-1, d)\nprint(t.num_rows, vecs.shape, vecs.dtype)                           # 3333 (3333, 384) float32\n```\n\nThe read skips the `chunk` column entirely. It cannot skip row groups here, because every row group\ncontains all three tenants. Had the table been sorted by tenant before writing, `t1` would occupy rows\n3,334 to 6,666 (0-based), which fall in the 2nd, 3rd and 4th row groups, so min/max statistics would let the reader skip\nthe other 2 of 5. Partitioning by tenant (`tenant=t1/` directories) would go further and skip the\ntwo-thirds of the data belonging to other tenants without even opening those files.","tradeoffs":["Parquet's compression and column pruning make selective reads cheap, but files are immutable and poor at single-row updates; frequently mutated data belongs in a database or a table format on top.","Arrow's shared layout makes library hand-offs nearly free, but zero-copy only holds when types match; nulls, string columns or non-contiguous chunks can force a copy when converting to NumPy or pandas.","Large row groups compress better and scan faster, while small ones allow finer skipping and lower memory when reading; the right size depends on whether workloads scan or seek.","Explicit schemas catch type drift at write time, at the cost of maintaining them as fields evolve; schema inference is convenient until two files disagree and a dataset read fails."],"pitfalls":["Writing many tiny Parquet files from a streaming job; the symptom is slow reads dominated by opening files and parsing footers, fixed by compacting into fewer, larger files.","Using 32-bit-offset `string` for huge text columns; the symptom is an offset overflow error once one array's text exceeds about 2 GB, fixed with `large_string` or smaller chunks.","Expecting filters to skip data on unsorted files; the symptom is a \"filtered\" read taking as long as a full scan, because row-group statistics overlap every predicate.","Letting schemas be inferred per file; the symptom is a dataset read failing because one file has an int column as double or a field missing entirely.","Assuming `to_numpy()` is always zero-copy; the symptom is an error or memory spike on columns with nulls or multiple chunks.","Calling `to_pandas()` on a large text table with default settings; the symptom is peak memory far above the Arrow table's `nbytes`, because every string became a Python object."],"when":"Use Parquet via pyarrow as the default storage for any tabular dataset you will read more than once:\neval results, traces, processed training data, chunk and embedding tables for RAG. Use Arrow IPC or\nFeather when you want fast, memory-mappable local files with no decoding. Use pyarrow directly when you\nneed explicit schemas, control over row groups and compression, or to move data between libraries;\notherwise let pandas, Polars or DuckDB call it for you. Keep JSONL for human-readable interchange and\nappend-only logs, and convert to Parquet for analysis. Measure file size, read time for typical queries\nand peak memory.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes Parquet with explicit schemas and `fixed_size_list` embeddings, reads only needed columns, and converts to NumPy without copies for scoring.","architect":"Makes Arrow and Parquet the interchange and storage contract across ingestion, training and analytics, including partitioning and file-size policy.","fde":"Asks the customer for a Parquet export instead of CSV, cutting transfer size and avoiding a week of type and encoding bugs."},"check":{"q":"Your embedding store is 20 million rows of text plus 768-d float32 vectors in one directory of\nParquet files. Retrieval for one tenant reads everything and is slow. What do you change, and why?","a":"Vectors alone are 20 M × 768 × 4 bytes, about 61 GB, so full scans are expensive. First, project:\nread only the ID and embedding columns, never the text, until results are chosen. Second, make filters\nskip data: partition the dataset by tenant (`tenant=x/` directories) or sort by tenant before writing so\nrow-group statistics exclude other tenants. Third, tune file and row-group sizes so there are not\nthousands of tiny files. If queries are frequent and latency matters, Parquet is the source of truth but\nnot the serving layer; build an ANN index per tenant from it."},"quiz":[{"q":"What lets a Parquet reader skip entire row groups when a query filters on `tenant = \"acme\"`?","options":["The validity bitmap marks irrelevant rows as null","Per-column-chunk min/max statistics in the file footer show no row in the group can match","Dictionary encoding stores each tenant in a separate file","Snappy compression indexes every distinct value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Readers consult footer statistics before fetching data. If a row group's tenant range excludes \"acme\", it is skipped, which only works well when data is sorted or partitioned by that column."},{"q":"How does Arrow represent a column of one million variable-length strings?","options":["As one million Python string objects referenced by pointers","As fixed-width padded strings sized to the longest value","As an offsets buffer, a contiguous data buffer and an optional validity bitmap","As a compressed Parquet column chunk kept in memory"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Offsets mark where each string starts and ends inside one shared byte buffer, so the column is a few contiguous buffers that any Arrow-speaking library can read without conversion."},{"q":"What is the key difference between Arrow and Parquet?","options":["Arrow is row-oriented and Parquet is columnar","Arrow is an in-memory format for fast computation and exchange; Parquet is an encoded, compressed on-disk format","Parquet is a Python library and Arrow is a file format","They are the same format with different file extensions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both are columnar. Arrow optimizes random access and zero-copy sharing in memory, while Parquet uses encodings, compression and statistics to be small and selectively readable on disk."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/scikit-learn-basics","sources":["Pedregosa et al., 2011 — Scikit-learn: Machine Learning in Python","Buitinck et al., 2013 — API design for machine learning software: experiences from the scikit-learn project","Official docs: scikit-learn"],"why":"Classical ML needs the same chores in every project: scale features, encode categories, vectorize text,\nfit a model, tune hyperparameters, estimate how it will do on unseen data. Before a common library, each\nalgorithm came with its own calling convention, so swapping logistic regression for a random forest\nmeant rewriting glue code, and every team rebuilt evaluation loops by hand.\n\nThe hand-rolled glue is also where the worst bug in applied ML lives: leakage. Fit a scaler or a TF-IDF\nvocabulary on the full dataset, then split, and statistics from the test rows have shaped the features\nthe model trains on. The reported score is optimistic and the production score is not. Nobody notices\nuntil after launch.\n\nscikit-learn's answer is an API contract more than an algorithm collection. Every component exposes the\nsame methods, learned state is kept separate from configuration, and composite objects (pipelines,\ncross-validators, grid searches) can treat any estimator uniformly. Once preprocessing lives inside a\npipeline, the library refits it per fold automatically, so the honest procedure becomes the easy one.\nIn AI work this matters because the questions recur: is the LLM router better than a TF-IDF classifier?\nCan a logistic regression on embeddings replace a per-request LLM call for intent detection?","how":"### The estimator contract\n- **Constructor = hyperparameters only.** `LogisticRegression(C=1.0, max_iter=1000)` stores settings\n  and touches no data, *because* that lets tools clone an estimator and refit it on new folds.\n- **`fit(X, y)` learns state** and returns `self`. Learned attributes end with an underscore (`coef_`,\n  `classes_`, `vocabulary_`), so you can tell configuration from fitted state at a glance.\n- **Predictors** add `predict`, and usually `predict_proba` or `decision_function`; **transformers** add\n  `transform` (and `fit_transform`). `X` is a 2-D array-like of shape `(n_samples, n_features)` (NumPy,\n  sparse matrices, or pandas frames); `y` is 1-D.\n\n### Pipelines and column transformers\n`Pipeline` (or `make_pipeline`) chains transformers ending in a model, and is itself an estimator.\nCalling `fit` fits each step on the training data and transforms it for the next step; `predict` only\ntransforms. `ColumnTransformer` applies different preprocessing to different columns (one-hot encode\n`plan_tier`, scale `tokens_used`, TF-IDF the `text`). Because the whole chain is one object, cross\nvalidation and deployment both use exactly the same preprocessing, and nothing is fit on held-out data.\n\n### Honest evaluation\n`train_test_split(..., stratify=y)` keeps class proportions in both splits, which matters with\nimbalanced intents. `cross_val_score` or `cross_validate` with `StratifiedKFold` gives a mean and\nspread across folds rather than a single lucky split. `GridSearchCV` or `RandomizedSearchCV` tunes\nhyperparameters by cross validation on the training set; the test set is touched once, at the end.\nGrouped splitters (`GroupKFold`) keep all rows of one customer or conversation on the same side, which\nprevents near-duplicate leakage. The concepts themselves (leakage, splits, imbalance) are covered in\ndepth in the ML fundamentals module; here the point is that the library encodes them.\n\n### What the standard text baseline computes\nKnowing the math tells you what the baseline can and cannot learn. `TfidfVectorizer` turns each\ndocument into a sparse vector with one dimension per vocabulary term (and n-gram). The weight of term\n*t* in document *d* is `tf(t, d) × idf(t)`, where with the default `smooth_idf=True`,\n`idf(t) = ln((1 + n) / (1 + df(t))) + 1`, *n* is the number of training documents and *df(t)* the number\ncontaining *t*. Rare terms get large weights because they discriminate; terms in every document get\nweight near 1. `sublinear_tf=True` replaces raw counts with `1 + ln(tf)` so a word repeated ten times\ndoes not count ten times, and each row is then L2-normalized so long tickets do not dominate. Both the\nvocabulary and *df* are learned state, which is exactly why fitting the vectorizer on test documents\nleaks.\n\n`LogisticRegression` then learns one weight per (class, feature) and scores a document as\n`z_k = w_k · x + b_k`; for multiclass it applies a softmax, `p_k = exp(z_k) / Σ_j exp(z_j)`, and\n`predict` returns the argmax. Fitting minimizes log-loss plus an L2 penalty whose strength is `1/C`, so\na smaller `C` means stronger regularization, which matters when there are far more n-gram features\nthan tickets. The model is linear in word evidence: it cannot represent negation or word order beyond\nthe n-grams you gave it, which is the boundary where embeddings or an LLM start to earn their cost.\n\n### Metrics and baselines\n`sklearn.metrics` provides `classification_report`, `confusion_matrix`, `f1_score`, `roc_auc_score`,\n`precision_recall_curve` and regression metrics, and many LLM eval harnesses call them underneath\nrather than reimplementing them. `DummyClassifier` gives the majority-class baseline every result should beat.\n\n### What it is not\nIt runs on CPU, mostly in memory, with no GPU deep learning. Persisting a model with `joblib` or pickle\nties it to the library version that saved it, and loading untrusted pickles executes code; the `skops`\nformat exists to load models without arbitrary code execution when artifacts cross a trust boundary.","example":"An intent classifier for support tickets, the kind of baseline you build before deciding an LLM router\nis needed.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.dummy import DummyClassifier\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.metrics import classification_report\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import StratifiedKFold, cross_val_score, train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\n\ndf = pd.read_json(\"tickets.jsonl\", lines=True)          # columns: text, intent\nX_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(\n    df[\"text\"], df[\"intent\"], test_size=0.2, stratify=df[\"intent\"], random_state=42)\n\npipe = make_pipeline(                                    # vectorizer is refit inside every fold\n    TfidfVectorizer(ngram_range=(1, 2), min_df=2, sublinear_tf=True),\n    LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, class_weight=\"balanced\"),\n)\ncv = StratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)\nf1 = cross_val_score(pipe, X_tr, y_tr, cv=cv, scoring=\"f1_macro\")\nbase = cross_val_score(DummyClassifier(strategy=\"most_frequent\"), X_tr, y_tr,\n                       cv=cv, scoring=\"f1_macro\")\nprint(f\"model macro-F1 {f1.mean():.3f} ± {f1.std():.3f} | baseline {base.mean():.3f}\")\n\npipe.fit(X_tr, y_tr)                                     # final fit on all training data\nprint(classification_report(y_te, pipe.predict(X_te)))   # test set used once\nprobs = pipe.predict_proba([\"I was charged twice this month\"])[0]\nprint(dict(zip(pipe.classes_, probs.round(2))))\n```\n\nSuppose there are 4,000 tickets across 6 intents and `billing` is 40% of them. The dummy baseline then\nscores 40% accuracy but a macro-F1 near 0.1, since it gets one class partly right and five wrong, which\nis why macro-F1 is the headline metric. If the pipeline reaches a macro-F1 close to what an LLM prompt\nachieves on the same test set, the classifier is the cheaper, faster router. Trying precomputed\nsentence embeddings instead is a small change: drop `TfidfVectorizer` from the pipeline and pass the\n`(n_samples, dim)` embedding matrix as `X`; the splits, scoring and baseline stay identical.","tradeoffs":["A uniform API makes swapping models trivial, but the abstractions assume in-memory, tabular `X`; data beyond RAM or GPU-scale deep learning needs PyTorch or a distributed framework.","Linear models on TF-IDF or embeddings train in seconds and are easy to inspect, but they cap out on tasks needing reasoning or long context, where an LLM or fine-tuned model wins despite cost.","Cross validation gives a far more honest estimate than one split, at the cost of k times the training time; for slow models or huge data, a single well-built validation split is acceptable.","`class_weight=\"balanced\"` raises recall on rare intents, but it shifts predicted probabilities, so scores used as confidence thresholds need recalibration."],"pitfalls":["Fitting a scaler or vectorizer on all data before splitting; the symptom is cross-validation scores clearly higher than performance on genuinely new data.","Splitting rows randomly when the same customer or conversation appears many times; the symptom is near-perfect validation scores that collapse in production, fixed with `GroupKFold`.","Reporting accuracy on imbalanced classes; the symptom is a model that \"scores 90%\" while never predicting the rare intents that matter.","Tuning hyperparameters against the test set; the symptom is a test score that keeps creeping up across experiments while live metrics stay flat.","Loading a pickled model with a different scikit-learn version; the symptom is warnings about inconsistent versions or subtly different predictions, fixed by pinning versions with the artifact."],"when":"Use scikit-learn whenever a problem is classification, regression or clustering on features you already\nhave: TF-IDF text, embeddings, tabular usage data. In AI products that means intent routing, spam or\nabuse filters, quality classifiers over LLM outputs, and baselines that any LLM solution must beat on\ncost and accuracy. Use its metrics and splitters inside LLM eval harnesses even when the model is an LLM.\nDo not use it for training neural networks or for data that does not fit in memory. In interviews,\nexpect to explain pipelines and leakage, and to justify a baseline before proposing an LLM.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds pipeline-based baselines and embedding classifiers, evaluates with stratified or grouped cross validation, and uses `sklearn.metrics` inside eval code.","architect":"Decides when a cheap classical model should front or replace an LLM call, and sets the evaluation protocol every candidate must pass.","fde":"Trains a quick classifier on a customer's labelled tickets to show what is achievable and to quantify where an LLM adds value."},"check":{"q":"A teammate reports 0.97 macro-F1 for a ticket classifier. They TF-IDF-vectorized all 10,000\ntickets, then ran `train_test_split`, and many customers filed several near-identical tickets. Do you\ntrust the number? What do you change?","a":"No. Two leaks inflate it. The vectorizer learned vocabulary and IDF weights from test tickets,\nso features already encode the test set; putting the vectorizer in a `Pipeline` refits it inside each\nfold. Worse, random row splits place a customer's near-duplicate tickets on both sides, so the model is\npartly memorizing. Use `GroupKFold` or a group-aware split keyed on customer ID, and ideally a\ntime-based holdout of the most recent tickets. Report macro-F1 against a `DummyClassifier` baseline. A\nreal score will be lower but will predict production."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does scikit-learn require that constructors only store hyperparameters and never touch data?","options":["It makes estimators smaller when pickled","Tools such as cross validation and grid search can clone an unfitted estimator and refit it on each fold","It forces all data to be passed as NumPy arrays","It allows models to train on the GPU"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cloning works by reading constructor parameters and creating a fresh, unfitted copy. If constructors learned from data, every fold would inherit state from data it should never have seen."},{"q":"What is the main reason to put a `TfidfVectorizer` inside a `Pipeline` before cross validation?","options":["Pipelines run the vectorizer faster in parallel","Vectorizers cannot be used outside a pipeline","The vectorizer is then fit only on each fold's training portion, preventing leakage of test vocabulary and IDF statistics","Pipelines automatically choose the best n-gram range"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fitting the vectorizer on all rows lets held-out documents shape the feature space. Inside a pipeline, each fold refits it on its own training data, so the validation score reflects truly unseen text."},{"q":"Your intent dataset is 85% one class. Which evaluation choice best reveals whether the model helps?","options":["Plain accuracy on a random split","Training accuracy, since it has the most data","Accuracy averaged over unstratified folds","Macro-F1 with stratified cross validation, compared against a majority-class dummy baseline"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Accuracy rewards predicting the majority class, which already scores 85%. Macro-F1 weights every class equally, stratification keeps rare classes in each fold, and the dummy baseline shows the floor to beat."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pytorch","sources":["Paszke et al., 2019 — PyTorch: An Imperative Style, High-Performance Deep Learning Library","Official docs: PyTorch"],"why":"Training a neural network needs three things NumPy does not give you: the derivative of a scalar loss\nwith respect to millions or billions of parameters, execution on accelerators whose memory is separate\nfrom the CPU's, and a way to keep a GPU busy while the next batch is being loaded and decoded.\n\nDeriving gradients by hand is error-prone past a few layers, and finite differences are hopeless: one\nextra forward pass per parameter means a billion forward passes per step for a 1B-parameter model.\nReverse-mode automatic differentiation computes all of them in a single backward pass whose cost is a\nsmall constant multiple of the forward pass (commonly quoted as about two to three times), independent of\nthe parameter count, but it needs a record of what was computed. Earlier frameworks such as TensorFlow 1.x asked you to\ndeclare that record as a static graph first and then execute it in a session; this made optimisation easy\nand debugging miserable, because errors surfaced inside a runtime you could not step through, and data-\ndependent control flow (a loop that runs a variable number of times) needed special graph operators.\n\nPyTorch's answer is \"define-by-run\": the graph is recorded as your Python executes, every iteration,\nso control flow is just Python. For an AI engineer the payoff today is less about writing training loops\nfrom scratch and more about literacy: every Hugging Face model, every LoRA adapter, every embedding\nmodel and most local inference stacks are PyTorch modules, and when one OOMs, produces NaNs or runs on\nthe CPU by accident, you need to know which of these five pieces is responsible.","how":"### Tensors\nA tensor is a typed, strided n-dimensional array: a pointer to storage plus `shape`, `stride`, `dtype`\nand `device`. Views (`.view`, `.transpose`, slicing) share storage and only change strides, which is why\nthey are free, and why `.view` fails on a non-contiguous tensor and you need `.reshape` or\n`.contiguous()`. Broadcasting follows NumPy rules. `dtype` matters more than in NumPy: `float32` is the\ndefault, `bfloat16` and `float16` halve memory and run on tensor cores, and mixing dtypes in one op\nraises or silently upcasts depending on the op.\n\n### Autograd\nAny tensor with `requires_grad=True` (all `nn.Parameter`s) makes subsequent ops record a node in a\ndirected acyclic graph: each output tensor holds a `grad_fn` pointing to the function that made it and\nto its inputs. `loss.backward()` walks that graph in reverse, applying the chain rule, and *accumulates*\nresults into each leaf's `.grad`. Accumulation, not assignment, is a deliberate choice: it lets\ngradient accumulation across micro-batches work for free, and it is why you must call\n`optimizer.zero_grad()` every step. The graph is freed after `backward()` because holding saved\nactivations is the main memory cost of training; `torch.no_grad()` (or `torch.inference_mode()`) stops\nrecording entirely, which is why inference should run inside it: no activations are saved for a backward\npass that will never happen.\n\nMechanically, backward never builds a full Jacobian. Starting from d(loss)/d(loss) = 1, each node\nreceives the gradient of the loss with respect to its output, multiplies it by its local derivative\n(a vector-Jacobian product) and hands the result to its inputs. That is why the cost is a small multiple\nof the forward pass, and why `backward()` with no argument only works on a scalar: for a vector output\nyou must supply the vector to multiply by. Three lines make it concrete:\n\n```python\nw = torch.tensor(3.0, requires_grad=True)\ny = 2 * w**2; y.backward(); print(w.grad)   # tensor(12.)  since dy/dw = 4w\ny = 2 * w**2; y.backward(); print(w.grad)   # tensor(24.)  accumulated, not replaced\n```\n\nOnly leaf tensors (ones you created, such as parameters) keep `.grad`; intermediate gradients are freed\nunless you call `retain_grad()`. `.detach()` returns a tensor that shares storage but is cut from the\ngraph, which is how you stop gradients flowing into, say, a frozen target. Because backward reuses the\ninputs each op saved, modifying a saved tensor in place before `backward()` would silently corrupt the\ngradient; autograd keeps a version counter per tensor and raises \"modified by an inplace operation\"\ninstead.\n\n### nn.Module\nA module is a class whose attributes that are `nn.Parameter`s or other modules get registered\nautomatically. That registration is what makes `model.parameters()` (for the optimizer),\n`model.to(device)`, `model.state_dict()` (for saving) and `model.train()` / `model.eval()` work across an\narbitrarily nested tree. `train()`/`eval()` flip behaviour of layers such as dropout and batch norm; they\ndo not disable gradients. You write `forward`, and call the module as `model(x)` so hooks run.\n\n### DataLoader\nA `Dataset` answers `len()` and `__getitem__(i)`. A `DataLoader` wraps it with batching, shuffling, a\n`collate_fn` that stacks samples (or pads variable-length sequences), and `num_workers` subprocesses that\nprefetch batches so tokenisation or image decoding overlaps GPU compute. Worker processes, not threads,\nare used because of the GIL. `pin_memory=True` puts batches in page-locked host memory so the copy to\nGPU can be asynchronous when you also pass `non_blocking=True` to `.to(device)`.\n\n### device\nTensors live on exactly one device (`cpu`, `cuda:0`, `mps`). Ops require all inputs on the same device;\nmoving is explicit (`.to(device)`) because a host-to-GPU copy crosses PCIe and is slow relative to GPU\ncompute, and PyTorch refuses to hide that cost. CUDA calls are asynchronous: Python returns before the\nkernel finishes, so naive timing measures launch time unless you call `torch.cuda.synchronize()`.","example":"A complete minimal training loop; every line maps to one of the five pieces.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom torch import nn\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader, TensorDataset\n\ndevice = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\"\nX = torch.randn(1024, 16)                       # 1024 samples, 16 features\ny = (X.sum(dim=1, keepdim=True) > 0).float()    # toy binary label\nloader = DataLoader(TensorDataset(X, y), batch_size=64, shuffle=True)\n\nclass MLP(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(16, 32), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(32, 1))\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.net(x)                       # logits, shape [batch, 1]\n\nmodel = MLP().to(device)                         # moves every registered parameter\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-3)\nloss_fn = nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()\n\nfor epoch in range(5):\n    model.train()\n    for xb, yb in loader:\n        xb, yb = xb.to(device), yb.to(device)    # data must be on the model's device\n        opt.zero_grad()                          # grads accumulate otherwise\n        loss = loss_fn(model(xb), yb)\n        loss.backward()                          # fills p.grad for every parameter\n        opt.step()\n    model.eval()\n    with torch.no_grad():                        # no graph, less memory\n        acc = ((model(X.to(device)) > 0).float() == y.to(device)).float().mean()\n    print(epoch, round(loss.item(), 4), round(acc.item(), 3))\n```\n\nThe model has 16×32+32 + 32×1+1 = 577 parameters; `loss.item()` copies one scalar back to the CPU,\nwhich forces a sync, so in a real loop you log it every N steps rather than every step.","tradeoffs":["Eager define-by-run execution makes debugging trivial, but per-op Python overhead hurts small models; `torch.compile` recovers speed by tracing to fused kernels, at the cost of compile time and graph breaks.","Explicit device placement makes data movement visible and predictable, but every boundary is a place to forget a `.to(device)`; frameworks like Accelerate hide it once you run multi-GPU.","Lower precision (bf16/fp16) halves memory and speeds tensor-core math, but fp16 needs loss scaling to avoid gradient underflow; bf16 does not, so prefer it whenever the hardware supports it.","Storing activations for backward is what makes autograd cheap in compute, but it dominates training memory; gradient checkpointing trades roughly one extra forward pass for much lower activation memory."],"pitfalls":["Forgetting `optimizer.zero_grad()`: gradients from every step add up, and the symptom is a loss that diverges or oscillates wildly after a few steps.","Running inference without `torch.no_grad()` or `inference_mode()`: every forward pass saves activations for a backward that never comes, so peak memory is higher, and if any output is kept (cached, appended to a list) its graph stays alive and memory climbs per request until a CUDA out-of-memory error.","Forgetting `model.eval()` before evaluation: dropout stays active, so the same input gives different outputs and validation metrics look noisy and worse than expected.","Accumulating `total += loss` instead of `loss.item()`: you keep every step's graph referenced, and the symptom is steadily rising GPU memory across an epoch.","Creating tensors inside `forward` with `torch.zeros(n)` after moving the model to GPU: the new tensor lands on the CPU, and the symptom is \"Expected all tensors to be on the same device\"; pass `device=x.device` or use `torch.zeros_like(x)`.","Holding a large dataset as Python lists of strings or dicts with many DataLoader workers: reference counting touches every object, so copy-on-write pages get copied into each worker, and the symptom is host RAM growing per worker until the machine OOMs; store data as NumPy arrays or Arrow instead.","Timing GPU code without `torch.cuda.synchronize()`: kernels are asynchronous, so measurements look impossibly fast and then the cost appears at the next `.item()` or `.cpu()` call."],"when":"Reach for raw PyTorch when you need control a higher-level library hides: a custom loss, a non-standard\ntraining step, inspecting activations, or debugging why a Hugging Face model misbehaves. For fine-tuning\nstandard LLMs, use Transformers, PEFT and TRL on top of it rather than hand-writing loops; for serving\nLLMs at scale, use an inference engine (vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM) rather than a PyTorch `generate` loop. What\nto measure: GPU utilisation, peak memory (`torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()`), and samples per second;\na GPU idling at low utilisation usually means the DataLoader is the bottleneck. In interviews, expect to\nexplain why gradients accumulate, what `no_grad` saves, and the difference between `eval()` and\n`no_grad()`.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes and debugs training or inference loops, fixes device mismatches, dtype errors and OOMs, and reads model code in Transformers to understand what a checkpoint actually does.","architect":"Decides when the team owns PyTorch-level code versus buying an API, and sets the GPU, precision and framework standards that training and serving pipelines inherit.","fde":"Gets a customer's model running on their hardware, often diagnosing CUDA/driver mismatches, CPU fallbacks, or memory limits on whatever GPU the customer actually has."},"check":{"q":"A colleague's inference service leaks GPU memory: every request adds a little until it crashes.\nThe code calls `model.eval()` at startup. What is the likely cause, and why doesn't `eval()` fix it?","a":"`eval()` only changes layer behaviour such as dropout and batch norm; it does not stop autograd\nfrom recording. If the forward pass runs without `torch.no_grad()` or `torch.inference_mode()`, each\noutput tensor carries a graph with saved activations, and if any output (or a running total of them) is\nkept referenced, for example appended to a list or stored in a cache, that memory is never released.\nThe fix is to wrap inference in `inference_mode()`, convert results to Python values or detached CPU\ntensors before storing them, and check `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()` stays flat across requests."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does PyTorch accumulate gradients into `.grad` instead of overwriting them?","options":["Because overwriting would break the chain rule inside a single backward pass","So that gradients from several backward passes can be summed, enabling gradient accumulation","Because GPU memory cannot be overwritten in place","So the optimizer can compute momentum without storing extra state"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accumulation lets several micro-batches contribute to one update without extra code. The price is that you must zero gradients each step; momentum is stored separately by the optimizer, not in `.grad`."},{"q":"What does `model.eval()` change?","options":["It disables gradient tracking for all parameters","It moves the model to the CPU for deterministic evaluation","It switches layers like dropout and batch norm to inference behaviour","It freezes parameters so the optimizer cannot update them"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`eval()` sets the training flag that dropout and normalisation layers read. Gradient recording is controlled separately by `no_grad`/`inference_mode`, which is why production code needs both."},{"q":"A GPU sits at low utilisation during training while CPU cores are busy. What is the most likely fix?","options":["Increase the learning rate so each step does more work","Switch the model from bf16 to fp32","Raise DataLoader `num_workers` and enable `pin_memory` so batches are prepared in parallel","Call `torch.cuda.synchronize()` after every step"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The GPU is waiting for data. Worker processes prefetch and collate batches while the GPU computes, and pinned memory allows asynchronous copies; synchronising more often would make the stall worse."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/hugging-face","sources":["Wolf et al., 2020 — Transformers: State-of-the-Art Natural Language Processing","Hu et al., 2021 — LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","Official docs: Hugging Face Transformers, Datasets, Accelerate, PEFT, TRL"],"why":"An open model is not just a weights file. To use one you need the architecture code that matches the\nweights, the config (layer count, heads, rope settings), the tokenizer with its exact vocabulary and\nspecial tokens, and the chat template that formats a conversation. Before a common hub, each research\nrelease came with its own repository and conventions, so swapping models meant rewriting glue code, and\na subtle mismatch, such as the wrong tokenizer or a missing BOS token, produced a model that ran but\ngenerated garbage with no error.\n\nTraining adds a second layer of glue. Datasets of tens of gigabytes do not fit in RAM as Python lists;\nmulti-GPU training needs process launching, gradient synchronisation and mixed precision; full fine-tuning\nof a 7B model needs memory for weights, gradients and optimizer states. In the usual mixed-precision\nAdamW accounting that is about 16 bytes per parameter (bf16 weights and gradients, plus fp32 master\nweights and two fp32 moment estimates), so roughly 112 GB for 7B before a single activation is stored,\nmore than an 80 GB GPU holds. And preference tuning (DPO, PPO-style RLHF) needs\npaired data handling, a frozen reference model and specific loss functions that are easy to get subtly\nwrong.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"write it in plain PyTorch\", is possible but means re-solving each of these per\nproject. The Hugging Face libraries standardise them behind `from_pretrained` and a few trainer classes,\nso the expensive knowledge (correct tokenisation, correct loss masking, correct sharding) lives in one\ntested place.","how":"### transformers\n`AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"org/name\")` downloads `config.json`, reads its `model_type`,\npicks the matching PyTorch class, and loads the weights (preferably `.safetensors`) into it. Task heads\nare separate classes (`...ForSequenceClassification`, `...ForCausalLM`) because the same backbone feeds\ndifferent output layers. `device_map=\"auto\"` (via Accelerate) spreads layers across available GPUs and\nCPU when a model does not fit on one device, and `dtype` (called `torch_dtype` in older releases, still\naccepted as a deprecated alias) selects precision at load time, for example `torch.bfloat16` or `\"auto\"` to\nuse the dtype stored in the checkpoint, so you do not materialise an fp32 copy first. `generate()` implements the decoding loop with the KV cache, sampling\nand stopping criteria. `pipeline()` wraps tokenise-model-decode for quick experiments.\n\n### The Hub and the local cache\nEvery `from_pretrained` call resolves a repository name plus a `revision` (branch, tag or commit hash)\nto files, downloads them once into a content-addressed cache (under `HF_HOME`), and reuses them after.\nPinning `revision` to a commit hash is what makes a load reproducible, because a branch such as `main`\ncan be rewritten by the repository owner. Setting `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` makes loads resolve only from the\ncache, which is how air-gapped deployments work once the cache has been pre-populated.\n\n### tokenizers\nThe fast tokenizers are written in Rust and exposed to Python, because tokenising a large corpus in pure\nPython is a bottleneck. `AutoTokenizer` loads the vocabulary and merges for that exact model, returns\noffsets for span alignment, and provides `apply_chat_template`, which renders a list of messages into the\nmodel's expected prompt format, using the Jinja template stored with the tokenizer. Using it, rather than\nhand-concatenating `\"User: ...\\nAssistant:\"`, is what keeps a chat model on-distribution. The same\nlibrary also trains new tokenizers (BPE, WordPiece or Unigram) from a corpus, which matters only when\nyou pre-train or adapt a vocabulary; for fine-tuning you always reuse the base model's tokenizer.\nFor batched generation with decoder-only models, pad on the left (`padding_side=\"left\"`), because\ngeneration appends to the right end of each row and right padding would put pad tokens between the\nprompt and the new tokens.\n\n### datasets\n`load_dataset` stores data as Apache Arrow files on disk and memory-maps them, so a 50 GB dataset costs\nlittle RAM and random access is cheap. `.map(fn, batched=True)` applies tokenisation in batches and caches\nthe result keyed by a fingerprint of the function and inputs, so re-running a notebook does not\nre-tokenise. `streaming=True` iterates without downloading everything first.\n\n### accelerate\nAccelerate wraps a plain training loop: `accelerator.prepare(model, optimizer, loader)` handles device\nplacement, distributed data parallel, mixed precision and FSDP/DeepSpeed backends, and `accelerate\nlaunch` starts one process per GPU. Your loop stays ordinary PyTorch; the configuration decides the\ntopology.\n\n### PEFT\nPEFT freezes the base model and injects small trainable modules. With LoRA, a frozen weight `W` gets an\nupdate `ΔW = B·A`, where `A` is r×k and `B` is d×r with rank r much smaller than d or k, so trainable\nparameters drop from d·k to r·(d+k) per adapted matrix. The forward pass computes\n`h = W·x + (α/r)·B·A·x`; `B` starts at zero, so at step 0 the adapted model is exactly the base model and\ntraining begins from known-good behaviour, and the `α/r` scale keeps the update's magnitude roughly\nstable when you change r. Optimizer state shrinks proportionally, the saved adapter is megabytes instead\nof gigabytes, and one base model can serve many adapters. For deployment you either merge,\n`W' = W + (α/r)·B·A`, which removes any extra inference cost but fixes one adapter into the weights, or\nkeep adapters separate so a server can swap them per request. LoRA is one of several PEFT methods\n(prompt/prefix tuning and IA³ are others), but it is the default because it adds no inference latency\nonce merged. QLoRA\ncombines this with a 4-bit quantized frozen base (via bitsandbytes) to fit larger models on one GPU.\n\n### TRL\nTRL provides trainers built on the `transformers` Trainer: `SFTTrainer` for supervised fine-tuning on\nchat or instruction data (applying the chat template and, optionally, computing loss only on assistant\ntokens), `DPOTrainer` for preference pairs (it needs a frozen reference model to measure how far the\npolicy has moved; with a PEFT adapter, TRL can use the base model with the adapter disabled as that\nreference instead of loading a second copy), and trainers for reward modelling and online RL methods such\nas PPO and GRPO. They accept a `peft_config`, so adapters and preference tuning compose. TRL's API has\nchanged frequently across releases, so pin the version and read the docs for the one you install.","example":"LoRA supervised fine-tuning of a small chat model with TRL and PEFT. Argument names follow recent TRL\nreleases; check them against your pinned version.\n\n```python\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nfrom trl import SFTConfig, SFTTrainer\n\n# Each row: {\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", ...}, {\"role\": \"assistant\", ...}]}\nds = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files=\"support_chats.jsonl\", split=\"train\")\nds = ds.train_test_split(test_size=0.05, seed=0)\n\npeft_cfg = LoraConfig(\n    r=16, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.05,\n    target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"k_proj\", \"v_proj\", \"o_proj\"],  # names depend on architecture\n    task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n)\nargs = SFTConfig(\n    output_dir=\"out/support-lora\",\n    per_device_train_batch_size=4, gradient_accumulation_steps=4,   # effective batch 16\n    learning_rate=2e-4, num_train_epochs=2, bf16=True,\n    logging_steps=10, eval_strategy=\"steps\", eval_steps=100,\n)\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(\n    model=\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\",   # loaded via transformers; chat template from its tokenizer\n    args=args, train_dataset=ds[\"train\"], eval_dataset=ds[\"test\"],\n    peft_config=peft_cfg,\n)\ntrainer.train()\ntrainer.save_model()   # writes only adapter weights (adapter_model.safetensors) plus config\n```\n\nParameter arithmetic for one 4096×4096 projection with r=16: full update is 16.8M parameters; LoRA\ntrains 16×(4096+4096) = 131,072, under 1% of that matrix, and with α=32 the learned update is scaled by\nα/r = 2. The effective batch is per-device batch × accumulation steps × number of GPUs, so the same\nconfig on 4 GPUs trains with batch 64, which is why learning rates must be revisited when the topology\nchanges. `trainer.save_model()` writes megabytes of adapter weights; to serve without PEFT at runtime,\nload the base, attach the adapter and call `merge_and_unload()`.","tradeoffs":["`from_pretrained` by name gives instant access to thousands of models, but it fetches weights at runtime (and, with `trust_remote_code=True`, Python code too) from whatever the repository's branch currently holds; pin a revision hash and mirror artifacts once you leave experimentation.","Trainer classes remove boilerplate and encode correct defaults, but they hide the loop; when you need a custom loss or schedule, subclassing can become harder than writing the PyTorch loop directly.","LoRA cuts memory and storage dramatically and allows many adapters per base, but it can underfit tasks that need broad behaviour change, where full fine-tuning (if affordable) does better.","`transformers.generate` is fine for experiments and batch jobs, but production serving with many concurrent users is better handled by an inference engine such as vLLM or SGLang, which add continuous batching and a paged KV cache.","`device_map=\"auto\"` makes a too-large model run, but layers offloaded to CPU make generation dramatically slower; it trades failure for latency, which is only acceptable offline."],"pitfalls":["Formatting prompts by hand instead of `apply_chat_template`: the model answers but quality is oddly poor, ignores the system prompt, or keeps generating past where it should stop.","Loss computed on prompt tokens as well as completions during SFT: the model learns to imitate users, and the symptom is outputs that echo or continue the question instead of answering it.","`trust_remote_code=True` on an unvetted repository: it executes arbitrary Python from the hub at load time; the symptom may be nothing visible at all, which is the danger.","Unpinned library versions: TRL or transformers upgrades rename arguments or change defaults, and a training script that worked last month fails or silently trains differently.","Right-padding batched prompts for a decoder-only model in `generate()`: outputs for shorter prompts are degraded or nonsensical, and transformers warns that right-padding was detected; set `tokenizer.padding_side = \"left\"` for generation.","Mismatched tokenizer and model (for example after adding special tokens without resizing embeddings): index errors on new token ids, or generations that never emit the end-of-turn token."],"when":"Use `transformers` + `tokenizers` whenever you run an open-weight model in Python for experiments,\noffline batch work, evaluation or embedding; use `datasets` whenever training or eval data exceeds a few\nhundred megabytes or needs reproducible preprocessing. Reach for PEFT + TRL when prompting and retrieval\nhave plateaued and you have a few thousand high-quality examples of the target behaviour; reach for\nAccelerate when one GPU is not enough. Do not use this stack to serve high-concurrency traffic; export\nthe model (merged weights or base plus adapter) to an inference engine. Measure eval loss and a task\nmetric on a held-out set, not training loss. Interviewers commonly ask about LoRA's parameter\narithmetic, why chat templates matter, and when to fine-tune instead of prompt.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Loads checkpoints with correct dtype and template, writes SFT/DPO jobs with PEFT, and debugs tokenisation, loss masking and version drift.","architect":"Decides fine-tune versus prompt-and-retrieve, sets the hub mirroring and revision-pinning policy, and chooses adapter-per-tenant versus merged models for serving.","fde":"Fine-tunes or evaluates an open model inside a customer's air-gapped environment, which means pre-downloading weights, datasets and wheels, and loading them offline."},"check":{"q":"A team fine-tuned a 7B instruct model with LoRA on 5,000 support transcripts. Training loss fell\nnicely, but in testing the model rambles, sometimes writes the customer's next message, and ignores the\nsystem prompt. What do you check first?","a":"These symptoms point at data formatting, not capacity. First, was the data rendered with the base\nmodel's own chat template via `apply_chat_template`, including its end-of-turn token? If not, the model\nlearned a new format that conflicts with its instruction tuning and never learned to stop. Second, was\nloss masked to assistant tokens only? Training on user turns teaches the model to write user turns. Third,\nwas the system prompt present in training examples? Then verify inference uses the same template. Only\nafter formatting is correct would I tune rank, learning rate or epochs, and I would judge with a task\nmetric on held-out conversations, since falling training loss says little about behaviour."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does LoRA reduce GPU memory during fine-tuning so much more than it reduces inference memory?","options":["Because LoRA quantizes the base model to 4 bits automatically","Because gradients and optimizer states are only kept for the small adapter matrices, not the frozen base","Because LoRA removes the KV cache during training","Because the adapter replaces the attention layers entirely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Training memory is dominated by gradients and AdamW states for every trainable weight. Freezing the base removes those for almost all parameters; the frozen weights still must be loaded, so inference barely changes."},{"q":"What does `datasets` gain by storing data as memory-mapped Arrow files?","options":["It compresses text so tokenisation is faster","It guarantees the data is deduplicated","Large datasets can be accessed randomly without loading them fully into RAM","It encrypts the data for compliance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Memory mapping lets the operating system page in only the rows you touch, so a dataset far larger than RAM is usable. Compression and deduplication are separate concerns that Arrow does not imply."},{"q":"Why use `tokenizer.apply_chat_template` instead of building the prompt string yourself?","options":["It makes the prompt shorter so it costs fewer tokens","It is required for the model to accept a system prompt at all","It reproduces the exact special tokens and turn format the model was trained on","It automatically removes prompt-injection attempts from user messages"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Chat models are trained on one specific format of role markers and end-of-turn tokens. Deviating keeps the model running but off-distribution, which degrades quality and stopping behaviour without any error."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/sentence-transformers","sources":["Reimers & Gurevych, 2019 — Sentence-BERT: Sentence Embeddings using Siamese BERT-Networks","Official docs: Sentence Transformers"],"why":"A BERT-style encoder produces one vector per token, not per sentence. The obvious shortcuts, taking the\n`[CLS]` vector or averaging token vectors from a model trained only on masked-language modelling, give\nvectors whose cosine similarity correlates poorly with semantic similarity; the model was never trained\nto place paraphrases close together. The accurate alternative, feeding both sentences into one model\nand classifying the pair (a cross-encoder), works well but costs a full forward pass per pair: to find\nthe best match for one query among a million documents you would run a million forward passes per\nquery.\n\nSentence-BERT's insight was to fine-tune the encoder in a siamese setup, two copies with shared\nweights, so that each sentence is encoded independently and a simple similarity between the two vectors\nis trained to reflect relatedness. Independent encoding is the key property: document vectors can be\ncomputed once, stored in a vector index, and each query costs one forward pass plus a nearest-neighbour\nlookup. The library grew around that idea and now packages the model loading, pooling, batching,\nmulti-GPU encoding, contrastive training losses and the cross-encoder rerankers you put after\nretrieval. Without it, every team re-implements pooling and normalisation, and small differences (mean\nversus CLS pooling, forgetting to normalise, missing query prefixes) silently degrade retrieval.","how":"### Bi-encoder: `SentenceTransformer`\nA model is a pipeline of modules saved together: a Transformer module (a Hugging Face encoder with its\ntokenizer and max sequence length), a Pooling module (mean of token vectors masked by attention, CLS, or\nlast-token for decoder-based embedders), and optionally a Dense projection and a Normalize step.\n`encode(texts, batch_size=..., normalize_embeddings=True)` tokenises, sorts inputs by length to reduce\npadding waste, runs batches on the chosen device under no-grad, pools and returns a NumPy array or\ntensor of shape `[n, dim]`. Normalising to unit length means dot product equals cosine similarity,\nwhich is what most vector indexes compute fastest.\n\nMean pooling is `e = Σᵢ mᵢ·hᵢ / Σᵢ mᵢ`, where `hᵢ` is token i's output vector and `mᵢ` is 1 for real\ntokens and 0 for padding. The mask matters because padding vectors are not zero; averaging them in would\nmake the same sentence embed differently depending on what else was in its batch. Which pooling is\ncorrect is a property of how the model was trained, not a free choice, which is why the library stores\nthe pooling config with the weights instead of asking you to pick.\n\n### Prompts and asymmetry\nMany retrieval models are trained asymmetrically: queries and passages get different instruction\nprefixes (for example E5's `\"query: \"` and `\"passage: \"`). The library stores such prompts in the model\nconfig and applies them via `prompt_name` (and, from v5, through `encode_query` / `encode_document`,\nwhich pick the right prompt for you), because forgetting the prefix measurably hurts retrieval for\nthose models while raising no error.\n\n### Cross-encoder: `CrossEncoder`\nA cross-encoder takes the concatenated `(query, document)` pair and outputs one relevance score.\nAttention runs across both texts, so it captures interactions a dot product cannot, and it is usually\nmore accurate, but it cannot precompute anything. The standard pattern is retrieve-then-rerank: a\nbi-encoder fetches the top 50-200 candidates cheaply, then a cross-encoder rescores only those.\n\n### Training\nFine-tuning uses contrastive losses. `MultipleNegativesRankingLoss` takes (anchor, positive) pairs and\ntreats every other positive in the batch as a negative. For anchor `aᵢ` in a batch of B pairs it is a\nB-way softmax cross-entropy:\n\n```\nloss_i = -log( exp(s·cos(aᵢ, pᵢ)) / Σⱼ exp(s·cos(aᵢ, pⱼ)) )      # j = 1..B, s is a scale (default 20)\n```\n\nso each step asks \"pick your true positive out of B candidates\". Larger batches mean more, and on\naverage harder, negatives for free, which is why batch size matters more here than in classification;\n`CachedMultipleNegativesRankingLoss` reaches large effective batches without the memory by computing\nembeddings in chunks. Adding a mined hard negative per pair (anchor, positive, negative), a passage that\nlooks relevant but is not, teaches the distinctions random in-batch negatives never test. One trap:\nduplicates in a batch (two pairs with the same positive) become false negatives, so deduplicate. Recent versions train through a\n`SentenceTransformerTrainer` built on the Hugging Face Trainer, with evaluators that report retrieval\nmetrics such as recall@k and NDCG on a held-out set.\n\n### Matryoshka embeddings\nModels trained with `MatryoshkaLoss` pack the most important information into the leading dimensions, so\nyou can keep, say, the first 256 of 768 dimensions (`truncate_dim=256`) and accept a modest recall loss\nfor a 3× smaller index. A model not trained this way degrades badly when truncated, so check the model\ncard before cutting dimensions, and re-normalise after truncation.\n\n### Truncation\nEach model has a `max_seq_length`; longer inputs are silently truncated. That limit, not the model's\ntheoretical context, decides your chunk size.","example":"Retrieve-then-rerank over a small corpus. `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` produces 384-dimensional vectors.\n\n```python\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer, CrossEncoder\n\ndocs = [\n    \"Refunds are issued within 14 days of a returned item being received.\",\n    \"Our warehouse ships orders Monday to Friday.\",\n    \"To reset your password, use the link on the sign-in page.\",\n    \"Returned items must be unused and in original packaging.\",\n]\nbi = SentenceTransformer(\"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\ndoc_emb = bi.encode(docs, normalize_embeddings=True, batch_size=64)   # shape (4, 384), computed once\n\nquery = \"how long until I get my money back after returning something\"\nq_emb = bi.encode(query, normalize_embeddings=True)\nscores = doc_emb @ q_emb                       # dot product == cosine because vectors are unit length\ntop = scores.argsort()[::-1][:3]               # cheap first stage: top-k candidates\n\nce = CrossEncoder(\"cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2\")\npairs = [(query, docs[i]) for i in top]\nrerank = ce.predict(pairs)                     # one forward pass per pair, only for k candidates\nfor i, s in sorted(zip(top, rerank), key=lambda t: -t[1]):\n    print(f\"{s:7.3f}  {docs[i]}\")\nprint(bi.max_seq_length)                       # inputs beyond this many tokens are truncated\n```\n\nWhat to look for when you run it: both refund-related passages score well with the bi-encoder because\nthey share the topic of returns, while the cross-encoder, which reads the query and passage together,\nshould separate the one that answers \"how long\" (the 14-day passage) from the one that only mentions\nreturns. Exact scores vary by model version; the ordering is the lesson.\n\nCost shape: with N documents and k candidates, a query costs 1 bi-encoder pass plus k cross-encoder\npasses, instead of N cross-encoder passes. At N = 1,000,000 and k = 50 that is 51 forward passes per\nquery instead of a million.","tradeoffs":["Bi-encoders allow precomputed document vectors and millisecond search, but compress each text to one vector and miss fine query-document interactions; add a cross-encoder when top-k precision matters.","Running embeddings locally avoids per-token API cost and keeps data in-house, but you own GPU capacity and batching; hosted embedding APIs win when volume is small or ops capacity is scarce.","Small models (MiniLM-class) are fast enough for CPU, but larger or instruction-tuned embedders usually retrieve better; the gap must be measured on your queries, not read from a public leaderboard.","Fine-tuning on in-domain pairs often beats a bigger generic model, but requires re-embedding the whole corpus with the new model, since vectors from different models are not comparable."],"pitfalls":["Forgetting `normalize_embeddings=True` with a model that does not already end in a Normalize module, when the index uses dot product: documents whose vectors happen to have a large magnitude get inflated scores, and the symptom is retrieval favouring the same few documents for every query.","Omitting a model's required query or passage prefix: no error, just noticeably worse recall than the model card claims.","Chunks longer than `max_seq_length`: the tail is silently dropped, so facts near the end of a chunk are never retrievable.","Mixing vectors from two model versions in one index after an upgrade: similarity scores become meaningless, and the symptom is a sudden, unexplained retrieval quality drop.","Thresholding cross-encoder scores (\"keep passages scoring above 0.5\") and then swapping the model: scores are model-specific logits or sigmoid outputs, not calibrated probabilities, and the symptom is a reranker that suddenly keeps nothing or everything; re-tune thresholds per model, or keep a fixed top-k.","Encoding one text at a time in a loop: throughput collapses because the GPU processes batches of one; pass lists and let `encode` batch."],"when":"Reach for sentence-transformers when you need embeddings or reranking from open models: self-hosted\nRAG, data that cannot leave the network, high-volume offline embedding where API costs add up, semantic\ndeduplication, clustering, or fine-tuning an embedder on your domain. Prefer a hosted embedding API when\nvolume is modest and you do not want to run GPUs. Before choosing a model, build a small labelled set of\nreal queries with their correct passages and measure recall@k and MRR for two or three candidates;\nchoose on that, and record the model name and version alongside every stored vector. In interviews,\nexplain bi-encoder versus cross-encoder cost and why in-batch negatives make batch size matter.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Embeds corpora in batches, applies correct prefixes and normalisation, adds a cross-encoder reranker, and fine-tunes with contrastive loss when generic models miss domain terms.","architect":"Owns the embedding model choice and versioning policy, the re-index plan for upgrades, and the decision between self-hosted encoders and a hosted embedding API.","fde":"Runs local embedding inside a customer's network when documents cannot be sent to an API, and evaluates candidate models on the customer's own queries before committing."},"check":{"q":"Your RAG system uses a bi-encoder for top-5 retrieval. Users complain answers cite related but\nwrong passages. Why might adding a cross-encoder fix this, and how would you size it?","a":"A bi-encoder compresses each passage into one vector before seeing the query, so it ranks by\ntopical closeness and can miss which passage actually answers the specific question. A cross-encoder\nreads query and passage together, so attention can match details such as the exact entity, date or\nnegation. I would retrieve a larger candidate set, say top 50, with the bi-encoder to protect recall,\nrerank with a cross-encoder and keep the top 5. Latency grows with the candidate count, so I would\nmeasure recall@50 to make sure the right passage is in the pool, then tune the pool size against p95\nlatency and a precision metric on labelled queries."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a bi-encoder search a million documents quickly while a cross-encoder cannot?","options":["Bi-encoders use smaller transformer models than cross-encoders","Bi-encoders encode documents independently of the query, so document vectors are precomputed once","Cross-encoders cannot run on GPUs","Bi-encoders skip tokenisation entirely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Independent encoding lets you store document vectors and answer queries with one forward pass plus a vector lookup. A cross-encoder needs the query and each document together, so nothing can be precomputed."},{"q":"With `MultipleNegativesRankingLoss`, why does increasing batch size often improve the embedder?","options":["Larger batches reduce the learning rate automatically","Larger batches prevent truncation of long inputs","Every other positive in the batch acts as a negative, so larger batches supply more negatives per example","Larger batches make embeddings higher-dimensional"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The loss contrasts each anchor with its positive against all other in-batch positives. More items per batch means a harder, more informative contrast; dimension and truncation are fixed by the model."},{"q":"Dot-product scores in your index favour the same few documents regardless of the query. What is the likely cause?","options":["The cross-encoder was trained on short texts","The model's max sequence length is too large","Embeddings were not normalised, so vector magnitude influences the score","The tokenizer is case-sensitive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without unit normalisation, dot product mixes direction with magnitude, and magnitude can correlate with text properties such as length or rarity, so high-norm documents win everywhere. Normalising makes dot product equal cosine similarity, which compares direction only."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/tiktoken","sources":["Sennrich et al., 2016 — Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units","Official docs: tiktoken"],"why":"Everything an LLM API constrains is measured in tokens, not characters or words: the context window, the\nmaximum output, rate limits (tokens per minute) and the bill. If you only learn the count after the\nrequest, from the response's usage field, you cannot prevent the failures that matter: a prompt that\noverflows the context window and is rejected, a retrieved context that crowds out the answer budget, or\na batch job whose cost you could not estimate in advance.\n\nThe naive alternative is a heuristic such as \"about four characters per token\". That is a reasonable\naverage for English prose, but it is wrong by large factors exactly where it hurts: code, JSON, URLs,\nnumbers, and most non-English scripts tokenise into many more tokens per character, so a heuristic that\nis fine for a blog post overflows the window on a stack trace or a Japanese contract. The other naive\noption, calling an API to count, adds latency and a network dependency to every prompt you assemble.\n\ntiktoken solves this by shipping the exact vocabulary and merge ranks for OpenAI encodings and applying\nthem locally, quickly enough to run on every request and over whole corpora during chunking.","how":"### Byte-level BPE\nText is first split into chunks by a regular expression (separating words, numbers, punctuation and\nwhitespace runs in an encoding-specific way), then each chunk is converted to UTF-8 bytes. BPE then\nrepeatedly merges the adjacent pair of byte sequences with the lowest rank in the learned merge table\nuntil no mergeable pair remains; each final piece maps to one token id. Working on bytes means any\nstring, including emoji or invalid-looking input, can be encoded without an unknown-token fallback,\nbecause every byte is in the base vocabulary.\n\nA toy walk-through with made-up ranks shows the algorithm. Suppose the merge table ranks `(l, o)` = 1,\n`(lo, w)` = 2 and `(e, r)` = 3, and the chunk is `lower`. Start from bytes `l o w e r`. The\nlowest-ranked adjacent pair present is `(l, o)`, giving `lo w e r`; next `(lo, w)`, giving `low e r`;\nnext `(e, r)`, giving `low er`. No adjacent pair (`low`, `er`) has a rank, so encoding stops with two\ntokens. Ranks come from training: the most frequent pairs in the training corpus were merged first, so\ncommon words collapse into single tokens and rare strings stay in many small pieces. That is the whole\nreason token counts depend on how \"typical\" your text is.\n\nThe regex pre-split exists so merges never cross certain boundaries. A leading space stays attached to\nthe following word (`\" world\"` is one token, not a space plus `world`), punctuation does not fuse onto\nwords, and in `cl100k_base` and `o200k_base` digit runs are split into groups of at most three. Without\nthe split, the vocabulary would waste entries on accidental cross-word fragments, and numbers would\ntokenise unpredictably.\n\n### Encodings\nAn encoding is the regex plus the merge ranks plus special tokens. `r50k_base` and `p50k_base` belong to\nGPT-2/GPT-3-era models, `cl100k_base` to the GPT-3.5/GPT-4 generation, and `o200k_base` to GPT-4o and\nlater models. `tiktoken.encoding_for_model(name)` maps a model name to its encoding; use it rather than\nhard-coding, because newer models may use a different encoding. Larger vocabularies tend to encode the\nsame text in fewer tokens, especially for non-English text.\n\n### API surface\n`enc.encode(text)` returns a list of ints, `enc.decode(ids)` returns text, `enc.encode_ordinary(text)`\nignores special tokens entirely, and `encode_batch` parallelises over many strings. By default `encode`\nraises an error if the text contains a special-token string such as `<|endoftext|>`; this is a deliberate\nsafety choice, because silently encoding user text as a real control token would let input inject\ndocument boundaries. You opt in with `allowed_special` or treat them as plain text with\n`disallowed_special=()`.\n\n### Chat overhead\nChat requests add per-message formatting tokens (role markers and separators) that are not in your text.\nThe exact overhead depends on the model and is not a stable public contract, so treat local counts of\nmessage content as close estimates and reconcile against the `usage` field the API returns.\n\n### Caching\nThe first time an encoding is used, tiktoken downloads its rank file and caches it on disk\n(configurable with the `TIKTOKEN_CACHE_DIR` environment variable). In air-gapped or locked-down\nenvironments the first call fails unless the file is pre-placed in that cache.","example":"Budgeting a RAG prompt so retrieved chunks never crowd out the answer.\n\n```python\nimport tiktoken\n\nenc = tiktoken.encoding_for_model(\"gpt-4o\")      # resolves to o200k_base\nCONTEXT, RESERVED_OUTPUT, OVERHEAD = 128_000, 4_000, 200   # overhead: rough allowance for chat formatting\n\ndef n_tokens(s: str) -> int:\n    return len(enc.encode(s, disallowed_special=()))   # treat '<|endoftext|>' in user text as plain text\n\ndef pack(system: str, question: str, chunks: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n    budget = CONTEXT - RESERVED_OUTPUT - OVERHEAD - n_tokens(system) - n_tokens(question)\n    kept = []\n    for c in chunks:                              # chunks already sorted by relevance\n        cost = n_tokens(c)\n        if cost > budget:\n            break\n        kept.append(c)\n        budget -= cost\n    return kept\n\nfor s in [\"hello world\", '{\"user_id\": 98213, \"ok\": true}', \"東京都の天気\"]:\n    ids = enc.encode(s)\n    print(len(s), \"chars ->\", len(ids), \"tokens\", [enc.decode_single_token_bytes(t) for t in ids])\n# \"hello world\" -> [b'hello', b' world']: the space belongs to the second token.\n# For the Japanese string some pieces may be partial UTF-8 byte sequences, not whole characters.\n# The chars-to-tokens ratio differs sharply between English, JSON and Japanese; that spread is\n# exactly why a fixed chars/4 heuristic misbudgets prompts.\n```\n\nTruncating by tokens, `enc.decode(ids[:n])`, can cut a multi-byte character in half; the decoder then\nemits a replacement character, so truncate on chunk or sentence boundaries where possible.","tradeoffs":["Local exact counts for OpenAI models are fast and free, but they are only estimates for Anthropic, Gemini or open models; use those providers' own token-counting endpoints or tokenizers when exactness matters.","Counting every prompt prevents context-overflow errors before they happen, but adds CPU work per request; it is negligible for chat, but worth batching with `encode_batch` over large corpora.","Token-based chunking gives predictable chunk sizes against model limits, but cuts across sentences unless combined with structural splitting, which can hurt retrieval quality.","The `usage` field in responses is authoritative and costs nothing, but arrives after the money is spent; use local counts for admission control and the usage field for billing and reconciliation."],"pitfalls":["Using tiktoken counts to budget a non-OpenAI model: prompts that \"fit\" get rejected or truncated by the provider, especially for code and non-English text where tokenizers diverge most.","Hard-coding `cl100k_base` for every model: counts drift for newer models on a different encoding, and the symptom is cost estimates that disagree with billed usage.","Calling `encode` on raw user input without setting special-token handling: a document containing `<|endoftext|>` raises an exception and the request fails with a confusing error.","Forgetting chat-format and tool-schema overhead: requests near the limit fail even though your content-only count said they fit, because tool definitions and role markers also consume tokens.","Deploying to an environment without internet access: the first encode call fails trying to download the encoding file, unless the cache directory was pre-populated."],"when":"Use tiktoken whenever you assemble prompts dynamically for OpenAI models: packing retrieved context,\ntrimming conversation history, enforcing per-user token limits, chunking documents to a token size, and\nestimating cost for a batch job before you run it. For other providers, use it only as a rough\nestimate and prefer their official counter, or the Hugging Face tokenizer for an open model. Measure the\ngap between your local estimate and the API's reported input count (`usage.prompt_tokens` in Chat\nCompletions, `usage.input_tokens` in the Responses API) on real traffic; a stable\nsmall gap is chat overhead, a large or growing gap means the wrong encoding. Interviewers like asking why\ncharacter heuristics fail for code and non-English text.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Counts tokens to pack context, trims history to a budget, and chunks corpora by token length using the encoding that matches the target model.","architect":"Sets context-budget policy (reserved output tokens, retrieval share) and decides where local estimates suffice versus where provider-reported usage drives billing.","fde":"Estimates a customer's monthly token spend from a sample of their real documents, and pre-seeds the tiktoken cache for offline deployments."},"check":{"q":"A team estimates monthly cost for a code-review assistant by counting characters in sample\ndiffs and dividing by four. Actual bills come in much higher. Explain why and how you would fix the\nestimate.","a":"The four-characters-per-token rule is an average for English prose. Code has short identifiers,\nsymbols, indentation runs and numbers that BPE splits into more tokens per character, so the heuristic\nundercounts. The fix is to tokenise a representative sample of real diffs with the target model's\nencoding, via `encoding_for_model`, measure the actual token distribution including system prompt,\ntool definitions and chat overhead, and multiply by expected volume and output length. Then reconcile\nwith `usage` numbers from a pilot week, because billed input also includes formatting and any retries."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does tiktoken's `encode` raise an error by default when text contains `<|endoftext|>`?","options":["Because that string cannot be represented in UTF-8","To avoid silently turning user-supplied text into a real control token","Because special tokens are only valid in the output, never the input","Because the encoding file does not contain special tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Encoding the string as the actual special token would let untrusted text insert document boundaries. The default forces you to choose: allow it explicitly, or treat it as ordinary text."},{"q":"Your tiktoken count says a prompt is 7,500 tokens, but a non-OpenAI model reports 9,000. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["tiktoken skips whitespace when counting","The provider double-counts the system prompt","The other model uses a different tokenizer, so the same text produces a different number of tokens","tiktoken counts only the last message in a conversation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Token counts are a property of a specific vocabulary and merge table. tiktoken is exact only for OpenAI encodings; for other providers, use their own token-counting tool when the number matters."},{"q":"Why does byte-level BPE never need an \"unknown token\"?","options":["Because its vocabulary includes every word in every language","Because unknown characters are removed before encoding","Because every possible byte is in the base vocabulary, so any string can be encoded","Because it falls back to character-level encoding using Unicode code points"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Text is converted to UTF-8 bytes and all 256 byte values are base tokens, so any input decomposes into known pieces. Merges only make common sequences shorter; they are never required for coverage."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/safetensors","sources":["Official docs: safetensors","Official docs: PyTorch serialization semantics"],"why":"For years the default way to save PyTorch weights was `torch.save`, which uses Python's `pickle`. Pickle\nis not a data format; it is a small program that the unpickler executes to rebuild objects, and that\nprogram can call any importable function, including `os.system`. So a `.bin` or `.pt` checkpoint\ndownloaded from the internet is executable code. Once model hubs made it routine to pull weights from\nstrangers, \"load a model\" became \"run an untrusted program\", and malicious pickles were found in the\nwild. The mechanism fits in four lines: any object can tell pickle how to rebuild it via `__reduce__`,\nand the rebuild recipe is \"call this function with these arguments\".\n\n```python\nimport pickle\nclass Payload:\n    def __reduce__(self):\n        return (print, (\"this ran during load\",))   # an attacker would return (os.system, (\"...\",))\npickle.loads(pickle.dumps(Payload()))               # prints: the call happens inside loads()\n```\n\nNothing in a checkpoint file distinguishes \"rebuild a tensor\" from \"call os.system\"; both are calls.\n\nThere was also a performance problem. Unpickling builds tensors by copying data into freshly allocated\nmemory, so loading a large model needs the bytes read and copied, sometimes twice when moving to GPU,\nand you cannot read one tensor without parsing the whole file. Partial loading, needed to shard a model\nacross GPUs or to load one layer for inspection, was awkward.\n\nThe naive fix, `torch.load(..., weights_only=True)`, restricts the unpickler to an allow-list of types\nand is the right default for legacy files (recent PyTorch releases made it the default). But it still\nruns a pickle interpreter over untrusted input, and its safety rests on the allow-list having no holes.\n(`torch.load(..., mmap=True)` on the newer zip-based checkpoint format narrows the speed gap, but not\nthe security one.)\nsafetensors removes the interpreter entirely: the format has no way to express code, so there is nothing\nto restrict.","how":"### File layout\n1. **8 bytes:** an unsigned little-endian 64-bit integer N, the size of the header.\n2. **N bytes:** a UTF-8 JSON object. Each key is a tensor name mapping to\n   `{\"dtype\": \"BF16\", \"shape\": [4096, 4096], \"data_offsets\": [begin, end]}`; an optional\n   `\"__metadata__\"` key holds string-to-string metadata such as the format or training info.\n   The reference writer pads the header with spaces so the data buffer starts 8-byte aligned, which\n   lets loaders view tensors in place without an alignment copy.\n3. **The rest:** one contiguous byte buffer; each tensor occupies `[begin, end)` relative to the start\n   of this buffer, stored row-major with no padding inside a tensor.\n\nEvery design choice follows from \"the loader should only ever read\". JSON can only describe data. The\nheader is parsed first and validated (offsets must be in bounds and not overlap, sizes must match\n`dtype × shape`) before any tensor bytes are touched, which blocks malformed files that try to trigger\nout-of-bounds reads.\n\n### Zero-copy and lazy loading\nBecause each tensor sits at a known offset in raw form, the file can be memory-mapped: the operating\nsystem maps it into the address space and pages in only the bytes actually read. Locating a slice is\npure arithmetic: for a row-major `[rows, cols]` tensor of element size `b` starting at `begin`, row `i`\nlives at bytes `begin + i·cols·b` to `begin + (i+1)·cols·b`, so no parsing of the payload is needed. `safe_open` returns a\nhandle where `get_tensor(name)` materialises one tensor and `get_slice(name)[a:b]` reads only a slice,\nwhich is how tensor-parallel loaders give each GPU just its shard of a weight matrix without reading the\nrest.\n\n### Sharding\nLarge models are split into several files (`model-00001-of-00004.safetensors`, ...) plus\n`model.safetensors.index.json`, which maps each tensor name to its shard. Loaders read the index,\nthen open only the shards they need.\n\n### Framework neutrality\nThe format stores dtype and raw bytes, not Python objects, so the same file loads into PyTorch, NumPy,\nJAX/Flax, TensorFlow or Rust code (`safetensors.torch`, `safetensors.numpy`, ...).\n\n### What it deliberately does not store\nNo optimizer state objects, no model class, no arbitrary Python. Tensors that share memory (tied\nembedding weights) cannot be saved as aliases; `save_file` refuses them, and helpers such as\n`save_model` deduplicate them and rely on the loading code to re-tie.","example":"Save, load lazily, and read the header by hand to see that it is just JSON.\n\n```python\nimport json, struct, torch\nfrom safetensors.torch import save_file, load_file\nfrom safetensors import safe_open\n\nweights = {\n    \"embed.weight\": torch.randn(32000, 512, dtype=torch.bfloat16),\n    \"head.weight\": torch.randn(10, 512),\n}\nsave_file(weights, \"model.safetensors\", metadata={\"format\": \"pt\", \"run\": \"exp-42\"})\n\nwith open(\"model.safetensors\", \"rb\") as f:            # no library needed to inspect\n    n = struct.unpack(\"<Q\", f.read(8))[0]\n    header = json.loads(f.read(n))\ne = header[\"embed.weight\"]      # {'dtype': 'BF16', 'shape': [32000, 512], 'data_offsets': [begin, end]}\nprint(e[\"data_offsets\"][1] - e[\"data_offsets\"][0])     # 32768000 bytes\n\nwith safe_open(\"model.safetensors\", framework=\"pt\", device=\"cpu\") as f:\n    rows = f.get_slice(\"embed.weight\")[:1000]          # reads 1000 rows, not the whole matrix\n    print(rows.shape, f.metadata())\n\nfull = load_file(\"model.safetensors\")                  # dict[str, Tensor]\n```\n\nThe embedding is 32,000 × 512 × 2 bytes = 32,768,000 bytes, exactly `end - begin` in its\n`data_offsets`; where each tensor starts depends on the writer's ordering, so read offsets, never assume them.\nThe 1,000-row slice touches 1,000 × 512 × 2 = 1,024,000 bytes, about 3% of the tensor, which is the\nwhole argument for lazy loading when each of eight tensor-parallel ranks needs only an eighth of every\nmatrix. Note the header is readable with `json` and `struct` alone: an auditor can list every tensor,\ndtype and shape in a file without installing any ML library or trusting any code.","tradeoffs":["No code execution on load removes a whole class of supply-chain attacks, but the format can only hold tensors; tokenizer files, configs and training state need their own (also non-executable) formats.","Memory-mapping makes loading fast and lazy on local disk, but on network filesystems or object storage mounts page faults can be slow; copying to local NVMe first often wins for large models.","Framework-neutral raw bytes make files portable, but you lose the convenience of pickling an entire object graph; full training checkpoints with optimizer and scheduler state still often use `torch.save`.","safetensors is the standard for Transformers-style checkpoints, but llama.cpp-style local inference uses GGUF, which also embeds tokenizer metadata and quantized types; conversion is a deployment step."],"pitfalls":["Assuming safetensors makes a model trustworthy: it only guarantees loading runs no code; the weights can still be backdoored, and `trust_remote_code` model classes can still execute arbitrary Python.","Loading legacy `.bin` files with `weights_only=False` because a load error appeared: this re-enables arbitrary code execution, and the symptom you were fixing was the safety check working.","Saving a model with tied weights via `save_file` directly: it raises an error about shared tensors, and naive workarounds that clone the tensors store the bytes twice; the tie then survives only if the loading code re-ties the parameters, which `save_model` / `load_model` handle for you.","Forgetting the index file when copying sharded checkpoints: loaders fail to find tensors or load only part of the model, and the error names a missing key rather than a missing file."],"when":"Use safetensors for any weights you publish, download, mirror or move between teams, and convert legacy\npickle checkpoints when you take them in. Make \"no pickle from outside\" a policy: CI or the model\nregistry rejects `.bin`/`.pt`/`.pkl` artifacts from external sources unless they are converted in a\nsandbox. Keep `torch.save` only for internal training-resume checkpoints that hold optimizer state, and\nnever load those from untrusted locations. When loading time matters, measure time to first token after\na cold start with memory-mapped local files versus network storage. In interviews, be ready to explain\nwhy pickle is unsafe and what memory mapping buys.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Saves and loads weights with safetensors, converts legacy checkpoints, and uses `safe_open` slices to inspect or shard weights without loading the whole file.","architect":"Sets the model-artifact policy: allowed formats, conversion and scanning in a sandbox, provenance metadata, and storage layout that keeps cold-start loading fast.","fde":"Meets customers whose security teams block pickle files, and shows that safetensors artifacts can be audited as plain data before they enter the customer's network."},"check":{"q":"A security reviewer says, \"We switched to safetensors, so models from the hub are now safe to\nrun.\" What is right and what is wrong about that statement?","a":"It is right that loading a safetensors file cannot execute code: the format is a JSON header plus\nraw bytes, so the pickle-based remote-code-execution path is gone. It is wrong to conclude the model is\nsafe. Weights can encode backdoors or poisoned behaviour that only an evaluation reveals; repositories\ncan still require `trust_remote_code`, which runs Python model code; tokenizers and configs come from the\nsame source; and other files in the repo may still be pickles. A sound policy combines safetensors-only\nweights, pinned revisions, disallowing remote code unless reviewed, provenance checks, and behavioural\nevaluation before deployment."},"quiz":[{"q":"What makes loading a pickle-based PyTorch checkpoint dangerous?","options":["Pickle files are not compressed, so they can exhaust disk space","Unpickling executes instructions that can call arbitrary Python functions","Pickle stores tensors in a format GPUs cannot read","Pickle files cannot be checksummed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pickle is a small program run by the unpickler to rebuild objects, and it may invoke any importable callable. safetensors stores only a JSON header and raw bytes, so there is no program to run."},{"q":"How does safetensors let each GPU load only its shard of a large weight matrix?","options":["It splits every tensor into one file per GPU at save time","It compresses tensors so they decompress faster","Tensors are stored raw at known offsets, so a slice can be read from a memory-mapped file","It stores the model class, which knows how to shard itself"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Known offsets plus row-major raw storage let a loader compute and read only the bytes for the requested rows. Sharded files exist too, but slicing works within a single tensor."},{"q":"Why might you still use `torch.save` inside a training job?","options":["Because safetensors cannot store bf16 tensors","Because safetensors files cannot exceed 2 GB","To checkpoint optimizer and scheduler state, which are Python objects rather than plain tensor dicts","Because safetensors loads slower than pickle in every case"],"answer":2,"explanation":"safetensors deliberately stores only named tensors. Training-resume checkpoints often include non-tensor state, so `torch.save` remains common internally, as long as those files never come from untrusted sources."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/openai-sdk","sources":["Official docs: OpenAI API reference","Official docs: openai-python"],"why":"Calling an LLM over raw HTTP looks easy: POST JSON, read JSON. The details are where production code\nbreaks. Rate-limit responses (429) and transient 5xx errors are routine at volume and need backoff;\nrequests can hang for minutes unless a timeout is set; streaming responses arrive as server-sent events\nthat must be parsed incrementally; tool calls arrive as JSON strings inside nested structures whose\nshape changes when new features land; and every field you read from a dict is an unchecked guess about\nthe schema. Teams that hand-roll this tend to reinvent retries badly (retrying non-idempotent work, or\nretrying 400s forever) and discover schema changes as `KeyError`s in production.\n\nThe SDK encodes the API's contract in code generated from its OpenAPI specification, so request\nparameters and response objects are typed and autocompletable, errors are an exception hierarchy you\ncan catch precisely, and the transport behaviour (connection pooling, retry with backoff, timeouts,\nstreaming) is implemented once. The reason to learn it well, rather than hiding it behind a framework\nimmediately, is that most debugging of LLM features comes down to what this client sent and received:\nthe exact messages, the tool schema, the `usage` counts and the request ID you give to support.","how":"### Client and transport\n`OpenAI()` reads `OPENAI_API_KEY` (and optionally organisation and project) from the environment and\nbuilds an `httpx` client with connection pooling. `AsyncOpenAI` exposes the same methods as coroutines\nfor servers built on asyncio. Create one client per process and reuse it, because each client owns a\nconnection pool; constructing one per request defeats keep-alive. `base_url` points the same client at\nany compatible server; Azure has a dedicated `AzureOpenAI` client because its classic deployment-based\nURLs, `api-version` parameter and Entra ID auth differ (Azure's newer v1-style endpoints can also be\nreached with the plain client and a `base_url`; check which one your deployment exposes).\n\n### Two generation APIs\n- **Chat Completions** (`client.chat.completions.create`) takes a list of role-tagged `messages` and\n  returns `choices[0].message`. It is stateless: you resend the whole history each call. This is the\n  shape other providers copied.\n- **Responses** (`client.responses.create`) is OpenAI's newer API, taking `input` plus `instructions`,\n  returning a list of typed output items, with optional server-side conversation state and built-in\n  tools. The convenience property `response.output_text` concatenates text output.\nNew OpenAI-specific features tend to arrive in Responses first; portability favours Chat Completions.\n\nServer-side state (`previous_response_id`, or a conversation object) saves you from resending history\nover the wire, but it does not make history free: the model still reads the earlier turns, and they are\nbilled as input tokens on each call. It also moves the transcript into the provider's storage, which\nmatters for retention policies; `store=False` opts out, at the cost of managing history yourself.\n\n### Typed responses and structured output\nResponses are Pydantic models, so `resp.usage.prompt_tokens` is checked attribute access, not a dict\nlookup. For structured output, the `parse` helpers accept a Pydantic class, convert it to a strict JSON\nSchema, and return the validated object (`output_parsed` on Responses, `message.parsed` on Chat\nCompletions). Strict schema mode constrains decoding to the schema, which is stronger than asking for\nJSON in the prompt; you still handle refusals and truncated outputs.\n\n### Tools\nYou pass JSON Schema function definitions; the model may return tool calls whose `arguments` field is a\nJSON string. Your code parses and validates the arguments, runs the function, and sends the result back\nin a follow-up message tied to the call's ID. The SDK does not execute tools for you; that loop is your\nagent code.\n\n### Streaming\n`stream=True` returns an iterator of chunks (Chat Completions) or typed events (Responses) parsed from\nserver-sent events, so you can forward tokens to a user as they arrive. Usage totals arrive only at the\nend: Chat Completions includes them in a final chunk only if you pass\n`stream_options={\"include_usage\": True}`, while Responses carries them on the closing\n`response.completed` event, so log them from the final event.\n\n### Errors, retries, timeouts\nExceptions subclass `openai.APIError`: `APIConnectionError` and `APITimeoutError` for transport,\n`APIStatusError` subclasses such as `RateLimitError` (429), `BadRequestError` (400) and\n`AuthenticationError` (401). By default the client retries connection errors, 408, 409, 429 and 5xx\nresponses twice (`max_retries=2`) with exponential backoff and jitter, honouring a `retry-after` header\nwhen the server sends one, because those failures are plausibly transient; 4xx errors like 400 are not\nretried, because resending the same bad request cannot succeed. The default timeout is 600 seconds, far\ntoo long for an interactive path, so set your own per client or per call with `with_options`.\n\nTwo consequences follow. Worst-case wall-clock time is roughly `timeout × (max_retries + 1)` plus\nbackoff. And a generation request is not idempotent: if a timeout fires after the server started work,\nthe retry is a second, separately billed generation, so aggressive timeouts on long outputs can raise\ncost as well as latency.\n\n### Raw responses and headers\nTyped objects hide the HTTP layer, which is usually what you want, except for the headers.\n`client.with_raw_response.chat.completions.create(...)` returns the raw response, whose headers include\nthe request ID and rate-limit state (`x-ratelimit-remaining-requests`, `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens`),\nand `.parse()` on it gives you the usual typed object. Reading remaining-token headers lets a batch\nworker slow down before it hits 429s instead of after.","example":"A support classifier with structured output, a tight timeout and explicit error handling. Model names\nchange; read them from configuration.\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nimport openai\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nMODEL = os.environ.get(\"CLASSIFIER_MODEL\", \"gpt-4o-mini\")\nclient = OpenAI(timeout=20.0, max_retries=3)        # one client per process; worst case ~4 × 20 s + backoff\n\nclass Triage(BaseModel):\n    category: Literal[\"billing\", \"bug\", \"how_to\", \"other\"]\n    urgent: bool\n    summary: str\n\ndef triage(ticket: str) -> Triage | None:\n    try:\n        resp = client.responses.parse(\n            model=MODEL,\n            instructions=\"Classify the support ticket.\",\n            input=ticket,\n            text_format=Triage,                     # Pydantic class -> strict JSON Schema\n        )\n    except openai.RateLimitError:\n        raise                                       # already retried; let the queue back off\n    except openai.BadRequestError as e:\n        print(\"bad request, not retrying:\", e.status_code, e.message)\n        return None\n    print(\"request id:\", resp._request_id, \"tokens:\", resp.usage.input_tokens, resp.usage.output_tokens)\n    return resp.output_parsed                       # a validated Triage, or None on refusal\n\nprint(triage(\"I was charged twice this month and need it fixed today.\"))\n```\n\nWhat happens on the wire: `parse` converts `Triage` into a JSON Schema (an enum for `category`, a\nboolean, a string, every field required, no extra properties), sends it as a strict text format, and the\nserver constrains decoding so the reply can only be JSON matching that schema; the SDK then validates it\nback into a `Triage`. A 429 is retried up to three times before `RateLimitError` reaches the `except`, so\nre-raising there hands the failure to an outer queue rather than retrying a fourth time in-process.\n\nPointing the same code at a local vLLM server is `OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\",\napi_key=\"unused\")`, provided that server implements the endpoint you call.","tradeoffs":["The official SDK tracks new features immediately, but couples your code to one provider's shapes; a thin internal interface or a router like LiteLLM is worth it once you genuinely use several providers.","Built-in retries handle transient failures with no code, but they multiply latency and cost on overload; in queued batch jobs you may prefer `max_retries=0` and let the queue own backoff.","The Responses API offers server-side state and built-in tools, but other providers do not mirror it, so Chat Completions remains the better choice when portability matters more than new features.","Strict structured outputs guarantee schema conformance, but restrict the JSON Schema features you can use; complex unions or open-ended dicts may need a looser schema plus Pydantic validation."],"pitfalls":["Creating a new client per request: connection setup on every call raises latency, and under load you see socket exhaustion or many TLS handshakes in traces.","Leaving the default long timeout in a user-facing endpoint: one stuck upstream call ties up a worker for minutes, and the symptom is request pile-ups during provider incidents.","Calling the sync client inside an async web server: the event loop blocks, and every concurrent request stalls while one LLM call is in flight; use `AsyncOpenAI`.","Trusting tool-call `arguments` as valid: they are model-generated JSON strings, and the symptom is occasional `JSONDecodeError` or wrong-typed fields crashing the tool layer.","Not logging request IDs and usage: when a customer reports a bad answer or a billing spike, you cannot match it to a specific call or explain the cost."],"when":"Use the OpenAI SDK directly when OpenAI (or Azure OpenAI) is your primary provider and you want new\nfeatures as they ship; use it with `base_url` against OpenAI-compatible servers for local or self-hosted\nmodels. Wrap it behind your own small interface (one function per capability: classify, generate, embed)\nso tests can fake it and a second provider can be added without touching callers. Move to LiteLLM or a\ngateway when you need routing, fallbacks or central spend tracking across providers. Measure latency\npercentiles, retry counts, error rates by type and tokens per call. Interviewers probe retry semantics,\nstreaming, and how you validate structured output.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the client wrapper with timeouts, retries, structured output and streaming, and logs request IDs and usage for every call.","architect":"Decides whether product code calls the SDK directly or through an internal interface or gateway, and which API surface (Chat Completions or Responses) the platform standardises on.","fde":"Points the client at a customer's Azure deployment or private compatible endpoint, often behind proxies and custom certificates configured through the underlying HTTP client."},"check":{"q":"During a provider incident your API's p99 latency jumps to several minutes and workers are\nexhausted, even though most calls eventually fail. What is happening in the SDK and what do you change?","a":"Each failing call may wait for the client's long default timeout and then be retried several\ntimes with backoff, so one user request can hold a worker for the timeout multiplied by the attempts.\nI would set an explicit per-call timeout sized to the endpoint's latency budget, lower `max_retries` for\ninteractive paths (keeping more retries for background jobs), use the async client so waiting does not\nblock workers, and add a circuit breaker or fallback model so that after repeated failures requests fail\nfast or route elsewhere. I would also alert on `APITimeoutError` and `RateLimitError` rates separately,\nbecause they need different fixes."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the SDK retry 429 and 5xx responses by default but not 400?","options":["Because 400 errors are always caused by network problems","Because 429 and 5xx are plausibly transient, while resending an invalid request will fail the same way","Because the API forbids retrying 400 responses","Because 400 responses are retried by the server automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rate limits and server errors often clear after a short wait, so backoff retries help. A 400 means the request itself is wrong; retrying wastes time and quota without any chance of success."},{"q":"What does changing `base_url` on the OpenAI client accomplish?","options":["It enables the Responses API on older models","It switches the client to a different OpenAI region for lower latency","It lets the same client call any server implementing the compatible endpoints, such as a local vLLM instance","It disables authentication for local testing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Many servers implement OpenAI's request and response shapes. Pointing `base_url` at them reuses the client, though only the endpoints and features that server actually implements will work."},{"q":"A model returns a tool call. What is the SDK's responsibility versus yours?","options":["The SDK executes the function and returns the final answer automatically","The SDK returns the call with its JSON-string arguments; your code validates, executes and returns the result","The SDK validates the arguments against your Python function signature","The SDK sends the tool result back to the model on the next request automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The client only transports the model's request to call a tool. Parsing arguments, running the tool and sending results back with the call ID is the agent loop you write and must guard."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","sources":["Official docs: Anthropic API (Messages API)","Official docs: anthropic-sdk-python"],"why":"Calling Claude means solving the same transport problems as any LLM API (authentication, retries on\n429 and 5xx, timeouts, server-sent-event streaming), plus problems specific to its design. A single\nresponse can mix several kinds of content: reasoning blocks, text, and one or more tool calls. Agent\nloops must echo assistant content back faithfully, including blocks your code does not display,\nbecause the API expects the conversation history to contain them. Prompt caching only pays off if the\ncached prefix is byte-identical across requests and the breakpoints sit in the right places.\n\nHand-rolled HTTP clients tend to treat the response as one string, which silently drops tool calls,\nloses reasoning blocks needed for the next turn, or concatenates partial stream deltas incorrectly.\nGeneric multi-provider wrappers solve portability but often lag behind provider-specific features such\nas cache breakpoints, citations, batches or extended reasoning controls, or flatten them away.\n\nThe SDK exists to make the API's real shape the path of least resistance: typed request parameters,\ntyped content-block classes you branch on with `.type`, streaming helpers that accumulate the final\nmessage for you, an exception hierarchy that separates retryable from fatal failures, and helpers for\ntoken counting, batches and the tool-use loop. It also provides client classes for Claude on Amazon\nBedrock, Google Vertex AI and Microsoft Foundry, so the same calling code works across those platforms\n(feature availability still differs per platform).","how":"### Client\n`anthropic.Anthropic()` resolves credentials from the environment (typically `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`);\n`AsyncAnthropic` is the asyncio twin. Like any pooled HTTP client, create it once per process. Timeouts\n(default ten minutes) and `max_retries` (default 2, covering connection errors, 408, 409, 429 and 5xx\nwith exponential backoff) are set on the client or per call via `client.with_options(...)`. Note that\ntimeouts are retried too, so worst-case wall-clock time is the timeout times the number of attempts.\n\n### The Messages call\n`client.messages.create(model=..., max_tokens=..., system=..., messages=[...])`. Design choices:\n- **`system` is top-level**, not a message, because it is operator configuration rather than a turn,\n  and keeping it separate makes it a stable, cacheable prefix.\n- **`max_tokens` is required** so every call has an explicit output ceiling; hitting it ends the\n  response with `stop_reason == \"max_tokens\"`, which your code must treat as truncation.\n- **`messages` is a sequence of user and assistant turns** (consecutive turns with the same role are\n  merged), and each message's `content` may be a string or a list of blocks (text, image, document,\n  tool_result).\n\n### Content blocks and stop reasons\n`response.content` is a list. Branch on `block.type`: `\"text\"`, `\"thinking\"` (reasoning; opt-in on older models, on by default on\nrecent ones, and its visible text may be summarised or empty depending on the display setting),\n`\"tool_use\"` (with `id`, `name`, `input` already parsed as a dict). `stop_reason` tells you what to do\nnext: `end_turn` (done), `tool_use` (run tools and continue), `max_tokens` (truncated), `stop_sequence`,\n`pause_turn` (a long server-side tool turn to resume) and `refusal` (declined; check it before reading\ncontent).\n\nThinking blocks carry a cryptographic `signature`. Echo them back unchanged on the next turn with the\nsame model, because the server verifies them and uses them to continue the reasoning across tool\ncalls. The practical rule this produces: treat conversation history as append-only. Rewriting or\ntrimming earlier assistant turns can invalidate the thinking attached to them, and on recent models\nthe exact enforcement varies by model and account, so check the current docs before building a harness\nthat edits history.\n\n### Tool loop\nYou declare tools as `name`, `description`, `input_schema` (JSON Schema). When `stop_reason` is\n`tool_use`, append the assistant's full `response.content` as an assistant message, run each requested\ntool, and send one user message containing a `tool_result` block per call, each with the matching\n`tool_use_id` (set `is_error: True` for failures rather than dropping them). All results for one turn go\nin a single message; splitting them across messages breaks the parallel-call pattern. The SDK's beta\ntool runner can drive this loop for decorated Python functions.\n\n`tool_choice` controls whether the model may or must call tools (`auto`, `none`, and on some models\nforced `any` or a named tool). Forcing is not supported on every current model, so the portable way to\nguarantee a well-formed call is `auto` plus a clear instruction, with `strict: true` on the tool\ndefinition so `input` always validates against `input_schema`.\n\n### Structured output\nWhen you want JSON rather than a tool call, `output_config={\"format\": ...}` with a JSON Schema constrains\nthe response to that schema, and `client.messages.parse(...)` accepts a Pydantic model and returns the\nvalidated object. This replaces the older trick of defining a fake tool just to get structured\narguments back. You still check `stop_reason`: a refusal or a `max_tokens` cut-off means there is no\ncomplete object to parse.\n\n### Prompt caching\nMark a block with `cache_control={\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}` (or use top-level automatic caching). The API\ncaches the prefix up to that breakpoint, in render order tools, then system, then messages. Later\nrequests with a byte-identical prefix read it at a large discount and lower latency. `usage` reports\n`cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens`, which is how you verify it works. Any change\nearlier in the prefix (a timestamp in the system prompt, reordered tools) invalidates everything after.\n\n### Streaming, counting, batches\n`with client.messages.stream(...) as s:` yields events and `s.text_stream`, and `s.get_final_message()`\nreturns the assembled message, so you never hand-merge deltas. Streaming is recommended for long\noutputs and large `max_tokens`, where a non-streaming request can hit HTTP timeouts; the Python SDK\nrefuses a non-streaming request whose `max_tokens` implies an expected duration beyond its timeout and\ntells you to stream instead.\n`client.messages.count_tokens(...)` returns the exact input token count for a request, the right tool\nfor Claude because local OpenAI tokenizers do not match. `client.messages.batches` submits many\nrequests for asynchronous processing at a discount, with results keyed by your `custom_id`.","example":"A minimal tool-use loop with caching of the stable system prompt. Model IDs change; take them from\nconfiguration.\n\n```python\nimport os, anthropic\n\nMODEL = os.environ.get(\"CLAUDE_MODEL\", \"claude-opus-5-5\")\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic(max_retries=3)\nSYSTEM = [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"You are an order-support agent. Use tools for facts.\",\n           \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}}]  # caches only above the model's minimum prefix length\nTOOLS = [{\"name\": \"get_order\", \"description\": \"Look up an order by id.\",\n          \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                           \"required\": [\"order_id\"]}}]\n\ndef get_order(order_id: str) -> str:\n    return f'{{\"order_id\": \"{order_id}\", \"status\": \"shipped\", \"eta\": \"2 days\"}}'\n\nmessages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Where is order A-1042?\"}]\nwhile True:\n    resp = client.messages.create(model=MODEL, max_tokens=8192, system=SYSTEM,   # thinking counts too\n                                  tools=TOOLS, messages=messages)\n    print(resp._request_id, resp.usage.input_tokens, resp.usage.cache_read_input_tokens)\n    if resp.stop_reason != \"tool_use\":\n        break\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": resp.content})   # echo every block back\n    results = []\n    for block in resp.content:\n        if block.type == \"tool_use\":\n            out = get_order(**block.input) if block.name == \"get_order\" else \"unknown tool\"\n            results.append({\"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": block.id, \"content\": out})\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": results})               # all results, one message\n\nif resp.stop_reason == \"refusal\" or resp.stop_reason == \"max_tokens\":\n    print(\"did not finish normally:\", resp.stop_reason)\nprint(\"\".join(b.text for b in resp.content if b.type == \"text\"))\n```\n\nTrace of a typical run: call 1 returns `stop_reason=\"tool_use\"` with content such as\n`[thinking, tool_use(id=\"toolu_…\", name=\"get_order\", input={\"order_id\": \"A-1042\"})]`. History becomes\nthree messages: the user question, the assistant turn with every block intact, and a user turn holding\none `tool_result` whose `tool_use_id` matches. Call 2 resends all three, the model reads the result and\nreturns `stop_reason=\"end_turn\"` with a text block. Each call re-reads the whole history, so input tokens\ngrow every iteration, which is exactly the cost that caching the stable prefix attacks.\n\nThis toy system prompt is far shorter than the minimum cacheable prefix (model-dependent, from a few\nhundred to a few thousand tokens), so here `cache_read_input_tokens` stays at zero; with a realistic\nmulti-thousand-token system prompt and tool list it rises from the second call. A production loop also\ncaps iterations, validates `block.input` before running a tool, and catches\n`anthropic.RateLimitError`, `anthropic.APIStatusError` and `anthropic.APIConnectionError` separately.","tradeoffs":["The native SDK exposes Claude-specific features fully (cache breakpoints, content blocks, batches, reasoning controls), but code written against it is not portable; a router helps once several providers are genuinely in production.","Explicit cache breakpoints give precise control over what is cached and what it costs, but require disciplined prompt layout; automatic caching is simpler when you do not need per-block placement.","Batches cut cost substantially for offline work, but results arrive asynchronously and in any order, so they suit evals and backfills, not interactive requests.","Built-in retries absorb transient failures cheaply, but on long timeouts they multiply worst-case latency; interactive paths should set short timeouts and few retries."],"pitfalls":["Reading `response.content[0].text` blindly: when the first block is thinking or a tool call, you get an attribute error or an empty answer, and tool calls are silently ignored.","Appending only the text of an assistant turn instead of `response.content`: the next request fails validation because `tool_result` blocks reference `tool_use` ids that are no longer in history.","Putting a timestamp or request ID at the top of the system prompt: `cache_read_input_tokens` stays at zero on every call and costs never drop.","Setting a small `max_tokens` and not checking `stop_reason`: answers or JSON are cut off mid-way, and the symptom is parse errors that look like model mistakes.","Using tiktoken to budget Claude prompts: counts differ, and requests near the context limit fail; use `count_tokens` instead."],"when":"Use the Anthropic SDK whenever Claude is a primary model in your product and you want its features at\nfull fidelity: tool-using agents, long cached system prompts or documents, citations, batch evaluation,\nor reasoning controls. Wrap it behind your own narrow interface so tests can fake it. Use the Bedrock or\nVertex client classes when the customer's contract or data residency requires those platforms, keeping\nthe same calling code. Choose LiteLLM or a gateway only when multi-provider routing is a real\nrequirement, and check that the features you depend on survive the translation. Measure cache read\nratio, stop-reason distribution, tool-loop iterations per task and error rates by class. In interviews,\nexpect to walk through the tool loop and explain why a cache hit rate is zero.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the tool loop with correct block handling, streams long outputs, lays out prompts so caching hits, and logs request IDs and usage for every call.","architect":"Decides provider abstraction boundaries, which platform (first-party, Bedrock, Vertex) each workload uses, and where batches replace synchronous calls to cut cost.","fde":"Integrates Claude through the customer's cloud account on Bedrock or Vertex, and debugs auth, region and quota differences while keeping application code unchanged."},"check":{"q":"A team's Claude agent has a 6,000-token system prompt and tool list, and they enabled prompt\ncaching, yet `cache_read_input_tokens` is always zero. Walk through how you would diagnose it.","a":"Caching is a prefix match over tools, then system, then messages, so anything that varies early in\nthat order breaks every hit. I would diff two consecutive request bodies byte for byte: typical culprits\nare a timestamp or user name interpolated into the system prompt, tools built from an unordered set so\ntheir order changes, or JSON serialised without sorted keys. I would check the breakpoint is placed after\nthe stable content and that the prefix exceeds the model's minimum cacheable length. I would confirm\nrequests arrive within the cache lifetime and use the same model, since caches are model-scoped. Then I\nwould move volatile data after the last breakpoint and watch the read counter rise."},"quiz":[{"q":"After a response with `stop_reason == \"tool_use\"`, what must the next request contain?","options":["Only the tool result text appended to the system prompt","A new user message summarising the tool call","The assistant's full content blocks, then a user message with a `tool_result` block per `tool_use` id","A request with `tool_choice` reset so the model cannot call tools again"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The API validates that each `tool_result` matches a `tool_use` in the preceding assistant turn. Echoing the full content and returning every result in one user message keeps history consistent."},{"q":"Why might a correctly marked cache breakpoint still produce no cache reads?","options":["Because caching only works for streaming requests","Because something earlier in the prefix changes between requests, such as a timestamp in the system prompt","Because cache reads are reported only in the billing dashboard","Because caching requires the Batches API"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Caching matches the exact prefix up to the breakpoint. Any byte that varies before it, in tools or system, creates a new prefix, so every request writes a cache entry and none read one."},{"q":"Why is `max_tokens` a required parameter, and what should code do with it?","options":["It sets the input context limit; code should truncate prompts to fit it","It controls billing tier; higher values cost more per token","It caps output length explicitly; code must check for `stop_reason == \"max_tokens\"` as truncation","It limits the number of tool calls per turn"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Requiring an output ceiling makes cost and latency bounds explicit. When the model hits it, the response is incomplete, so treating that stop reason as success produces cut-off answers and broken JSON."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/litellm","sources":["Official docs: LiteLLM"],"why":"Teams rarely stay on one model. They want a cheap model for classification and a strong one for\nreasoning, a second provider for when the first has an outage or rate-limits them, a self-hosted model\nfor sensitive data, and the ability to evaluate a new model without rewriting call sites. Each provider\nhas its own SDK, message format, tool-call shape, error classes, streaming events and usage fields. If\nproduct code calls them directly, every new provider touches every call site, fallbacks are implemented\nad hoc in each service, and nobody can answer \"what did we spend on model X for feature Y last month\"\nbecause usage is logged in five shapes.\n\nThe naive fix is an internal wrapper. That works for two providers and one capability, but it grows\ninto a maintained translation layer for tool schemas, streaming, error mapping and pricing tables,\nwhich is precisely the boring, fast-changing work a shared library amortises. LiteLLM centralises that\ntranslation, and its proxy mode moves it out of application code entirely: services talk to one\nOpenAI-compatible endpoint, and the platform team controls which models exist, who may use them, and\nhow much they may spend. The trade is a dependency that sits on every LLM call, so its correctness,\nlatency and update cadence become yours.","how":"### Unified call\n`litellm.completion(model=\"<provider>/<model>\", messages=[...])` (and `acompletion` for asyncio) parses\nthe provider prefix (`openai/`, `anthropic/`, `bedrock/`, `vertex_ai/`, `ollama/`, `hosted_vllm/` and\nmany more), translates the OpenAI-format request into that provider's native request, calls it, and\ntranslates the response back into an OpenAI-format object: `choices[0].message`, `tool_calls`, and\n`usage`. Credentials come from provider-specific environment variables. Parameters a provider does not\nsupport raise an error by default; `drop_params=True` silently drops them instead, which is convenient\nand dangerous in equal measure.\n\n### What translation actually does\nTake one OpenAI-format request sent to `anthropic/...`. The `system` message is pulled out of `messages`\ninto Anthropic's top-level `system` field; a `max_tokens` value is supplied if you omitted one, because\nAnthropic requires it; each OpenAI `tools[].function` becomes a `name`/`description`/`input_schema`\ntool; an assistant message's `tool_calls` become `tool_use` blocks, and each `role: \"tool\"` message\nbecomes a `tool_result` block inside a user message. On the way back, `tool_use` blocks become\n`tool_calls` with `arguments` re-serialised as a JSON string, `stop_reason` maps to `finish_reason`\n(`end_turn` to `stop`, `tool_use` to `tool_calls`, `max_tokens` to `length`), and `input_tokens` /\n`output_tokens` become `prompt_tokens` / `completion_tokens`. Every one of these mappings is a place where\na provider feature with no OpenAI equivalent (thinking blocks, cache breakpoints, citations) has to be\ncarried in an extra field or is lost, which is why translation fidelity is the thing to test.\n\n### Errors\nProvider errors are mapped onto OpenAI-style exception types (rate limit, context window exceeded,\nauthentication, bad request), so retry and fallback logic can be written once against one hierarchy.\n\n### Router\n`litellm.Router` takes a `model_list` where several deployments can share one logical `model_name`\n(for example two Azure regions plus OpenAI direct). It load-balances across them with a configurable\n`routing_strategy` (random `simple-shuffle` by default, or options that prefer the least busy, lowest\nlatency or least used deployment), tracks failures and puts a deployment in cooldown once it exceeds\n`allowed_fails` (for `cooldown_time` seconds), retries with `num_retries`, and applies `fallbacks` that\nmap one logical model to others, plus specialised `context_window_fallbacks` (overflow to a\nlonger-context model) and `content_policy_fallbacks`. Callers ask for the logical name; topology lives\nin config. The order matters: retries happen within the group first, and fallback to another group only\nwhen the group as a whole has failed, because a different model is a bigger behavioural change than a\ndifferent deployment of the same one.\n\n### Cost and callbacks\nLiteLLM maintains a model price map and computes per-call cost from usage (`completion_cost`), as\nprompt tokens × input price + completion tokens × output price, looked up by model name. A custom\ndeployment name (an Azure deployment called `my-gpt4o-mini-eu`) is not in that map, so you declare its\n`base_model` in the deployment's `model_info` for the lookup to work.\nCallbacks send each call's inputs, outputs, latency and cost to logging and observability backends.\n\n### Proxy (gateway) mode\nThe proxy runs the same machinery as a server exposing OpenAI-compatible endpoints, configured by a\nYAML file of models, routing and fallbacks. It adds virtual API keys mapped to teams or users, budgets\nand rate limits per key, spend logs in a database, and central guardrail hooks. Applications then use any\nOpenAI-compatible client, in any language, pointed at the proxy's `base_url`. The core is open source,\nbut some proxy features aimed at enterprises (for example SSO and certain audit and admin controls) sit\nbehind a commercial licence, so check which ones you need before standardising on it.","example":"A router with a logical \"chat\" model, two deployments, retries and a cross-provider fallback.\nModel names are illustrative; take them from config.\n\n```python\nimport litellm\nfrom litellm import Router\n\nrouter = Router(\n    model_list=[\n        {\"model_name\": \"chat\", \"litellm_params\": {\"model\": \"openai/gpt-4o-mini\"}},\n        {\"model_name\": \"chat\", \"litellm_params\": {\"model\": \"azure/my-gpt4o-mini-eu\",\n                                                  \"api_base\": \"https://example-eu.openai.azure.com\"}},\n        {\"model_name\": \"chat-backup\", \"litellm_params\": {\"model\": \"anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5\"}},\n    ],\n    num_retries=2,                                # retries within the group; may pick another deployment\n    fallbacks=[{\"chat\": [\"chat-backup\"]}],        # if all \"chat\" deployments fail\n    timeout=20,\n)\n\nresp = router.completion(\n    model=\"chat\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise: the refund was issued on 3 May.\"}],\n    metadata={\"feature\": \"ticket-summary\", \"tenant\": \"acme\"},   # for cost attribution in callbacks\n)\nprint(resp.model, resp.choices[0].message.content)\nprint(resp.usage.prompt_tokens, resp.usage.completion_tokens, litellm.completion_cost(resp))\n```\n\nScenario: OpenAI returns 429s for a minute. By default the router spreads \"chat\" traffic across both\ndeployments (simple shuffle); the failing calls are retried, the OpenAI deployment is put in cooldown\nafter repeated failures, so traffic lands on the EU Azure deployment, and only if the \"chat\" group as a\nwhole fails does the request go to the Anthropic backup, with the caller still receiving an\nOpenAI-shaped response.","tradeoffs":["One interface across providers makes switching and A/B testing cheap, but the lowest common denominator hides features; Claude cache breakpoints or Responses-API tools may need provider-specific params.","The in-process library adds no network hop, but every service needs credentials and config; the proxy centralises keys, budgets and logs at the price of an extra hop and a component you must run highly available.","Automatic fallbacks raise availability, but a fallback model may behave differently on your prompts; without evals per fallback pair, an outage silently becomes a quality regression.","Built-in price maps make cost visible immediately, but they can lag price changes and negotiated rates; reconcile against provider invoices before using them for chargeback."],"pitfalls":["Enabling `drop_params=True` globally: a parameter you rely on (structured output, a stop sequence) is quietly discarded for one provider, and outputs change format only when a fallback fires.","Assuming tool calling and streaming behave identically everywhere: translation edge cases show up as malformed tool arguments or missing usage in streamed responses from one provider.","Fallback chains without evals: during an incident, users see a different model's style or lower accuracy, and support tickets spike with no error in the logs.","Unpinned versions of a fast-moving dependency on every LLM call: an upgrade changes defaults or pricing data, and the symptom is a behaviour or cost change nobody deployed deliberately.","Stacking retry layers: application retries × router `num_retries` × any retries the underlying client performs × each fallback group; the symptom is a single user request turning into a dozen upstream calls during an incident, amplifying the provider's rate limiting. Own retries in exactly one layer.","Cost reports showing zero or raising for some deployments: the custom deployment name is not in the price map; declare `base_model` so spend attribution is not silently undercounted.","Running the proxy as a single instance: it becomes the single point of failure for every AI feature, and one restart takes them all down."],"when":"Reach for LiteLLM when you genuinely use, or must be able to switch between, several providers: routing\nby cost or capability, cross-provider fallbacks, evaluation harnesses comparing many models, or a\nplatform team that needs central keys, budgets and spend attribution across many services (proxy mode).\nStay with a native SDK when one provider is strategic and you depend on its newest features, or when the\ncall volume is small and an extra abstraction is not worth owning. Measure fallback rate, per-model\nlatency and error rates, cost per feature and tenant, and quality on an eval set for each fallback pair.\nInterviewers often ask how you would build provider failover and where the abstraction should stop.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Calls models through one interface, writes retry and fallback config, attaches metadata for cost attribution, and handles provider-specific parameters where translation falls short.","architect":"Decides library versus gateway, which providers and fallback pairs are allowed, how budgets and keys are governed, and keeps a vendor-exit path that does not depend on one SDK.","fde":"Deploys the proxy inside a customer's network to route between their approved cloud models and a local model, giving their security team one place to audit and cap usage."},"check":{"q":"Your product runs on one provider. Leadership asks for \"no single point of failure for AI\". A\ncolleague proposes adding LiteLLM with a fallback to a second provider. What else must be true for this\nto actually deliver resilience?","a":"The fallback must be tested, not just configured. Prompts, tool schemas and output parsing need\nto work on the second model, so I would run the eval suite against it and fix prompt or schema\ndifferences first. Features that do not translate, such as provider-specific caching or structured\noutput modes, need equivalents or graceful degradation. Rate limits and quotas on the backup must cover\nfailover traffic. The routing layer itself must not become the new single point of failure, so the\nproxy runs with multiple replicas or the library is embedded. Finally, alert on fallback rate, so a\nsilent permanent failover with worse quality is noticed."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does LiteLLM's response translation give application code?","options":["A guarantee that every provider produces identical outputs for the same prompt","One OpenAI-format response shape, including usage and tool calls, regardless of provider","Automatic prompt rewriting optimised for each model","Access to every provider-specific feature through standard OpenAI parameters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Translation normalises the shape of requests and responses, so parsing and logging are written once. It cannot make models behave the same, and some provider-specific features have no standard equivalent."},{"q":"When is LiteLLM's proxy mode a better choice than the in-process library?","options":["When a single Python service calls one provider","When the lowest possible latency is the only goal","When many services and languages need shared keys, budgets, rate limits and spend logs","When you need offline access to models without any server"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The proxy centralises governance behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint any client can use. For one service and one provider, the extra hop and operational burden rarely pay off."},{"q":"Why is `drop_params=True` risky as a global default?","options":["It doubles the token cost of each request","It disables retries on rate-limit errors","Unsupported parameters are silently removed, so behaviour can change only when a different provider serves the call","It prevents streaming responses from working"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Silently dropping a parameter such as a response format or stop sequence turns a loud error into a quiet behaviour change, often visible only during a fallback when debugging is hardest."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/instructor","sources":["Official docs: Instructor","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"Most LLM features in real systems are extraction or classification: pull an invoice's line items,\nlabel a ticket's intent, turn an email into a calendar event. The downstream code wants a typed object,\nnot prose. The naive approach, a prompt that says \"respond in JSON with fields a, b, c\" followed by\n`json.loads`, fails in several distinct ways: the model wraps the JSON in a Markdown fence or adds a\npreamble; a field comes back as `\"twelve\"` instead of `12`; an enum value is invented; a required field\nis missing; a date is in the wrong format; or the JSON is truncated because the output hit the token\nlimit. Each failure needs its own handling, and teams end up writing the same bundle by hand: build a\nschema from the type, put it in the request, strip fences, parse, validate, and if it fails, tell the\nmodel what was wrong and try again.\n\nProvider-native structured output (JSON mode, strict schemas, forced tool calls) closes part of this gap,\nbecause it can guarantee syntactically valid JSON that matches the schema's shape. It cannot enforce\n*business* rules: that `end_date >= start_date`, that a cited quote actually appears in the source, that\nline items sum to the total. Those live in Pydantic validators, and only a validate-then-retry loop can\nuse them to correct the model. Instructor is that loop, packaged once, across many providers.","how":"### Patching the client\nYou wrap an existing SDK client, for example `instructor.from_openai(OpenAI())` or\n`instructor.from_anthropic(Anthropic())` (recent versions also offer a `from_provider(\"openai/...\")`\nshortcut). The wrapped client keeps the provider's normal call signature and adds a few keyword\narguments, chiefly `response_model` and `max_retries`. It wraps rather than replaces the SDK *because*\nthe SDK already handles auth, HTTP, streaming and provider quirks; Instructor only owns the schema and\nvalidation layer.\n\n### Schema in, object out\n1. **Schema generation.** Pydantic's `model_json_schema()` turns your `BaseModel` into JSON Schema,\n   including field descriptions, enums from `Literal` or `Enum`, and nested models. The class docstring\n   and each `Field(description=...)` land in the schema, so they become prompt text the model actually\n   reads, which is why good descriptions matter (measure it on your own eval set).\n2. **Mode selection.** The `mode` decides how the schema reaches the model. `Mode.TOOLS` (the usual\n   default for OpenAI) sends it as a single tool definition and forces that tool to be called; there are\n   provider-specific tool modes for Anthropic and others; `Mode.JSON` uses the provider's JSON mode; and\n   `Mode.MD_JSON` asks for JSON in a Markdown block for models with no native support. Tool calling is\n   preferred where available *because* providers train models to emit well-formed arguments for tools.\n3. **Parse and validate.** The reply's arguments are parsed into your model with\n   `model_validate_json`, which runs type coercion and every `field_validator` and `model_validator`.\n4. **Reask.** If validation raises, Instructor appends the model's previous output and the\n   `ValidationError` text to the conversation as a new message and calls again. The error message is\n   the correction signal: a validator that raises `ValueError(\"end_date is before start_date\")` tells\n   the model precisely what to fix. Retries are driven by Tenacity, so `max_retries` accepts an integer\n   or a full Tenacity retry policy. An integer is turned into a stop-after-N-*attempts* rule, so it counts\n   total calls including the first, not extra reasks on top of it (check this against the version you\n   pin). After the last attempt it raises an `InstructorRetryException` that carries the number of\n   attempts and the last completion.\n\n### What a reask actually sends\nIn tool mode the retry request is the original messages, plus an assistant message containing the\nfailed tool call, plus a message carrying the `ValidationError` text and an instruction to fix it. Every\nretry therefore re-sends the whole prompt *and* grows it, so retry *k* costs more input tokens than the\nfirst call. This matters for long documents: reasking over a 20k-token contract repeats the 20k tokens\neach time.\n\nThe expected cost follows from the first-pass failure rate. If each attempt failed independently with\nprobability *p* and you allow *N* attempts, expected calls are 1 + p + p² + … + p^(N−1) and the residual\nfailure rate is p^N; at p = 0.1 and N = 3 that is about 1.11 calls and 0.1% residual failures. Real\nfailures are *not* independent: an input that defeats the model once tends to defeat it again with the\nsame error, so the residual rate is far higher than p^N and most of the retry spend lands on a small set\nof hard inputs. Measure retry success rate per attempt number; if the second attempt rarely fixes what\nthe first broke, lower `max_retries` and route failures to review or a stronger model instead.\n\n### Giving validators something to check against\nValidators can only enforce rules about the data they can see. To check that an extracted quote really\nappears in the source, pass the source at call time as a context dict (`context=` in recent versions,\n`validation_context=` in older ones); it reaches validators through Pydantic's `ValidationInfo.context`.\nThis turns \"the quote must be verbatim\" from a prompt instruction into an enforced rule that triggers a\nreask. Equally, make fields the source may not contain `Optional`: a required field forces the model to\nproduce *something*, and that something is a plausible invention.\n\n### Beyond a single object\n`create_partial` streams a model whose fields fill in as tokens arrive, useful for UIs that render a\nform progressively. `create_iterable` extracts a stream of objects (every person mentioned in a\ndocument). `create_with_completion` returns both the parsed object and the raw provider response, which\nyou need for token usage and logging.\n\n### What it is not\nInstructor does not constrain decoding. The model can still emit anything; Instructor detects bad output\nafter the fact and pays for another call. Libraries such as Outlines mask tokens during generation so\ninvalid output is impossible, but that requires access to the logits, which hosted APIs do not expose.","example":"Extracting a meeting from an email, with a business rule the schema alone cannot express.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\nfrom typing import Literal\nimport instructor\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, model_validator\n\nclass Meeting(BaseModel):\n    title: str = Field(description=\"Short title, max 8 words\")\n    start: date\n    end: date\n    kind: Literal[\"internal\", \"customer\", \"interview\"]\n    attendees: list[str] = Field(description=\"Email addresses only\")\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def end_not_before_start(self):\n        if self.end < self.start:\n            raise ValueError(f\"end {self.end} is before start {self.start}; re-read the dates\")\n        return self\n\nclient = instructor.from_openai(OpenAI())\n\nmeeting, raw = client.chat.completions.create_with_completion(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",                 # any tool-calling model\n    response_model=Meeting,\n    max_retries=3,                       # at most 3 calls: the first try plus up to 2 reasks\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\":\n        \"Offsite with Acme from 3 to 5 March 2025. Invite ana@acme.com and raj@us.io.\"}],\n)\nprint(meeting.model_dump())\nprint(raw.usage.total_tokens)            # recent versions sum usage across all attempts here\n```\n\nIf the first reply sets `end` before `start`, the validator's message goes back to the model and the\nsecond call usually fixes it; if all three attempts fail, the exception tells you which validation kept\nfailing. Recent Instructor versions accumulate token usage from every attempt into the returned\ncompletion's `usage` for the major providers; confirm that for your version and provider before you\nbuild cost reporting on it.","tradeoffs":["One `response_model` argument replaces schema, parsing and retry glue, but you inherit a dependency whose mode defaults and provider adapters change between releases; pin it and test upgrades.","Validate-and-reask enforces arbitrary Python rules, at the cost of extra full LLM calls; when failures are mostly syntactic, native strict structured output removes them for free and the reask loop is idle.","Running on hosted APIs means no logit access is needed, but also no guarantee; if you self-host and need validity on the first pass every time, constrained decoding (Outlines, vLLM guided decoding) wins.","Rich Pydantic schemas improve extraction quality, but deep nesting and long descriptions add input tokens on every call; for high-volume classification, a flat model with a `Literal` field is cheaper."],"pitfalls":["Leaving `max_retries` high with no budget: one pathological input triggers several full-price calls; the symptom is a cost spike traced to a handful of requests with repeated identical errors.","Validators that raise vague messages like \"invalid\"; the model cannot act on them, and the symptom is retries that return the same wrong value each time.","Building cost reporting on per-call logs or a provider adapter that does not accumulate usage across retries; failed attempts then vanish from your numbers, and the symptom is cost dashboards that under-report against the provider invoice. Verify with a forced validation failure, or log each attempt through Instructor's hooks.","Placing the answer field before a reasoning field in the model; field order is generation order, so the answer is committed before the reasoning exists and accuracy drops on harder cases.","Making every field required when the source often omits some; the model must fill them, and the symptom is plausible but absent values (a made-up phone number, a guessed date) that pass validation.","Treating a validated object as true; validation proves the shape and your rules, not that the extracted facts are in the source, which shows up as confident, well-typed hallucinations in evals."],"when":"Reach for Instructor when you call hosted LLMs and need typed objects with rules beyond what a JSON\nschema can say, especially across more than one provider. If your only need is valid JSON of a fixed\nshape from one provider, the SDK's native structured output (for example OpenAI's `parse` helper) may\nbe enough without another dependency. If you self-host and need hard guarantees or very low latency,\nprefer constrained decoding. Measure first-pass validation rate, retries per request and cost per\nsuccessful extraction, plus the fix rate of each retry attempt; a falling first-pass rate is an early signal of prompt or model drift. In\ninterviews, expect to explain why validation-plus-reask and constrained decoding solve different halves\nof the structured-output problem.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the Pydantic models, validators with actionable error messages, and retry limits, and logs every attempt with its token usage.","architect":"Decides where structure is enforced: provider strict mode, reask loops or constrained decoding, and sets the retry and cost budget per feature.","fde":"Turns a customer's messy documents into a typed schema quickly, using validators to encode their business rules so extraction errors surface as explicit failures."},"check":{"q":"Your extraction pipeline uses provider strict JSON mode and still produces wrong records. A\ncolleague says adding Instructor's retries is pointless because the JSON is already valid. Are they right?","a":"Partly. Strict mode guarantees syntax and schema shape, so Instructor's reask will never fire on\nthose failures. But the wrong records are semantic: dates swapped, totals not matching line items,\nvalues not present in the source. Those can be expressed as Pydantic validators, and Instructor feeds\neach validator's message back so the model can correct itself. The trade is extra calls per failure, so\nyou would cap retries, measure first-pass and post-retry accuracy on a labelled set, and keep a\nhuman-review path for records that still fail validation."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does Instructor do when the model's output fails Pydantic validation?","options":["It masks invalid tokens and resamples only the failing field","It sends the validation error back to the model in a new message and calls again, up to max_retries","It silently fills missing fields with their Pydantic defaults and returns the object","It switches automatically to a larger model for the next attempt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Instructor works on hosted APIs without logit access, so it cannot mask tokens. It reasks: the ValidationError text becomes a corrective message, and each retry is a full new LLM call."},{"q":"Why is the tool-calling mode usually preferred over plain JSON-in-prompt mode?","options":["Tool calls are billed at a lower token rate than normal completions","Tool calls let Instructor skip Pydantic validation entirely","Providers train models to emit well-formed arguments for declared tools, so first-pass validity is higher","JSON-in-prompt mode cannot represent nested models"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tool modes send the schema as a tool definition the provider is trained to honour, which reduces malformed output. Validation still runs afterwards; nothing about billing or nesting differs."},{"q":"Which requirement can Instructor enforce but provider strict schema mode alone cannot?","options":["That a field is an integer rather than a string","That an enum field only takes listed values","That the sum of extracted line items equals the stated invoice total","That every required field is present"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Types, enums and required fields are expressible in JSON Schema and strict mode enforces them. A cross-field arithmetic rule needs code, which lives in a Pydantic validator that drives the reask."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/outlines","sources":["Willard & Louf, 2023 — Efficient Guided Generation for Large Language Models","Official docs: Outlines"],"why":"A language model samples one token at a time from a distribution over its whole vocabulary. Nothing in\nthat process knows that you wanted a JSON object with an integer `age`. Prompting raises the\nprobability of valid output; it never makes it certain, and smaller open models, the ones you can afford\nto self-host, break formats far more often than frontier APIs. The after-the-fact fix, parse and retry,\nhas two costs. Each retry is another full generation, so tail latency multiplies. And retries do not\nconverge on hard inputs: a model that keeps writing `\"age\": \"about 40\"` will keep doing it.\n\nThe observation behind Outlines is that if you control sampling, you can refuse to sample invalid\ntokens at all. At each step, compute which vocabulary tokens could continue a valid string, set every\nother token's logit to negative infinity, and sample from what remains. The output is valid on the first\npass, always. The naive version of this, checking every vocabulary token against the pattern at every\nstep, costs a full scan of a vocabulary of tens or hundreds of thousands of tokens per generated token,\nwhich is too slow. Outlines' contribution was making the check effectively a lookup.","how":"### From pattern to automaton\n1. **Compile the constraint to a regular expression.** A regex is used directly. A JSON Schema is\n   translated into a regex that matches exactly the JSON strings that satisfy it (keys in schema order,\n   types as sub-patterns, enums as alternations). A `choice([\"yes\",\"no\"])` is an alternation.\n2. **Regex to finite-state machine.** The regex becomes a deterministic finite automaton over\n   *characters*: states, and transitions labelled by characters.\n3. **Index the vocabulary against the FSM.** This is the key step. For every FSM state, walk each\n   vocabulary token's string character by character from that state; if the walk never falls off the\n   automaton, the token is allowed in that state, and you record the state it lands in. The result is a\n   map `state -> {allowed token -> next state}`. It is built once per (pattern, tokenizer) pair *because*\n   it is expensive, then cached and reused across every request with the same schema.\n4. **Generate.** At each decoding step, look up the current state's allowed set, mask all other logits,\n   sample (greedy, temperature, whatever you configured) from the survivors, then follow the transition\n   to the next state. Per-step overhead is a lookup and a mask, not a vocabulary scan.\n\nFormally, with allowed set A(s) in state *s*, the sampled distribution is\n`p'(t) = p(t) · 1[t ∈ A(s)] / Σ_{u ∈ A(s)} p(u)`: the model's probabilities restricted to legal tokens and\nrenormalized. Building the index costs roughly (number of FSM states) × (vocabulary size) × (average token\nlength) character steps. That is why a schema with long enumerations or many optional fields, which\nmultiplies states, takes visibly longer to compile than a two-field one, and why the work happens once.\n\nWorking at the token level, not the character level, matters *because* tokens span several characters:\na single token such as `name` moves the FSM four characters forward in one step, and the index records\nthat, including tokens that are valid only because they straddle a boundary such as `\":\"`.\n\n### Grammars\nRegular expressions cannot describe arbitrarily nested structures. JSON with recursive schemas, or\ncode in a programming language, needs a context-free grammar, which requires a pushdown automaton\n(a state plus a stack). Outlines supports grammar-constrained generation too, but it is costlier and\nless mature than the regex path; engines such as vLLM also offer alternative grammar backends for this\nreason. Outlines' own core was later rewritten as a separate `outlines-core` package, and recent\nversions can delegate to other backends; which one runs is a version detail to check.\n\n### Where it runs\nOutlines wraps local model runtimes (Hugging Face Transformers, llama.cpp, MLX and others) and its core\nFSM logic is used inside serving engines; vLLM's guided decoding has offered an Outlines backend. The\npublic Python API has changed between major versions (older code uses `outlines.generate.json(model,\nSchema)`; the 1.x API passes the output type when calling the model), so check the version you install.\n\n### What constraining does to quality\nMasking renormalizes probability over the allowed tokens. If the model strongly wanted a token you\nforbade, it is pushed onto a path it considered unlikely, and the content on that path can be worse even\nthough the format is perfect. Two mechanisms make this worse than it sounds:\n- **The mask is greedy, not look-ahead.** It only asks \"can this token still lead to a valid string?\",\n  not \"is this a good continuation?\". If the model's mass sits on prose like `Sure! {\"intent\"...`, the\n  mask removes `Sure` and the model is renormalized into a state it was not planning for. Describing the\n  format in the prompt (or showing an example) keeps the model's own preference aligned with the mask, so\n  little probability is removed.\n- **Non-canonical tokenization.** The index allows any token sequence that spells a valid string, including\n  splits the tokenizer would never produce for that text (for example `\"` + `int` + `ent` where training\n  data always had `\"intent`). The model has rarely seen such sequences, so its later predictions degrade.\n  Mature implementations handle forced boundaries carefully; it is still a reason to measure accuracy, not\n  just parse rate.\n\nConstraints guarantee syntax, never correctness.","example":"A worked scenario: classifying 50,000 support tickets on a self-hosted 7B model into\n`{\"intent\": one of 6 labels, \"urgent\": true|false}`.\n\nUnconstrained with a careful prompt, suppose 4% of outputs fail to parse (an illustrative figure; measure\nyour own). With parse-and-retry, 2,000 tickets need at least one more generation, some need several, and\nthe p99 latency is now two or three generations long. With Outlines, the JSON Schema compiles once into an\nindex. Apart from whatever optional whitespace the generated pattern permits, the model can only emit\n`{\"intent\":\"`, then the tokens of one of the six labels, then the closing quote, `,\"urgent\":`, then\n`true` or `false`, then `}`. Every output parses; no retries exist.\n\nTwo more things happen. First, the model emits far fewer free tokens: most of the structure is forced, and\nwhen only one token is allowed in a state there is nothing to sample, and some implementations append\nsuch forced tokens directly and process several of them in a single forward pass. Second, field order is generation order, so if you want the model to reason, put a bounded\n`reasoning` string *before* `intent` in the schema; otherwise the label is chosen with no reasoning to\ncondition on.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nimport outlines\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nclass Ticket(BaseModel):\n    intent: Literal[\"billing\", \"bug\", \"how_to\", \"account\", \"feature\", \"other\"]\n    urgent: bool\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\"   # any local causal LM\nmodel = outlines.from_transformers(\n    AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name), AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name))\nraw = model(\"Classify: 'I was charged twice this month, fix it today.'\", Ticket, max_new_tokens=50)\nprint(Ticket.model_validate_json(raw))   # always parses; may still be the wrong label\n```","tradeoffs":["Validity is guaranteed on the first pass and retries vanish, but only when you own the inference stack; on hosted APIs you rely on the provider's own structured output or on validate-and-retry tools.","Index construction is paid once per schema and tokenizer; for a few fixed schemas it amortizes to nothing, but per-request dynamic schemas pay compile latency each time unless cached.","Hard constraints remove format errors yet can lower content quality when the mask fights the model's preferences; a looser pattern plus validation sometimes scores better on accuracy evals.","Regex-based JSON is fast and robust for flat or bounded schemas; deeply recursive structures need grammar mode, which costs more per step and is where alternative backends may perform better."],"pitfalls":["Unbounded strings or whitespace in the pattern let the model fill tokens until `max_new_tokens`; the symptom is outputs truncated at the limit, which then fail to parse despite the constraint.","Assuming constrained output is correct output; the symptom is a perfect parse rate alongside poor accuracy on a labelled set, because format and truth are different properties.","Putting the answer field before any reasoning field; the label is committed first, and the symptom is lower accuracy than the same model achieves in free-form chain-of-thought.","Copying tutorial code across major versions; the API was reorganised (the `outlines.generate.*` functions and the old `outlines.models.transformers(...)` loader are pre-1.0 idioms), and the symptom is `AttributeError` or `TypeError` on those calls after an upgrade.","Constraining the output without describing the format in the prompt; the mask then fights the model's preferred opening, and the symptom is valid JSON with odd or degenerate field values on easy inputs.","Forgetting the chat template for instruct models; passing a bare prompt degrades quality, which shows up as worse labels than the same model gives through its usual chat interface."],"when":"Use Outlines (or an engine's guided decoding) when you self-host open models and need structured output\nat volume: classification, extraction, routing, agent tool arguments on a small model. It is most\nvaluable where retries are expensive or latency-bound, and where small models break formats often. Do not\nreach for it with closed APIs, which do not expose logits; use their native structured output or a\nvalidate-and-retry library like Instructor. Measure parse rate (should become 100%), task accuracy with\nand without the constraint, and compile latency for new schemas. In interviews, explain the FSM index and\nwhy constraining fixes syntax but not semantics.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines schemas with bounded strings and sensible field order, wires constrained decoding into local inference, and compares accuracy with and without the constraint.","architect":"Decides whether structure is enforced at decode time on self-hosted models or by validation on hosted APIs, and which serving engine backend carries it.","fde":"In air-gapped or on-prem deployments with only small open models, uses constrained decoding to get reliable structured output where retries would blow latency budgets."},"check":{"q":"Why does Outlines precompute an index from FSM states to allowed tokens instead of checking the\nregex against each candidate token at generation time, and what does that cost you?","a":"Checking at generation time means testing every vocabulary token, tens or hundreds of thousands,\nagainst the pattern from the current state on every step, which adds a large per-token overhead. Since the\nset of valid tokens depends only on the FSM state, the pattern and the tokenizer, it can be computed once:\nfor each state, walk each token's characters through the automaton and record whether it survives and\nwhere it lands. Generation then becomes lookup plus masking. The cost moves to compile time and memory\nper schema, which is cheap for a few stable schemas but painful for per-request dynamic schemas, so you\ncache indexes and avoid generating schemas on the fly."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't Outlines enforce a schema on a closed hosted model API?","options":["Hosted models use a different tokenizer format that Outlines cannot parse","It must mask logits before each token is sampled, and hosted APIs do not expose that step","Hosted APIs reject requests containing regular expressions","JSON Schema is only supported by open-weight models"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Constrained decoding intervenes inside the sampling loop. Hosted APIs return finished tokens, so the only options there are the provider's own structured output or validating and retrying afterwards."},{"q":"A constrained classifier now parses 100% of outputs but its accuracy did not improve. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The FSM index was built for the wrong tokenizer","Constrained decoding always lowers temperature to zero","Constraints guarantee format, not correctness, so the model can still choose the wrong valid label","The schema compiled to a context-free grammar instead of a regex"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Masking removes invalid tokens but leaves the model's choice among valid ones untouched. Previous parse failures may also have been the hard cases, so accuracy can stay flat or even drop."},{"q":"Which schema design choice tends to cause truncated, unparseable outputs under constrained decoding?","options":["Using a Literal field with six allowed values","Putting a boolean field last in the object","An unbounded free-text string field, which lets generation run until max_new_tokens","Declaring all fields as required"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The constraint allows any length string, so a looping model keeps emitting valid characters until the token limit, leaving the JSON unclosed. Bounding length with maxLength prevents it."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/mcp-python-sdk","sources":["Official docs: Model Context Protocol","Official docs: MCP Python SDK"],"why":"Before MCP, every agent host defined tools its own way: an OpenAI function schema here, a LangChain tool\nclass there, a custom plugin format in each IDE. A team that wanted its internal search, ticketing and\ndatabase tools available in three assistants wrote three integrations and kept them in sync. MCP fixes\nthe interface: a server advertises tools, resources and prompts over a standard protocol, and any\ncompliant host can use them.\n\nImplementing that protocol by hand is not trivial, though. A server must speak JSON-RPC 2.0, negotiate a\nprotocol version and capabilities during `initialize`, describe every tool's input as JSON Schema, route\nrequests to handlers, handle notifications and cancellation, and do all of this over at least two\ntransports with different framing. Hand-rolled implementations get the details wrong: schemas drift from\nthe actual function signature, a stray `print` corrupts the stdio stream, version negotiation is skipped.\nThe SDK exists so that the protocol is handled once, correctly, and your code is just the tool logic.","how":"### Two layers\n- **FastMCP (high level).** `FastMCP(\"name\")` creates a server; decorators register capabilities. It\n  inspects each function's signature and type hints, builds a Pydantic model from them, and emits that\n  model's JSON Schema as the tool's `inputSchema`. The docstring becomes the tool description the LLM\n  reads. It does this *because* the function signature is already the source of truth, so deriving the\n  schema from it means the two cannot drift. Incoming arguments are validated against the same model\n  before your function runs.\n- **Low-level `Server`.** Explicit handlers for `list_tools`, `call_tool` and so on, for when you need\n  dynamic tool lists or full control over the wire format.\n\n(A separately maintained project, also called FastMCP, extends the original API that was folded into the\nofficial SDK; imports differ, so check which one a codebase uses.)\n\n### Three primitives\n- `@mcp.tool()` exposes an action the model may choose to call, for example `create_ticket`.\n- `@mcp.resource(\"tickets://{id}\")` exposes readable data addressed by URI, which the host (not\n  necessarily the model) decides to load into context.\n- `@mcp.prompt()` exposes a reusable prompt template a user can pick.\n\nThe split exists *because* control differs: tools are model-controlled, resources are application-\ncontrolled and prompts are user-controlled, and hosts present them differently.\n\n### The session lifecycle\n1. The client starts or connects to the server through a transport.\n2. `initialize`: client and server exchange protocol versions and capabilities.\n3. Discovery: `tools/list`, `resources/list`, `prompts/list`.\n4. Use: `tools/call` with JSON arguments; the result is a list of content items (text, image,\n   embedded resource), with an `isError` flag for tool-level failures. Recent protocol versions also\n   allow structured output validated against an output schema, and the SDK can derive it from a typed\n   return value.\n\nOn the wire, step 4 is one JSON-RPC request and one response matched by `id`, which is what lets many\ncalls be in flight on one connection:\n\n```\n-> {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":3,\"method\":\"tools/call\",\n    \"params\":{\"name\":\"create_ticket\",\"arguments\":{\"title\":\"VPN down\",\"priority\":\"high\"}}}\n<- {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":3,\"result\":{\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"{\\\"id\\\": 1, ...}\"}],\n    \"isError\":false}}\n```\n\nNote the two failure channels. A malformed request or unknown tool is a JSON-RPC `error` (a protocol\nfailure the host handles). A tool that ran and failed returns a normal `result` with `isError: true`,\n*because* that result is fed back to the model, which can read the message and try something else. Logs\nand progress travel as notifications (messages with no `id`) and need no reply.\n\n### Transports\n`mcp.run()` defaults to **stdio**: the host spawns your server as a subprocess and exchanges\nnewline-delimited JSON-RPC messages over stdin and stdout. It is simple and needs no network, which suits\nlocal tools. **Streamable HTTP** (`mcp.run(transport=\"streamable-http\")`) serves the same protocol over\nHTTP for remote, multi-client servers and is where authentication becomes your concern. The older\nHTTP+SSE transport is deprecated in favour of it. By default a Streamable HTTP server is *stateful*: it\nissues a session ID header after `initialize`, and later requests must reach the process holding that\nsession, so behind a load balancer you need sticky routing. FastMCP can instead run stateless\n(`stateless_http=True`), treating each request independently, which scales horizontally but gives up\nserver-initiated messages that depend on a live session.\n\n### Context, lifespan and server-to-client requests\nA tool can accept a `ctx: Context` parameter, which FastMCP injects (and hides from the schema), to send\nlog messages and progress notifications back to the client during long operations. MCP is\nbidirectional, so the same session also lets a server *ask the client* for things the client has\nadvertised as capabilities: **sampling** (have the host's LLM generate text, so the server needs no model\nkey of its own) and **elicitation** (ask the user for missing input mid-call). Hosts vary in which of\nthese they support, so check capabilities rather than assuming.\n\nShared resources such as a database pool belong in a `lifespan` async context manager passed to\n`FastMCP(...)`: it runs once at startup and shutdown and its yielded object is reachable from the\ncontext, *because* opening a connection per tool call repeats setup and leaks under load.\n\n### Client side\n`ClientSession` plus a transport helper such as `stdio_client` lets Python code act as a host: initialize,\nlist tools, call them. Everything is async, built on `anyio`.","example":"A small ticket server and a client that calls it over stdio.\n\n```python\n# server.py\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP, Context\n\nmcp = FastMCP(\"tickets\")\nDB: dict[int, dict] = {}\n\n@mcp.tool()\nasync def create_ticket(title: str, priority: Literal[\"low\", \"high\"], ctx: Context) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Create a support ticket. Use only after confirming the issue with the user.\"\"\"\n    tid = len(DB) + 1\n    DB[tid] = {\"id\": tid, \"title\": title, \"priority\": priority}\n    await ctx.info(f\"created ticket {tid}\")      # goes to the client, never to stdout\n    return DB[tid]\n\n@mcp.resource(\"tickets://{tid}\")\ndef read_ticket(tid: int) -> str:\n    return str(DB.get(int(tid), \"not found\"))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    mcp.run()                                    # stdio by default\n\n# client.py\nimport asyncio\nfrom mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def main():\n    params = StdioServerParameters(command=\"python\", args=[\"server.py\"])\n    async with stdio_client(params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            print([t.name for t in (await session.list_tools()).tools])\n            res = await session.call_tool(\"create_ticket\", {\"title\": \"VPN down\", \"priority\": \"high\"})\n            print(res.content[0].text, res.isError)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nThe `priority` hint becomes an `enum` in the schema, so the model sees exactly two legal values, and a\ncall with `\"urgent\"` is rejected before your code runs. The generated `inputSchema` for `create_ticket`\nis roughly `{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"title\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"priority\":{\"enum\":[\"low\",\"high\"],\n\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"title\",\"priority\"]}`; `ctx` is absent because FastMCP injects it. With the\nSDK's CLI extra installed, `mcp dev server.py` opens the MCP Inspector, where you can see that schema and\ncall the tool by hand before any LLM is involved.","tradeoffs":["Writing one MCP server makes a tool usable from every compliant host, but you pay a process boundary and protocol overhead; for a tool used by exactly one in-process agent, a plain function call is simpler.","stdio is zero-config and has no network attack surface, but runs one server process per client and cannot be shared; Streamable HTTP is shareable and remote, at the cost of auth, TLS and deployment.","FastMCP derives schemas from type hints so they never drift, but anything the hints cannot express (cross-field rules) must be enforced in the function; drop to the low-level server for dynamic tools.","Many small, focused tools give the model precise choices, but every tool's schema occupies context on every turn; past a few dozen tools, selection accuracy and token cost both suffer."],"pitfalls":["Using `print()` in a stdio server; stdout is the protocol channel, and the symptom is the host reporting parse errors or the server \"disconnecting\" right after a log line.","Docstrings written for humans rather than models; the model picks tools from these descriptions, and the symptom is the wrong tool chosen or a destructive tool called without the intended precondition.","Raising vague exceptions for expected failures; FastMCP catches an exception from a tool and returns it as an `isError` result carrying the exception's message, so `raise Exception(\"failed\")` gives the model nothing to act on, and the symptom is it retrying the same bad call in a loop. Raise (or return) a message that says what was wrong and what to do instead.","Blocking calls (sync database drivers, `time.sleep`) inside async tools; the whole server stalls, which shows up as every tool timing out whenever one slow call is in flight.","Running a stateful Streamable HTTP server behind a round-robin load balancer; requests land on a process that never saw the session, and the symptom is intermittent \"session not found\" or 4xx errors that vanish with a single replica.","Exposing a Streamable HTTP server without authentication or origin checks; the symptom is discovering that anything that can reach the port can invoke your tools."],"when":"Use the SDK when you want tools or data usable across several agent hosts (IDE assistants, desktop\nchat apps, your own agents), or when a customer's platform already speaks MCP. Prefer stdio for local,\nsingle-user tools and Streamable HTTP for shared services behind proper auth. Do not wrap every internal\nfunction as an MCP tool when one application calls it in-process. Measure tool-call error rate, latency\nper tool and how often the model picks the intended tool. For the protocol design, security model and\nwhen MCP is the right boundary, see the agents module's MCP lessons; in interviews, expect to explain the\nlifecycle and why stdout matters on stdio.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes FastMCP tools with precise type hints and model-facing docstrings, logs through the context or stderr, and tests servers with a real client session.","architect":"Decides which capabilities become shared MCP servers versus in-process functions, which transport each uses, and where authentication and authorization are enforced.","fde":"Wraps a customer's internal APIs as an MCP server so their existing assistant hosts can use them without bespoke integrations per tool."},"check":{"q":"A stdio MCP server works when you run it by hand but the host shows \"connection closed\" as soon\nas one particular tool is called. What do you suspect, and how do you confirm it?","a":"On stdio, stdout carries the JSON-RPC stream, so anything else written there, a `print`, a\nlibrary's progress bar, a warning sent to stdout, corrupts the framing and the host drops the session.\nRunning by hand hides this because you are not parsing the stream. Confirm by checking the host's MCP\nlogs for a parse error, grepping that tool's code path for prints, or running it under a real\n`ClientSession`. Fix by logging to stderr or through `ctx` logging, and redirecting noisy libraries.\nA second suspect is the process itself dying in that code path (a native-extension crash, `sys.exit`,\nan out-of-memory kill), which the server's stderr or exit code would show; an ordinary Python exception\ninside a FastMCP tool does not do this, because it is returned to the client as an `isError` result."},"quiz":[{"q":"How does FastMCP produce a tool's input schema?","options":["You must write the JSON Schema by hand in the decorator","It builds a Pydantic model from the function's type hints and emits that model's JSON Schema","The host infers the schema by calling the tool with sample arguments","It parses the docstring for parameter types"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FastMCP inspects the signature and type hints, so the schema cannot drift from the code. The docstring supplies the description the model reads, not the types."},{"q":"Why should a stdio MCP server never write logs to stdout?","options":["stdout is buffered and logs would arrive out of order","The protocol forbids any logging during a session","stdout carries the JSON-RPC messages, so extra output corrupts the stream the host parses","Hosts discard stdout, so the logs would be lost"],"answer":2,"explanation":"On stdio the two processes talk over stdin and stdout. Logging belongs on stderr or in protocol log notifications through the context; stdout output breaks message framing."},{"q":"When is Streamable HTTP the better transport than stdio?","options":["When the tool runs on the same laptop as a single desktop host","When you want to avoid writing any authentication code","When the server must print debugging output freely","When one server should be shared by many clients or run remotely as a service"],"answer":3,"explanation":"stdio spawns a private subprocess per client, which is ideal locally. A shared or remote server needs HTTP, which also brings authentication and network security into scope."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/httpx","sources":["Official docs: HTTPX"],"why":"`requests` defined the ergonomic Python HTTP API, but it is synchronous. An AI service that fans out to\nan LLM, a vector store and two tools per user request spends nearly all its time waiting on the network;\nwith a blocking client inside an async web framework, each wait blocks the event loop and the server\nhandles one request at a time. Threads work around that but scale poorly to thousands of concurrent\nstreams. `aiohttp` is async-only, with a different API, so code and tests cannot be shared with sync\nscripts.\n\nLLM traffic adds two further needs. Responses are streamed as server-sent events over many seconds, so\nthe client must expose bytes or lines incrementally rather than buffer the body. And requests are\nexpensive and long, so timeouts, connection reuse and pool limits stop being defaults you ignore and\nbecome things you must set deliberately. httpx provides one API, `httpx.Client` and\n`httpx.AsyncClient`, with identical methods, streaming, explicit fine-grained timeouts and a transport\nlayer you can swap for a mock in tests.","how":"### Clients own connection pools\n`httpx.get(...)` is a convenience that opens and discards a connection. A `Client`/`AsyncClient` keeps a\npool of keep-alive connections, so repeated calls to the same host skip TCP and TLS handshakes, often the\nlargest part of a short request's latency. That is *why* you create one client per process (or per\nupstream) and reuse it, closing it on shutdown with a context manager or `aclose()`. Pool size is set by\n`httpx.Limits(max_connections=..., max_keepalive_connections=...)` (defaults 100 and 20); when the pool\nis full, further requests wait for a free connection, and that wait has its own timeout. The pool is\ntherefore also your concurrency limit to that upstream: by Little's law, sustainable throughput is about\n`max_connections ÷ average request duration`, so 50 connections holding 10-second streams carry about 5\nrequests per second, whatever the CPU could do.\n\n### Timeouts are per phase\n`httpx.Timeout` separates **connect** (establishing the socket), **read** (waiting for the next chunk\nof data), **write** (sending a chunk) and **pool** (waiting for a free connection). The default is 5\nseconds for each. Crucially, a read timeout bounds the gap *between* chunks, not the total duration. A\nstreamed completion that sends a token every 100 ms never trips a 5 s read timeout even if it runs for\nten minutes, while a reasoning model that thinks for 30 s before its first byte trips it immediately. For\na total deadline you wrap the call in `asyncio.timeout(...)` (or anyio's equivalent).\n\n### Streaming\n`client.stream(\"POST\", url, json=...)` is a context manager that returns once headers arrive; you then\niterate `aiter_bytes()`, `aiter_text()` or `aiter_lines()`. Leaving the context closes the response and\nreturns the connection to the pool, *which is why* the context-manager form matters: a streamed\nresponse that is never closed keeps its connection checked out of the pool.\n\n### Behaviour that differs from requests\n- Redirects are **not** followed by default (`follow_redirects=True` to enable).\n- Timeouts are on by default; `requests` waits forever unless told otherwise.\n- `raise_for_status()` raises `httpx.HTTPStatusError`; network-level failures are `httpx.RequestError`\n  subclasses such as `ConnectError`, `ReadTimeout` and `PoolTimeout`.\n\n### Transports\nEvery client sends through a transport. `httpx.HTTPTransport(retries=n)` (or `AsyncHTTPTransport` for\nan `AsyncClient`) retries only failures to establish a connection, not HTTP 429 or 5xx, so application retries with backoff remain your job (Tenacity, or the\nSDK's built-in retries). `httpx.MockTransport(handler)` routes requests to a Python function, giving\nfast deterministic tests with no network. `httpx.ASGITransport(app=app)` sends requests straight into an\nASGI app such as FastAPI, in-process. HTTP/2 needs the optional extra (`httpx[http2]`) and `http2=True`,\nand multiplexes many requests over one connection.\n\n### Hooks and SDK integration\n`event_hooks={\"request\": [...], \"response\": [...]}` runs your functions on every request and response,\nthe usual place to add trace headers, log latency per upstream or redact payloads, *because* it covers\nevery call made through the client, including calls an SDK makes for you. Provider SDKs accept a\npre-built client (`OpenAI(http_client=httpx.Client(...))`, `AsyncOpenAI(http_client=httpx.AsyncClient(...))`)\nso you control proxies, certificates, limits and hooks; the SDK still applies its own timeout and retry\nsettings on top, so set those on the SDK rather than assuming httpx's 5 s default is in force.","example":"Streaming an OpenAI-compatible completion with deliberate limits, a total deadline and a mock for tests.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json, httpx\n\nTIMEOUT = httpx.Timeout(connect=5.0, read=60.0, write=10.0, pool=2.0)  # slow first token is normal\nLIMITS = httpx.Limits(max_connections=50, max_keepalive_connections=20)\n\nasync def stream_chat(client: httpx.AsyncClient, prompt: str) -> str:\n    body = {\"model\": \"my-model\", \"stream\": True,\n            \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}]}\n    parts = []\n    async with asyncio.timeout(120):                      # total deadline (Python 3.11+)\n        async with client.stream(\"POST\", \"/v1/chat/completions\", json=body) as r:\n            r.raise_for_status()\n            async for line in r.aiter_lines():\n                if line.startswith(\"data: \") and line != \"data: [DONE]\":\n                    delta = json.loads(line[6:])[\"choices\"][0][\"delta\"]\n                    parts.append(delta.get(\"content\") or \"\")\n    return \"\".join(parts)\n\ndef fake(request: httpx.Request) -> httpx.Response:       # test double, no network\n    chunk = {\"choices\": [{\"delta\": {\"content\": \"hi\"}}]}\n    return httpx.Response(200, text=f\"data: {json.dumps(chunk)}\\n\\ndata: [DONE]\\n\\n\")\n\nasync def main():\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient(base_url=\"https://llm.internal\", timeout=TIMEOUT, limits=LIMITS,\n                                 transport=httpx.MockTransport(fake)) as client:\n        print(await asyncio.gather(*(stream_chat(client, f\"q{i}\") for i in range(3))))\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nDrop the `transport=` argument and the same code talks to a real server. Now walk the limits against a\nburst of 200 concurrent calls whose streams last about 8 s each. The first 50 get connections; the other\n150 wait in the pool. Throughput is 50 ÷ 8 ≈ 6 requests per second, so the burst takes about 32 s to drain\nand the last request would wait about 24 s for a connection. With `pool=2.0`, every request still waiting\nafter 2 s fails with `PoolTimeout`, so roughly 150 of the 200 fail fast instead of queueing. That is a\ndeliberate choice: fail fast and shed load (return 503, let the caller retry) versus a long pool timeout\nwhere users wait in line. Pick it consciously, and if you want queueing, put an explicit `asyncio.Semaphore`\nor job queue in front so the wait is visible and bounded, rather than hiding it in the pool.","tradeoffs":["One API for sync and async lets scripts, services and tests share code, but async only helps if every other call on the path is async too; one blocking call still stalls the loop.","A shared long-lived client gives pooling and lower latency, at the cost of lifecycle management: creating it at startup, closing it at shutdown, and not sharing it across event loops or forked processes.","Per-phase timeouts let you allow slow first tokens while failing fast on dead hosts, but they never express a total budget; long streams need an outer deadline as well.","HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests over one connection, which helps with many small calls to one host, but adds a dependency and can concentrate load on one backend behind a connection-level load balancer."],"pitfalls":["Sizing `max_connections` for short calls, then routing long streamed completions through the same client and short pool timeout; the symptom is bursts of `PoolTimeout` during spikes while the upstream itself reports normal latency.","Creating a new `AsyncClient` per request; every call repeats the TLS handshake, and the symptom is higher latency plus \"too many open files\" or ephemeral-port exhaustion under load.","Keeping the 5 s default read timeout for LLM calls; long prompts or reasoning models delay the first byte, and the symptom is `ReadTimeout` only on big inputs while small ones succeed.","Assuming the read timeout caps total duration; a slowly trickling stream runs far past your budget, which shows up as requests hanging for minutes without any timeout firing.","Expecting `HTTPTransport(retries=3)` to retry 429 or 503; it only retries connection failures, and the symptom is rate-limit errors surfacing straight to users.","Opening streams with `client.send(request, stream=True)` and abandoning the response without `aclose()` (for example on an early `break` or exception); connections leak, and the symptom is `PoolTimeout` after a period of normal traffic."],"when":"Use httpx whenever Python code makes HTTP calls in an AI system: direct calls to model servers, tool\nAPIs, webhooks, and as the `http_client` you pass to provider SDKs to control proxies, timeouts and\nlimits. Choose `AsyncClient` inside async frameworks and agents; the sync client is fine for scripts and\nbatch jobs. Prefer `requests` only in legacy synchronous code where nothing else changes. Measure p50/p99\nlatency per upstream, time to first byte, timeout counts per phase, and pool wait time; pool wait rising\nwhile upstream latency is flat means your connection limit, not the provider, is the bottleneck. In interviews, expect questions on why\na read timeout is not a deadline and why clients must be shared.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Creates one shared `AsyncClient` per upstream with explicit timeouts and limits, streams with context managers, and tests with `MockTransport` instead of patching.","architect":"Sets the timeout, retry and concurrency budgets for each dependency so that one slow model provider cannot exhaust connections for the whole service.","fde":"Configures proxies, custom CA bundles and mutual TLS through the client so SDK traffic works inside a customer's locked-down corporate network."},"check":{"q":"Your service streams completions through httpx with `timeout=30`. Short prompts work; 100k-token\nprompts fail with `ReadTimeout`, and occasionally a request hangs for ten minutes. Explain both.","a":"A single number sets every phase, so read is 30 s between chunks. With a 100k-token prompt the\nserver's prefill, plus any queueing on a busy server, can take longer than 30 s before the first byte,\nso the read timeout fires. The ten-minute\nhang is the opposite case: once tokens flow, each chunk resets the read timer, so a stream that keeps\ntrickling never trips it. Fix both by raising the read timeout to cover worst-case time to first token,\nand wrapping the whole call in `asyncio.timeout` for a total deadline, plus metrics on time to first\nbyte and total duration per request."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does httpx's read timeout actually bound?","options":["The total time from sending the request to receiving the full response","The maximum wait for the next chunk of data from the server","Only the time to establish the TCP and TLS connection","The time a request waits for a free pooled connection"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Read timeouts reset whenever data arrives, so a slow but steady stream never trips them. Total deadlines need an outer timeout; connect and pool waits are separate phases."},{"q":"Why should an async service create one shared AsyncClient rather than one per request?","options":["AsyncClient objects cannot be garbage collected","The shared client's pool reuses keep-alive connections, avoiding repeated handshakes and socket exhaustion","Per-request clients cannot stream responses","httpx raises an error if more than one client exists per process"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Connection pooling lives in the client. A new client per call pays TCP and TLS setup every time and can run out of file descriptors under load, while streaming works either way."},{"q":"A team sets HTTPTransport(retries=3) and still sees 429 errors reach users. Why?","options":["The retries parameter only applies to synchronous clients","429 responses are retried, but without backoff they always fail again","Transport retries cover connection failures only, not HTTP status codes like 429","Retries are disabled when HTTP/2 is enabled"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The transport retries failed connects, not error responses. Retrying on 429 or 5xx with backoff and respect for Retry-After belongs in application code or the provider SDK."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/jupyter","sources":["Official docs: Project Jupyter"],"why":"Much AI work is exploratory: look at 50 failing eval cases, try three prompt variants, plot score\ndistributions, inspect what the retriever returned. In a script, each question means re-running\neverything from the start: reloading a model, re-reading a dataset, re-calling an API you pay for. Seconds\nor minutes of setup dominate a two-second question, and output scrolls away in a terminal. A plain REPL\nkeeps state but loses history and cannot show tables, images or charts well.\n\nJupyter separates a **kernel** that holds state from a **front end** that sends code to it, so you load\nthe model or dataframe once and then ask questions in small cells, each with its output rendered inline\nand saved next to the code. The result doubles as a record others can read. The naive alternative,\nscripts with print statements, is reproducible but slow to iterate; the notebook trades some\nreproducibility for iteration speed, and knowing exactly what you gave up is the skill.","how":"### Architecture\n- **Kernel.** A separate process (for Python, `ipykernel`, built on IPython) that executes code and\n  holds all variables. It is separate *because* the front end can then crash, reconnect or be a different\n  program entirely without losing state, and kernels for other languages plug in the same way.\n- **Messaging protocol.** Front ends talk to kernels over ZeroMQ sockets using a documented message\n  protocol: execute requests, streamed output, rich display data, interrupts. That protocol is why\n  JupyterLab, the classic Notebook interface, VS Code and hosted notebook services can all drive the same\n  kernel.\n- **Server.** Jupyter Server manages kernels, files and the browser-facing API.\n- **Document.** An `.ipynb` is JSON: a list of cells, each with source, an execution count and outputs\n  (text, HTML, base64 images). Outputs are stored *in* the file, which is why a notebook can be read\n  without re-running it, and also why it can carry data and secrets you did not intend to share.\n\n### State is the execution history, not the file\nThe kernel's state is whatever cells you ran, in the order you ran them, including cells you have since\nedited or deleted. The file on disk shows the current text of each cell, which may not be the code that\nproduced the variables in memory. Execution counts (`[7]`, `[3]`) out of order are the visible clue.\nThe only proof that a notebook works is **Restart Kernel and Run All** producing the same results.\n\n### Kernels, environments and memory\nWhich Python a notebook uses is set by its **kernelspec**, a small JSON file naming the interpreter to\nlaunch, not by the shell you started Jupyter from. Registering a virtual environment\n(`python -m ipykernel install --user --name myenv`) is how a project's environment becomes a selectable\nkernel. This explains the install trap: `!pip` runs whatever `pip` is first on the server's `PATH`, which\nmay belong to another environment, while `%pip` installs with the kernel's own interpreter.\n\nBecause the kernel lives until you restart it, so does everything it references. IPython also keeps\nevery displayed result in its output history (`Out`, `_`, `__`), so a large DataFrame or tensor you\ndisplayed stays referenced even after you `del` the variable. Re-running a \"load model\" cell creates a\nsecond copy before the first can be freed. On a GPU that is the classic notebook out-of-memory error:\nthe first load fits, the re-run does not, and only a kernel restart reliably gives the memory back.\n\n### Useful machinery\n- Magics: `%timeit` for micro-benchmarks, `%autoreload` to pick up edits to imported modules, `!pip`\n  and `%pip` (the latter installs into the kernel's environment, which is what you usually want).\n- Top-level `await` works in IPython kernels *because* the kernel already runs an event loop; the same\n  fact is why `asyncio.run()` fails inside a notebook with \"cannot be called from a running event loop\".\n- Headless execution: `jupyter nbconvert --execute` runs a notebook top to bottom; papermill injects\n  parameters and runs notebooks as batch jobs, which some teams use for scheduled reports.\n- Version control helpers: tools such as nbstripout remove outputs before commit, and Jupytext pairs a\n  notebook with a plain `.py` file so diffs and reviews are readable.","example":"A worked scenario: error analysis on an eval run of 2,000 RAG answers.\n\nCell 1 loads the eval results JSONL into a pandas DataFrame and, once, the embedding model (about 30\nseconds). Cell 2 filters the 140 rows the judge scored as failures (illustrative numbers). Cell 3 shows\nten of them with question, retrieved chunks and answer side by side. You notice many failures involve\ntables, so cell 4 adds a `has_table` flag by regex over the retrieved chunks and stores a cross-tab of\ndocument type against `has_table` for the failing rows in a variable, `by_type`. Cell 5 plots `by_type`.\nEach question takes seconds because the model and data stay in memory.\n\nThen the trap. You edit cell 2's filter to include \"partial\" scores and re-run cells 2 and 5 but not\ncell 4, so `by_type` still holds counts from the old subset and the chart is silently wrong; nothing\nerrors. The notebook file shows the new filter, so a reviewer cannot tell. Before sharing, you Restart and Run All: cell 4 now runs on the new\nsubset and the chart changes. That is the habit that makes the notebook trustworthy. Finally, the\n`has_table` heuristic moves into `analysis/features.py` with a test, and the notebook imports it, so the\nlogic outlives the exploration.\n\n```python\n%load_ext autoreload\n%autoreload 2\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom analysis.features import has_table      # logic lives in a module, not a cell\n\ndf = pd.read_json(\"runs/eval-2025-03.jsonl\", lines=True)\nfails = df[df.score < 3].assign(has_table=lambda d: d.chunks.map(has_table))\npd.crosstab(fails.doc_type, fails.has_table)\n```","tradeoffs":["Persistent kernel state makes iteration fast when setup is expensive, but hidden state means results may not reproduce; the balance flips the moment the output informs a decision others rely on.","Outputs saved in the file make notebooks readable without re-running, yet they bloat diffs and can leak customer data or keys; strip outputs for code review, keep them only for curated reports.","Notebooks are excellent for exploration and teaching, but poor for modular, tested code; once logic is reused or scheduled, it belongs in a package that the notebook imports.","Parameterized notebook execution turns analyses into repeatable jobs cheaply, but a scheduled notebook is harder to test, type-check and monitor than a script with the same logic."],"pitfalls":["Trusting results from out-of-order execution; the symptom is a colleague running the notebook top to bottom and getting different numbers, or a `NameError` for a variable you deleted.","Committing outputs with API keys, tokens or customer records; the symptom is secrets or personal data appearing in the repository history and in rendered previews.","Calling `asyncio.run()` in a cell; it raises because the kernel's loop is already running, and the usual hack, patching nested loops, hides the problem instead of using top-level `await`.","Re-running a model-loading cell, or displaying large tensors, in a long session; the symptom is CUDA out-of-memory on a cell that worked an hour ago, cleared only by restarting the kernel.","Installing with `!pip` into a different interpreter than the kernel's; the symptom is a successful install followed by `ModuleNotFoundError` in the next cell.","Shipping a notebook as the production service; the symptom is no tests, no error handling, and an outage when someone runs cells in a new order."],"when":"Use Jupyter for exploration and communication: data inspection, error analysis on eval results, prompt\niteration, quick model comparisons, and demos for stakeholders. Move code out as soon as it is reused,\nscheduled or needs tests. Do not treat a notebook's numbers as final until Restart and Run All reproduces\nthem, and pin the environment so others can. For tracking runs and making evaluations reproducible, see\nthe LLMOps lessons; notebooks complement experiment tracking rather than replace it. In interviews,\nexpect to explain hidden state and how you keep notebook analysis honest.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Explores data and failure cases in notebooks, then promotes stable logic into tested modules the notebook imports, with outputs stripped before commit.","architect":"Sets the boundary: notebooks for exploration and reports, packages and pipelines for anything scheduled or served, plus rules on data allowed in saved outputs.","fde":"Uses a notebook to walk a customer through their own data and model results live, then hands over a cleaned, re-run version as the record of findings."},"check":{"q":"A colleague's notebook shows a 12-point accuracy improvement from a new prompt. What would you\ncheck before believing it, and why is a notebook specifically prone to this kind of error?","a":"I would restart the kernel and run all cells in order, because the kernel's state reflects the\nexecution history, not the file: an edited cell, a variable left over from an earlier experiment or a\nfilter applied to only one arm can produce a difference that the visible code does not explain. I would\nalso check that both prompts ran on the same rows, with the same model and settings, that the evaluation\nfunction is identical for both, and that the sample is large enough for a 12-point gap to be meaningful.\nThen I would move the comparison into a script or eval harness so it can be re-run."},"quiz":[{"q":"What determines the values of variables in a running Jupyter kernel?","options":["The current text of the cells, read top to bottom","The cells actually executed, in the order they were executed, including since-edited ones","The last saved version of the .ipynb file","Only the most recently executed cell"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The kernel is a live process that accumulates state from every execution. The file shows current cell text, which may differ from what produced the state, hence Restart and Run All."},{"q":"Why does asyncio.run() fail inside a standard IPython notebook cell?","options":["Notebooks do not support asynchronous code at all","The kernel uses threads instead of an event loop","The kernel already runs an event loop, and asyncio.run() refuses to start one inside a running loop","asyncio is not installed in the default kernel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"IPython kernels run their own event loop, which is also why top-level await works directly in a cell. asyncio.run() wants to create and own a new loop, so it raises."},{"q":"A team wants readable code reviews of notebooks without leaking data. Which practice best addresses both?","options":["Converting every notebook to PDF before committing","Committing notebooks with full outputs so reviewers can see results","Stripping outputs before commit and pairing notebooks with plain-text .py files","Storing notebooks only on a shared drive outside version control"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Outputs cause noisy diffs and can contain sensitive data. Stripping them, and pairing with a text representation such as Jupytext, gives clean diffs while keeping the notebook usable."}]}]});
